8,36-83-4001,36,83,4001,8496.0,1983-12-16,1999-03-18 13:49:10,PEAVEY OIL COMPANY,101 N HWY 99W,MCMINNVILLE,97128,(503)472-6138,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"(ARL 04/16/20) A map showing the approximate location of the disposal characterization soil sample is attached (base map is from the CMMP). The soil sample was collected from the bottom of a new sewer line trench in Baker Street at approximately 5.5 feet below ground surface. The contaminated soils encountered in the trench on Monday, April 13, 2020 extended from approximately the northwest corner of the Train Chevron #33 Offices building to the southwestern corner of the building. The trench excavation stopped at a location that roughly lined up with the southwest corner of the building.  The contaminated soils that were encountered were gray and had a gasoline odor. ","36-90-4062 Peavy Oil (no file; contents merged into 36-83-4001)
ECSI #376","The earliest site uses are unknown. However, the property was developed as a fuel station by at least 1954. Since that time, site use has not changed and layout has not significantly changed. Contamination was discovered in 1983 when leaks were discovered from two product pumps and several product lines. Tanks were later removed and also found to have leaked. Soil and groundwater contamination (gasoline, diesel, and related constituents) were found on-site and extended out into Baker Street and Highway 99W right-of-ways. Contaminants onsite exceed vapor intrusion standards, and soil gases have not been sampled but are assumed to exceed screening levels. Initially a groundwater cutoff trench and product recovery well were installed on the north and east sides of the fueling canopy. About 1,229 tons of contaminated soil were disposed of at the Riverbend Landfill.  A dual-phase extraction system was installed and operated from 2002 to 2011, processing about 3.6 million gallons of contaminated groundwater. In March 2018, the concrete slab on the first floor of the onsite building was sealed with an epoxy coating to mitigate potential vapor intrusion into the building. Contaminated soil and groundwater remain across the site, slightly into Baker Street, and across Highway 99W.  Land use at the site and surrounding properties is expected to remain commercial with the exception of the McMinnville High School ball fields, which are not expected to change.  An opportunity to comment on the closure was given to all neighbors, utilities, and the city of McMinnville.  No significant comments were received. An Easement and Equitable Servitudes document was recorded on May 14, 2018 on the property and restricts the following: residential and urban residential property use is prohibited; the existing onsite building must have the sealed concrete floor maintained and new construction must have an engineered vapor barrier below the foundation; and the approved Contaminated Media Management Plan must be followed within the area of restrictions defined in the document. This determination applies to soil, groundwater, and soil gas on the site and within the Baker Street and Highway 99W right-of-ways.",39940.0,0.0,ALEIDEL,2020-06-16 08:04:04.703
