49,24-86-4003,24,86,4003,4210.0,1986-04-24,1999-03-18 13:49:10,ARCO AM-PM (Brooks),4220 BROOKLAKE RD NE,BROOKS,97303,(423)588-7488,False,,,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : Applied GeoSystems - Consultant *** Letter of 4-25-1990, indicates CAP already submitted.LLJ  Letter received by WVR which request delay in submission of new sampling plan and data.  New date to be March 29, 1991 for submission of report.LLJ",ECSI # 729,"A petroleum release was reported at the site in 1986 and a second diesel release was reported in June 2007. The underground storage tanks are located in the northern portion of the property which is where the petroleum release and subsequent cleanup activities have occurred. Petroleum cleanup activities have included source removal, groundwater monitoring, and soil vapor extraction. Seven shallow monitoring wells are currently located in the northern portion of the property and are sampled semi-annually for natural attenuation monitoring. The monitoring wells are screened from approximately 8 to 30 feet below ground surface. 

One onsite domestic well currently provides water to the site building for the use of drinking, showering, fountain drinks, and food preparation. This well is estimated to serve 500 people per day, 25 or more of these are served on a daily basis. This well was drilled to a total depth of 180 feet with perforations from 126 to 134 feet. Between July 2003 and December 2016 this well was sampled for volatile organic compounds eight times. 1,2-dichloroethane/ethylene dichloride (EDC) was detected in all eight samples at concentrations ranging from 1.8 µg/L in July 2003 to 5.8 µg/L in May 2015. Two of the sampling events (May 2015 and May 2016) show concentrations are above the EPA Maximum Contaminant Level. The source or sources of the EDC and the extent of impacts are not known. The investigation at this site is currently ongoing.",57467.0,0.0,TDANOVI,2017-10-18 18:59:21.833
50,36-86-4004,36,86,4004,7340.0,1986-05-10,1999-03-18 13:49:10,ALEXANDER OIL COMPANY,2600 E HANCOCK ST,NEWBERG,97132,538-2513,False,4.0,GW,4.0,NFA,True,False,,,False,,,,,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : Roger N. Smith, Consultant *** Bulk plant that should be referred to ECD, Gas and Diesel impacts to SOIL and GW...RS 6-30-93.  Pjt Mgr = JLP",See Also: 36-91-4165,,22968.0,,GDECK,2002-10-25 17:10:36.000
51,24-87-4019,24,87,4019,,1987-03-27,1999-03-18 13:49:10,HURLBUTT PROPERTY,201 S WATER ST,SILVERTON,97381,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : Tanks pumped, filled on sitee with sand product. *** One test pit will probably go off site as of 11/22/95;  obvious gasoline impacts.",,"Petroleum contamination was initially reported to DEQ in early 1987 petroleum was observed in the City of Silverton storm drain system. In response to this discovery the property owner emptied and decommissioned the five underground storage tanks in-place by filling them with sand and gravel. In 1995 the owner had the tanks and an estimated 300 cubic yards of contaminated soil removed from the north side of the property. Contamination was observed throughout the former tank pit and was documented to have impacted both soil and groundwater.  After review of the soil and groundwater sample results, the property owner conducted an investigation to determine the extent of the contamination on the subject property. This included installation of three groundwater monitoring wells and quarterly groundwater monitoring. In 2007, the current property owner expanded the investigation by installing a series of push-probe sampling points in the Water and Lewis Streets, as well as on properties north and west of the site. Although the petroleum pollution was confirmed beneath both Streets, the remaining contamination has not significantly impacted adjacent properties and does not extend to Silver Creek.
Based on the results of the completed cleanup actions, the on-site monitoring and off-site investigations, and risk-assessment of the remain areas of contaminated soil and groundwater, DEQ has determined that, given the current and reasonably likely future land uses, the remaining contamination has been reduced to levels protective of human health and the environment. Remaining contamination will continue to naturally degrade in place.",24632.0,0.0,GDECK,2010-09-24 15:19:14.033
