Site address originally reported as Webb & Water St.  Update to correct specific address of 621 N Water St.... gdeck 9/11/2019","SEE ALSO USTC NO. 24-87-4018 (KEY BANK - WEBB & WATER STREETS, SILVERTON.",,20431.0,0.0,GDECK,2019-09-11 13:33:26.857
72,36-87-4016,36,87,4016,,1987-11-05,1999-03-18 13:49:10,Butler Chevrolet,411 E 1ST ST,NEWBERG,97132,503-538-4593,False,1.0,RBCA,4.0,,True,False,False,3.0,True,False,False,False,False,Referred to ECD chlorinated solvents...RS 6-30-93.   JLG assigned as cross-program coordinator 3/4/03.,ECSI # 1876,"The property is the former location of the Butler Chevrolet dealership from the 1930s until April 2001.  The dealership operations included a service/gas station until the 1950s and an auto body shop from the 1970s to 1980s.  

Petroleum and solvent contamination was discovered in 1987 during an odor complaint investigation, which lead to discovery of leaking underground storage tanks at the auto dealership.  Two underground storage tanks were decommissioned during the following months.  A waste-oil tank was removed and a gasoline tank, found partially under the building, was cleaned out and filled with cement.  In 2001, the City of Newberg acquired the property after DEQ, in cooperation with the Environmental Protection Agency, conducted a Targeted Brownfields Assessment to evaluate the known and suspected sources of contamination at the site.  

Removal of the building, cleanup of the site, compliance sampling, and an exposure risk evaluation were funded through a Community Development Block Grant from Oregon Economic and Community Development Department. Site cleanup included the removal of over 300 cubic yards of contaminated soil followed by groundwater sampling which was conducted quarterly between August 2005 and June 2006.  

DEQ has determined that, given the site’s current and reasonably likely future land use as commercial or commercial with residential above the 1st floor, the remaining contamination from the historical use of the property has been reduced to levels that do not pose a threat to human health and the environment.  The closure plan proposes to allow the remaining contamination in soil and shallow groundwater to biodegrade naturally in place.",123965.0,0.0,iPMO#38,2016-10-14 15:09:19.980
