78,02-88-4003,02,88,4003,923.0,1988-08-15,1999-03-18 13:49:10,UNOCAL Washington,429 SW 3RD,CORVALLIS,97330,(503)753-3800,False,1.0,GW,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : 05-dec-88 well #2 has 6"" product installing skimmer. *** Free product removal, CAP developement

Revised remedial plan submitted to install 2 recovery wells on NE corner i
of site.  Two add'l MWs to be installed off-site.

Free product continues to be measured at MWs 1,",ECSI 715 (oversight by DEQ Tanks Program),"The site was an operating fuel service station beginning in 1930 until the facility closed in 1991. A site investigation was initiated in 1988 that identified a petroleum release. Four monitoring wells were installed  and free phase petroleum was discovered. Abatement measures included vacuum-enhanced fluid recovery, and free product was present in wells until at least 2002. The original tank system (two 10,000-gallon gasoline tanks, 550-gallon heating oil, and a 550-gallon waste oil tank) was decommissioned by removal in 1989. Approximately 716 cubic yards of petroleum-contaminated soil was excavated from the tank cavity during decommissioning activities. In 2004, contractors removed 6,500 tons of PCS from the site, with the excavation encompassing almost the entire property and extending to 20 feet bgs. A Masonry Well was encountered in the center of the excavation (soil was removed here to 25 feet bgs). Soil samples  identified petroleum contaminants remaining at the south and west sidewalls, as well as in the northeast corner of the excavation. The highest soil concentrations were at WW-3 (gasoline at 4,920 ppm) and WW-4 (benzene at 1.17 ppm). Additional monitoring wells were installed and at one time included twelve wells located on- and off-site. Off-site assessments have also been completed to the east, south, and north on the Owen Property. Borings were installed within the right-of-way for SW Third Street and SW Washington Avenue to collect soil and groundwater samples in December 2004 and April 2005, with 29 boreholes advanced on both sides of the city streets. Soil samples identified petroleum at 17.5 to 26 feet bgs, with concentrations of gasoline at 1,190 ppm and benzene at 1.52 ppm. Water samples were collected from several borings and confirmed an off-site contaminant plume, including SB-7 where gasoline was at 13,300 ppb within SW Washington Avenue. Eight borings completed to 26 feet bgs were placed on the Owen Property (commercial property addressed as 421, 423, and 425 SW Third Street) in 2006. Soil and groundwater was impacted by petroleum on the southern portion of the Owen Property. In 2013, additional investigation was completed to evaluate potential vapor intrusion to indoor buildings in future development. Certain conditions are necessary to protect site workers and construction crews from remaining petroleum contamination by direct contact. Refer to DEQ closure documents for specific conditions.",9055.0,0.0,CRODDA,2014-11-12 11:34:42.307
79,20-88-4043,20,88,4043,655.0,1988-10-27,1999-03-18 13:49:10,ALBERTSON PROPERTY,3091 HILYARD,EUGENE,97405,(503) 344-1499,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : Strongly suspect on water. *** No information needs non RS 4-22-93

Site discovered during Phase II ESA in August 1988 for property transaction.  USTs decomm'd in Oct. 1988.  Additional contamination found at south side of property in 1989 during sewer pipe installation.

NFA approved with conditions for no residential land use or groundwater consumption - 5/2008, C. Rodda",see also Environmental Cleanup Site Information file # 1059 for additional information,":  The property was utilized as an auto repair and fuel service station named Sunny Oil from the 1960’s to the 1980’s.  In 1988, a site assessment was conducted that identified a petroleum release from the underground storage tank (UST) system.  The USTs were removed following the assessment.  In 1989, petroleum-contaminated soil was discovered during the installation of a storm sewer pipe at the southern portion of the property.  Contaminants were measured in soil and groundwater at concentrations exceeding DEQ’s risk-based standards.  During these activities in the 1980’s, approximately 1,120 cubic yards of impacted soil were removed from the property.  

Additional assessment was conducted at the property in February 2007.  Sampling showed that soil and groundwater do not contain petroleum or solvent-related contaminants at concentrations above DEQ’s risk-based standards.  The DEQ recommends that no further action be required.
