126,22-88-4028,22,88,4028,3579.0,1988-05-10,1999-03-18 13:49:10,YOUNGER OIL COMPANY,33380 HWY 34 SE/ I-5 & HWY 34,ALBANY,97321,(503)926-0990,False,1.0,MATRIX,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : Remediation unecessary. NFA ISSUED IN 1988.  SITE REOPENED FALL 2001 FOR FURTHER INVESTIGATION. *** remediation program underway at the site.  Call from Larry Younger on July 12, 1989 reports that no product has been detected sinced September 28, 1988.  Summary report will follow. Letter with sample results show no detect on groundwater sampl","22-06-0975 TRUAX HARRIS ENERGY # 205
22-08-1533  PetroCard (I-5 & Hwy34 Cardlock)
ECSI #5044","The LUST site was created in 1988 when free-phase petroleum was discovered in a ditch adjacent to the station. The facility was under the operation of Younger Oil Company with the Texaco brand name and owned by Glen White. Four new gasoline tanks were installed in 1988. One 550-gallon waste oil UST was removed in 1988 and free product was found in the tank excavation. Evaluation of the gasoline tank system identified a leak. Assessment confirmed soil and groundwater were contaminated with petroleum by historic UST operations. Petroleum-contaminated soil was excavated in 2011 during facility renovations and removal of the old steel tanks. Contaminated groundwater was pumped from the tank excavation as part of remedial actions. Both materials were disposed of at approved off-site facilities. Petroleum remained in soil following cleanup activities. Groundwater concentrations have decreased since the facility upgrade, but contaminants remain above risk levels for workers. The site will likely remain in use as a fuel service station at the Interstate 5 and Highway 34 interchange. The area adjacent to the site will likely remain as commercial or agricultural. The site is currently owned by Virk LLC. The company has agreed to follow recommendations for future building construction (vapor barrier) and excavation at the site (use of HASP and CMMP) to protect workers. The site’s soils consist of clayey silt with sand based on boreholes advanced to 20 feet below ground surface. Soil permeability is relatively low and groundwater elevation is considered “flat” at the site. Regional groundwater flow is expected to be north-northeast. The nearest surface water is immediately adjacent to the property and approximately 120 feet east of the former tank cavity and dispensers. Contractors measured depth to water in site monitoring wells at 1.74 to 6.44 feet below ground surface and calculated groundwater flow to the northeast.",91143.0,0.0,CRODDA,2016-10-21 10:30:21.243
