131,24-88-4042,24,88,4042,7139.0,1988-10-28,1999-03-18 13:49:10,HIGHWAY FUEL COMPANY,2390 FAIRGROUNDS ROAD NE,SALEM,97303,(503)363-6444,False,1.0,RBCA,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Co-managed by VAE & JLG, Aug 2007.

Originally closed in 1989 based on site and smell.  Neighboring property analysis revealed significant levels of TPH and Benzene in soil & gw...re-opened 6-1999 - JLG

2 USTs removed by GEI under sidewalk NW of HFC Building

--------------------
Tax lot 01500 remains in the ownership of Reta J. Stortz as Trustee of the Stortz Revocable Living Trust.

As of August 13, 2018, 2390 Fairgrounds Rd NE, 1300 Smith St NE, and 1360 Smith St NE, in Salem Oregon, (tax lots 00300, 00400, 00600, and 00700) has been sold to:
Rod Charles Johnson & Kristin Suzanne Coleman
26527 SW Labrousse Rd
Sherwood OR 97140

Tax lot 01500 remains in the ownership of Reta J. Stortz as Trustee of the Stortz Revocable Living Trust.",,"On October 28, 1988, DEQ received notice that gasoline and diesel had leaked from underground storage tanks located on this property. The contamination did extend beyond the property lines into the Smith Street and Fairgrounds Road right-of-ways.  Investigation and cleanup activities began upon the removal of the main UST systems in 1988. The site has operated as a bark and landscaping supply since 1928. Although three UST systems were used for business and personal vehicle fueling, Highway Fuel Company did not sell petroleum products to the public. During the course of this site investigation and cleanup five USTs were removed along with approximately 400 cubic yards of petroleum contaminated soil. Several groundwater monitoring wells were installed and soil vapor assessment was completed next to the Highway Fuel Co. office.  Based on the results of the soil, groundwater and soil-gas monitoring and assessments completed by the city of Salem for utility upgrades, it appears that with limitations on site use, plans for future contaminated media management, and proper planning of future work, the remaining contamination is stable and should not pose a threat to human health and the environment given the current and reasonably likely future use.  The subject property is as of this writing occupied by the Highway Fuel Co. business, which is predominantly outside storage of landscaping supplies.",22831.0,0.0,GDECK,2020-04-06 14:32:13.120
