306,20-89-4043,20,89,4043,7327.0,1989-05-05,1999-03-18 13:49:10,SUMPTER FAMILY MARKET,87754 TERRITORIAL RD,VENETA,97487,(503)935-3051,False,1.0,MATRIX,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"3 USTs were removed in 1989 with 80cy of soil
An additional 500-gal gasoline tank and 10cy of soil was removed in 1993 under another LUST number.  
Dispensers and shop also shown to be a source of soil and groundwater contamination.  Extensive investigation has been completed on and off the site.  See admin record for details. ","See Also: 20-93-4176 (NELSON FAMILY MARKET) - REMOVAL OF 4TH UST IN 1993.

Listed in Facility Profiler as ""Veneta Water Commission Complaint"" - C. Rodda, 11/1/2011","A petroleum release was suspected in December 1988 when the Sumpters contacted DEQ upon discovering water in a fuel tank. In May 1989, the Sumpters hired a contractor to remove three USTs while a fourth gasoline tank (550-gallon) remained at the southwest corner of the market. Soil samples were collected by a consultant following tank removal that confirmed a petroleum release had occurred. Six samples were collected from excavated soil, bottom and sidewalls. Results showed total petroleum hydrocarbons at 3,600 ppm in sample SUMP-01-SE10 from 10 feet bgs at the excavation's southeast corner. The consultant stated 80 cubic yards of petroleum contaminated soil was removed and disposed of at a landfill. Groundwater did not enter the tank cavity. In 1990, Mr. Sumpter reported that another contractor scraped 6 to 12 inches of contaminated soil from the excavation sidewalls and these parties determined cleanup was complete based on a “smell test”. The excavation was backfilled with pea rock, debris, and gravel. No soil samples were collected for laboratory analysis. In October 1993, the new property owners (Gordon and Delores Nelson) hired a consultant to remove the remaining 550-gallon gasoline tank from the south side of the property. Ten cubic yards of contaminated soil was removed and two soil samples were collected. All concentrations were below soil matrix clean up levels, and this tank site was issued a letter of no further action in March 2000 (LUST 20-93-4176). In November 2002, DEQ was informed that petroleum was in  the water supply at 87812 Blek Dr. across the street from the market. This residence was connected to the City of Veneta municipal water supply only nine months earlier. City staff investigated the source of petroleum by excavating a service connection from the house, across Territorial Road to the water main at the market property. The City found contaminated soil and replaced the water line serving the residence. After several years of insurance claim negotiation, investigation resumed in 2009 and cleanup (soil removal) was completed in 2010. Groundwater monitoring, soil-gas testing, and soil sampling have been conducted to evaluate potential risk at the site. Air from crawlspaces at nearby homes has been sampled to determine if petroleum vapors are reaching indoor air through utility lines, but results showed detections may be related to ambient (background) air.",47694.0,0.0,AFARRIS,2020-03-31 07:45:39.890
