1,000 GAS & 3000-GAL DIESEL DECOM BY REMOVAL IN 1989.  USTS REUSED ON SITE.    GW TREATMENT STARTED IN LATE 1989.  RBDM-CSM completed, conditional NFA proposed, rhr, 2-17-2005.","(MOTT 09/14/17) The site was previously listed as being located at 200 N 5th Street in Harrisburg, Oregon.  However, over time, streets and street names were reconfigured and changed.  Current Linn County records indicate the site address is now 500 Territorial Street, Harrisburg, Oregon; Map ID 15S04W155BB, Tax Lot 03101.","(MOTT 08/10/17) In February 1989, one 1,000-gallon gasoline and one 3,300-gallon diesel underground storage tank (UST) were decommissioned by removal.  Approximately 220 cubic yards of petroleum-contaminated soil was excavated from beneath the former USTs. This soil was reportedly aerated on-site by bioremediation and utilized on-site for fill material.  No records were available to verify successful treatment of the soil.  Elevated concentrations of TPH and BTEX were detected in soil and groundwater. A groundwater recovery trench and treatment system was installed in the former UST excavation in 1989 and was reported to have operated through 1990.  Treated water was approved for disposal into the City of Harrisburg’s sanitary sewer system. In 2001, additional soil and groundwater sampling was completed at the site. During this investigation, a separate petroleum release was discovered on the adjacent property (ESCI #4370). Due to the compositional similarities of the releases, it was not possible to accurately determine a boundary between the two plumes. Although some constituents were detected at concentrations above the most stringent RBCs during the investigation activities (at the source area), the source of contamination has now been removed (USTs and petroleum-contaminated soil).  Constituent concentrations are expected to continue to decline with natural attenuation. The site has since been redeveloped with a large slab-on-grade warehouse, constructed with a vapor barrier.  Exterior portions of the warehouse will have an asphalt cap, which will mitigate the potential for any possible vapor intrusion from remaining pockets of contamination. Municipal water is available and utilized at the site, and it is unlikely that wells will be installed in the future.  Public comment periods were held between December 10, 2004, and January 14, 2005 and again between December 22, 2016 to January 22, 2017, to accept comments from adjacent property owners and the City of Harrisburg regarding the proposed risk-based site closure.  No comments regarding the proposed site closure were received. (MOTT 11/20/17) The site was issued a NFA on 11/17/17.",7117.0,0.0,MOTT,2017-11-20 08:57:25.780
