547,15-90-0027,15,90,0027,,1990-03-30,1999-03-18 13:49:10,"LAURANCE, L HOT",115 SHERMAN,ASHLAND,97520,,False,,,4.0,NFA,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : Home heating oil tank discovered during remodeling. Spilled contents of tank during removal. Meets soil MATRIX guidelines.  No further action required. ***,,,24755.0,0.0,GDECK,2011-04-19 16:56:37.150
548,15-90-0030,15,90,0030,4102.0,1990-03-08,1999-03-18 13:49:10,PACIFIC ELECTRICAL CONTRACTORS,920 S GRAPE STREET,MEDFORD,97501,(503)773-7751,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,,,"The site was developed by at least the mid 1960s when four underground storage tanks (USTs) were installed at the site. Presumably, the site has been a commercial/warehouse facility since that time. Contamination is the result of a leak from a 550-gallon gasoline UST and was discovered in January of 1990 during decommissioning of two USTs, which resulted in the opening of LUST file No. 15-90-0030. Two other USTs were reportedly filled in place in 1988 on the site and have not been investigated under this file. Soil and a limited amount of groundwater immediately surrounding the former UST is contaminated with gasoline. The leaking UST and another 550-gallon gasoline UST were decommissioning in 1990. At that time, the leaking UST pit was reportedly over excavated by about four feet on each side. Contaminated soil likely remains at the bottom of the UST excavation, about seven feet below ground and extends beneath the onsite building. The remaining soil contamination is below acceptable risk levels for this site. Contaminated groundwater has been encountered between six and seven feet below ground within the former UST excavation. A sample of groundwater in 2007 contained gasoline-range contamination at elevated levels, however this water is expected to be limited in volume and extent as borings from outside the UST excavation encountered groundwater about five to seven feet deeper than in the former excavation and did not detect contaminated groundwater. The site is expected to remain commercial with residential properties located west of the site. All properties in the immediate vicinity are connected to the City of Medford’s municipal water system and are expected to remain connected to the municipal water system in the future.",20754.0,0.0,CRICHAR,2020-07-23 13:52:41.060
