Claudia notes in ECSI:
 Bulk fuel distribution facility constructed in 1915 with three above-ground fuel tanks. Two additional ASTs were installed in 1921 and 1931. A semi-buried tank was installed in 1948. The 1915 tanks were replaced in 1950 and the semi-buried tank was removed in 1955. An additional AST was installed in 1955. All tanks were removed in 1989 except an UST for heating oil, an AST for heating oil , and an above-ground waste oil tank. Two new lube oil tanks were installed during mid-1989.

8/25/2009: project assigned to Cathy Rodda for LUST/ECSI site - no USTs at site but originally recorded with Tanks Program as 20-90-4087

8/1/2012: historical release from UNOCAL bulk petroleum operations is responsibility of ConocoPhillips/Phillips 66 and located only at 101 Blair Blvd. (east of Blair Blvd). The Tyree Oil spill (OERS 09-0254) was on west side of Blair Blvd. at 1355 W. First Ave. referred to as Standard/Chevron plant by Ron Tyree. (C. Rodda)","ECSI #4379, Tosco Bulk Plant 0968

ECSI #3764, Tosco/Unocal Bulk Plant #0968 (Former) - this is updated in ECSI, as #4379 is read-only and noted as a historical release in ECSI (C. Rodda 4/13/2011)

8/1/2012: 101 Blair Blvd. is ECSI 4379. OERS 09-0254 was on same property as ECSI 3764 (1355 W. 1st Ave)","The site historically used above-ground storage tanks (ASTs) for bulk fuel storage. The site was transferred to DEQ's Tanks Program for oversight, though the majority of petroleum impacts is related to ASTs. Subsurface conditions generally consist of silt and sand to 15 feet bgs where gravel and cobbles are encountered. Groundwater flows west, southwest, or south. The property was operating under Tyree Oil for bulk distribution using two 12,000-gallon lube oil ASTs, one waste oil AST, a drum storage area, and a pumping station until 2012. Thirteen monitoring wells were installed in the early 1990’s to 14 feet bgs/10-foot screens. Petroleum-contaminated soil and groundwater were found in most borings on-site as well as some off-site locations. In 1993, 625 cubic yards of impacted soil were excavated from two locations, and numerous confirmation soil samples were collected. Sheen was noted on soil within each excavation. During 1997 assessment activities, a shallow soil sample (HB-1) from 1-foot bgs contained oil-range hydrocarbons at 21,100 ppm. Two more off-site wells were installed adjacent to the railroad, and push-probe assessments were conducted in 2005 and 2006. SECOR installed a remediation system at the site in December 2004. The in situ Submerged Oxygen Curtain (iSOC) system consisted of four wells (I-1, I-2, I-3, and O-1) advanced to 20 feet bgs. This remedial effort was abandoned in 2006 due to poor performance in low permeability soils. Well I-2 is still monitored and regularly contains free-phase petroleum. Well I-1 and I-3 generally contain high concentrations of petroleum. Groundwater monitoring has been conducted at the site since October 1997, and in 2009 water had decreased concentrations in the majority of wells. Free-product remains at I-2 and high gasoline is present in I-1 and MW-5. Constituents of potential concern include gasoline-, diesel and oil-range hydrocarbons, as well as benzene and ethylbenzene. A soil-gas assessment was initiated in October 2014 to evaluate potential vapor intrusion concerns due to free product and historic soil concentrations. Subslab samples of soil-gas beneath the warehouse and office were below risk levels.",24780.0,0.0,GDECK,2020-05-18 10:54:41.213
