763,10-89-0001,10,89,0001,189.0,1989-01-11,1999-03-18 13:49:10,COWBOY SHELL,107 W 'B' ST,DRAIN,97435,(503)836-2821,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,True,False,False,False,False,"RP determined to have no ATP, DEQ performed SA work in Feb. '02

risk-based investigation is on-going through MSBA consulting, off-site residential impacts are the porject driver",,"[July 2010]  This was a service station from about 1931 to the early 1990s. The site is currently a vacant lot.  The service station building was demolished during early 2009, leaving the building and pump island concrete foundations.

Contamination was initially found in 1989 along with at least one historically abandoned UST believed to be associated with historical service station activities at the site.  In February 2002, seven soil borings (GP-1 to GP-7) were advanced at the site.  Petroleum contamination was documented in all seven soil borings.  Groundwater samples indicated impacts to groundwater were present. In October 2003, the seven USTs were decommissioned.  Petroleum contamination was documented in all soil samples collected from the site.

Additional Site Investigation was performed with ARRA funding in Dec. 2009. The data indicate petroleum contamination has migrated south and west. The extent of contamination has been delineated, except to the west, and is present beneath the block on which the site is situated and the street north of the site. Contamination is generally between 4 and 10 feet deep. 

A CSM was developed for the site. Based on the risk screening and the current uses of the site and adjacent properties, there are currently unacceptable risks present and further investigation is needed. Future on-site risks from vapor intrusion can easily be mitigated through future engineering controls (e.g., subslab venting) and/or limited removal of contaminated soil exceeding vapor intrusion RBCs. Prohibitions on shallow groundwater and residential uses on the site would address other risk pathways. Risk assessment of impacts to the adjacent residential property to the south of the site is not yet complete.  

A PRP is taking over corrective actions at the site.  Additional site investigation, groundwater monitoring and soil gas sampling is scheduled.  A new site investigation report has been recieved (July 2014) and a copy report is located in the site document section of this database.

Site investigation and risk assessment work has been completed.  contamination remains at on-site soil and groundwater as well as beneath the adjacent highway 38 and residential property to the south of the former gas station.  formal control (easement) are now recorded for both the former gas station and the adjacent residential property.  Public comment has been completed with no significant comments or objections (EClough, 11-jan-2018)",18046.0,0.0,GDECK,2018-05-03 15:13:43.973
