831,22-89-4015,22,89,4015,9207.0,1989-03-06,1999-03-18 13:49:10,Shell Ridenour,33166 SE HWY 34,ALBANY,97321,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : ORIGINALLY REPORTED AS 3170 SE HWY 34; CHANGED TO 33166 SE HWY 34 AROUND AUG 1991. *** Site assessment was done by Hart-Crowser on this site for Shell Oil.  Found contamination on the site as a result of the site assessment. July 25 - as of letter received July 17, the address of the site has changed to 33166 from 3170 SE Hwy 34.","see: 22-94-4040 Ramsey Oil 

(4/29/2015 CJR) Note that Ramsey Oil is a separate release identified in 1994 from the same UST system. Ramsey Oil was not the RP, Shell Oil maintained liability for release. However, Younger Oil Company purchased station from Shell and acquired environmental liability (RP for entire LUST project at facility).","The facility has three 10,000-gallon gasoline tanks, first reported release in 1989. Three 550-gallon tanks for waste oil, heating oil, and fuel oil were removed in March 1988. The original release was reported in 1989 after a site assessment. A contractor investigated the release on behalf of Shell Oil, the facility owner until 1988 when purchased by Ridenour Oil Company. Contaminated soil was found at the oil tanks. Groundwater monitoring was conducted on a quarterly basis from September 1989 through March 1994 by Shell. In 1993, Ridenour sold to Ramsey Oil.  A new release was reported in April 1994 after a fuel delivery overfill at the gasoline tanks and a spike in benzene at MW-6. Following the spill event, Shell no longer conducted groundwater monitoring. Ramsey Oil did not complete any assessment. Remediation included removed of 300 cubic yards of petroleum-contaminated soil in February 1993 between the dispensers and tanks. The soil was taken to Oregon Hydrocarbon for thermal treatment. Confirmation soil samples had gasoline at 110 ppm from 13 feet bgs. Water (10,000 gallons) was pumped from the excavation, treated in a mobile tray aeration system, and discharged to a storm drain at levels below permitted levels. MW-4 was removed with the excavation. Groundwater monitoring and follow-up soil sampling was completed between 2004 and 2014. Some contamination remains in soil and groundwater, but at concentrations considered safe for site workers. Contaminants in shallow groundwater are above risk levels for Ingestion, but samples from the on-site drinking water well had no petroleum. As a precaution, DEQ requires the drinking water well be tested for petroleum contaminants annually for 5 years after the station re-opens. If a new well is proposed in the future, the owner will need to contact DEQ and OWRD for approval.",17755.0,0.0,CRODDA,2015-07-02 08:21:57.653
832,36-90-4067,36,90,4067,1605.0,1990-03-06,1999-03-18 13:49:10,Reggie's Shell,150 N YAMHILL,CARLTON,97111,(503)852-7381,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,True,False,False,False,False,"On-site soil 3-10-92, GW may be impacted...RS 6-30-93
Pjt Mgr = JLG

See Carlton Corners for most up top date information - JLG","36-94-4117 Carlton Corners 
36-18-0807 Carlton Corners II","The property is an active gasoline service station and convenience store, known historically as the Reggie’s Shell and more recently as Carlton Corners.  The current owner and operator is Vince Larson.  Soil contamination was initially reported to DEQ in 1990, when petroleum vapors were detected in the City’s sanitary sewer system.  The source of the contamination was traced back to leakage from piping connections between the tanks and dispensers.  A service station has operated at the site since the 1950s.  There is currently a gas station, convenience store, and deli at this location.  Since the 1990 report of petroleum contamination, the owner and operators have completed significant soil cleanup and a limited groundwater investigation at the site.  Based on soil sampling results, DEQ has determined that, given the current and reasonably likely future land use, the remaining contamination from the underground storage tanks has been reduced to levels protective of human health and the environment.  The closure plan proposes to allow the remaining contamination in soil and groundwater to naturally attenuate in place.",19026.0,0.0,GDECK,2018-07-17 15:09:08.813
