967,20-89-4202,20,89,4202,,1989-11-14,1999-03-18 13:49:10,Emerald Hydraulics,3422 OLYMPIC STREET,SPRINGFIELD,97478,(503)747-6218,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Pjt Mgr = Cathy Rodda.
No action taken yet..RS 5-23-93","Emerald Hydraulics II, USTC #20-94-4015 for two other USTs located at facility but different address (3478 Olympic St.)","The site is located within an industrial district of Springfield.  One UST and associated oil-water separator were historically utilized by Emerald Hydraulics.  In addition, two separate USTs were present at the facility and were addressed under USTC #20-94-4015 (Emerald Hydraulics II). A site assessment was conducted in March 2000 prior to the UST decommissioning.  Nineteen probes were installed near the UST, oil-water separator, and various locations at the property based on the use of hydraulic oils for manufacturing operations by Emerald Hydraulics.  Probes P1 and P2 were installed near the UST for the oil-water separator.  The UST, used as overflow storage for an adjacent oil-water separator, also removed at this time, was decommissioned by removal in April and May 2000.  The capacity of the UST was not reported but appears to have been approximately 1,000-gallons.  2,900 gallons of oil and water mix were pumped from the UST, oil-water separator, two catch basins, and remedial excavations at the property.  The contaminated water was disposed of at Oil Re-refining in Woodland, Washington. Petroleum contaminated soil was encountered around the UST system and removed to 8.5 feet bgs .  Confirmation soil samples were collected from the sidewalls at 6.5 feet bgs and from the center of the pit at 8.5 feet bgs.  Results identified diesel-range hydrocarbons in soil sample ERT-PIT-CENTER-8.5’ at 9,100 ppm , and in the sidewall samples from 990 ppm to 7,500 ppm.  Analysis for BTEX  and PAHs found the maximum concentration of benzene at 0.095 ppm and naphthalene at 0.31 ppm.  Approximately 93.4 tons of soil was removed from the tank excavation and temporarily stockpiled on-site.  The excavated soil was disposed of at Riverbend Landfill near McMinnville, Oregon. Probes were installed at the former UST location to delineate the extent of soil contamination.  The assessment was conducted by BB&A in 2000.  Soil samples were obtained from P21 at the center of the tank at 12 feet bgs.  All samples measured diesel-range hydrocarbons below laboratory detection levels.  Groundwater was encountered during the decommissioning and soil removal activities at 6.5 feet bgs.  Approximately 1,800 gallons of water was purged from the remedial excavation.  A sample (ERT-PIT-H20) was collected after water recharged to the tank cavity and contained ethylbenzene at 1.2 ppb, naphthalene at 1.6 ppb, fluorene at 1.2 ppb, and benzo(a)anthracene at at 0.22 ppb.",2560.0,0.0,CRODDA,2009-11-02 09:23:27.627
