1206,26-90-0368,26,90,0368,2233.0,1990-09-18,1999-03-18 13:49:10,"PORTLAND AUTO POET, MUNICIPAL GARAGE",1221 SW 1ST ,PORTLAND,97202,(503)248-4341,False,7.0,MATRIX,,,True,False,,,False,,,,,*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : Failed tightness test-will decommission this spring.  Testers felt that tightness test was invalid - could not get adequate seal. *** needs proof tanks passed tightness test.  8.72 tons of soil taken to Arlingron Hazardous Waste Landfill due to high lead levels in soil.,,,9698.0,,SPATTER,2001-08-17 15:53:58.000
1207,26-89-0213,26,89,0213,3494.0,1989-10-02,1999-03-18 13:49:10,J & H MOBIL,6115 NW ST HELENS RD,PORTLAND,97210,(503) 248-0744,False,1.0,GW,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Petroleum in the soil and groundwater was discovered during tank replacement in October 1989.  Two monitoring wells were installed in April 1990, followed by seven additional wells in August 1991.  An AS/SVE system was installed in January 1993 and reportedly ran until January 1995.  Free-product (14.5 inches) was encountered in MW-5 in April 1995.  The AS/SVE system was re-started in January 1997 and apparently operated through at least June 2001.  Groundwater was sampled quarterly in 1997 & 1998, but only annually in 2000 & 2001.  The most recent sampling report (from June 2001) showed up to 15,000 ppb benzene and 4,000 ppb ethylbenzene in MW-1.  The contamination extends off-site beneath St. Helens Road.  At least four quarters of confirmation groundwater sampling are probably needed.  (7/8/09 KPD).  

Letter and cost recovery agreement sent to probable RP on 04-27-10. No response received to either to date (JKS 09-16-10).  Call from Prospectve Purchaser; Jill Dever wanted to know the status of the leaking UST incident, as well as what DEQ would require to get the station back in operation.  I put her in touch with UST compliance inspectors.  Explained current groundwater data needed to evaluate options for closure (JKS 8-3-11).  Contacted by Chuck Spear of Environmental Inspection Services on 10-24-11.  He was planning to meet with a prospective purchaser and wanted to discuss the nature of work requested by DEQ.  After providing this and blank Cost Recovery Agreement, no futher contact from consultant (JKS 12-14-11).

In June 2015, prior to discussions related to a Prospective Purchaser Agreement, Hahn and Associates, Inc. (HAI) redveloped and sampled MW-1, MW-4 and MW-6.  Benzene concentrations were significantly lower than data collected in June 2001.  Sub-slab soil samples collected from the building did not reveal VOCs above residential RBCs for inhalation in soil gas pathway. 

In June 2016, GBR, An Alaska Property Company, entered into a PPA Consent Order with DEQ.  The planned use of the property was a waste transfer station by daughter company ECC. The PPA was amended in September 2016 and executed and recorded with Multnomah County in October 2016.

The scope of work for the PPA included the following: 1) decommissioning of four 8,000-gallon USTs and associated assessment; 2) removal of up to 50 tons of contaminated soil if compliance samples exceeded occupational worker RBCs for vapor intrusion; 3) Execute E&ES requiring additional cleanup or vapor mitigation engineering control in future buildings prior to construction; 4) Inspect, rehabilitate, redevelop and resurvey selected site monitoring wells if found to be usable; 5) Perform four consecutive quarters of groundwater monitoing and associated reporting; 6) Prepare a CMMP; and 7) Permanently decommision monitoring wells. 

The USTs were decommissioned and removed in September 2017.  Approximately 101 tons of contamintated soil were removed.  Confirmation samples revealed concentrations of benzene, ethylbenzene and naphthalene above occupational worker RBCs for the soil vapor intrusion pathway. GBR entered into an E&ES with DEQ in March 2019 requiring additinal cleanup and/or incorporation of vapor mitigation engineering controls into the design of future buildings and DEQ review of stamped development plans. The E&ES was recorded in April 2019.

A CMMP for the site was approved by DEQ in December 2018.

HAI performed four consecutive quarters of groundwater monitorning.  Levels of gasoline and BTEX in site wells were one to three orders of magnitude lower than in the June 2001 data.  Gasoline, VOCs and PAHs were not detected in offsite wells MW-8 and MW-9.  The plume of impacted groundwater is restricted to teh northern portion of the site and NW St. Helens Road to the north.  Shallow groundwater is unlikely to be used as a source of drinking water.

With decommissioning of the wells, GBR will have completed the scope of work for the PPA. DEQ opened comment on the proposed issuance of a Certificate of Completion and conditional NFA on May 1, 2019. No comments were received. DEQ has determined the site meets the requirements for a conditional NFA.  On-site and off-site monitoring wells abandoned August 13-22, 2019. (JKS 9-10-19).",ECSI #2423,"Petroleum in the soil and groundwater was discovered during tank replacement in October 1989.  Two monitoring wells were installed in April 1990, followed by seven additional wells in August 1991.  An AS/SVE system was installed in January 1993 and reportedly ran until January 1997.  The groundwater samplng in June 2001 showed up to 15,000 ppb benzene and 4,000 ppb ethylbenzene in MW-1.  

In June 2016, GBR, An Alaska Property Company, entered into a PPA Consent Order with DEQ.  The planned use of the property was a waste transfer station by daughter company ECC.

The scope of work for the PPA included the following: 1) decommissioning of four 8,000-gallon USTs and associated assessment; 2) removal of up to 50 tons of contaminated soil if compliance samples exceeded occupational worker RBCs for vapor intrusion; 3) Execute E&ES requiring additional cleanup or vapor mitigation engineering control in future buildings prior to construction; 4) Inspect, rehabilitate, redevelop and resurvey selected site monitoring wells if found to be usable; 5) Perform four consecutive quarters of groundwater monitoing and associated reporting; 6) Prepare a CMMP; and 7) Permanently decommision monitoring wells. 

The USTs were decommissioned and removed in September 2017.  Approximately 101 tons of contamintated soil were removed.  Confirmation samples revealed concentrations of benzene, ethylbenzene and naphthalene above occupational worker RBCs for the soil vapor intrusion pathway. GBR entered into an E&ES with DEQ in March 2019 requiring additinal cleanup and/or incorporation of vapor mitigation engineering controls into the design of future buildings and DEQ review of stamped development plans. The E&ES was recorded in April 2019.  A CMMP for the site was approved by DEQ in December 2018.

HAI performed four consecutive quarters of groundwater monitorning.  Levels of gasoline and BTEX in site wells were one to three orders of magnitude lower than in the June 2001 data.  Gasoline, VOCs and PAHs were not detected in offsite wells MW-8 and MW-9.  The plume of impacted groundwater is restricted to teh northern portion of the site and NW St. Helens Road to the north.  Shallow groundwater is unlikely to be used as a source of drinking water.

GBR has completed the scope of work for the PPA. DEQ opened comment on the proposed issuance of a Certificate of Completion and conditional NFA on May 1, 2019. No comments received. Wells abandoned August 2019 (JKS 9-11-19).",20334.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2019-09-10 15:46:16.753
