1390,26-89-0202,26,89,0202,,1989-09-19,1999-03-18 13:49:10,EAST HILL CHURCH,539 N MAIN AVE,GRESHAM,97030,,False,1.0,GW,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"The City of Gresham encountered petroleum contamination beneath NW 5th Street between Main & Miller Avenues in September 1989 while installing a utility trench.  The City and DEQ identified a former service station (torn down in 1975) on the corner of 5th and Main as the likely source.  Four groundwater monitoring wells were installed at the site (now a parking lot for the administrative offices of East Hill Church) in July 1990.  Gasoline contamination was encountered in the soil and groundwater.

No further action occurred until December 2009, when Terracon was retained by East Hill Church to investigate the site.  The work included the collection of indoor air samples, which showed benzene and ethylbenzene at levels above occupational worker RBCs. Terracon installed six additional monitoring wells on and around the site in March 2010.  Sampling found that the contamination was largely confined to the parking lot.  (7/13/10 KPD).  Remedial Action PIlot Study Work Plan prepared by Terracon received 3-15-11. Included was a feasbility analysis of various technologies. Pilot study of Dual Phase Extraction (remvoal of water, product, and soil-gas) using a portable vacuum system was proposed.  Initial pilot study test proposed for July 2011.  Pilot study work plan approved 4-8-11 (JKS 4-20-11). Pilot stufy work plan implemented in two phases (July and October 2011). Report documenting pilot study  received by DEQ on 12-14-11.  In January 2012, DEQ commented on report.  DEQ requested additional monitoring and product gauging.  Measurable product not observed in wells since Pilot Study.  

In quarterly monitoring performed berween May 2012 and February 2013, free product was not detected in MW-1 and MW-3.  Furthermore, quarterly sampling revealed stable levels of gasoline and VOCs in MW-1 and MW-3.  Only the detected concentrations of gasoline and 1,2,4-TMB in these wells exceeded construction and excavation worker RBCs for the Groundwater in Excavation exposure pathway. Based on historical depth to water data, future excavations at site and in adjacent rights-of-ways could encounter groundwater.  On behalf of East Hill Church, Terracon prepared a CMMP and submitted to DEQ and the City of Gresham for comment. 

In November 2013, to further address potential vapor intrusion risks, Terracon collected two sub-slab soil-gas samples from the site buidling.  There were no detections exceeding residential RBCs for soil-gas.  

Groundwater in vicinty of site does not have a current or likely future beneficial use as drinking water.  Although zoning allows residential use, the long-term commercial use of the site by the Church is likely.   The November 2013 soil-gas data is more appropriate to use for evaluating the vapor intrusion pathway than the December 2009 indoor air data, in part because the air data was collected prior to DPE remediation and removal of product to the extent practicable.  Comment period opened in January 2014.  Only comments from the City of Gresham were recieved.  RP put on notice of responsibility for future impacts in ROW reasonably likely to be related to the 1989 release (JKS 3-17-14).",,"In quarterly monitoring performed berween May 2012 and February 2013, free product was not detected in MW-1 and MW-3.  Furthermore, quarterly sampling revealed stable levels of gasoline and VOCs in MW-1 and MW-3.  Only the detected concentrations of gasoline and 1,2,4-TMB in these wells exceeded construction and excavation worker RBCs for the Groundwater in Excavation exposure pathway. Based on historical depth to water data, future excavations at site and in adjacent rights-of-ways could encounter groundwater.  On behalf of East Hill Church, Terracon prepared a CMMP and submitted to DEQ and the City of Gresham for comment. 

In November 2013, to further address potential vapor intrusion risks, Terracon collected two sub-slab soil-gas samples from the site buidling.  There were no detections exceeding residential RBCs for soil-gas.  

Groundwater in vicinty of site does not have a current or likely future beneficial use as drinking water.  Although zoning allows residential use, the long-term commercial use of the site by the Church is likely.   The November 2013 soil-gas data is more appropriate to use for evaluating the vapor intrusion pathway than the December 2009 indoor air data, in part because the air data was collected prior to DPE remediation and removal of product to the extent practicable.  Comment period opened in January 2014.  Only comments from the City of Gresham were recieved.  RP put on notice of responsibility for future impacts in ROW reasonably likely to be related to the 1989 release (JKS 3-17-14).",142429.0,0.0,iPMO#38,2016-10-14 15:09:31.393
