1397,34-88-0084,34,88,0084,6016.0,1988-11-04,1999-03-18 13:49:10,SLIGER-MONROE OIL COMPANY,4815 SE TUALATIN VALLEY HWY,HILLSBORO,97123,541-923-4977,False,4.0,GW,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Gasoline in the shallow groundwater was found to extend off-site and impact and/or threaten three or four residential irrigation wells.  Groundwater has been monitored (primarily for BTEX) about once a year since 1996.  Several wells, including two of the off-site wells, are still impacted with moderate levels of BTEX.  The last monitoring report was received in 2006.  Four quarters of confirmation groundwater sampling likely needed.  (6/8/09 KPD)",,"In the fall of 1988, the 20,000-gallon underground storage tank at the Sliger-Monroe Oil Company property was decommissioned. Compliance sampling associated with the tank decommissioning revealed that a gasoline release had impacted soil and groundwater. Approximately 7,000 cubic yards of contaminated soil were removed from the site following decommissioning of the USTs. Free product on groundwater was removed weekly or bi-weekly from 1991 to 1996 and subsequently on an as-needed basis. In addition, Oxygen Release Compound (ORC®) was deployed in dedicated wells installed at the site and in 48th Avenue west of the site to promote degradation of petroleum hydrocarbons by naturally occurring microorganisms. Depth to water data collected in the area of the site indicate groundwater flows generally to the northwest. Periodic groundwater sampling was conducted from 1990 to 2019 utilizing on-site and off-site monitoring wells and other wells in the area associated with residential properties. Contamination has been detected in the groundwater beyond the north, west, and northwest boundaries of the former Sliger-Monroe Oil Company property. The primary chemicals of concern are volatile organic compounds including benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, and total xylenes. Groundwater analytical data indicate an overall decline in the levels of these contaminants over time consistent with the effects of degradation by petroleum-consuming microorganisms. [KMHD 12-18-2019]",7481.0,0.0,KHACK,2020-01-10 13:25:34.103
