1447,26-90-0055,26,90,0055,1102.0,1990-02-05,1999-03-18 13:49:10,UNOCAL 5953,12205 N CENTER ,PORTLAND,97217,(503)285-2657,False,7.0,MATRIX,,,True,False,,,False,,,,,,,,4073.0,,MALBERT,1990-12-26 00:00:00.000
1449,26-89-0114,26,89,0114,1031.0,1989-06-19,1999-03-18 13:49:10,UNOCAL 5133,5065 SW SCHOLLS FERRY RD,PORTLAND,97225,(503) 292-2788,False,4.0,GW,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"The service station opened in 1963.  Four USTs (two 8,000-gallon gasoline, one 550-gallon heating oil, and one 280-gallon waste oil) were removed in June 1989, along with 450 cubic yards of PCS (from around the gasoline USTs).  Six monitoring wells were installed by March 1990.  In late 1994 a Stage II Vapor Recovery system was installed, along with MW-7 thru MW-10.  In October 1998 the replacement USTs (two 12,000-gallon gasoline tanks) were themselves removed, along with 510 cubic yards of PCS.  Approx 250 gallons of ""enzyme treatment"" were added to the excavation pit.  Free-product was detected on the groundwater in 1998 and again in 2000, due to an active system leak.

Soil contamination is believed to be limited in extent (site and adjacent access road).  Groundwater contamination extends off-site.  Fourteen monitoring wells (seven on-site, seven off-site) are being sampled quarterly.  No remediation is occurring, and according to Bijan, multiple RPs are involved and are disputing the cleanup costs.  (6/10/09 KPD).
  
Work plan for off-site investigation reviewed at request of Tanks Manager in June 2009.  Comments to work plan provided in letter dated 25 June 2009.  DEQ commented that constituent data was rare for site and identified areas needing additional sampling for data gaps.  On November 6, 2009, DEQ notified that work plan was partially implemented (offsite investigation performed but not data gap portion).  DEQ was also informed that a Remedial Action Plan was in preparation, along with report documenting groundwater delineation investigation.  Report documenting the November 2009 limited subsurface investigation was finally recieved 8-30-10. Investigation only partly addressed items in letter of June 25, 2009.  Data gap portion of investigation not completed and still needed to close project.  Soil borings B-1 through B-4 advanced at site, in adjacent access road, and at Fred Meyer property.  Soil contamination detected in access road (boring B-2), but not in borings advanced on Fred Meyer property (B-3 and B-4).  Stantec also proposes to reinstall MW-11, which was decomissioned due to being in footprint of propsed construction.  Due to zoning allowing urban residential use, and detection of gasoliner-range TPH at levels exceeding RBC for vapor intrusion, vapor evaluation should be completed (JKS 09-09-10). Comments provided in letter dated September 2009.  DEQ notified in April 2011 that ARCADIS is the new consultant and Chevron/Union Oil has assumed liability.  DEQ contacted on 10-21-11.  Consultant reports overhead utility conflict in area of requested data gap investigation and client procedures that do not allow drilling into gravel fill.  Replacement well MW-11R and soil-gas sampling point VP-1 installed May and August 2012. VP-1 screened at 4 and 8 feet bgs.  Samples collected from VP-1 contained TPH-G at levels well above occupational worker RBCs. (JKS 1-25-13).  Work plan requested in March 2013.

Work plan submitted in May 2014. Work plan scope called for installation of four permanent soil-gas sampling points in the commercial building north of the station and two permanent soil-gas points in the station building. ARCADIS has worked to obtain access to the Fred Meyer property diligently for nearly two years.  Fred Meyer has not given final approval, however.  With levels of gasolien and VOCs declining in site wells, the evaluation of VI risks is the remaining barrier to closing out the project. (JKS 1-24-17)",,,50422.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2020-01-23 09:20:30.577
