1663,20-90-4008,20,90,4008,1860.0,1990-01-22,1999-03-18 13:49:10,FRANKO OIL #7,3650 E MAIN ST,SPRINGFIELD,97477,(503)741-9958,False,1.0,,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS :  *** Pjt Mgr = C Johansen

9/25/2008: In July 2006, Claudia J. reviewed this file and responded to the RP regarding the location monitoring wells to be installed.  The RP was going to continue manual FP removal until ""a more automated system can be designed"".  EnviroLogic has been the consultant.  This is a free product site.

8/14/2012: updated DEQ project manager to Rodda as of 12/28/2011 and ""Release Stopped"" as 11/24/1994 when the USTs were decommissioned by removal - C. Rodda",,"In August 1994, DEQ received the ""Final Report on Subsurface Hydrocarbon Assessment"" report from RZA, Inc., submitted on behalf of Pride of Oregon Stations and dated October 1990. In August and October 1990, RZA had conducted an investigation, collecting soil samples and installing 4 monitoring wells. Petroleum odors were evident in soil, and analysis classified the release as “degraded gasoline”. Groundwater was in borings at 12.5 to 15 feet below ground surface (bgs) and in monitoring wells at 8.5 to 10 feet bgs.  Groundwater flow was reported to the northwest. Contaminants were noted during the initial sampling event at each monitoring well, detected in excess of the Basic Numeric Groundwater Cleanup Levels (standards used at the time to evaluate petroleum sites).  Benzene was measured at as high as 1,950 parts per billion (ppb). An assessment report from AGRA Environmental dated December 5, 1994 was also provided to DEQ. The report documented three soil samples collected in November 1994 and analyzed for petroleum hydrocarbons. The results indicated no detectable concentrations of petroleum were present in the samples; however, the chain of custody was initiated at the laboratory (also AGRA) and it was not reported who collected the samples. These may have been confirmation soil samples collected from the excavation following removal of underground tanks in November 1994. A decommissioning checklist indicated that groundwater was present in the tank cavity following removal of three gasoline tanks (two 12,000-gallon and one 4,000-gallon). In correspondence dated June 7, 1999, DEQ requested further action after reviewing the referenced October 1990 site investigation and tank decommissioning reports. DEQ requested a corrective action plan to address the remaining petroleum contamination.The property owner completed intermittent groundwater testing and free product removal from well MW-3. In 2013, a soil-gas assessment found no vapor intrustion risk even with free product remaining in site groundwater.",19220.0,0.0,CJBROWN,2018-05-11 11:35:57.983
