1690,34-90-0432,34,90,0432,7166.0,1990-11-13,1999-03-18 13:49:10,TRUAX HARRIS #5,17455 SW TUALATIN VALLEY HWY,ALOHA,97006,(503) 222-4201,False,1.0,GW,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"A release reported in 1990. Semi-annual GW monitoring and remedial activities indicated gasoline and diesel-impacted soil and groundwater in 1990 and 1992 after UST decommissioning.  The current ongoing groundwater remediation  includes a GW pump-and-treat and SVE system.  The general GW flow is westerly with off-site impact.  There is a decreasing trend of concentrations of petroleum constituents in the onsite monitoring wells.
A recent Due Diligence investigation indicates high concentrations of gasoline and constituents in soil and particularly groundwater to the west of the site.  Further investigation and remediation  should follow. In-situ chemical oxidation pilot study of site made.  ISCO found to be effective for treating EW-4 and areas to east.  ISCO probably not effective in remediating groundwater plume downgradient of EW-2 and EW-3 or areas off-site. Last SA monitoring event in February 2009.  Recommended continuing SA monitoring.  DEQ letter dated 6-11-10 requested evaluation of potential risks to residential and commercial properties located within 100 feet of impacted groundwater.  Work plan for soil-gas investigation received on 9-16-10 and approved.  Soil-gas investigation was performed in September 2011.  Seven soil-gas samples were collected at various locations at the site.  No detections were reported above residnetial RBCs.for soil-gas.  In December 2012, Kleinfelder submitted a CMMP as a risk management measure.  Risk-Based Evaluation Report submitted on 1-16-13.  Review of risk-based evaluation showed that extent of contaminatino generally delineated.  Although September 2011 soil-gas testing did not reveal detections exceeding residenitial RBCs, additional testing during the wet season of the year was not performed. The owners of the four properties comprising the ""site"" (Taxt Lots 100, 201, 400, 490) agreed to sign E&ES documents prohibiting construction of residential or commercial buidlings on these four parcels without incorporating a vapor mitigation engineering control and agreeing to allow DEQ the opportunity to review construction plans and the plans for the vapor mitigation system.  Public comment period initiated 7-19-13.  Comments received from owners of two residential and one residential/commercial property.  DEQ informed that properties at 17560 (Mace Property) and 17610 (Cadman Property) SW Alexander Streets have out of service, covered over (buried) hand-dug wells.  DEQ directed RP to sample wells.  Well at 17560 SW Alexander Street sampled in October 2013.  Kleinfedler working to secure access to property at 17610 SW Alexander Street.
DEQ has determined that it is appropriate to issue a Partial NFA for the four properties comprising the site and nearby properties.  The Partial NFA does not apply to the properties at 17560 and 17610 SW Alexander Streets.  Additional assessment of those properties (groundwater sampling, soil-gas) is warranted.  DEQ may request that Harris Enterprises and/or PacWest Energy LLC decommision the hand-dug wells (possible future beneficial use.  (JKS 11-13-13).

In October and November 2013 Kleinfelder collected groundwater samples from the dug wells at the Cadman and Mace properties, respectively.  Laboratory analysis showed the Cadman well to be largely unimpacted by petroleum hydrocarbons, volatile organic compounds (VOCs) and polynuclear aromatic hydrocarbons.  The sample collected from the Mace well contained concentrations of diesel, heavy oil and benzene exceeding only the residential RBCs for the Groundwater Ingestion & Inhalation from Tapwater exposure pathway.

Concurrent with sampling of the dug wells, Kleinfelder collected sub-slab soil gas samples from residences at the Cadman and Mace properties to evaluate potential risks from vapor intrusion.  Gasoline and VOCs were detected in the soil gas samples at concentrations below residential RBCs for the Vapor Intrusion into Building pathway. 


In May 2014 Kleinfelder attempted to collect additional sub-slab soil gas samples from the Cadman and Mace residences to further evaluate vapor intrusion risks.  Groundwater was encountered at a depth of approximately 0.5 feet below ground surface at the Cadman residence.  Due to an inadequate thickness of unsaturated soil above groundwater, a soil gas sample was not collected from the Cadman residence.  Although a soil gas sample was successfully obtained from the Mace residence, helium was detected in the sample at a concentration exceeding the upper limit for the leak detection compound recommended in DEQ’s vapor intrusion guidance (5%) and the data were rejected.  

In August 2014 Kleinfelder collected additional sub-slab soil gas samples from the Mace and Cadman properties.  Consistent with the 2013 sampling results, gasoline and selected VOCs were detected at concentrations below residential RBCs for the Vapor Intrusion into Building pathway.  Based on these findings, DEQ concluded that residual soil and groundwater contamination at the Mace and Cadman properties did not pose unacceptable risks from vapor intrusion. 

In January 2015 Kleinfelder supervised the abandonment , the dug wells at the Cadman and Mace properties by filling them 4000 psi cement.  

In January 2016 Timothy and Gail Mace settled a lawsuit against Harris Enterprises, Inc., H.E. Properties, Inc., 17535 SW TV Highway, Inc. and PacWest Energy, LLC concerning a past inability to use shallow groundwater as a resource.  With the permanent decommissioning of the dug well in January 2015 and considering that municipal water is currently supplied to the property, shallow groundwater at the Mace property is not likely to be used for lawn irrigation or other purposes in the future.  DEQ has determined that the Partial Conditional NFA determination should be extended to include the Mace and Cadman properties. (JKS 3-18-16)

Issued addedum to Partial Conditional NFA letter extending scope of letter to Mace and Cadman properties on 4-27-16.

In late April 2016, DEQ became aware that the Black Rock Coffee stand was built new and construction/development plans were never made available to DEQ.  The E&ES executed for the former station property (Tax Lot 201) required incorporation of a vapor mitigation engineering control.  It may be necessary to reopen case for the former station parcel to 1) review construction plans and approve vapor mitigation control or 2) perform soil-gas testing to rule out risks from vapor intrusion. (JKS 5-2-16). Site re-opened on : 5/3/2016 By Gerald Gamolo. Previous Final Invoice Request Date: 4/13/2016.",,"A release reported in 1990. Semi-annual GW monitoring and remedial activities indicated gasoline and diesel-impacted soil and groundwater in 1990 and 1992 after UST decommissioning.  The current ongoing groundwater remediation  includes a GW pump-and-treat and SVE system.  The general GW flow is westerly with off-site impact.  There is a decreasing trend of concentrations of petroleum constituents in the onsite monitoring wells.
A recent Due Diligence investigation indicates high concentrations of gasoline and constituents in soil and particularly groundwater to the west of the site.  Further investigation and remediation  should follow. In-situ chemical oxidation pilot study of site made.  ISCO found to be effective for treating EW-4 and areas to east.  ISCO probably not effective in remediating groundwater plume downgradient of EW-2 and EW-3 or areas off-site. Last SA monitoring event in February 2009.  Recommended continuing SA monitoring.  DEQ letter dated 6-11-10 requested evaluation of potential risks to residential and commercial properties located within 100 feet of impacted groundwater.  Work plan for soil-gas investigation received on 9-16-10 and approved.  Soil-gas investigation was performed in September 2011.  Seven soil-gas samples were collected at various locations at the site.  No detections were reported above residnetial RBCs.for soil-gas.  In December 2012, Kleinfelder submitted a CMMP as a risk management measure.  Risk-Based Evaluation Report submitted on 1-16-13.  Review of risk-based evaluation showed that extent of contaminatino generally delineated.  Although September 2011 soil-gas testing did not reveal detections exceeding residenitial RBCs, additional testing during the wet season of the year was not performed. The owners of the four properties comprising the ""site"" (Taxt Lots 100, 201, 400, 490) agreed to sign E&ES documents prohibiting construction of residential or commercial buidlings on these four parcels without incorporating a vapor mitigation engineering control and agreeing to allow DEQ the opportunity to review construction plans and the plans for the vapor mitigation system.  Public comment period initiated 7-19-13.  Comments received from owners of two residential and one residential/commercial property.  DEQ informed that properties at 17560 (Mace Property) and 17610 (Cadman Property) SW Alexander Streets have out of service, covered over (buried) hand-dug wells.  DEQ directed RP to sample wells.  Well at 17560 S",2190.0,0.0,GGAMOLO,2016-05-03 10:50:18.790
