1762,26-90-0477,26,90,0477,8424.0,1990-12-07,1999-03-18 13:49:10,TRUAX HARRIS #250,10000 SW BARBUR BLVD,PORTLAND,97219,(503)222-4201,False,1.0,GW,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Mobile DPE unit has been used to remove free product.  Groundwater appears to occur in two distinct horizons. Semi-annual GW monoitoring is being conducted until constituent concentrations in groundwater drop below the applicable generic RBC cleanup levels, before quarterly Gw monitoring is initiated for the site closure.  Other active remediation processes may be explored to expedite the cleanup.  Call from Multnomah County property assessor Jason Baribeault on 11-17-09 - discussed project status.    Following review of the report documenting second quarter 2010 sampling, DEQ sent a comment letter dated 06-03-10.  Noting that benzene levels in KMW01 and RZA01 have consistently exceeded generic RBC standard for occupational vapor intrusion (2800 ppb), DEQ requested preparation of a soil-gas investigation work plan.  As of September 2010 monthly update, work plan was still in preparation.  Contacted by Jason Baribeault of Multnomah County for update over the last twelve months. Await work plan from Kleinfelder (JKS 10-27-10).  Reviewed monitoring report for 4Q2010.  Continue to see decreases in concentrations.  Due to exceedance of occupational worker RBC standards for vapor intrusion, Kleinfelder is reportedly preparing a soil-gas investigation work plan (JKS 11-22-10).  Work plan approved January 2011.  Work anticipated to be implemented in 2nd or 3rd quarter 2011.  Called Kleinfelder on 6-14-11 to request update on status of implementation (JKS 6-14-11).Work plan implemented in August 2011 and October 2011.  Report received 12-7-11.  Soil-gas with gasoline exceeding occupational worker RBCs detected in SG-4 along west property boundary.  Gasoline and/or constituents detected above urban residential RBCs in three borings (not including point near building); Future risks must be addressed via a remedy;  Risk-Based Evalution Report subitted to DEQ in May 2012.  Review of document confirmed that there were unacceptable vapor intrusion risks to occupants of future buildings (current Starbucks is >100 feet from elevated soil-gas concentrations).  In November 2012, Kleinfelder submitted a CMMP revised in accordance with DEQ comments. On July 31, 2013, DEQ sent a letter to nearby property owners soliciting comment on the proposed closure of the 1990 release. Property owners of contiguous properties comprising the site (Tax Lots 3300 and 3400) were notified that institutional controls would be needed to mitigage vapor intrusion risks to occupants of future buildings constructed at the site. E&ES documents were signed and executed by Truax Douglas LLC for Tax Lot in December 2013 and January 2014.  The E&ES for Tax Lot 3400 has been signed by DEQ and mailed to the property owners for signature and recording.  The E&ES documents prohibit construction of urban residential and/or commercial buildings at the site without incorporating a DEQ approved engineering control for vapor intrusion and require DEQ review and approval of future development/construction plans.  The owners are likewise required to maintain the CMMP at the site and implement as appropraite if future work exposes contaminated media (JKS 1-16-14).

In March 2017, prior to UST decomissioning work, MFA on behalf of Petrocard advanced a boring (B01) in the area of the USTs to document baseline conditions in soil and groundwater.  Analysis of soil and groundwater samples from B01 revealed levels of petroleum hydrocarbons and constituents generally consistent or lower than levels known at the time of issuance of the 2014 No Further Action letter.  In June 2017, Petrocard decommissioned the USTs, dispensers and piping at the site as part of lease termination activities.  In accordance with an understanding that historical groundwater contaminatino was widespread across the site, a pit water sample was not collected from the excavation.  Instead, soil samples were collected at the soil water interface on four sidewalls of the excavation.  Analysis of the interface samples revealed petroleum hydrocarbons and constituents generally consistent or lower than historical data.  The only exception was diesel hydrocarbons, which were detected in soil beneath the former piping and dispensers at higher levels than in historical investigations.  Due to the evidence for attenuation of dissolved phase contaminants in groundwater and the fact that most COIs were detected at lower levels than in historic data, DEQ considers this to more represent a lack of historical sampling in those areas than evidence for a new release.  Although the area of soil needing capping with an engineered surface or buiding is larger than thought, the E&ES documents require DEQ approval of development plans. The remedy remains protective and the Conditional NFA remains in force. (JKS 8-15-17)",,"In March 2017, prior to UST decomissioning work, MFA on behalf of Petrocard advanced a boring (B01) in the area of the USTs to document baseline conditions in soil and groundwater.  Analysis of soil and groundwater samples from B01 revealed levels of petroleum hydrocarbons and constituents generally consistent or lower than levels known at the time of issuance of the 2014 No Further Action letter.  In June 2017, Petrocard decommissioned the USTs, dispensers and piping at the site as part of lease termination activities.  In accordance with an understanding that historical groundwater contaminatino was widespread across the site, a pit water sample was not collected from the excavation.  Instead, soil samples were collected at the soil water interface on four sidewalls of the excavation.  Analysis of the interface samples revealed petroleum hydrocarbons and constituents generally consistent or lower than historical data.  The only exception was diesel hydrocarbons, which were detected in soil beneath the former piping and dispensers at higher levels than in historical investigations.  Due to the evidence for attenuation of dissolved phase contaminants in groundwater and the fact that most COIs were detected at lower levels than in historic data, DEQ considers this to more represent a lack of historical sampling in those areas than evidence for a new release.  Although the area of soil needing capping with an engineered surface or buiding is larger than thought, the E&ES documents require DEQ approval of development plans. The remedy remains protective and the Conditional NFA remains in force. (JKS 8-15-17)",14936.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2017-08-15 08:33:12.463
