2227,20-90-4333,20,90,4333,8806.0,1990-09-18,1999-03-18 13:49:10,Chuck & Ken's Mobil,129 N NINTH,COTTAGE GROVE,97424,(503) 942-3094,False,1.0,,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"PCS is being remediated on-site 9-23-91 RS 5-26-93
Talked to Shirley Nease who indicated that everything had been completed in regards to the PCS remediation.  A file review revealed a report indicating that to be the case, even though the sam","20-91-4019, Chuck & Ken's Mobil - two LUST sites created appear to be for two separate tank basins - #1) 4 gasoline USTs decommissioned in Sept. 1990 at eastern half of site, and #2) 1 waste oil tank removed in Sept. 1990 at western side of property - C. Rodda 1/5/2011","In August 1990, a contractor removed four gasoline tanks and a single waste oil tank. The gasoline tanks (one 2,000-gallon, two 4,000-gallon, and one 3,500-gallon) were located in a single tank basin to the east of the station building. The waste oil tank was located southwest of the station building. Contractors noted soil contamination near the gasoline tanks and groundwater was present in the excavation. The initial groundwater sample from the gasoline tank pit gasoline and related constituents (BTEX). In August 1990, 500 tons of contaminated soil was removed from the gasoline tank basin. Six soil samples were taken from the gasoline and one from the waste oil tank pit. No petroleum was detected in any sample. Groundwater that entered the excavation was removed with the contaminated soil, and no water returned to the pit within 24 hours. The excavated soil was transported to a nearby property for treatment until August 1991, at which time samples of both the treated and underlying soils were collected. No petroleum was measured. DEQ requested follow-up sampling of the waste oil tank pit and installation of an observation well in the tank pit. Contractors took soil from the waste oil tank pit in June 1991. Low levels of petroleum and background concentrations of barium were detected. An observation well was then installed in the former gasoline tank location. Three monitoring wells were installed in August 1991. Contamination was noted in soil collected from borings for MW-1 and MW-2. Gasoline was detected in soil from MW-1 at 1,100 ppm and benzene was 0.7 ppm. Based on the monitoring well data, depth to water at the site varies from five to eight feet below ground surface and groundwater flows to the northeast. The monitoring in October 2004 showed MW-1 and MW-2 with continued groundwater contamination. MW-3 to the south showed no detections of petroleum. Another water sample was taken from MW-2 in March 2015, with signficant reduction in petroleum concentrations (benzene at 1,010 ppb). If off-site contamination, likely at safe levels for commercial land use (ie Occupational receptor benzene standard is 2,800 ppb).",23817.0,0.0,CJBROWN,2018-01-11 10:39:37.180
