2233,30-91-0003,30,91,0003,25.0,1991-01-14,1999-03-18 13:49:10,CITY OF STANFIELD-Stanfield Sewer Treat.,245 Old Hinkle Road,STANFIELD,97875,449-3831,False,7.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,,,"[June 2010] 1.    A 550-gallon gasoline underground storage tank (UST) was decommissioned by removal in November 1990.  According to the release report form, gasoline was detected in two soil samples collected during the decommissioning at 8,100 micrograms per kilogram (mg/kg) and 1,600 mg/kg.  No documentation related to the decommissioning or cleanup has been located by DEQ or the City of Stanfield.

2.    A phone memorandum documenting conversations between DEQ and the City of Stanfield from April 2002 and a DEQ letter dated August 8, 2002 indicate that approximately 35 cubic yards of petroleum contaminated soil (PCS) was excavated and transported to the city landfill for disposal.  Three additional confirmation soil samples were collected and reportedly were “clean”.  No documentation of these activities has been located by either DEQ or the City of Stanfield.  

3.    The City of Stanfield provided additional information to DEQ in a letter received on May 11, 2010.  The City provided a photograph showing the location of the former UST and the new secondary clarifier.  The new secondary clarifier was installed in 1992 in the same area as the former UST.  As part of the installation, approximately 300 cubic yards of soil was excavated to a depth of 20 feet.  If any PCS remained as a result of the UST, the PCS would have been removed as part of excavation activities associated with the new secondary clarifier.  

4.    Groundwater is used for domestic and community purposes.  DEQ reviewed OWRD’s GRID (well log) database for wells located within ¼ mile of the site.  The domestic and community wells in this area obtain groundwater from an unconfined alluvium aquifer located generally 70 feet below ground surface (bgs) and from interbeds in the Columbia River Basalt Formations general greater than 135 feet bgs.  The depth to groundwater makes it unlikely that petroleum contamination associated with this site would create a risk to users.

Based upon the age of the leak, the probability that any residual contamination was removed during construction of the secondary clarifier, and the depth to groundwater, DEQ has determined that no further action is needed. 

A copy of the NFA letter is in the site document section of this database.",25164.0,0.0,KROBERT,2010-06-15 14:00:09.833
