10/9/18 entered FIR date and requested estimated invoice. JClaussen",,"The site address has changed several times and was originally reported as 2151 Three Mile Lane which was changed to 2151 NE Cumulus Ave sometime after the original release report. The address was changed again and is currently listed with the Yamhill County Tax Assessor as 2149 NE Cumulus Ave. The Oasis Shell fueling station (formerly ARCO) was operated until the late 1980s. In 1991, three gasoline USTs and one heating oil UST were decommissioned by removal. Contamination was reported at the time of UST decommissioning but an exact cause of the contamination (tanks, piping, and/or dispensers) is unknown. Soil onsite, shallow groundwater onsite and extending about 15 feet onto the property to the west, and soil gases are impacted with gasoline and related constituents. A total of over 2,300 cubic yards of impacted soil was either treated onsite, apparently not under a Solid Waste Letter of Authorization, or was disposed of at the Riverbend Landfill. Over 50 borings and wells have been completed at the site to investigate the magnitude and extend of contamination in soil, groundwater, and soil gas. Remaining contaminated soil appears limited to the southern half of the site and may be present from about 5 to 25 feet below the ground surface. Groundwater impacts may be found across the site and about 15 feet onto the property to the west and is typically encountered between one and 16 feet below the ground surface depending on the season. The property and surrounding areas are expected to remain connected to municipal water and for commercial uses. An opportunity to comment on the proposed site closure was given to the neighboring properties, utility operators, the City of McMinnville and the Oregon Department of Transportation. No comments were received. Due to remaining contaminant concentrations an Easement and Equitable Servitude document was filed with the County Recorder on the property deed on October 2, 2018. The EES restricts among other things, any residential use of the property, the installation of a drinking or irrigation well on the site, and required that the approved Contaminated Media Management Plan be followed for any subsurface work on the site.",17863.0,0.0,CRICHAR,2018-10-15 11:24:45.460
