2565,22-91-4131,22,91,4131,10682.0,1991-04-23,1999-03-18 13:49:10,McCollum Automotive,2020 S SANTIAM HWY,LEBANON,97355,(503) 258-3116,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,Site re-opened on : 10/8/2010 By Virginia Deck. Previous Final Invoice Request Date: 10/8/2010.,"Most of the information has been moved to ECSI 1587 due to chloroform and 1,1,1-TCA in the groundwater.  SA conducted 8/12/1994 determined site priority is low.  TMD 8/3/07

Assessment performed in 2009 found no solvent-related contaminants remaining in shallow groundwater. Petroleum contaminants were present in 2009 investigation of soil and groundwater - C. Rodda, 10/13/2010.","The property is an active sales and service business that previously held numerous underground tanks holding petroleum products. Multiple releases have been reported and investigated at the facility, and three separate areas of contamination are addressed in this report. Contractors removed 158 cubic yards of contaminated soil from the three areas of concern during tank decommissioning activities at the site in 1991. In 1992, contractors installed three monitoring wells to evaluate groundwater conditions. The wells were constructed to 17 feet below grade. Soil samples obtained from the boreholes during well installation identified limited contamination. Groundwater monitoring occurred in 1992 and 1993. No petroleum-related compounds were detected in any of the sampling events. Chloroform and 1,1,1-TCA were measured in water samples during each monitoring event at maximum concentrations of 18 ppb and 2 ppb, respectively. Additional site assessment was not completed until 2009. Fifteen borings were installed at the property to collect soil and groundwater samples. Two of the three monitoring wells that were still intact were also sampled. Seven borings were placed outside of the service building, and eight were installed within the structure. Groundwater measured in the monitoring wells at approximately 6 feet bgs, and 7 to 8.5 feet bgs in the boreholes. Five borings were advanced near the gasoline tank, and soil samples measured relatively little contamination remaining. Diesel (164 ppb) and heavy oil (1,180 ppb) were present in groundwater from boring RF-GW-2, but no VOCs were detected. Three borings were placed at the former waste oil tank location. Diesel was detected in water at 157 ppb, but no contaminants were detected in soil samples from this area inside the service building. In the 2009 investigation, soil and groundwater samples were collected at the former hydraulic hoist location inside the service building. Five borings were placed within and surrounding the limits of the excavation completed in 1991. Soil samples identified some diesel and oil-range hydrocarbons at 88.7 ppm and 214 ppm, respectively. A groundwater sample did not contain gasoline, diesel, or PAHs. No gasoline, diesel, or VOCs were detected in water collected from MW-1 and MW-3.",16552.0,0.0,CRODDA,2010-10-13 13:47:42.680
