2849,02-91-4225,02,91,4225,7837.0,1991-08-02,1999-03-18 13:49:10,BP - Kings Blvd,820 NW KINGS BLVD,CORVALLIS,97330,(503) 758-1500,False,1.0,GW,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,CAP SUBMITTED AND DEQ COMMENTED 1-13-99.  MORE SOIL AND GROUNDWATER DELINEATION NEEDED OFF-SITE.  SIGNIFICANT SOURCE REMOVAL DONE  (802 TONS PCS HAULED OFF-SITE).  CURRENTLY CONDUCTING GW MONITORING.,Pjt Mgr = BCS,,15734.0,0.0,GDECK,2008-09-09 14:12:54.873
2850,22-91-4226,22,91,4226,3448.0,1991-08-05,1999-03-18 13:49:10,M & M Mobil II,501 PACIFIC BLVD S,ALBANY,97321,(503) 920-2555,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,No report in file last heard 5-8-91...RS 6-30-93,See Also: 22-90-4316,"DEQ oversight on this property began in December 17, 1990, when the release of gasoline contamination, from leaking underground storage tank(s) or associated piping, was reported to DEQ by UST Environmental Services Inc. (USTE). The site is and has been a commercial business (motor fueling facility) located at 501 Pacific Boulevard, Albany, Oregon 97321 for over 30 years.  Three UST systems were upgraded and additional contamination discovered in 1991 followed by removal of ~500 yd3 of petroleum contaminated soil in 1993. The last generation of USTs were decommissioning by removal in 1996. Between 2002 and 2006 BB&A Env. did some further investigation on-site and to the north onto the adjacent property.  In 2011 a ""Trap-and Treat"" groundwater remediation system that uses an activated carbon and bacteria concentrate mixture was put into persistent contamination zones. By 2012 concentrations had significantly decreased (primarily off-site). The well array was not monitored again until the fall of 2013.  In June of 2014 a revised conceptual site model was submitted and after the sale of the neighboring property, the site was proposed for a risk-based conditional closure in 2015. All soil and groundwater investigations, cleanup, monitoring, and a risk assessment were completed by the fall of 2015. Some contamination associated with leaks from the underground storage tank system remains. The responsible party has proposed that no further cleanup or monitoring is needed at this site.  DEQ has evaluated efforts to contain, clean up, and assess the site, right-of-ways, and adjacent properties and now believes that, given the current and reasonably likely future use of this site, the remaining contamination does not pose a significant threat to human health or the environment",20310.0,0.0,JGLASS,2016-08-11 17:49:12.600
