2891,15-91-0056,15,91,0056,70.0,1991-07-26,1999-03-18 13:49:10,REX BOUNDS,2366 HIGHWAY 66  (Ashland St),ASHLAND,97520,(503)482-3166,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"8/9/2012: first release reported in 1991 (15-91-0056) during site assessment. A second release was reported in 1998 (15-98-0037). USTs decommissioned and PCS removed December 15, 2000. SWLA signed 12/20/2000 for on-site treatment of PCS. Entered Release Stopped and Cleanup Started date of 12/15/2000 - C. Rodda.",15-98-0037 REX BOUNDS/BOUNDS 66 SERVICE,"In July 1991 during a site assessment, a release from overfills/spills was discovered at the gasoline underground storage tanks. (LUST File #15-91-0056) Low levels of soil contamination were discovered near the dispensers and tank pit but one sample showed more contamination (1,180 mg/kg gasoline) at 9 feet below ground on the north side of the tank pit. In January 1998 a second release was reported due to a failed tightness test. (LUST File #15-98-0037) In 2001, three 10,000-gallon gasoline tanks and one 500-gallon used oil tank were decommissioned by removal. About 570 cubic yards of impacted soil was excavated and aerated onsite under a Solid Waste Letter of Authorization between December 2001 and November of 2002. Confirmation samples from the gasoline UST excavation were all non-detect for gasoline except for one sample (23 mg/kg) which is below the most stringent soil matrix cleanup standard. In 2001, the excavation was backfilled with clean soil from a nearby construction site. The treated soil was reportedly disposed of at 2394 Emigrant Creek Road in Ashland. Confirmation samples from the used oil tank pit were all non-detect for hydrocarbons. No groundwater was encountered during the excavation and it was not investigated. Shallow groundwater may have been impacted from the releases but is unlikely to have been impacted above applicable screening levels.",17971.0,0.0,CRICHAR,2019-10-15 08:29:22.740
