ECSI #4444","The property was previously used as a retail gas station from 1976 until closure in 1991.  Ownership passed through several other hands until it became Lane County’s property through tax foreclosure in 2004.  

Petroleum contamination was discovered in 1991 during utility work along McVay Highway.  Five underground storage tanks were removed in 1996.  Since the 1991 report of contamination, environmental assessments and some contaminated soil removal have taken place. The primary source of contamination is from the former dispenser islands.  

In an effort to help revitalize the abandoned property, Lane County entered into negotiations with DEQ and SeQuential Biofuels to reuse the site.  In the fall of 2004, Lane County applied for a Brownfields Cleanup Grant from the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) to facilitate redevelopment of the site.  In the spring of 2005, DEQ assessed the site to determine the extent of contamination.  The first phase of cleanup was completed in the fall of 2005.  A plan for Phase II of the cleanup was proposed in January 2006 and implemented in between 2006 and 2007.  The property reuse is now complete.  A no further action determination was not made due to discovery of deeper groundwater contamination.  An investigation of potential sources in the area failed to clearly identify any off-site cause of the deeper contamination.  All known beneficial uses (former drinking water wells) in the area of remaining contamination have been removed and area property owners advised not to use the shallow or deeper groundwater in the area.  For more information please see the documents and pictures below.",22093.0,0.0,JGLASS,2011-07-07 17:59:07.320
2906,24-91-4244,24,91,4244,429.0,1991-08-20,1999-03-18 13:49:10,Fairview Training Center II,2250 STRONG RD SE,SALEM,97310,(503)378-5117,False,1.0,RBCA,4.0,,True,False,False,,True,False,False,False,False,*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : Duane Stark called 10-10-91 to report groundwater entered pit after a drain line was intercepted.  Product and water then entered pit.  Product appears to be bunker. They proceeded to remove free product (SUSPECTED RELEASE) 1/27/92 SOIL LETTER  SENT LINT GWLG BEGIN CR *** Pjt Mgr = JLG,See Also: 24-90-4046; 24-01-6790 (HOT),"Petroleum contamination was discovered during removal of two underground storage tanks in October 1991. Approximately 80 cubic yards of petroleum-contaminated soil was removed and taken to Northern Wasco County Landfill in The Dalles.  After the removal of the most heavily contaminated soil, areas of gasoline and non-gasoline petroleum contamination from diesel and heavier oil remained.  Additional assessments performed between August 2001 and April 2006 fully defined the extent of the contamination and evaluated the potential human and environmental exposure concerns. 

DEQ has determined that, given the site’s current and reasonably likely future land use, the remaining contamination from the leaky underground storage tank systems has been reduced to levels protective of human health and the environment.  This determination is based on historical and current soil, shallow groundwater, and former drinking water well sampling results.  The closure plan proposes to allow the remaining contamination in soil and shallow groundwater to biodegrade naturally in place.
",6088.0,0.0,JGLASS,2009-02-20 16:04:04.920
