3121,18-91-0080,18,91,0080,6120.0,1991-10-24,1999-03-18 13:49:10,FRANKO #44,503 E FRONT STREET/P.O. BOX 552 ,MERRILL,97633,(503) 588-0455,False,,,,,True,False,,,False,,,,,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : Now Merritt Truax (Merrill) RISKBASED NO FURTHER ACTION OKAYED NOVEMBER 26, 2001 EXCAVATION WORKERS ON SITE REQUIRED TO BE TRAINED IN HAZARDOUS WASTE SAFETY ISSUES REGARDING POTENTIAL EXPOSURE TO PETROLEUM HYDROCARBONS DEQ NOTIFICATION REQUIRED PRIOR TO USE OF GROUNDWATER ON ANY WELL CONSTRUCTED ON SITE.  GROUNDWATER SAMPLING AND ANALYSIS REQUIRED. *** ",UST Cleanups 18-91-0074 & 18-91-0064,,22078.0,,CDEMPSE,2002-01-17 15:30:02.000
3122,19-91-0081,19,91,0081,3502.0,1991-10-22,1999-03-18 13:49:10,LAKEVIEW RANGER DISTRICT,HWY 395 NORTH ,LAKEVIEW,97630,(503)947-3334,False,,,3.0,ADMCLO,True,False,,,False,,,,,*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : Extensive excavation underway. *** (8/29/05) This site was administratively closed on 8/29/2005 by direction of Sheila Monroe/Katie Robertson. The site was transferred to cleanup in 1996.  See ECSI #1387,ECSI # 1387,,20340.0,,CDEMPSE,2004-12-23 09:46:25.000
3123,26-91-0431,26,91,0431,5911.0,1991-10-24,1999-03-18 13:49:10,GENERAL MOTORS CORPORATION,9225 NE AIRPORT WAY,PORTLAND,97220,(503) 249-4900,False,4.0,GW,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Port of Portland discovered contamination while drilling dewatering wells for sewer line construction. Delineation of soil and groundwater contamination was initiated in 1991.  Efforts to remediate site have included SVE, injection of oxygen-release compunds, installation of oxygen saturation curtains and enhanced fluir recovery (EFR) events. 

Draft Corrective Action Plan submitted for site in 2009 by CRA proposing groundwater circulation, air stripping, air sparging and soil vapor extraction as a remedy.  General Motors Corporation declared bankruptcy before the CAP was approved by DEQ, however.  Despite this, work continued to finalize groundwater modeling to evaluate potential risks to deeper beneficial aquifers from a hypothetical well installed in the CRSA and TGA aquifers in the location of the shallow groundwater plume.  The modeling results allowed calculation of Maximum Allowable Source Area Concentrations (MASACs) for shallow groundwater that would be protective of deeper aquifers assuming a production well was installed at the site and operated in a manner consistent with other production wells in the wellfield.

In November 2011, Antea Group submitted a revision to the CRA Corrective Action Plan proposing excavation in the area of the dispensers and a pilot test of persultate injection in the area of the sewer utility corridor.  DEQ commented and approved work plan in December 2011.  In July 2012, Antea Group advanced borings in area of dispensers to demarcate soil to be removed. Following canopy removal, soil removal was completed between October and December 2012.  Approximately 1,900 tons of soil removed.  Confirmation sampling at the limits of the excavation revealed gasoline-range TPH at maximum concentration of 89 ppm and a maximum benzene concentration of 1 ppm in soil.  Confirmation sampling results indicate the majority of the source in soil was removed. Semiannual compliance groundwater monitoring underway.  By 2018, there were exceedances of MASACs in only three wells (MW-16, MW-18 and MW-19), all located in the footprint of the sewer corridor in the center of the site. Length of plume approximately 40'x20'.

Sulfate piltot test implemented in July 2016.  Results seemed to indicate decreased levels of gasoine and VOCs, suggesting that full-scale implementation could be effective.  Modification to CAP received in September 2017 proposing to implement full scale sulfate application remedy.  Sulfate application events were performed in December 2018, February 2018 and August 2018.

Final groundwater monitoring event performed in January 2019. Mann Kendall analysis for the most historically impacted monitoring wells showed trends of ""no trend"", ""stable"", ""probably decreasing"" or ""decreasing"".  90% UCLs of the mean were calculated.  Although the 90% UCL concentration of benzene in MW-16 exceeded the MASAC, Mann-Kendall trend analysis showed a decreasing trend.  Based on the limited extent of groundwater exceeding MASACs, and low probability that a future production well will be installed in a sewer corridor, the remaining contamination does not pose unacceptable risks to Portland residents or site employees.  The site qualifies for an unconditional NFA DEQ has approved Revised CMMP dated 10-3-19. (JKS 10-9-19).",,"Draft Corrective Action Plan submitted for site in 2009 by CRA proposing groundwater circulation, air stripping, air sparging and soil vapor extraction as a remedy. The CAP was never approved by DEQ. However, groundwater modeling was performed to evaluate potential risks to deeper beneficial aquifers from a hypothetical well installed in the CRSA and TGA in the location of the shallow groundwater plume. The modeling results allowed calculation of Maximum Allowable Source Area Concentrations (MASACs) for shallow groundwater that would be protective of deeper aquifers assuming a production well was installed at the site and operated in a manner consistent with other production wells in the wellfield.

In November 2011, Antea Group submitted a revision to the CRA Corrective Action Plan proposing excavation in the area of the dispensers and a pilot test of persultate injection in the area of the sewer utility corridor. Following canopy removal, soil removal was completed between October and December 2012. Approximately 1,900 tons of soil removed.  Confirmation sampling at the limits of the excavation revealed gasoline-range TPH at maximum concentration of 89 ppm and a maximum benzene concentration of 1 ppm in soil. By 2018, semiannual monitoring revealed exceedances of MASACs in only three wells (MW-16, MW-18 and MW-19), all located in the footprint of the sewer corridor in the center of the site. Length of plume approximately 40'x20'.

Sulfate piltot test implemented in July 2016.  Results seemed to indicate decreased levels of gasoline and VOCs, suggesting that full-scale implementation could be effective. Modification to CAP received in September 2017 proposing to implement full scale sulfate application remedy. Sulfate application events were performed in December 2018, February 2018 and August 2018.

Final groundwater monitoring event performed in January 2019. Mann Kendall analysis for the most historically impacted monitoring wells showed trends of ""no trend"", ""stable"", ""probably decreasing"" or ""decreasing"". 90% UCLs of the mean were calculated.  Although the 90% UCL concentration of benzene in MW-16 exceeded the MASAC, Mann-Kendall trend analysis showed a decreasing trend. Based on the limited extent of groundwater exceeding MASACs, and low probability that a future production well will be installed in a sewer corridor, the remaining contamination does not pose unacceptable risks to Portland residents or site employees.",14923.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2019-10-09 10:21:04.370
