3207,10-91-0079,10,91,0079,272.0,1991-10-15,1999-03-18 13:49:10,GLENDALE SHELL SERVICE,107 SETHER AVE,GLENDALE,97442,(503)832-2290,False,1.0,MATRIX,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"1991 sample results indicate >20,000 ppm 418.1 in soil at 19-21 ft BGS, need confirmation sampling to close, should be GW present at that depth?

site assessment was conducted in the vicinity of the existing UST nest and dispensers in April 2009, no contamination was found (Esc, 28-may-09)

sent CRA to Lynn Diltz 4-jan-11 (Esc)

2/11/2013: SI documents GW contamination; changed ""Site Type"" to Risk-based standards (C. Rodda) Site re-opened on : 2/6/2015 By Virginia Deck.",,"The site has operated as a fuel service station since at least 1960 when the first recorded tanks were installed at the property west of the building. The active gasoline tanks were installed in 1992 after four other tanks (3 gasoline and 1 used oil) were decommissioned by removal. Soil samples collected in 1991 revealed high levels of petroleum hydrocarbons in soil, including concentrations at 21,000 ppm. This sample was collected from 16 feet bgs. Approximately 200 cubic yards of petroleum-contaminated soil was removed from the tank cavity and taken to an off-site property for treatment. In a letter from March 1992, Mr. Weast stated the gasoline tanks and lines had not leaked, but that the waste oil tank was a source of contamination. This document also reported some contamination remained as far as 7 feet under the site building. The new tanks were installed in a separate excavation north of the station. In 2009, RYS Environmental conducted site investigation activities as part of temporary closure requirements for the current tanks. Four borings were advanced to collect soil samples near the dispensers, which had not previously been investigated, and adjacent to the active tank nest. Borings near the former tank cavity were not included this phase of assessment. The 2009 soil samples were collected from 5 to 10.5 feet bgs and tested for the presence of hydrocarbons. No petroleum was detected in the four soil samples. DEQ asked for further assessment of the original tank cavity in 2012. Samples were collected and confirmed petroleum remained in soil and groundwater at the former tank location. After evaluating potential receptors and risk to remaining petroleum contamination, DEQ agreed to closure with certain conditions on the property. RYS developed a Health & Safety Plan to be used by excavation workers to prevent direct contact with contaminated soil and groundwater. DEQ also requires no drinking water wells be installed and limits residential use of the property.",18132.0,0.0,GDECK,2016-08-18 13:32:55.520
