[GDeck 3/15/2021] - First NFA letter issued 11/20/2020.  Site reopened 3/15/2021 so we could reevaluate the restrictions.  We expect to issue a new NFA letter and date. City of Springfield is the new owner. 

Site re-opened on : 3/15/2021 By Virginia Deck. Previous Final Invoice Request Date: 11/20/2020.. Previous Closed Date: 11/20/2020.",See Also: 20-90-4057,"DEQ is currently accepting public comments through May 21, 2021 on its proposal to issue an unconditional No Further Action for the site and remove the deed restrictions recorded on the site in November 2020. Details are in the Staff Memo at the link below. There were two historical releases at the Panoco site. The first was in April 1990, LUST #20-90-4057.  Fifty cubic yards of petroleum contaminated soil (PCS) were removed, aerated on-site, and reused as fill material.   DEQ issued an NFA on September 5, 1990. In June 1992 a second release was discovered during the decommissioning of three 10,000-gallon gasoline USTs and one 10,000-gallon diesel UST and the DEQ assigned  LUST #20-91-4345 to the site.
November 5, 2020 an Easement and Equitable Servitudes was recorded with the county restricting groundwater use, and on November 20, 2020 a conditional No Further Action letter was issued to ODOT.  Subsequently the property was purchased by the city of Springfield.  Springfield entered the VCP to re-assess the risks at the site and DEQ has determined that deed restrictions in the form of the EES are not required to maintain protectiveness of the site.",10471.0,0.0,DHANSON,2021-05-21 08:21:31.547
