3367,24-91-4379,24,91,4379,4272.0,1991-12-17,1999-03-18 13:49:10,Leathers Truck Stop,12334 EHLEN RD NE,AURORA,97002,(503) 661-1244,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : Pipe leak 0.16 gallons per hour premium gas, pressurized system, scheduling to get it repaired. *** Pjt Mgr=CJR.  
4.5 yd3 of contaminated soils were removed and taken to maintenance shop property for aeration.  No Permit Addendum on file. 3-16-93 jlg",,"The original release  was reported to DEQ in December 1991 when a leak was detected in product lines for the south dispenser island. The leak was repaired and 4.5 cubic yards of petroleum contaminated soil  was removed from a 4-ft trench and transported to a Leathers Oil facility in Portland and no samples were collected. New tanks were installed in July 1998, creating two separate tank nests with a total of five 20,000-gallon tanks. No additional investigation was done until a facility upgrade in June 1998. The old tanks were:one 20,000-gallon diesel, one 10,000-gallon unleaded gasoline, one 8,000-gallon regular gasoline, and one 6,000-gallon premium gasoline tanks located in a single nest near the central entrance to the facility. These tanks were decommissioned by removal in April 1999, while the old dispensers were removed earlier in February. Approximately 900 cubic yards of contaminated soil was excavated at the tanks and dispensers. A Solid Waste Letter of Authorization to treat the soil on-site using aeration in separate treatment cells was approved in September 1999. Three treatment cells were set up at the southern portion of the property parallel to I-5. Monitoring wells were installed in 2000. Groundwater sampling in January 2001 identifed free product in MW-6. Absorbent socks were used to collect product (1-½ ft thick). The socks were replaced three times until March 2001 when product was no longer present, though 2 inches of free product were found in MW-1 and MW-5 in July 2001. In 2013, Leathers planned to sell the facility to Flying J/Pilot Truck Centers and the treated soil, site assessment, and risk to workers was evaluated by DEQ. The 1999 treated soil was sampled (low levels), removed from the berm, and disposed at Leathers property adjacent to the site in a plan approved by DEQ. The original on-site drinking water well was sampled for petroleum in June 2001, and EDC was detected at 1.8 ppb. A treatment system (carbon filter) was installed and a water sample was again collected in July 2002, with EDC at 1.23 ppb. With facility upgrades, the on-site well was abandoned in 2013 with approval by Oregon Water Resources Department (OWRD). The station is now supplied drinking water by a public water system called Bents Court Water Works, located west of the station. The cleanup and investigation provided evidence that the 1991 petroleum release was addressed and the site is protective of human health and the environment.",78896.0,0.0,CRODDA,2014-11-03 08:27:25.670
