11/29/2007: SUB wells are located 1,350 ft. NW and 1,650 feet NE of the site; there is also a record for a domestic well 300 feet west of the site - C. Rodda",,"The site was used as a gasoline service station for approximately thirty years. Two underground gasoline tanks were located at the center of the property, with a heating oil tank and one waste oil tank located together to the southeast. A site assessment was conducted at the operating facility in December 1991 with five monitoring wells. Soil had gasoline at 2,000 ppm and water samples confirmed petroleum contamination. More than 12 inches of free product was measured in MW-3 during initial investigation activities. In October 1992, four tanks, associated product lines, and dispensers were decommissioned by removal and the station was razed. Approximately 1,000 cubic yards of petroleum contaminated soil was excavated. Contaminated soil was taken from three separate excavations that included the former waste/heating oil tanks, hydraulic lifts, product lines, pump islands, and gasoline tanks. The excavation at the gasoline tank basin extended to 14.5 feet below ground surface. Contaminated soil remained in the bottom and sidewalls of this excavation. A soil sample from 13.5 feet below the former dispensers had gasoline at 9,000 ppm. DEQ provided a Solid Waste Letter of Authorization in 1994 for treatment of contaminated soil. Cleanup included hot air vapor extraction technology, which treated 975 cubic yards of PCS to within acceptable levels. Twenty soil samples taken after the HAVE treatment found no detectable gasoline. Pit water was left in place with crushed rock backfilled above the water level before treated soil was returned to the excavations. Five additional wells were installed in 1992. Wells MW-1 through MW-4 were destroyed during remedial excavations in 1992, and MW-5 was lost during site activities in 1994. In 1999, 7 borings were advanced for post-remedial assessment of soil and groundwater. The highest petroleum in soil was at GP-4, with gasoline at 5,080 ppm. Following a proposal for site closure, Springfield Utility Board requested further groundwater testing, and MW-11 and 12 were sampled beginning in 2005. Investigation in 2010 provided new information on groundwater flow direction, and further assessment was initated including the installation of four replacements wells. Groundwater monitoring continued through 2012, and a soil-gas assessment was conducted to ensure the new on-site structures were not at risk from petroleum vapors. Groundwater and soil-vapor samples confirmed no risk to likely receptors, including site workers.",1690.0,0.0,LLO,2016-03-25 13:41:14.430
