3712,26-92-0124,26,92,0124,3100.0,1992-04-29,1999-03-18 13:49:10,GREYHOUND LINES INC,2521 SW WATER AVE,PORTLAND,97201,(503) 243-2332,False,1.0,RBCA,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Repeated Enhanced Fluid Recovery (EFR) events are conducted along with GW monitoring as needed. Free product are being extracted from various wells including MW-15, WP-1, WP-7 and MW-11.  TPH as gasoline, TPH as diesel and TPH as oil-concentrations and dissolved benzene are being detected in MW-16, MW-18 and MW-19.  During February 2010 event, approximately four feet of SPH was detected in MW-15.  EFR events conducted in January, February, and March 2010.  Product not detected in MW-15 after January 2010 (JKS 4-1-10).   Redevelopment as PGE substation reportedly deferred to 2012.  Contacted ATC Associates on 1-22-13.  ATC confirmed that FirstGroup had not authorized work at that site in over a year.  Following transfer to PGE, FirstGroup began to question their continued liability.  According to consultant, old building has been raized and it is possible the wells have been destroyed.  Status of site development plans were not known by ATC Associates. 

In July 2013, Green Star submitted a CAP proposing surfactant-enhanced ISCO.  Two treatment areas, roughly corresponding to the locations of former UST were defined at the site.  Green Star proposed a network of 39 permanent and temporary injection points at the site.  DEQ put the CAP proposal to public comment on 7-31-13.

ODOT, who owns infrastructure related to the Marquam Brige immediately north of the site commented extensively.  ODOT cited concerns about untreated wood piling supporting the bridge bents and nearby retaining walls.  Green Star reduced the proposed injection rates and pressures.  In addition, Green Star submitted a plan proposing to monitor ORP values in sentinel wells located near critical infrastructure. Changes in ORP above a certain threshold value would result in decreased injection rates and increased monitoring frequency.  Green Star also proposed to install elevation control points on critical infrastructure and survey them periodically.  ODOT approved the monitoring plan in October 2014.  DEQ approved the CAP in November 2013.

Green Star installed the injection points in February 2014.  The first injection event was performed the same month.   Based on the appearance of product in March 2014, Green Star performed additional injection events in June and July 2014.  A total of approximately 24,000 gallons of ISCO fluids were injected among these events.  Post-remedial monitoring was iniitated in July 2014.  Additional gauging in October 2014 did not reveal measurable product.  Post-remedial soil and soil-gas investigations revealed extremely low concentrations of gasoline and related constituents. (JKS 2-2-15).",,"Repeated Enhanced Fluid Recovery (EFR) events are conducted along with GW monitoring as needed. Free product are being extracted from various wells including MW-15, WP-1, WP-7 and MW-11.  TPH as gasoline, TPH as diesel and TPH as oil-concentrations and dissolved benzene are being detected in MW-16, MW-18 and MW-19.  During February 2010 event, approximately four feet of SPH was detected in MW-15.  EFR events conducted in January, February, and March 2010.  Product not detected in MW-15 after January 2010 (JKS 4-1-10).   Redevelopment as PGE substation reportedly deferred to 2012.  Contacted ATC Associates on 1-22-13.  ATC confirmed that FirstGroup had not authorized work at that site in over a year.  Following transfer to PGE, FirstGroup began to question their continued liability.  According to consultant, old building has been raized and it is possible the wells have been destroyed.  Status of site development plans were not known by ATC Associates. 

In July 2013, Green Star submitted a CAP proposing surfactant-enhanced ISCO.  Two treatment areas, roughly corresponding to the locations of former UST were defined at the site.  Green Star proposed a network of 39 permanent and temporary injection points at the site.  DEQ put the CAP proposal to public comment on 7-31-13.

ODOT, who owns infrastructure related to the Marquam Brige immediately north of the site commented extensively.  ODOT cited concerns about untreated wood piling supporting the bridge bents and nearby retaining walls.  Green Star reduced the proposed injection rates and pressures.  In addition, Green Star submitted a plan proposing to monitor ORP values in sentinel wells located near critical infrastructure. Changes in ORP above a certain threshold value would result in decreased injection rates and increased monitoring frequency.  Green Star also proposed to install elevation control points on critical infrastructure and survey them periodically.  ODOT approved the monitoring plan in October 2014.  DEQ approved the CAP in November 2013.

Green Star installed the injection points in February 2014.  The first injection event was performed the same month.   Based on the appearance of product in March 2014, Green Star performed additional injection events in June and July 2014.  A total of approximately 24,000 gallons of ISCO fluids were injected among these events.  Post-remedial monitoring was iniitated in July 2014 - no measurable product was found. ",396.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2015-02-02 08:25:37.660
