4019,34-92-0236,34,92,0236,3685.0,1992-08-19,1999-03-18 13:49:10,MERIDIAN PARK HOSPITAL,19300 SW 65TH ,TUALATIN,97062,(503) 692-2196,False,7.0,MATRIX,,,True,False,,,False,,,,,,,,49715.0,,DSMITH,1997-07-16 14:31:44.000
4020,03-92-0235,03,92,0235,3451.0,1992-08-19,1999-03-18 13:49:10,DBA OLSON BROS. TEXACO,10700 SE MCLOUGHLIN BLVD,MILWAUKIE,97222,(503)654-2220,False,1.0,RBCA,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"On 3/31/05 file was reopened due to change in land use for a mixed commercial/urban residential development by Metro through a PPA.  Site investigations conducted in April and May 2005 revealed that soil impacts extend under adjacent rights-of-way.  TPH-G and associated constituents detected at concentrations exceeding urban residential vapor intrusion RBC standards.  Metro entered into PPA in September 2005 - the terms of the agreement indicated that the USTs would be decommissioned prior to development of site.  Further, the PPA stated that the site could not be developed for urban residential use unless vapor intrusion risks were mitigated. Per 1-14-09 letter from Metro, site (now used as parking lot) will not not likely be developed in the next 18 to 24 months. Metro assures DEQ any request for development proposals will contain a full disclosure of the terms of the PPA.  Should follow up with Metro regarding revisions to schedule (JKS 09-21-10).  Call to Metro Attorney Joel Morton on 2-9-11.  He reports that exploratory feeler received from City of Milwaukie, but Metro not interested.  In his opinion, developement unlikely for another 18-24 montsh or longer.  Discussed how provision of PPA allows DEQ to terminate agreemetn with 30 days written notice.  He would like to explore extension of PPA for period of 5 days.  I told him we'd need to consult with Charlie Landman and Bruce Gilles  (JKS 2-8-11).",,,20298.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2011-02-09 15:56:39.327
4021,26-92-0234,26,92,0234,3308.0,1992-08-19,1999-03-18 13:49:10,BURLINGTON NORTHERN RR - PORTLAND #214B,1105 NW 9TH AVE,PORTLAND,972,(503) 241-6238,False,,MATRIX,3.0,,True,False,False,,False,True,False,False,False,*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : 1800 GAL LUBE OIL.  TANK WAS SURROUNDED  HEAVY DIESEL CONTAMINATION - PART OF OVERALL ECD CONCERN.  THIS FILE ONLY ADDRESSES THE LUBE OIL FROM THE TANK.ADMINISTRATIVELY CLOSED 04-FEB-94 9000 PPM DIESEL AND OIL.  COORDINATION W/DEQ BUT NOT IDENTIFIED AS REPORTED RELEASE. DISPOSAL: 70 YDS DIESEL ONLY - NOT PUT BACK DUE TO COMPACTION  30 YDS (OIL AND DIESEL). *ADMINISTRATIVELY CLOSED 2/4/94* *** Referred to VCS,,,8899.0,0.0,LLO,2016-03-25 13:41:14.430
4022,26-92-0237,26,92,0237,4766.0,1992-08-20,1999-03-18 13:49:10,PLAID PANTRY #83,9101 NORTH LOMBARD,PORTLAND,97203,(503) 286-9048,False,1.0,MATRIX,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Gasoline was encountered in soil at the site in August 1992 during trenching for a storm sewer line.  Approx. 20 cubic yards of PCS was excavated from the trench and shipped off-site for thermal treatment.  A confirmation soil sample from 14 feet bgs found 2,400 ppm gasoline.  Terry Pyle of Plaid Pantries Inc asked to delay further investigation and cleanup work until they installed a Stage II vapor recovery system.

In February 1994 DEQ asked for a cleanup report, plus tank tightness testing and inventory control records.  When the information was submitted in July 1994, Terry indicated that the tightness testing (in May 1994) had been conducted soon after installation of the Stage II vapor recovery system.  DEQ had no further response from Terry Pyle since then.

During system upgrades in May 2012, the contractor excavated to the top of the four tanks.  Soil samples collected from each shallow excavation contained only minimal levels of gasoline and heavy oil.  Additional borings advanced in the area of the USTs and dispenser islands did not identify evidence for a release. 

In November 2012 DEQ received a (Cleanup Program) Intent to Participate form from Mark Conan with Plaid Pantries, along with a Site Characterization Report. 

I July 2013, at the request of DEQ, EES advanced borings in the vicinity of 1992 sample PP-SS-1-14.2 to reproduce the historical data and further delineate the extent of contamination.  The July 2013 investigation showed that contamination in the area of the 1992 sample is extremely limited in extent.  In addition, soil-gas samples collected at the northeast and northwest property boundaries near residential dwellings did not coontain gasoline or VOCs at levels abover soil-gas RBCs. Although zoining allows residential use, based on the size, corner location and historic use, DEQ believes continued residential use is likely.

Groundwater not encountered to maximum exploration depth of 60 feet bgs.  The minimum depth of the top of screened interval for water wells located outside the 0.25-mile search radius is 75 feet.  Because contaminatino is limited in extent and depth, impacts to groundwater are considered unlikely.  Groundwater does not have a beneficial use. Site is appropraite for closure (JKS 10-31-13).",,"Gasoline was encountered in soil at the site in August 1992 during trenching for a storm sewer line.  Approx. 20 cubic yards of PCS was excavated from the trench and shipped off-site for thermal treatment.  A confirmation soil sample from 14 feet bgs found 2,400 ppm gasoline.  Terry Pyle of Plaid Pantries Inc asked to delay further investigation and cleanup work until they installed a Stage II vapor recovery system.

In February 1994 DEQ asked for a cleanup report, plus tank tightness testing and inventory control records.  When the information was submitted in July 1994, Terry indicated that the tightness testing (in May 1994) had been conducted soon after installation of the Stage II vapor recovery system.  DEQ had no further response from Terry Pyle since then.

During system upgrades in May 2012, the contractor excavated to the top of the four tanks.  Soil samples collected from each shallow excavation contained only minimal levels of gasoline and heavy oil.  Additional borings advanced in the area of the USTs and dispenser islands did not identify evidence for a release. 

In November 2012 DEQ received a (Cleanup Program) Intent to Participate form from Mark Conan with Plaid Pantries, along with a Site Characterization Report. 

I July 2013, at the request of DEQ, EES advanced borings in the vicinity of 1992 sample PP-SS-1-14.2 to reproduce the historical data and further delineate the extent of contamination.  The July 2013 investigation showed that contamination in the area of the 1992 sample is extremely limited in extent.  In addition, soil-gas samples collected at the northeast and northwest property boundaries near residential dwellings did not coontain gasoline or VOCs at levels abover soil-gas RBCs. Although zoining allows residential use, based on the size, corner location and historic use, DEQ believes continued residential use is likely.

Groundwater not encountered to maximum exploration depth of 60 feet bgs.  The minimum depth of the top of screened interval for water wells located outside the 0.25-mile search radius is 75 feet.  Because contaminatino is limited in extent and depth, impacts to groundwater are considered unlikely.  Groundwater does not have a beneficial use. Site is appropraite for closure (JKS 10-31-13).",21167.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2013-10-31 12:05:29.927
