4208,21-92-4193,21,92,4193,786.0,1992-10-08,1999-03-18 13:49:10,South Beach State Park,5580 S COAST HIGHWAY,NEWPORT,97365,(503) 867-7451,False,7.0,MATRIX,4.0,NFA,True,False,,,False,,,,,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : INITIAL GROUNDWATER.  MINOR IMPACTS ONLY, READY FOR NFA 2/27/03, ESC. NO IMPACT TO DRINKING WATER, NO OFF-SITE MIGRATION. *** Pjt Mgr=ESC 2/13/03       25 yd3 PCS permit still valid 2-4-93...RS 7-93.  PCS sent to OR-HC for thermal treatment 8-93...all samples ND...JLG",,,18487.0,,GDECK,2003-04-17 09:54:16.000
4209,22-92-4195,22,92,4195,4469.0,1992-10-12,1999-03-18 13:49:10,Russ Day-N-Nite,34100 BREWSTER RD,LEBANON,97355,(503)928-1954,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : INITIAL GROUNDWATER ***,,"The northwest corner of site has been the location of a small gas station and country store since at least the early 1980s. In September 1992, two gasoline USTs (1-500 gallon and 1- 1,000 gallon) were decommissioned by removal. Holes were observed in the USTs and groundwater was reported to have entered the excavation. Soil samples from the two removed USTs did not contain gasoline range hydrocarbons, while the groundwater sample contained low level BTEX compounds.
During decommissioning, a third gasoline UST, estimated at 1,000 gallons, was discovered. The 1992 decommissioning report states the third UST was left in the ground pending ownership determination. DEQ’s UST database reports that four USTs were decommissioned by removal from the site in 1992. The database reports the following tank sizes; 2- 500 gallons, 1- 550 gallons, and 1-1,000 gallons. Identified contractor invoices indicated two additional USTs were removed from the site during 1993.
According to WRD records, a water supply well at the site was abandoned in 1996 and a new one was installed. The new well was completed to 102 feet, with a surface seal to 18 feet, and screen from 22-32 feet.
Between 2018 and 2019, further assessment was completed and included a magnetometer survey, sampling of the onsite water well, and soil and groundwater sampling within and around the former UST nest. The magnetometer survey identified a possible anomaly which reportedly was rust in soil. 
Low-level gasoline detections in soil ranged up to 61.5 mg/kg and lead was detected at 11.2 mg/kg. A groundwater sample collected near the former fuel pump island and contained gasoline (513 µg/l) and naphthalene (19.2µg/l) above residential and occupational ingestion of groundwater RBCs. All other groundwater detections were non-detect and/or below residential RBCs. The onsite water well, located approximately 90 feet south and cross gradient of the groundwater exceedance, also possessed no VOC detections, including naphthalene.
Based on sample results acceptable risk levels are not exceeded. Water well construction details appear to be protective for human consumption. Prior to consumption use, the well should be tested for a full suite of petroleum hydrocarbon-related contaminants. Because the well has not been used in some time, testing should be completed after the well has been pumped and allowed to run for a predetermined amount of time.",20990.0,0.0,GDECK,2019-08-27 13:11:39.450
