4923,34-93-0006,34,93,0006,8766.0,1993-01-11,1999-03-18 13:49:10,ARDEN DANIELSON,1040 NE 25TH AVENUE ,HILLSBORO,97124,(503)640-5576,False,7.0,MATRIX,,,True,False,,,False,,,,,,,,34191.0,,MALBERT,1997-04-30 10:13:15.000
4924,26-93-0005,26,93,0005,7851.0,1993-01-11,1999-03-18 13:49:10,"RADER COMPANIES, INC.",4435 NE 148TH AVE,PORTLAND,97230,(503) 255-5330,False,1.0,GW,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"A 6,000-gallon UST (subdivided into three 2,000-gallon compartments holding gasoline and diesel) was decommissioned by removal at this site in January 1993.  Approximately 500 cubic yards of PCS were excavated from around the UST and stockpiled on-site for aeration treatment from August 1993 through September 1994, before being placed back into the excavation pit.  Additional excavations (February-April 2000) expanded the pit before being limited by natural gas lines and other obstructions.  The amount of soil excavated and where it was disposed of were not reported to DEQ.  Confirmation sampling found contamination remaining in the pit walls.  DEQ requested further documentation of the extent of residual contamination, but no additional information was received.  The project was backlogged in August 2010.

That same month, a separate investigation on the property to the north found 5,530 ppb gasoline, 59,500 ppb diesel, and 186,000 ppb heavy oil in the shallow groundwater near the former Rader UST.  The apparent off-site migration of contaminants makes this site a high priority for further action.  (12/21/10 KPD).

In July 2011, DEQ was contacted by Jones Heavy Equipment concerning impacts from former Rader Company UST detected on his  property.  Mr. Jones reported he was unable to get financing due to contamination on his property.  DEQ decided to evaluate whether Mr. Jones qualified for protection under a Contaminated Aquifer Policy Letter.  In August 2011, Mr. Jones indicated that he used the Rader Company UST for a brief time in 1986-87, although such use may continued until at least February 1990. On 10-4-11, DEQ informed Mr. Jones he would not qualify for a Contaminated Aquifer Policy Letter.  At the same time, a Warning Letter was sent to Henry Moshberger, owner of former Rader Company property on 10-11-11.  A work plan for a delineation investigation was requested. On 11-17-11, DEQ received brief letter proposing one soil boring near a historic soil sample with high levels of diesel.  In March 2012, RPS-BER Ltd. on behalf of the property owner submitted a work plan for the installation of monitoring wells to delineate the extent of contamination detected in the August 2010 Hemphill boring.  The monitoring wells (MW-1 through MW-4) were installed in March 2012.  Only very low levels of TPH were detected in soil from one of the wells. MW-4 was installed on the north side of the Jones building, in a location generally believed to be hydraulically down-gradient of the former UST.   Compliance monitoring over four quarters did not reveal detectable concentrations of gasoline, diesel or heavy oil, VOCs or PAHs in groundwater.  These findings indicate the extent of contamination is limited.  

Due to incomplete documentation for ealier work by EIS and differences in the locations of selected samples as portrayed in fiugres by different workers, some uncertainty in the locations of samples collected by DBA (1993) and EIS (2000) remains. However, follow-up investigations (2001 and 2002) were not able to reproduce the elevated diesel detections reported by EIS in 2000.  Soil and groundater data for test pits advanced in the vicinity of the EIS confirmation samples detected significantly lower levels.  In particular, VOCs and PAHs were detected at very low detections in grab samples from the test pits, suggestging that the volume of soil impacted by high levels of diesel is limited. 

Based on information provided by the City of Portland Water Bureau concerning nearby monitoring and production wells associated with the Wellfield, petroleum hydrocarbon impacts to production wells P-10 and P-16 are unlikely due to distance and depth of the producing intervals. 

DEQ does not believe that site conditions pose unacceptable health risks. Prior to  future construction work near the south and west limits of the EIS remedial excavation that could uncover soil contaminated by high levels of diesel, a CMMP should be prepared (JKS 2-25-14).",,"A 6,000-gallon UST (subdivided into three 2,000-gallon compartments holding gasoline and diesel) was decommissioned by removal at this site in January 1993.  Approximately 500 cubic yards of PCS were excavated from around the UST and stockpiled on-site for aeration treatment from August 1993 through September 1994, before being placed back into the excavation pit.  Additional excavations (February-April 2000) expanded the pit before being limited by natural gas lines and other obstructions.  The amount of soil excavated and where it was disposed of were not reported to DEQ.  Confirmation sampling found contamination remaining in the pit walls.  DEQ requested further documentation of the extent of residual contamination, but no additional information was received.  The project was backlogged in August 2010.

That same month, a separate investigation on the property to the north found 5,530 ppb gasoline, 59,500 ppb diesel, and 186,000 ppb heavy oil in the shallow groundwater near the former Rader UST.  The apparent off-site migration of contaminants makes this site a high priority for further action.  (12/21/10 KPD).

In July 2011, DEQ was contacted by Jones Heavy Equipment concerning impacts from former Rader Company UST detected on his  property.  Mr. Jones reported he was unable to get financing due to contamination on his property.  DEQ decided to evaluate whether Mr. Jones qualified for protection under a Contaminated Aquifer Policy Letter.  In August 2011, Mr. Jones indicated that he used the Rader Company UST for a brief time in 1986-87, although there is information indicating that such use lasted until at least February 1990. On 10-4-11, DEQ informed Mr. Jones he would not qualify for a Contaminated Aquifer Policy Letter.  At the same time, a Warning Letter was sent to Henry Moshberger, owner of former Rader Company property on 10-11-11 concernign the need for additional investigation.  A work plan for a delineation investigation was requested.  In March 2012, RPS-BER Ltd. on behalf of the property owner submitted a work plan for the installation of monitoring wells to delineate the extent of contamination detected in the August 2010 Hemphill boring.  Monitoring wells (MW-1 through MW-4) were installed in March 2012.  No detections in GW over four quarters. No adverse risk conditions to the wellfield were identified.  (JKS 2-25-14)",23322.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2014-03-07 08:04:14.063
