4946,36-93-4091,36,93,4091,10086.0,1993-06-22,1999-03-18 13:49:10,Fox Towing & Gas,1902 PORTLAND RD,NEWBERG,97132,(503) 538-4525,False,1.0,GW,4.0,,True,False,False,,True,False,False,False,False,"NFA issued 5/11/2007. Site reopened 7/18/2018 to evaluate new information...gdeck. 
Site re-opened on : 7/18/2018 By Virginia Deck. Previous Final Invoice Request Date: 5/11/2007.

Casey Michaels w/4-C's Env. reported contamination found during UST decommissioning/replacement on-site. Site was re-opened to evaluate contamination and determine if it is consistent w/previous release. CRA received from new owner who was not notified of deed restriction. EES has now been transferred to new owner. J.Claussen 8/8/18

Contamination determined be from previous release in 1993 under the 2007 conditional NFA. However, more PCS was removed during 2018 site work.  New NFA issued due to changes in site conditions. 2019 NFA supersedes the earlier NFA. ... C Jacobsen
",,"In 1993, during underground storage tank (UST) decommissioning activities, petroleum contaminated soil was discovered at the site creating  DEQ created leaking underground storage tank (LUST) File # 36 93-4091 at this time.
In June 1997, K & S Environmental conducted a limited subsurface investigation to identify the extent of contamination at the site.  In September 1997, five groundwater monitoring wells were installed at the site.  
In April 1998, one 350-gallon waste oil UST and one 500-gallon heating oil UST were decommissioned by removal.  In addition, one 10,000-gallon split compartment UST for gasoline and diesel was installed adjacent to the existing two 10,000-gallon gasoline USTs. Approximately 712 tons of petroleum-contaminated soil (PCS) was removed during the decommissioning activities.On May 15, 2007, DEQ issued a conditional No Further Action Letter (NFA) for the site.  

In 2018, two of the three USTs were replaced per new owners insurance requirements.  Upon tank removal, discovery of petroleum-contaminated soil occurred in August 2018.  At this time, new piping and containment sumps were installed on each of the two newly installed USTs as well as the existing split UST connecting to the existing dispensers. 216 tons of PCS was excavated and disposed at Riverbend Landfill.  10,760 gallons of groundwater from the tank pit was transported to the Newberg Waste Water Treatment Plant for treatment and disposal.  DEQ believes the residual contamination discovered during the August 2018 UST replacement activities were from the previous release in 1993.  Both gasoline, diesel and gasoline range VOCs were reported in the soil and groundwater analyses suggesting the contamination was related to the 1993 release.  DEQ maintains that the residual contamination on site does not present an unacceptable risk to human health, safety, welfare or the environment as stated in the May 15, 2007 NFA.  It appears that the soil excavation activities in 2018 further removed 216 tons of residual PCS attributed to the 1993 UST release.
An EES  will continue to implement the following restrictions at the site, per the 2007 EES:
Implementation of a Health and Safety Plan (HASP) is necessary for the handling and disposal of residual PCS for future construction and excavation workers.  DEQ continues to require a restriction on groundwater use at the site. Owner shall not install any new wells or extract or use groundwater at the property. 
-C.Jacobsen 3/6/2019",10724.0,0.0,GDECK,2019-09-17 17:38:10.460
