4995,02-93-4105,02,93,4105,,1993-07-16,1999-03-18 13:49:10,OSU - Heating Plant,501 SW Benton Pl,CORVALLIS,97331,,False,,,,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : CALL FROM LANCE JONES/OSU PHYSICAL PLANT 3-24-2005, BUNKER C PCS ENCOUNTERED AT 2' BGS WHILE ELECTRICAL CONTRACTOR TRENCHING ABOVE FORMER USTS (CLOSED IN PLACE).  OSU TO ENSURE PERSONNEL PROTECTION, PROPER PCS MGMT. AND FILE REPORT TO CLEAR *** Call from Lance Jones/OSU Physical Plant 3-24-2005, Bunker C PCS encountered at 2' bgs while electrical contractor trenching above former USTs (closed in place).  OSU to ensure personnel protection, proper PCS mgmt. and file report to cleanup file,HR

Complaint from City of Corvallis 3/4/09 regarding free product in a sump of OSU heating plant basement (E wall of basement is one end of old concrete bunker C tank); OSU thinks it is from fractionation of old bunker-C fuel, but analysis shows mostly diesel; are diesel AGTs, but piping is all above ground so OSU does not think this is the source -- could also be some unknown UST leaking, but OSU does not want to open a ""new release"" since Dan K. of OSU thinks it is the old '93 release.  Additional site investigation and report was done in Jan. 2011

(gdeck) 9/17/2019: Site address originally reported as BENTON PLACE ST.  updated to more correct address of 501 SW Benton Pl, Corvallis, OR 97331",File to Teresa Danovich 5/25/06.,"In 1993, DEQ was notified of a leak at this location. There are three 63,000 gallon compartments constructed of reinforced concrete that used to hold bunker C oil for heating as a back-up to natural gas. The west side of the large reinforced concrete tank forms a wall in the basement of the fuel header room that is on the east side of the OSU Heating Plant. The tanks were decommissioned in place in 1994 by UST Environmental Services, a company that was later discredited for performing fradulent work. An investigation was not completed at that time. In March 2005, DEQ was notified that an electrical contractor, who was trenching above the tanks, encountered petroleum contaminated soil. OSU was to ensure personnel protection, proper soil management, and file a report. In March 2009, DEQ received a complaint from the City of Corvallis regarding diesel free product in a stormwater monitoring manhole.  They traced the source back to a sump of the OSU heating plant basement. OSU thinks the fuel from fractionation of old bunker-C fuel. They installed an oil-water separator and plan to investigate when the heating plant closes.  Soil and groundwater investigations were conducted in January and March 2011, and there are plans to remove the tanks and for a follow-up investigation.
June 3, 2021: Approved work plan for the tank decommissioning.  Work expected to be completed July 2021.",156891.0,0.0,AFARRIS,2021-06-03 14:31:00.920
