5133,04-93-0153,04,93,0153,4324.0,1993-08-18,1999-03-18 13:49:10,D & D MARKET,"RT 6, BOX 240-HWY 30 & KOPPISCH RD ",ASTORIA,97103,(503)458-6801,False,4.0,GW,,,True,False,,,False,,,,,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : PUMPED TANKS DRY IMMEDIATELY *** 5 USTs decom by removal & taken to NW Truck Parts in Rainier for recycling.  1800 cu yds excavated, treated & disposed of on site.  Two small pockets of PCS remain on site.  5 GW mon wells installed.  Constituents in S & GW below occupational RBCs.",,,20906.0,,JSCHLAF,2005-07-11 14:11:43.000
5134,34-93-0155,34,93,0155,,1993-09-15,1999-03-18 13:49:10,HUDSON PLAZA,12950 SW PACIFIC HIGHWAY,TIGARD,97223,,False,4.0,GW,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,,,"Peavey Oil Company constructed a Texaco-branded service station at this site circa 1960.  Gasoline odors were reported in an adjacent building in April 1974, and free-product gasoline was discovered in the soil and groundwater in December 1974.  A leaking tank at the Texaco station was replaced in January 1975, but no cleanup was conducted.  Robert Hudson purchased the service station property in December 1984 for an expansion of Hudson Plaza, and the station was torn down in January 1985.  The soil and groundwater contamination was rediscovered in 1991 during an environmental assessment, and the property owners signed up for DEQ oversight in 1993.  A groundwater recovery well was installed, and an air sparging and soil vapor extraction (AS/SVE) system began operating by 1994.  The AS/SVE system was supplemented with a dual-phase extraction (DPE) system in late 1999, by which time over 11,000 pounds of petroleum hydrocarbons and nearly 400,000 gallons of groundwater had been extracted and treated.  Both the DPE and SVE systems failed in late 2001, and Peavey Oil declared bankruptcy a couple of years later.  By this time, nearly 20 groundwater monitoring wells had been installed at and downgradient from the site.  Four recirculation wells were installed at the site in 2007, and an In-Situ Well Air-Stripping system began operating in 2008.  By 2017, the treatment systems had been shut down, and confirmation sampling showed only two remaining concerns:  direct contact with contaminated groundwater, and the potential for vapors from residual soil contamination to impact any future buildings constructed in the current parking lot.  Deed restrictions were recorded on the property in 2020 requiring DEQ approval of future excavation work that may encounter groundwater, and requiring vapor mitigation controls beneath new or expanded buildings.",142466.0,0.0,KDANA,2020-05-28 11:05:27.330
