The Site was developed by at least 1959 and underground storage tanks were installed by at least 1966. In September 1993, the historic tanks were decommissioned and a release was reported to DEQ. In March 2010, a leak in a diesel fuel line was discovered and reported to DEQ. The older release is the result of leaking tanks and dispensers. The more recent release is the result of a leak in a diesel fuel line. Historically, soil and groundwater were impacted across the site from about 2 to 15 feet below ground. About 200 cubic yards of soil was treated onsite under a Solid Waste Letter of Authorization. This soil is now located underneath the parking lot. A groundwater extraction and air sparging system was operated at the site from 1995 until 2018. Contamination remains beneath the onsite building and along the locations of fuel lines. A pocket of elevated groundwater contamination remains south of the building near the transition sump and is the result of the 2010 diesel release. The site and surrounding properties are expected to remain commercial or be developed for commercial purposes and be connected to the Tri City municipal water supply. An opportunity to comment on the proposed conditional closure was given to all properties within 500 feet of the site to and the Tri City Sanitary Authority. No comments were received in the 30-day public comment period. On April 22, 2019 an Easement and Equitable Servitude was recorded with Douglas County on the property deed. The EES prohibits any residential use or mixed use of the property and prohibits beneficial use of groundwater beneath the property. The EES also requires that the approved Contaminated Media Management Plan be followed during any subsurface construction activities.",88786.0,0.0,CRICHAR,2019-07-02 09:45:44.433
