5421,17-93-0127,17,93,0127,3956.0,1993-12-30,1999-03-18 13:49:10,ARCO SERVICE STATION #4282,785 SE 7TH ST,GRANTS PASS,97526,(503)476-3824,False,1.0,GW,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : HOW DISCOVERED: DROP IN PRESSURE    12-29-93 SHUT DOWN. *** solvent in GW - source not identified, 10,500 ppb benzene in MW-4 Feb. '04, partial file for this project is in Coos Bay office - awaiting re-assignment

chlorinated solvent detections referred to EC Program (2-oct-09, Esc)","ECSI #1922
LUST 17-90-0181 ARCO SS #4282","Sanborn maps indicate the site was developed for automotive repair as early as 1930. The current building was constructed in 1959 as a gasoline fueling and service station. The service bays were later converted to a convenience store. The site remains an active retail fueling station with a convenience store. The exact source of the petroleum contamination is unknown as the decommissioned USTs and piping all appeared to be in good condition with no obvious holes present. Gasoline contamination remains in soil from about 2 feet to 12 feet below ground and is focused mostly beneath the north end of the property, in the vicinity of the western half of the UST pit, where new USTs are currently in use. Groundwater is impacted with gasoline and chlorinated solvents across much of the site, with the highest levels of solvents focused down gradient of the former service station building and extending beneath the width of M Street to the south. The source of the chlorinated solvent contamination has not been identified but may be from past auto maintenance and repair (including degreasing) historically conducted in the building. In August 2005, during the replacement of the USTs and piping, approximately 2,088 tons of soil and pea gravel were excavated and disposed of at the Riverbend landfill in McMinnville. Also during the decommissioning, about 20,000 gallons of contaminated groundwater were pumped from the excavation and treated onsite prior to being disposed of under a permit at the City of Grants Pass waste water treatment facility. Soil contamination remains primarily around the west end of the tank pit as described above. Groundwater contamination appears to remain primarily as solvents at decreasing levels at the southern end of the property and beneath M Street to the south. All remaining impacts to soil and groundwater are below applicable DEQ risk based concentrations. The site and surrounding properties are expected to remain commercial and connected to the municipal water supply. An opportunity to comment on the proposed closure was given to all neighboring property owners, the Oregon Department of Transportation, and the City of Grants Pass Public Works Department. No comments were received.",157326.0,0.0,CRICHAR,2020-12-14 13:30:50.440
