Sent out a SWLA packet 9-93 and advised RP that he must permit and treat or dispose of pcs within thirty days...9-18-93..jlg
January 21, 1994, sent NON for fa",,"The site is located at 843 Pacific Boulevard in Albany at the northeast corner of the intersection of Madison Street and Pacific Boulevard.  The site was historically used as a fuel service station from 1949 until 2005.  The subject property is currently used commercially and is zoned for commercial purposes.  The reasonably likely future use of the subject property will remain under commercial use.  Adjacent properties are also zoned and utilized for commercial purposes.  
In March 1993, a petroleum release was identified at the subject property after gasoline odors were present in a restaurant adjacent to the site.  A contractor was hired to conduct an investigation of the petroleum release.  A breach in the product line by the northeast dispenser pump was discovered that allowed petroleum to migrate through the subsurface on-site and along utility corridors towards the restaurant property.  The contractor excavated several trenches on the subject property and free-phase petroleum (free product) was present on water that entered the trenches.  Free product was removed and PCS  was excavated as part of initial remedial actions at the site.
In May 2006, BB&A decommissioned by removal two USTs located at the site, as well as associated dispensers and product lines.  BB&A also conducted a Phase I Environmental Site Assessment (ESA) at the subject property during June 2006, and in July 2006 initiated a Phase II ESA for the collection of soil and groundwater samples.  

In July 2006, BB&A conducted additional assessment of the former UST systems, installing seven probes (P-1 through P-5, MW-4, and MW-5) for the collection of soil and groundwater samples at the site.  Two of the borings were completed as monitoring wells to add to the three wells already present at the site.  

The last groundwater monitoring event for the site was completed on March 22, 2007.  Water samples from MW-1 through MW-6 were analyzed for gasoline-range hydrocarbons and all VOCs.  Gasoline was only detected in the water sample from MW-2 at a concentration of 317 µg/L.  Petroleum-related VOCs were not detected in water from MW-1 or MW-2.  The following concentrations of petroleum-related VOCs were detected in site wells during the March 2007 sampling event.",21848.0,0.0,GDECK,2008-05-01 12:23:07.810
