6039,20-94-7023,20,94,7023,11414.0,1994-10-19,1999-03-18 13:49:10,Hammer Lumber II,399 GARFIELD ST,EUGENE,97402,(503) 687-1400,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : RELEASE STOPPED AND CLEANUP STARTED. ***,,"9/25/2008: The site has been operated as a lumber processing facility containing several large buildings.  A petroleum release was reported in 1994 following the removal of one diesel tank adjacent to the warehouse building in the southwestern corner of the property, and one gasoline UST adjacent to the office building.

Two USTs were decommissioned by removal in October 1994.  Soil samples were collected from each cavity at the ends of the tanks by Bergeson-Boese & Associates.  Two samples were obtained from 10 feet bgs at the gasoline UST (Tank Pit #1), and detected benzene and toluene at relatively low concentrations.  Five soil samples were collected at the diesel UST cavity (Tank Pit #2) and dispenser.  Diesel-range hydrocarbons were measured in three of the samples, including under the dispenser at a concentration of 5,800 ppm , adjacent to a sanitary line at 1,400 ppm, and at the center of the warehouse building at 1,200 ppm.  Because of these obstructions, further excavation of contaminated soil was not completed.  

A site investigation was initiated in May 2005 by ESA with the installation of three push probes (GP1 through GP3) for the collection of soil and groundwater samples.  The assessment was documented in a 2006 report entitled Environmental Investigation.  Boring GP1 was installed at an angle underneath the warehouse building.  

Free-phase petroleum (free product) was present on soil and groundwater at GP1, located at the northeast corner of the site building.  The pump dispenser was also at this location prior to decommissioning.  Groundwater was encountered in GP1 at 10.2 feet bgs and product floated on the water surface.  The water sample from GP1 was not analyzed based on the obvious level of contamination, but a soil sample collected from this boring at 5 feet bgs measured diesel at 963 ppm as well as several PAHs and VOCs .

Water from GP2 detected several PAHs at relatively low concentrations and diesel at 937 ppb.  GP3 was advanced to the west of GP2, and groundwater at GP-3 did not contain concentrations of diesel-range hydrocarbons or PAHs above laboratory detection levels.  Soil samples from these borings did not detect diesel-range hydrocarbons.

Additional GW data was obtained in April 2000.  A groundwater sample was collected from an observation well placed in the backfill of the diesel UST excavation.  This water sample was analyzed for PAHs, none of which were detected above laboratory DL's.",24266.0,0.0,CRODDA,2011-05-10 08:42:27.260
