6322,20-94-7015,20,94,7015,1396.0,1994-09-22,1999-03-18 13:49:10,Texaco 025,1585 COBURG RD,EUGENE,97401,(503)344-9632,False,1.0,RBCA,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"This site listed in UST on Sequent as: Texaco Service Station.  It is i
listed in LUST on Sequent as: Texaco 025.",see memo,"A petroleum release from an operating underground tank system was suspected in 1994 when a leak was detected in the products lines. At that time, the site held three 10,000-gallon gasoline tanks, one 10,000-gallon diesel tank, as well as a 1,000-gallon heating oil and 550-gallon waste oil tank. Five borings were advanced to collect soil samples and install monitoring wells. Heavy oil was detected in some of the soil samples. Groundwater samples identified petroleum contamination, including benzene as high as 2,700 ppb in MW-1. The tanks were decommissioned and remedial excavation of petroleum contaminated soil was completed in July 2003 when the fuel station closed. The product lines, dispensers, canopy, and site building were also removed. Two previously unknown tanks were found near the waste and heating oil tanks at the southern end of the property. Approximately 1,700 tons of contaminated soil were removed to16 feet below ground surface. Soil samples were collected from the excavation limits at the north and south ends of the property. Gasoline, diesel, and heavy oil were detected in soil. Soil contaminants were highest at WA-13-15, with benzene at 0.12 ppm, naphthalene at 6.9 ppm, and 1,2,4-trimethylbenzene at 45 ppm. Groundwater monitoring has occurred since 1994. Additional wells were installed to further delineate the groundwater plume. Remedial efforts included biodegradation and the use of three injection points later used as monitoring wells. Soil-gas sampling was initiated in 2010 to assess potential vapor instrusion to the commercial building, and a final remedy for soil-vapors and groundwater contamination involved Enhanced Fluid Recovery. DEQ’s requirements for closure were met in 2013, though some contamination remains below risk levels for commercial operations. DEQ approved leaving this contamination because it did not present an unacceptable risk to human health, safety, welfare and the environment. Because the property has been the source of multiple petroleum releases, no drinking water wells should be installed without review by DEQ or Oregon Water Resources Department. DEQ also recommended residential use be restricted to second floor multi-family dwellings. A Health & Safety Plan/Contaminated Media Management Plan is available for use if excavation is planned at or adjacent to the site.",47726.0,0.0,GDECK,2014-09-22 10:52:51.117
