*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : OLD RELEASE REFERED FROM WM&C TO TANK PROGRAM AUGUST 9, 1995 *** 1983 (1000 gallon) release of gasoline. Site assessment completed. Current site use does not pose risk.  RP requested delay in NFA determination as it would include EES and current site conditions meet the limitation on site use.  Strong PCS re-discovered during Nov '06 UST system work, will conduct remedial excavation and confirmation sampling, then complete further subsurface investigation...","piping leak reported (#10-03-1780) after failed line tightness test

ECSI# 573 Truax Corp. #65","See ECSI# 573, LUST# 10-95-0041 & 10-03-1780. These cleanups have been closed under one NFA letter.   This determination is based on the DEQ regulations and the facts as we now understand them including, but not limited to the following:  
•  The DEQ Underground Storage Tank (UST) database indicates three USTs were installed at the site in May of 1971. From that time until 2013, the site operated as a fueling station.  
•    In 1983, a release in the fuel distribution system resulted in approximately a 1,000-gallon release and was assigned Environmental Cleanup Site Information (ECSI) # 573.  
•    In 1995, a release was referred to the Leaking Underground Storage Tank (LUST) Program and assigned the release number 10-95-0041. 
•    In 2003, a pipe small leak caused a release to the soil, which was reported to the LUST Program and assigned release number 10-03-1780.  
•    From August 5, 2013 to September 24, 2013, Western States Environmental Services, Inc. decommissioned by removal two 12,000-gallon steel USTs and one 5,000-gallon steel UST. All had been used to store petroleum. 
•    Approximately 470 tons of petroleum contaminated soil (PCS) were excavated from the site. The 
PCS was disposed at Coffin Butte Landfill in Corvallis, Oregon.
•    Within the impacted area, the properties are fully developed commercial and residential 
properties.
•    Impacted groundwater does not discharge to surface water or contact aquatic sediments. Based 
on this information, no additional ecological screening is required.
•    There are no water wells on the site or in the immediate vicinity. The property and 
neighborhood are provided municipal water from the City of Roseburg.
•    Despite the residual contamination remaining at the site, DEQ has determined the site does not 
pose an unacceptable risk, given the stated restrictions.
•    DEQ sent letters to neighboring property owners that may be impacted by this decision, asking 
for questions or comments on the proposed decision.  No comments were received.
•    An Easement and Equitable Servitudes was recorded on the property for the Vapor Barrier 
Engineering Control Use Restrictions and Contaminated Media Management Plan (CMMP).",20975.0,0.0,GDECK,2020-04-09 18:20:42.927
