7051,34-95-0258,34,95,0258,11640.0,1995-09-29,1999-03-18 13:49:10,AG WEST SUPPLY,185 W. MAIN EXTENSION,HILLSBORO,97123,(503) 363-2332,False,1.0,GW,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Gasoline and diesel in soil discovered during site assessment in Sept 1995.  Two USTs (700-gallon gasoline & 700-gallon diesel) removed in Sept 1998, along with about 400 tons of PCS.  Residual soil contamination (up to 1,150 ppm gasoline and 3,220 ppm diesel) remains.  Extent of contamination not determined.  Groundwater (at 11-12 ft bgs) has not been sampled.  (6/17/09 KPD).

Contacted by HydroCon (consultant) on 3-3-16.  In January 2016, HydroCon discovered siginficant soil and groundwater contamination in the area of the equipment storage yard and AST farm while doing a baseline investigation for a tentant proposing to operate the AST system. They are working on a plan to install monitoring wells to get more representative data than grab samples. Discussed unassigned status of this project and leaving oversight with the LUST program for now, unless wells show fresh product. (JKS 3-3-16).

Additional investigations continued in February, May, August and October 2016.  Seven permanent monitoring wells and a number of temporary wells were installed in the area of the storage yard, AST farm and former UST pit. Groundwater was determined to generally flow to the northwest. Groundwater samples showed that the worst gasoline, diesel and VOC contamination was present west of the USTs on the south side of the warehouse building. The investigations also showed that contaminant levels in groundwater samples from generally co-located borings and monitoring wells were very different in magnitude.  Samples collected from down-gradient monitoring wells had markedly reduced contaminants levels relative to nearby grab samples, indicating the grab samples were likely biased high due to turbidity.  Based on this fact and the the property configuration, groundwater contamination is unlikely to extend beyond the property.  A beneficial use determination indicated that groundwater within a 0.25 mile radius of the site is unlikely to be used as a source of drinking water.

With one exception, groundwater samples collected from site monitoring wells in 2016 (May-October) did not exceed urban residential or occupational worker RBCs for the vapor intrusion into building or direct contact in excavation exposure pathways. 

Soil gas samples were collected from the storage yard in August 2016.  In three of four samples, gasoline and/or benzene were detected at concentrations exceeding even occupational worker RBCs. A follow-up investigation in October 2016 resulted in four additional soil gas samples being collected from the warehouse building west of the AST farm.  Gasoine and VOCs were not detected in the warehouse samples at levels above urban residential RBCs for the vapor intrusion pathway. DEQ determined that an institutional control prohibiting future construction in a designated area of the site called the engineering control management area (ECMA). DEQ prepared a staff memo and initiated public comment in July 2017 on a proposed conditional NFA.  No comments were received.

In July 2017, during the comment period for the proposed LUST closure, an overfill incident resulted in a release of approximately 400 gallons of diesel.  Although product ponded in the containment area initially, within two hours the diesel product had drained through seams and cracks in the secondary containment. HydroCon recovered approxmately thirty gallons using absorbent pads.  HydroCon initiated site assesment shortly thereafter. Diesel was detected at levels up to 28,000 mg/kg in borings advanced west of the AST farm. Soil excavation was initiated shortly thereafter and the north and west walls of the secondary containment were removed. After product was observed flowing beneath the northernmost AST, it was removed from the containment to facilitate additional removal of diesel-saturated soils. Ultimately, 519 tons of contaminated soils were removed.  

In late July 2017 approximately 13 feet of free product was encountered in MW03 just west of the northernmost AST.  A pump was installed in the well and ultimately, 48 gallons of product were recovered. MW03 was subsequently destroyed during further soil removal.  In addition, pumping of the remedial excavation following excavation yielded approximately 500 gallons of oily fluids. HydroCon installed slotted horizontal and vertical piping in the excavation at depths of 3-8 feet to recover product and contaminated groundwater.  Repeated gauging of the piping did not reveal additional free product, however.  

HydoCon installed additional monitoring wells (MW08 through MW10) west and northwest of the remedial excavation. The wells were sampled between September 2017 and June 2018.  Although elevated levels of gasoline and BTEX were detected in MW09, no free product was present and levels in MW10 (further downgradient) were entirely consistent with conditions as known prior to the July 2017 diesel release. 

DEQ determined that the remedy originally proposed for the LUST incident was still protective and closure could proceed.  The risk conclusions were determined to be unchanged from the previous closure proposal  In November 2018, Ag West executed an Easement and Equitable Servitudes prohibiting construction in the Engineering Control Management Area (ECM) and requiring DEQ review of future development plans.  CMMP for site approved by DEQ in August 2018. (JKS 12-6-18).",,"In January 2016, HydroCon discovered siginficant soil and groundwater contamination in the area of the equipment storage yard and AST farm while doing a baseline investigation for a tentant proposing to operate the AST system. Site assessment in 2016 revealed contamination in the area of the AST farm comingled with contamination from former USTs.  High levels of gasoline and VOCs detected in soil gas - soil gas contamination poses risks to occupants of future buildings in a designated area of the site. E&ES proposed as part of conditional NFA for LUST release.
In July 2017 during the comment period for conditional NFA, a 400-gallon diesel release occured from the AST system.  More than 500 tons of diesel-saturated soil were removed from the site. Significant amout of free product was recovered from a recovery trench installed down-gradient of the release point.  Groundwater assessment (i.e., installation of additional monitoring wells) showed that product did not extend siginficantly beyond the remedial excavation. DEQ determined that site risk conditions were unchanged from the conditional NFA closure proposed in July 2017. E&ES recorded November 2018. The E&ES prohbits the owner from constructing future buildings in a designated engineering control management area without engineering controls to prevent vapor intrusion. Owner required to provide development plans to DEQ prior to construction.  Media Management Plan appoved by DEQ in August 2018.",52222.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2018-12-06 07:31:10.853
