7578,26-89-0305,26,89,0305,1999.0,1989-12-11,1999-03-18 13:49:10,CONSOLIDATED FREIGHTWAYS,2050 NW QUIMBY,PORTLAND,97209,(503)226-4681,False,1.0,GW,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Site re-opened on : 1/21/2009 By Elizabeth Clark. Previous Final Invoice Request Date: 6/4/2008.

Contamination was discovered when a waste oil UST was removed from the site in 1989.  Four groundwater monitoring wells were installed in November 1990, but have been sampled on a frequency that is intermittent at best (total of five times in 9 years)  TCE and PCE have been detected in all four wells at relatively low levels.  Sampling from four additional temporary wells in June 2009 found even higher levels of TCE and PCE in the parking lot area of the property (north of the building).  The results of this investigation effectively stoppped a proposed closure just prior to retirements of the previous project manager.  In March and April 2010, DEQ contacted the the consultant for the RP to discuss the project status.  Consultant informed that further delineation of solvents in groundwater in the northern portion of the site was necessary.  The consultant agued that low levels of TCE/PCE were consistent with a regional plume.  DEQ requested work plan for additional investigation.  Draft Work plan received in July 2010.    The work plan proposed  advancement of three borings along the eastern property boundary.  Work plan approved with comments in an email dated 07-19-10.  Potentially complete pathways incude groundwater ingestion and vapor intrusion under residential/urban residential exposure scenarios. Considering location in northwest Portland, groundwater ingestion pathway is probably incomplete. But the extent of the contamination has not been determined.  It's also not clear at this point if the groundwater contamination (55-60 ft bgs) is even related to the waste oil release.  Consultant reported on 10-5-10 that work plan was implemented.  Groundwater recovered in two of three borings.  Third boring met with refusal on a couple of attempts.  Results reportedly consistent with 2009 investigation.  Two borings along eastern boundary of property showed TCE/PCE detections - delineation not complete. Consultant again requested NFA, despite exceedances of urban residential vapor intrusion RBC in groundwater for TCE (JKS 12-9-10). DEQ contacted consultant in December 2011 to discuss.  DEQ informed Con-Way had performed investigation at down-gradient property and wished to submit this information to DEQ for consideration.  DEQ reviewed data in January 2012.  Data effectively show the limits of contamination, but evidence for regional or other source not clear. During a meeting in May 2012, DEQ agreed that although the source of TCE/PCE in site groundwater remains unclear, the magnitude of the detections are low enough that further evaluation is not warranted.  Public comment period initiated 5-22-12.  (JKS 6-5-12)",LUST #26-95-0308,,858.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2012-06-05 14:16:10.540
