8101,05-96-0407,05,96,0407,8727.0,1996-07-16,1999-03-18 13:49:10,"BERNHARDT, JOHN",305 S COLUMBIA RIVER HWY,SAINT HELENS,97051,(503) 397-5273,False,1.0,GW,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Site was an ARCO service station from at least 1959 until 1989.  Three gasoline USTs and one waste oil UST reportedly removed, but no report provided.  In July 1996, ODOT encountered soil and groundwater contamination during road and utility work on the highway.  Up to 1,400 ppm gasoline in the soil.  Groundwater not sampled but only 4 ft bgs.  Site owner John Bernhardt indicated an inability to pay in March 2003, but it's not clear if he completed ability-to-pay forms.  Extent of contamination still needs to be determined.  (7/23/09 KPD).

In April and August 2013, Alpha advanced 28 shallow soil borings at site to evaluate subsurface conditions in the vicinity of the former fueling USTs and a waste oil UST. Primarily heavy oil-range hydrocarons at low to moderate levels were found in the borings.  Only two heavy oil concentrations exceeded generic occupational worker and/or construction worker RBCs for diesel (used as an alternative comparison standard).  These ""hot spots"" were detected in distincly separate areas of the site. Based on heavy oil concentration data nearby for delineation, the pockets are extremely small in volume and exent and unlikely to pose unacceptable risks.  In soil samples containing the highest levels of heavy oil (13,900 mg/kg and 15,100 mg/kg), VOC constituents were low or ND.
Groundwater was encountered at depths ranging from 2.5 to 3 feet bgs. Groundwater not encountered everywhere, but in two borings in or near the former fueling USTs.  Groundwater appears to be discontinuous and likely perched on the shallow bedrock. Alpha surveyed properties in area of site and confirmed lack of water wells. Soil with low levels of gaosline, diesel and heavy oil extends into the southbound traffic lanes of Highway 30 but not at levels exceeding construction worker or excavation worker RBCs.  Contamination is reasonably well delineated and does not appear to pose unacceptable human health risks. (JKS 10-16-13).",,"Site was an ARCO service station from at least 1959 until 1989.  Three gasoline USTs and one waste oil UST reportedly removed, but no report provided.  In July 1996, ODOT encountered soil and groundwater contamination during road and utility work on the highway.  Up to 1,400 ppm gasoline in the soil.  Groundwater not sampled but only 4 ft bgs.  Site owner John Bernhardt indicated an inability to pay in March 2003, but it's not clear if he completed ability-to-pay forms.  Extent of contamination still needs to be determined.  (7/23/09 KPD)

In April and August 2013, Alpha advanced 28 shallow soil borings at site to evaluate subsurface conditions in the vicinity of the former fueling USTs and a waste oil UST. Primarily heavy oil-range hydrocarons at low to moderate levels were found in the borings.  Only two heavy oil concentrations exceeded generic occupational worker and/or construction worker RBCs for diesel (used as an alternative comparison standard).  These ""hot spots"" were detected in distincly separate areas of the site. Based on heavy oil concentration data nearby for delineation, the pockets are extremely small in volume and exent and unlikely to pose unacceptable risks.  In soil samples containing the highest levels of heavy oil (13,900 mg/kg and 15,100 mg/kg), VOC constituents were low or ND.
Groundwater was encountered at depths ranging from 2.5 to 3 feet bgs. Groundwater not encountered everywhere, but in two borings in or near the former fueling USTs.  Groundwater appears to be discontinuous and likely perched on the shallow bedrock. Alpha surveyed properties in area of site and confirmed lack of water wells. Soil with low levels of gaosline, diesel and heavy oil extends into the southbound traffic lanes of Highway 30 but not at levels exceeding construction worker or excavation worker RBCs.  Contamination is reasonably well delineated and does not appear to pose unacceptable human health risks. (JKS 10-16-13).",23911.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2013-10-16 09:03:18.090
