8425,02-96-4153,02,96,4153,5023.0,1996-10-17,1999-03-18 13:49:10,Marys River Lumber Co.,24515 HIGHWAY 20,PHILOMATH,97370,(541) 929-3146,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"only 20-day initial release form in file.  needs follow up.
Site address originally RT 1 BOX 48, 24515 HWY 20, changed to just 24515 HIGHWAY 20... g deck 9/11/12.",,"(MOTT 04/14/2017) In 1997, one 1,000-gallon gasoline UST located immediately south of the two diesel USTs was filled in place. During UST abandonment activities, soil samples were collected at each end of the UST and analyzed by NWTPH-HCID and NWTPH-Gx. TPH was not detected in any of the soil samples. In March 2004, the two 5,000-gallon diesel USTs were removed from the site. Four soil samples were collected from the sidewalls of the UST excavation at the soil water interface (approximately 7 feet below ground surface). Oil and grease was detected in one of the soil samples (MR-SW-3/31). Further analysis by NWTPH-Dx indicated a TPH-O concentration of 220mg/kg. Toluene, ethylbenezene, and xylenes were detected in all four soil samples; however, the concentrations were well below the most stringent RBCs. A water sample from the bottom of the excavation indicated detections of TPH-D (4,700µg/L), TPH-G (19,000µg/L), ethylbenzene (1.3µg/L), and xylenes (6.7µg/L). The pit water sample was not analyzed for polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs). In November 2016, Alpha Environmental attempted to collect groundwater samples at the site; however, refusal was encountered in all locations at depths ranging from 5 to 8 feet bgs and groundwater samples could not be collected.  (MOTT 12/28/17) The onsite water well was sampled for select VOCs and lead in 2004 and 2016. No VOCs were detected in the water well sample from 2004 or 2016. Lead was detected in the 2004 sample at a concentration of 2.4 µg/L, which is below the residential ingestion and inhalation from tapwater RBC.  Lead was not detected in the 2016 sample.  In July and October 2017, a water sample was collected from the adjacent property to the west’s domestic well (closest domestic well downgradient of the site). The water samples were analyzed for TPH-G, TPH-D, TPH-O, select VOCs, PAHs, and lead. The only compound detected was lead at 1.48 and 0.411 µg/L. Although elevated concentrations of TPH-G and TPH-D were detected in the groundwater sample collected from the diesel UST excavation in March 2004, nearby water wells do not appear to have been impacted by the petroleum release at the site. (MOTT 01/19/18) LUST 02-96-4153 was issued a NFA on Janaury 19, 2018.",46747.0,0.0,MOTT,2018-01-19 09:30:45.263
