New owner (John Oswald) paid Ficek's outstanding bill of $2837; DEQ agreed to not bill Oswald for any future time associated with rewriting NFA for his additional cleanup, unless additional contamination issues were discovered at the site...gad 10/18/06.",,"A service station has operated at the site from the 1950s until the present. Soil and groundwater contamination was initially reported to DEQ after the owner performed a site assessment in 1996.   Subsequent to the discovery of contamination, the owners have completed a significant amount of cleanup, determioned the full extent of contamination, operated a treatment system to cleanup groundwater while the property was undergoing redevelopment, and assessed the risk associated with the reduced contamination remaining. The closure plan for this former facility calls for restricting some use of the property and allowing any remaining contamination to degrade naturally in place. DEQ has reviewed the plan and has determined that the remaining contamination will not pose a significant threat to human health and the environment given the current and rreasonably likely future uses.",18162.0,0.0,JGLASS,2012-11-29 14:28:28.330
