8531,22-96-4168,22,96,4168,1191.0,1996-11-21,1999-03-18 13:49:10,Linn County Tractor,33965 HWY 99E,TANGENT,97389,(503)928-3359,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,True,False,False,False,False,NFA requires a CMMP be developed before any subsurface work is performed in the LOF.  See figure with NFA.,,"The site was historically farm land before being developed as an agricultural tractor business in about 1973. The property is currently used by a construction company. The exact cause of the contamination is unknown but believed to have been a leak from a 2,000 gallon gasoline tank. Soil and groundwater in the vicinity of the former tank are contaminated with gasoline compounds. The construction of the onsite water well is unknown. However, it is believed to be about 200 feet 
up gradient of the source area.  A sample collected from the well confirmed the well has not been impacted. Remedial actions include the removal of the tank in 1996 and a 2017 investigation of the magnitude and extent of the contamination. Contaminated soil remains onsite from about four to nine feet below the ground surface in an area around the former tank.  An estimated 100 cubic yards of soil remain. Contaminated groundwater also remains onsite in an elliptical area of about 20 feet by 30 feet. The property is expected to remain a commercial facility with no public utilities and only one drinking water well that is about 200 feet up gradient of the contaminated area. Any future subsurface construction greater than three feet in the vicinity of the former tank pit should be done in accordance with a contaminated media management plan (CMMP) and a health and safety plan (HASP) that will need to be developed by a qualified environmental professional before initiating the work. This only applies to an area approximately 20 feet by 30 feet around the former gasoline tank, as shown on the attached site plan.",18731.0,0.0,CRICHAR,2017-09-18 12:50:43.440
