9834,26-97-0756,26,97,0756,1116.0,1990-10-09,1999-03-18 13:49:10,CHEVRON #9-4383,710 SW COLUMBIA ST,PORTLAND,97201,(503) 224-3551,False,1.0,GW,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,,LUST #26-92-0062,"A release was reported in October 1997 during product pipeline replacement at the site.  Approximately 34 cubic yards of PCS were excavated and disposed of, but another 10 cubic yards could not be removed.  A total of 29 borings were advanced at the site from August 2003 to March 2005 as part of a site assessment.  Up to 38,000 ppm gasoline, 1,100 ppm diesel, and 710 ppm heavy oils were identified in two pockets: beneath and to the west of the northern pump island along Columbia Street, and beneath a former UST nest in the SE corner of the property.  No groundwater was encountered.

The service station was decommissioned in November 2005.  Three 10,000-gallon gasoline USTs, seven dispensers, two hydraulic hoists, and associated piping were removed from the site.  Final cleanup was placed on hold pending redevelopment of the site.

From July through October 2016 the majority of the site was excavated up to 75 ft bgs for a four-level underground parking garage for the future Broadway Tower.  About 7,000 cubic yards of clean soils were removed from the site, along with 74,000 tons of contaminated soils.  Contaminants (primarily gasoline and VOCs) extended to the limits of the excavation.  Residual contamination beneath the parking garage and beneath Columbia Street is unlikely to pose a future risk to human health.  Residual contamination beneath the northwest quarter of the block still needs to be investigated.",1987.0,0.0,KDANA,2017-04-25 13:02:36.240
