10101,22-97-7075,22,97,7075,4297.0,1997-12-15,1999-03-18 13:49:10,Leathers Albany II,33385 HWY 34 SE,ALBANY,97321,(503) 661-1244,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Pjt Mgr=CJR.

Note that this site will be used for CR charges and WebDocs records for both LUST 22-97-7075 and original release 22-94-4115 - C. Rodda 9/27/2011",Leathers Hwy 34 & I5 (LUST 22-94-4115),"The original release (22-94-4115) was reported by ODOT during reconstruction of Highway 34 in August 1994. ODOT hired a consultant to conduct assessment activities following the discovery of contaminated soil near the road. The consultant documented activities and confirmed the presence of gasoline, and also excavated three test pits. Petroleum contaminated soil (PCS) was found at TP-1 and PCS was stockpiled on-site. Soil piles were present on the north side of the property prior to ODOT activities, but the source of the soil was not documented. At the time the original release was discovered, the station had three gasoline underground tanks located at the southeast portion of the property. In 1997, these tanks were decommissioned by removal and two replacement tanks were installed at the west side of the property in May 1998. Three more tanks were decommissioned by removal from the north side of the station building that had stored waste oil, heating oil, and an unknown product. Soil samples here confirmed contaminants were present. Approximately 1,500 cubic yards of PCS was removed from the decommissioned tank nest. PCS was treated on-site and then disposed at the property designated in the approved SWLA application. Groundwater sampling was completed on shallow monitoring wells and on-site drinking water well. An off-site investigation was completed by MSBA in 2001 to determine if petroleum from the Younger Oil Company site (south) was migrating to the Leathers property. Sampling of the adjacent drainage ditch, a soil-gas investigation, and evaluation of potential risk was requested by DEQ between 2012 and 2014. The assessments defined the magnitude and extent of petroleum contamination remaining at the site, and led to a risk-based closure evaluation of the project. With certain restrictions on land and groundwater use, remaining contamination would not pose a risk to human health or the environmental. DEQ recommended No Further Action with a deed restriction prohibiting new drinking water wells, residential land use, and required annual testing of the on-site well for petroleum. An Easement & Equitable Servitudes document was recorded with Linn County property department on July 15, 2015.",11314.0,0.0,CRODDA,2015-08-10 16:31:24.970
