7/8/2018 from Randy Boese ""Soil samples from beneath the ends of the UST indicate minimal gasoline impact. Higher impact in soil noted by the pump. Impact noted in GW from MW1 @ N end of the UST. Need DEQ review to determine if new release has occurred."" GW: 1,160-Benzene, 400-Ethylbenzene, 296-Xylenes. Soil: 2,860-Benzene, 13,800-Ethylbenzene, 1,510-Toluene, 58,900-Xylenes, 907-GRO. Samples taken during decommissioning of UST#1958 J.Claussen 7/13/18 

Site re-opened on : 7/30/2018 By Virginia Deck. Previous Final Invoice Request Date: 3/22/2006.

Site file originally closed with NFA on 5/16/2006.  FILE REOPENED 7/30/2018 to evaluate new information and perhaps close under new NFA letter and date.",,"The site was first developed in 1975 and remains in its original configuration with an office, shop, and warehouse space in a ‘U’ shaped configuration. Contamination is reportedly the results of historically overfilling the tank, although soil sample results indicate the former dispenser may have been leaking. In March 1998, the 1,500 gallon gasoline tank was decommissioned and replaced with a new 1,500 gallon tank. About 35 tons of impacted soil was disposed of at Coffin Butte landfill in Corvallis. A Soil Vapor Extraction system was also operated from May to December of 1999 to treat residual contamination left in place. Soil, groundwater, and soil gas are impacted beneath the courtyard of the building as well as beneath the office and shop spaces. The contamination does not appear to have migrated off of the property. Figure 2 shows the approximate extent of contamination, or Locality of the Facility. Contamination remains from beneath the building down to about 12 feet below ground. Shallow groundwater flows generally to the south-southwest and has been measured between 5 and 10 feet below ground depending on the season. The site is expected to remain a commercial facility that is served by municipal water.",19289.0,0.0,CRICHAR,2020-01-14 10:20:04.637
