10558,34-98-0292,34,98,0292,,1998-04-22,1999-03-18 13:49:10,THRIFT STORE SITE,136 SE 3RD AVE,HILLSBORO,97123,,False,1.0,GW,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"The City of Hillsboro discovered two underground storage tanks, with capacities of 240-gallons and 550-gallons, beneath SE 3rd Avenue immediately NW of a Thrift Store (136 SE 3rd Avenue) while conducting road work in April 1998.  It was later determined the USTs were actually associated with the parcel immediately to the  north, known as Tax Lot 5400 (former 126 SE 3rd Avenue), which is now a parking lot for Premier Community Bank. Tax Lot 5400 has since been cancelled and incorporated into Tax Lot 5300 for Premier Community Bank. Both tanks were removed along with 123.69 tons of PCS.  Confirmation sampling found up to 11,000 ppm gasoline remaining in the soil.  Limited test pit investigations were performed in the area of the former USTs. The extent of remaining contamination needs to be determined, and shallow groundwater (14 feet bgs) should also be sampled.  The City of Hillsboro has not responded to any of DEQ's Backlog letters.  (8/11/10 KPD)

Closely associated with this release is contamination from USTs formerly located on the west side of SE 3rd Avenue (DEQ File No. 34-98-0403) which were decommissioned and removed in 1998.  These tanks were assigned to the property at 133 SE 3rd Avenue.  There is evidence that contamination from this release (""West Side USTs"") and the site (""East Side USTs"")  generally comingles west of SE 3rd Avenue. 

In 2014, the City of Hillsboro performed assesment of the site and contamination from former USTs on the west side of SE 3rd Avenue (34-98-0292) to further delinieate the extent of contamination.  Borings were advanced in the area of the ""East Side USTs"" and soil and groundawter samples were collected.  Soil samples from borings in the area of the former USTs contained lower levels of gasoline and VOCs than in the 1998 samples.  Groundwater contamination was found in three of seven samples collected, althought at depths and at magnitudes unlikely to pose unacceptable vapor intrusion or direct contact risks. Soil gas samples collected from properties near the ""East Side USTs"" did not contain gasoline or VOCs at levels posing risks to possible future residents.

A review of WRD records indicates groundwater within approximately 0.5 miles of the site is unlikely to be used as a source of drinking water.  The site and nearby properties are supplied with drinking water by the City of Hillsboro. 

In June 2018 DEQ notified property owners in the area of the East Side UST site of the proposed NFA status.  No comments were received.  

Soil gas samples collected from the West Side UST site contained naphthalene at concentrations above RBCs for ingestion in soil gas.  DEQ believes further assessment of the West Side UST site (34-98-0403) is necessary before a NFA can be issued.  Residual contamination at the East Side UST site is not considered to pose unacceptable risks to future residential or commercial occupants (JKS 7-17-18).",,"The City of Hillsboro discovered two underground storage tanks, with capacities of 240-gallons and 550-gallons, beneath SE 3rd Avenue immediately NW of a Thrift Store (136 SE 3rd Avenue) while conducting road work in April 1998.  It was later determined the USTs were actually associated with the parcel immediately to the  north, known as Tax Lot 5400 (former 126 SE 3rd Avenue), which is now a parking lot for Premier Community Bank. Tax Lot 5400 has since been cancelled and incorporated into Tax Lot 5300 for Premier Community Bank. Both tanks were removed along with 123.69 tons of PCS.  Confirmation sampling found up to 11,000 ppm gasoline remaining in the soil.  Limited test pit investigations were performed in the area of the former USTs.

In 2014, the City of Hillsboro performed assesment of the site and contamination from former USTs on the west side of SE 3rd Avenue (34-98-0292) to further delinieate the extent of contamination.  Borings were advanced in the area of the ""East Side USTs"" and soil and groundawter samples were collected.  Soil samples from borings in the area of the former USTs contained lower levels of gasoline and VOCs than in the 1998 samples.  Groundwater contamination was found in three of seven samples collected, althought at depths and at magnitudes unlikely to pose unacceptable vapor intrusion or direct contact risks. Soil gas samples collected from properties near the ""East Side USTs"" did not contain gasoline or VOCs at levels posing risks to possible future residents.

A review of WRD records indicates groundwater within approximately 0.5 miles of the site is unlikely to be used as a source of drinking water.  The site and nearby properties are supplied with drinking water by the City of Hillsboro. 

Soil gas samples collected from the West Side UST site contained naphthalene at concentrations above RBCs for ingestion in soil gas.  DEQ believes further assessment of the West Side UST site (34-98-0403) is necessary before a NFA can be issued.  Residual contamination at the East Side UST site is not considered to pose unacceptable risks to future residential or commercial occupants (JKS 7-17-18).",29854.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2018-07-17 11:12:50.670
