10965,04-98-0573,04,98,0573,1270.0,1998-07-22,1999-03-18 13:49:10,469 ONE STOP,469 W MARINE DR,ASTORIA,97103,503-325-6641,False,4.0,GW,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Petroleum-contaminated soil was discovered at this site in July 1998 during tank upgrade work.  A total of 27.42 tons of PCS were removed.  Confirmation sampling at the end of 1999 found up to 259 ppm gasoline in the soil, along with 29.7 ppb benzene and 8.83 ppb naphthalene in the groundwater.  DEQ requested additional sampling to define the extent of contamination, but the site owner (Johnson Oil) claimed an inability to pay.  DEQ placed a lien of $12,688.19 on the property in September 2003.

In February 2006, K&S Environmental conducted an additional investigation on behalf of a new owner.  Only 7.41 ppm gasoline was found in the soil, but up to 3,260 ppb gasoline was detected in the groundwater, along with 112 ppb benzene, 284 ppb ethylbenzene, and 163 ppb naphthalene.  K&S also noted an apparent fill pipe for an abandoned tank in the sidewalk along Marine Drive.

In July 2012, an investigation at an adjacent property (465 W. Marine Dr.) identified 572 ppm gasoline in the soil and 29,600 ppb gasoline in the groundwater (along with 28.1 ppb benzene), which was attributed to the 469 property.  Borings advanced just east of the prpoerty boundary with the site (adjacent to the multi-family residence) did not exhibit detectable levels of gasoline and VOCs. 

This site is upgradient from the Astoria Area-Wide cleanup project.  Laurey Cook recommended 5-6 additional borings to define the extent of contamination.  Four quarters of groundwater monitoring may also be needed at this point.  The site was backlogged in February 2008 due to a lack of progress.  (12/8/14 KPD)

In October 2016 HydroCon advanced borings in the northern portion of the site and in the area of the card lock to evaluate soil and groundwater conditions.  As with previous investigations, the highest levels of contamination were found in the northern portion of the ""ODOT Property"", a strip of land acquired by ODOT in 1962 and whose southern boundary roughly bisects the canopy (and which includes the former dispenser islands).  However, soila and groundwater samples from borings around the card lock equipment contained low to non-detect levels of gasoline and VOCs. A geophysical survey of the site revealed two anomalies suspected to be abandoned USTs in the ODOT Property.

Jackson and Son Oil plan to redevelop the property and expand the card lock. The former station building would be demolished as part of this work.  The property owner needs an NFA determination to proceed with the work and requested whether a partial NFA could be issued for the card lock portion of the property to facilitate the project. The partial NFA would not apply to the ODOT right-of-way or other properties suspected to be contaminated.  DEQ consulted with ODOT to inform the agency that high levels of groundwater contamination remain in the expanded right-of-way (ODOT Property).  ODOT agreed that it would not object to the partial NFA provided that further assessment and/or cleanup be performed in the right-of-way.  Also, geophysical anomalies suspected to be abandoned tanks will need to be addressed before a site-wide NFA can be issued.  DEQ opened a public comment period on the proposed Partial NFA with no comments received (JKS 4-17-17).",,"In October 2016 HydroCon advanced borings in the northern portion of the site and in the area of the card lock to evaluate soil and groundwater conditions.  As with previous investigations, the highest levels of contamination were found in the northern portion of the ""ODOT Property"", a strip of land acquired by ODOT in 1962 and whose southern boundary roughly bisects the canopy (and which includes the former dispenser islands).  However, soila and groundwater samples from borings around the card lock equipment contained low to non-detect levels of gasoline and VOCs. A geophysical survey of the site revealed two anomalies suspected to be abandoned USTs in the ODOT Property.

Jackson and Son Oil plan to redevelop the property and expand the card lock. The former station building would be demolished as part of this work.  The property owner needs an NFA determination to proceed with the work and requested whether a partial NFA could be issued for the card lock portion of the property to facilitate the project. The partial NFA would not apply to the ODOT right-of-way or other properties suspected to be contaminated.  DEQ consulted with ODOT to inform the agency that high levels of groundwater contamination remain in the expanded right-of-way (ODOT Property).  ODOT agreed that it would not object to the partial NFA provided that further assessment and/or cleanup be performed in the right-of-way.  DEQ opened a public comment period on the proposed Partial NFA with no comments received (JKS 4-17-17).",18778.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2017-04-14 08:09:45.057
