11321,15-98-0044,15,98,0044,,1998-08-19,1999-03-18 13:49:10,BATEY RESIDENCE HOT,2441 N KEENE WAY,MEDFORD,97504,,False,,,4.0,NFA,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : NO SAMPLE RESULTS IN YET; APPEARS TO BE MINOR RELEASE. ***,,,30451.0,0.0,GDECK,2011-05-10 16:08:13.727
11322,36-98-4157,36,98,4157,4259.0,1998-09-23,1999-03-18 13:49:10,Leathers Oil Co. #40,203 E 1ST ST,NEWBERG,97132,(503) 661-1244,False,1.0,RBCA,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : WATER AT 10 FEET. *** Pjt Mgr = C Rodda,,"The site has been a gasoline service station since the 1960s. As part of a site upgrade in 1998, petroleum tanks and hydraulic hoists were removed. A gasoline release was identified from tank pit soil samples. Subsurface materials consist primarily of sandy gravel overlying silty clay and clayey silt. The groundwater in this area fluctuates due to seasonal variations in precipitation, and was recorded at 1 to 6 feet below ground surface. Groundwater flow direction is generally towards the south. Investigation was initiated in September 1998 with the construction of three test pits near the former tank basin. Four more test pits were dug in October 1998 at the former dispensers. Gasoline was 251 ppm in TP-D from 7.5 feet bgs. Diesel (302 ppm) and heavy oil (130 ppm) were detected in TP-B at 8 feet bgs. Eight hand-auger borings were installed in November 1998 at the property boundary and soil samples showed gasoline at 555 ppm. Soil samples  were collected from the excavation in the center of the property for two new 20,000-gallon tanks. The soil samples were collected from 3.5 to 12 feet bgs and had gasoline at 1,140 ppm. Diesel and heavy oil were at 1,090 ppm and 780 ppm. Contaminated soils were removed but confirmation samples were not collected. Water samples collected from a test pit and the new tank excavation in September 1998 showed little to no gasoline constituents. Six monitoring wells  were installed in November 1998, and groundwater monitoring events took place through 2005. Six hand-auger borings (HA-9 to HA-14) were advanced in 2001 to obtain soil and groundwater samples at off-site locations. Soil samples were collected at various intervals from these borings and gasoline was detected at 1,130 ppm in soil from HA-9. Further analysis for petroleum constituents and total lead found benzene at 0.16 ppm in soil from 3.5 feet bgs at HA-9. Four of the six water samples collected from the hand-auger borings identified petroleum in groundwater, with benzene (1,570 ppb) at HA-13 on the south side of East First Street. In October 2002, a soil-gas sample was collected at the adjacent property, currently in use as a large grocery store. The vapor sample was collected from 4 feet bgs and analyzed for gasoline and BTEX. No contaminants were detected in the soil vapor sample. The cleanup site was closed in October 2012, with conditions recorded in an institutional control (ie, deed restriction) to protect workers from remaining contamination.",20863.0,0.0,CRODDA,2012-10-16 09:39:39.413
