11678,20-98-7070,20,98,7070,,1998-11-12,1999-03-18 13:49:10,B & G Auto,1330 WEST 6TH AVENUE,EUGENE,97401,541-687-2373,False,4.0,GW,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : DISCOVERY OF UNKNOWN TANK DURING SITE ASSESSMENT *** PrjMgr=DRB - tank discovered during site assessment.  Will install wells, conduct pilot test for site remediation in 3/2002.kwf

Site name originally reported as BNG Auto/Mac's Battery.  Corrected to simply B & G Auto - G. Deck 9/13/2011.","Site is for B&G Auto tank only. Mac's Battery is another business operating within the same tax lot (formerly Tax Lot #8500) as B&G Auto. Site historically mislabeled as BNG Auto in some DEQ records - C. Rodda 9/5/2011

Site name originally reported as BNG Auto/Mac's Battery.  Corrected to simply B & G Auto - G. Deck 9/13/2011.","The site is used by B&G Auto as a vehicle repair business. A gasoline underground storage tank was located within a service garage. The tank had been abandoned in place in the 1970’s but was decommissioned by removal in July 2000 after an assessment identified petroleum in soil and groundwater. Investigations at the property identified silty clay, sand, and gravels in the subsurface, with groundwater present at approximately 10 feet bgs. Groundwater elevation measurements indicated flow to the northwest, northeast, southeast, and east. In November 1998, a contractor completed an environmental site assessment and reported petroleum-contaminated soil and groundwater at the property. Four borings were installed, including one adjacent to the tank that had previously been abandoned in place. Groundwater at this location measured benzene at 11,000 ppb, and soil contained elevated concentrations of petroleum. The gasoline tank was decommissioned by removal in July 2000. Approximately eight cubic yards of contaminated soil was also removed and disposed of at a Lane County landfill. Petroleum impacts were visible at the north sidewall of the excavation but further removal could not be completed inside the site building. Confirmation testing revealed petroleum remained in soil, with gasoline detected at 15,000 ppm in the northwest corner of the excavation. Four monitoring wells were installed in 2002, and sampling was conducted until 2009. High levels of gasoline (maximum concentration 38,400 ppb) and benzene (max. conc. 4,400 ppb) were found throughout the groundwater monitoring. In March 2009, a contractor advanced two borings adjacent to the former tank location to collect soil samples. Clay was encountered in the borings, advanced to 6 feet bgs. Toluene, ethylbenzene, and xylenes were detected in soil. Another borehole was constructed in October 2009 at the same general location near the tank excavation. Soil was analyzed for petroleum contaminants, and several compounds were detected including ethylbenzene at 26.7 ppm, benzene at 0.662 ppm, and 1,2,4-trimethylbenzenes at 139 ppm. A final groundwater sample collected from MW-2 showed gasoline at 12,400 ppb, benzene at 1,840 ppb, ethylbenzene at 776 ppb, and naphthalene at 168 ppb. To protect site workers, a contaminated media management plan and notice of remaining petroleum in groundwater was recorded on the property deed in 2012.",31588.0,0.0,CRODDA,2012-05-23 08:30:17.150
