12294,26-99-0289,26,99,0289,,1999-03-25,1999-03-26 14:15:44,MEI - CHARLTON,2233 SW CANYON RD,PORTLAND,97201,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"A Site Investigation in March 1999 covered four adjacent lots centered on 2233 SW Canyon Road.  Records showed three USTs filled in place between 1978 & 1986 and a fourth UST (at 2183 SW Jefferson) removed in January 1997.  (See LUST #26-96-0746).  Eleven soil borings were advanced in March 1999, finding up to 29,700 ppm gasoline and 745 ppm heavy oil in the soil and up to 12,800 ppb benzene and 2,830 ppb ethylbenzene in the groundwater.  The worst of the contamination was at 2183 SW Jefferson.  The RP asked for an NFA for the other three lots, but DEQ concluded that additional sampling was needed to determine the extent of the contamination.  The RP has declined to do further work since then.  (1/13/10 TMB)

In May 2015, GeoDesign advanced borings at the properties comprising the site to address previously identified data gaps.  GeoDesign attempated to reproduce the 1999 gasoline detection of 29,700 ppm but was unsuccessful.  Soil samples revealed a maximum gasoilne concentration of 640 ppm.  Groundwater samples from six borings advanced near the property boundaries revealed non-detect levels of gasoline, diesel and heavy oil and VOCs and very low PAHs.The investigation included sampling of monitoring wells MW-1 through MW-3 on the adjacent property (2001 SW Jefferson), itself associated with historical UST releases. Very low levels of gasoline, diesel and PAHs were detected in MW-3.  Based on groundwater data from the site collected at the property boundaries, low levels of contamination detected in MW-3 are unrelated to the 1999 release at the site.

A pocket of soil contamination remains in the vicinity of 1999 boring S2, at approximate depths of 5 to 10 feet bgs.  Soil contamination does not seem to have impacted shallow site groundwater or groundwater on the down-gradient site. Soil-gas testing in the area of the pocket did not reveal detections in soil-gas above occupational worker RBCs for vapor intrusion.

Shallow groundwater has no current or future beneficial use as drinking water.  Likely future commercial use.  (JKS 9-28-15).",LUST #26-96-0746,"A Site Investigation in March 1999 covered four adjacent lots centered on 2233 SW Canyon Road.  Records showed three USTs filled in place between 1978 & 1986 and a fourth UST (at 2183 SW Jefferson) removed in January 1997.  (See LUST #26-96-0746).  Eleven soil borings were advanced in March 1999, finding up to 29,700 ppm gasoline and 745 ppm heavy oil in the soil and up to 12,800 ppb benzene and 2,830 ppb ethylbenzene in the groundwater.  The worst of the contamination was at 2183 SW Jefferson.  The RP asked for an NFA for the other three lots, but DEQ concluded that additional sampling was needed to determine the extent of the contamination.  The RP has declined to do further work since then.  (1/13/10 TMB)

In May 2015, GeoDesign advanced borings at the properties comprising the site to address previously identified data gaps.  GeoDesign attempated to reproduce the 1999 gasoline detection of 29,700 ppm but was unsuccessful.  Soil samples revealed a maximum gasoilne concentration of 640 ppm.  Groundwater samples from six borings advanced near the property boundaries revealed non-detect levels of gasoline, diesel and heavy oil and VOCs and very low PAHs.The investigation included sampling of monitoring wells MW-1 through MW-3 on the adjacent property (2001 SW Jefferson), itself associated with historical UST releases. Very low levels of gasoline, diesel and PAHs were detected in MW-3.  Based on groundwater data from the site collected at the property boundaries, low levels of contamination detected in MW-3 are unrelated to the 1999 release at the site.

A pocket of soil contamination remains in the vicinity of 1999 boring S2, at approximate depths of 5 to 10 feet bgs.  Soil contamination does not seem to have impacted shallow site groundwater or groundwater on the down-gradient site. Soil-gas testing in the area of the pocket did not reveal detections in soil-gas above occupational worker RBCs for vapor intrusion.

Shallow groundwater has no current or future beneficial use as drinking water.  Likely future commercial use.  (JKS 9-28-15)",32073.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2015-09-28 11:50:55.280
