12353,20-99-7017,20,99,7017,8737.0,1999-04-06,1999-04-06 16:17:00,ARCO #5294,4202 MAIN STREET,SPRINGFIELD,97478,(503)747-0106,False,1.0,LOWIMP,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Original release reported in 1999 with ARCO as owner/operator of station. ARCO continues to be RP, but property is now owned/operated by Zghoul Stations - C. Rodda 4/1/2013

Conditional closure with restrictions on land and groundwater use proposed for site: EES with HASP/CMMP - C. Rodda 4/10/2013",,"In April 1999, the service station was remodeled and new dispensers were installed as part of upgrade activities. During the removal of concrete at the east dispenser, a contractor severed a product line 4.5 inches below the slab. Approximately 3 gallons of fuel were released from the fuel line into the backfill material. As dispensers were removed, more petroleum contamination in soil was identified and a release was reported to DEQ. The soil and backfill beneath the dispenser island locations was excavated to 3.5 feet bgs based on field screening. Approximately 22 cubic yards of PCS were removed and transported to TPS Technologies Soil Recycling in Portland. In April 2001, the tanks, dispensers, and product lines were replaced. More contaminated soil was discovered at each area and additional excavation was completed. At the products lines, soil was removed to 11.5 feet bgs at the northeast portion and 6.5 bgs to the northwest. The dispenser islands were further excavated to 5 feet bgs. Finally, the tank cavity was excavated up to 14.5 feet bgs. Overall, 200 cubic yards of PCS was removed in 2001. Site assessment activities in October 2001 included the installation of nine push-probe borings. Fifteen soil samples were collected and nine water samples were obtained during this phase of work. Soil samples were collected from 2.5 to 15 feet bgs during the October 2001 assessment, and were analyzed for gasoline, diesel, BTEX, MTBE, trimethylbenzenes, and PAHs. The highest concentration of heavy oil (1,720 ppm) was found at 3 feet bgs from GP6. All gasoline constituents were reported below laboratory detection levels, but several PAHs were detected. Groundwater samples were obtained from each boring, with GP4 measuring benzene at 20,200 ppb, toluene at 62,800 ppb, ethylbenzene at 11,400 ppb, xylenes at 65,300 ppb, 1,2,4-trimethylbenzene at 16,000 ppb, MTBE at 545 ppb, and naphthalene at 1,230 ppb. The high levels of petroleum required further delineation in groundwater, and four monitoring wells were installed. Groundwater monitoring was completed through 2009 and additional assessment was performed as requested by DEQ in 2008 and 2010. While contamination remains in soil and groundwater, DEQ approved closure with conditions in 2013.",3488.0,0.0,CRODDA,2013-04-10 17:35:25.390
