12710,18-99-0028,18,99,0028,5199.0,1999-06-07,1999-06-08 13:49:06,ONE STOP AUTO PARTS,424 S 6TH ST,KLAMATH FALLS,97601,(541)882-7978,False,2.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : OLD LUST #18-94-0011 - HASA BEEN CLOSED OUT.  RE-OPENING SITE WITH NEW NUMBER. ***,18-94-0011,"1.    The Site is currently a vacant, partially asphalt paved lot, which is located to the south of 424 South 6th Street in the SE quarter of the NE quarter of Section 32 in T38S, R9E, City of Klamath Falls, Klamath County, Oregon. The Site is composed of one tax lot (TL 7901 or Block 93, lots 6 and 7) which encompasses approximately 0.23 acres. The adjacent lot to the north (TL 7900, or Block 93, lots 8, 9 and10) was the former location of four underground storage tanks. It appears that one UST was partially on Lots 6 and 7. Tax Lots 7900 and 7901 used to be one property. Historic land use includes an automobile dealership and automobile parts store.5.    In April 2011, six borings were advanced to define the extent of petroleum contamination in the vicinity of the 1999 utility trench excavation. Borings were advanced from 15 to 18 feet below ground surface. Soil and groundwater samples were collected and analyzed for TPH-Gx, TPH-Dx, and DEQ risk-based decision making guidance document (RBDM) volatile organic compounds (VOCs). Groundwater samples were also analyzed for polynuclear aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs). Soil samples did not have detectable TPH or constituents. One groundwater sample had a TPH-Dx concentration of 235 µg/l. Another groundwater sample had a toluene detection of 1.1 µg/l and fluorene of 0.11 µg/l. No petroleum hydrocarbon staining or odors were observed in any of these soil cores.7.    A conceptual site model was developed for the site. Applicable pathways/receptors include volatilization from soil and groundwater to outdoor air and vapor intrusion into buildings for residential, occupational workers, and direct contact of soil for excavation, and construction workers. The site is currently zoned for commercial uses, which allows limited residential uses. Public water is supplied to the site and surrounding properties. Thirty-nine domestic water supply wells were identified within Section 32. None of these wells appear to be located in the presumed downgradient direction from the Site. 
8.    Petroleum concentrations in soil and groundwater samples collected in 2001 and 2011 are all below applicable risk-based concentrations (RBCs) presented in DEQ’s Risk-Based Decision Making for the Remediation of Petroleum-Contaminated Sites, September, 2003, (updated 2009)  guidance document.",21431.0,0.0,MKIRK,2012-11-26 09:13:33.273
