12817,18-99-0031,18,99,0031,149.0,1999-06-23,1999-06-25 09:11:10,NEW BEATTY STORE,42615 HWY 140 E,BEATTY,97621,(541)533-2689,False,4.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,,,"[May 2010] The site was formerly a store that dispensed petroleum products from USTs that were installed in the mid 1980s. The site is currently a restaurant (Basso’s Bighorn Bar and Grill), and the property is flat and covered primarily with crushed rock, with one concrete pad underneath a former dispenser canopy.

In May 1999, three 1,000-gallon gasoline and one 550-gallon diesel USTs were abandoned by removal, and replaced with above-groundwater tanks (ASTs). Petroleum hydrocarbon contamination was discovered during the UST removal.

In September 2008, the DEQ completed four soil borings at the site to a maximum depth of 16 feet below the ground surface (bgs). Significant petroleum contamination was detected in all samples indicating contamination from the UST system had dispersed to the south, east, and north.

Additional assessment was performed in December 2009 and March 2010 to fully define the extent of the soil and groundwater plume. As part of this investigation, 17 push probe and 4 soil gas borings were completed on and off the site, and 24 soil and 19 groundwater samples were collected. Two commercial supply and one residential supply wells were sampled.  The report may be viewed in the site document section of this database.",106350.0,0.0,LLO,2016-03-25 13:41:14.430
