13053,36-99-4101,36,99,4101,11981.0,1999-08-02,1999-08-04 11:39:47,Release in 4th Street,980 NE 4TH ST,MCMINNVILLE,97128,(503) 472-2739,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : AS OF 8/2/99, THIS IS A SUSPECTED RELEASE WITH SOURCE NOT POSITIVELY IDENTIFIED.  LIKELY (LOGICAL) SOURCE IS IDENTIFIED.  JLP 8/2/99. ORIGINALLY REPORTED AS HOT.  NOW KNOW TO BE REGULATED...GAD 10/11/99. *** As of 8/2/99, this is a suspected release with source not positively identified.  Likely (logical) source is identified.  JLP 8/2/99;  originally reported as a HOT, now LUST.",36-99-4168 Release in 4th Street II,"OOn August 2, 1999, DEQ received notice that gasoline, diesel and heating oil had leaked from underground storage tanks. The contamination did extend beyond the property line into the 3rd Street and Johnson Street right-of-ways. Multiple involved parties including the current and previous owners of the Golden Valley Brewery and Restaurant, the City of McMinnville, and Northwest Natural Gas Company investigated this leak with DEQ oversight.  The initial investigation activities began on June 20, 1999, when a catastrophic gasoline release was discovered during installation of a natural gas pipeline on the west side of Johnson Street.  The horizontal auger equipment used to install that line damaged four of the six underground storage tanks adjacent to the property at 980 NE 4th Street in McMinnville, Oregon (Golden Valley Brewery and Restaurant).  Prior to removal, the tank systems were located in what is now the Johnson Street right-of-way, and were plumbed to a former dispenser island associated with previous use of the commercial property at 980 NE 4th Street.  The successful cleanup of this site required numerous parties to work through multiple site investigation and cleanup phases. DEQ evaluated efforts to contain, clean up  and mitigate current and future exposure to the contamination and now believes that this leak is not a threat to human health or the environment. The closure plan will ventilate petroleum vapors from soil to the air above the building and any remaining contamination will naturally degrade in place.",33257.0,0.0,JGLASS,2011-05-25 09:09:10.760
