13240,30-99-0034,30,99,0034,5874.0,1999-08-31,1999-09-01 13:29:31,BARNHART PROPERTIES INC: Floyds Truck R,I84 EXIT 202,PENDLETON,97801,(541)276-6709,False,7.0,MATRIX,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : LISTED RP IS NEW OWNER, SITE ASSESSMENT REPORT FROM SALE/PURCHASE HAS CONFIRMED RELEASE.  UPGRADE PLANS INCLUDE CLEANUP OF CONTAMINATED SOIL - DICK SHEDLON CONTRACTOR. *** New Owner has submitted Mod App for name change to ""Floyd's Truck Ranch"".",,,59819.0,0.0,ECLARK,2008-06-03 16:48:47.360
13241,17-99-0050,17,99,0050,6037.0,1999-09-01,1999-09-01 14:26:04,POWELL DIST CO - COLVIN/GRANTS PASS,1919 ROGUE RIVER HWY,GRANTS PASS,97526,(541)479-5343,False,1.0,GW,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"The site had been utilized as a service station since 1960 prior to the removal of the tanks. A petroleum release was discovered following the decommissioning of five tanks in September 1999. The tanks included three 6,000-gallon gasoline tanks in a cavity on the east side of the station building. One 1,500-gallon diesel and one 1,000-gallon stove oil tanks in a separate tank nest west of the building. The station was leased to Colvin Oil Company from 1972 to 1998. Following tank removal, approximately 495 cubic yards of petroleum contaminated soil was excavated from the tank nests and stockpiled on site. DEQ provided a Solid Waste Letter of Authorization to treat the PCS on-site in June 2000. Approval to dispose of the soil on-site in November 2000, with the condition that documentation of the final placement of treated soil be submitted. In August 2001, a remediation system was installed consisting of 20 sumps and trenches to collect groundwater for treatment through an air stripper. More than 3,000,000 gallons of water was treated. Three monitoring wells were installed in May 2001 by Agate Engineering. Samples were collected from wells intermittently through 2003, and identified the highest concentration of petroleum contaminants in MW-3 at the northern edge of the property. One additional monitoring well and eight probes were installed in 2006 to further delineate the groundwater contaminant plume. Soil samples were also collected from on- and off-site locations. Water samples were obtained from on- and off-site domestic wells. Gasoline was detected at 127 ppb in a September 2005 sampling event. Previously, this well contained diesel at 260 ppb in March 2002. The most recent domestic well sampling shows no petroleum in the water supply wells. Additional investigation was completed in 2007 with three more wells. A test remediation project was conducted but was not effective at reducing concentrations in groundwater. Sampling continues at the site's monitoring wells and nearby domestic wells.",,"The property had been utilized as a service station since 1960 prior to the removal of the tanks in 1999. A petroleum release was discovered following the decommissioning of five USTs at the site in September 1999. The tanks consisted of three 6,000-gallon gasoline USTs in a cavity on the east side of the station building, and one 1,500-gallon diesel and one 1,000-gallon stove oil UST in separate tank nest to the west of the site building. The station was leased to Colvin Oil Company from 1972 to 1998. Following removal of the USTs, approximately 495 cubic yards of petroleum contaminated soil were excavated from the two tank nests and stockpiled on site. DEQ provided a Solid Waste Letter of Authorization to treat the PCS on site in correspondence from June 2000. Approval to complete disposal of the PCS on site was given by the DEQ in November 2000, with the condition that documentation of the final placement of treated soil be submitted. In August 2001, a remediation system was installed at the site, consisting of 20 sumps and trenches to collect groundwater for treatment through an air stripper. More than 3,000,000 gallons of water had been treated at the site according to site reports.
Three monitoring wells (MW-1 to MW-3) were installed at the site in May 2001 by Agate Engineering to assess impacts to groundwater. Samples were collected from the wells intermittently through 2003, and identified the highest concentration of petroleum contaminants in MW-3 at the northern edge of the property boundary.  An additional monitoring well (MW-4) and eight probes (DP-1 to DP-8) were installed for the site investigation in January 2006 to continue delineation of the groundwater contaminant plume. Groundwater samples have been analyzed for gasoline and diesel-range hydrocarbons, BTEX, and MTBE, with select water samples further analyzed for PAHs. Soil samples were collected from on and off site locations during this most recent assessment, with analysis completed for gasoline and diesel-range hydrocarbons and BTEX constituents. Samples were also collected from on and off site domestic wells during the completed site investigation activities. Drinking water wells tested at and near the site have shown no impacts from shallow groundwater contamination. Soil-gas samples show vapors are present in the subsurface at safe levels.",32831.0,0.0,CRODDA,2016-10-14 13:06:54.980
