13673,17-99-0068,17,99,0068,6014.0,1999-11-29,1999-12-01 15:21:35,ANTLERS GROCERY & SERVICE STATION,8530 MONUMENT DR,GRANTS PASS,97526,(541) 476-4038,False,2.0,GENREM,4.0,NFA,True,False,,,True,,,,,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : ONE TANK DECOM'D BY REMOVAL & ONE TANK DECOM'D IN SITU. 30 YDS OF PCS TREATED & DISPOSED OF ON SITE. *** on-site SWLA issued, need to confirm if GW impacts exist, confirmation sampling done in August '01, report recieved March '04,  FI requested 29-mar-04, NFA sent 27-may-04",,,22012.0,,MBELSKY,2004-05-27 08:55:26.000
13674,36-99-4168,36,99,4168,,1999-08-02,1999-12-01 15:50:11,Release in 4th Street II,980 NE 4TH ST (NEARBY),MCMINNVILLE,97128,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : THIS RELEASE ASSOCIATED W/ INVESTIGATION & CLEANUP OF PETROLEUM CONTAMINATION EXACERBATED BY DAMAGE TO UST SYSTEMS FROM HORIZONTAL AUGERING EQUIPMENT BY NW NAT GAS. *** This site not set up until 12/1/99 when 2nd RP (NW Nat Gas) established.  This release assoc. w/ invest. & cleanup of petrol. contam. exacerbated by damage to UST systems from horizontal augering equipment operated by NW Nat Gas.,36-99-4101 Release in 4th Street,"On August 2, 1999, DEQ received notice that gasoline, diesel and heating oil had leaked from underground storage tanks. The contamination did extend beyond the property line into the 3rd Street and Johnson Street right-of-ways. Multiple involved parties including the current and previous owners of the Golden Valley Brewery and Restaurant, the City of McMinnville, and Northwest Natural Gas Company investigated this leak with DEQ oversight.  The initial investigation activities began on June 20, 1999, when a catastrophic gasoline release was discovered during installation of a natural gas pipeline on the west side of Johnson Street.  The horizontal auger equipment used to install that line damaged four of the six underground storage tanks adjacent to the property at 980 NE 4th Street in McMinnville, Oregon (Golden Valley Brewery and Restaurant).  Prior to removal, the tank systems were located in what is now the Johnson Street right-of-way, and were plumbed to a former dispenser island associated with previous use of the commercial property at 980 NE 4th Street.  The successful cleanup of this site required numerous parties to work through multiple site investigation and cleanup phases. DEQ evaluated efforts to contain, clean up  and mitigate current and future exposure to the contamination and now believes that this leak is not a threat to human health or the environment. The proposed plan would allow any remaining contamination to naturally degrade in place.",33257.0,0.0,GDECK,2011-10-21 16:40:51.140
13675,26-99-1311,26,99,1311,5599.0,1999-11-17,1999-12-02 10:00:55,PELLETROX INC,9127 N WILBUR AVE,PORTLAND,97217,(503) 285-0412,False,7.0,MATRIX,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,True,False,,,"A release of fuel from a fuel pump island was reported in November 1999.  The pump island and product lines were removed, along with 183.82 tons of contaminated soil.  Confirmation soil sampling was negative, but the wrong sampling method was used.  The soil was resampled in 2000 but the report was apparently lost.  The report was resubmitted to DEQ in 2008 and approved.",142548.0,0.0,iPMO#38,2016-10-13 14:58:33.333
