13932,36-00-4003,36,00,4003,,2000-01-04,2000-01-21 16:19:43,"Long, Kamila HOT",300 S DAYTON ST,MCMINNVILLE,97128,(503) 434-8677,False,2.0,GENREM,1.0,CRT,False,True,,,False,,,,,*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : $50 (CK#1710) + CONTRACTOR CERTIFICATE RCVD 1/24/00. 57.5 CU YD POCKET LEFT IN PLACE. *** $50 + Contractor Cert recvd 1/24/00.  57.5 cu yd pocket left in place.,,,35082.0,,CONV,2000-12-06 14:36:32.000
13933,24-00-4004,24,00,4004,1244.0,2000-01-05,2000-01-21 16:21:47,Terry's BP,137 OLD PACIFIC HWY,JEFFERSON,97352,(503)327-3033,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,1.0,False,False,False,False,False,assigned to VAE,,"The property is currently owned by Marion County, who acquired the site through tax lien foreclosure from the former property owner, Oberson Oil. The County plans to transfer the property to the City of Jefferson after the NFA is issued. The City plans to build a welcome kiosk on the site. The site is currently vacant. A gasoline service station formerly operated on the southwest part of the property between 1960 until the petroleum underground storage tanks (USTs) were decommissioned by removal in January 2000.  The buildings and structures were demolished sometime between 2011 and 2012.  Petroleum contamination was found in the soil, groundwater and soil gas beneath the site during removal of the USTs and subsequent contamination investigations completed between July 2000 and June 2020. The contamination is related to a past service station and UST operations.  Approximately 763 tons of petroleum contaminated soil was removed during UST decommissioning work in January 2000.  Some soil contamination remains on the site at depths between 5 to 14.5 feet below ground.  The highest levels of remaining contamination in soil is located beneath the southern portion of the site, but some low levels of petroleum contamination was also found beneath the sidewalk and Highway 99E.  There was little or no contamination found in the groundwater on the property and the concentration of petroleum-related contamination detected in soil gas was well below all applicable risk levels.

The site and adjacent properties are occupied by commercial businesses and residential homes.  The area is serviced with municipal water from the City of Jefferson and there are no known private drinking water wells on the site or within the contaminated area. There are no surface waters or significant ecological habitat on the site or adjacent properties. 

DEQ has determined that the remaining contamination at the site meets Oregon’s cleanup standards for the current and anticipated future use of the property and surrounding area and does not pose a threat to human health or the environment. Based on this, DEQ issued a no further action determination for the site on November 13, 2020.",18776.0,0.0,NSAWKA,2020-11-19 16:54:45.913
