14796,22-00-4099,22,00,4099,3555.0,2000-05-23,2000-05-23 15:39:26,West Albany Texaco,522 PACIFIC BLVD SW,ALBANY,97321,(541)928-7150,False,1.0,,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,True,False,"Proposing demolition of the building and building a new AM/PM store... KWF 3/27/02.
Pjt Mgr = BCS",,"The original release for this site dates back to July 2000 when the DEQ was notified of petroleum contamination discovered in soil and groundwater during a site assessment by Wohlers Environmental.  The site formerly held two different gasoline UST systems.  

In addition to the gasoline USTs, DEQ records indicate that one 550-gallon waste oil UST had been located at the property and was decommissioned in November 1989.  Wohlers Environmental stated in its reports that one 1,000-gallon heating oil and one 550-gallon waste oil UST were decommissioned by removal in April 1990.  An unknown amount of PCS was also removed from the site at that time.  

Following decommissioning of the eight USTs in 2002, approximately 1,580 tons of PCS was excavated and removed from the tank cavities.  The PCS was disposed of at Coffin Butte Landfill in Corvallis, Oregon.  Confirmation samples were collected from the sidewalls and bottom of each UST excavation, which extended up to 18 feet bgs.  

A soil sample collected from the east sidewall at the north tank excavation detected gasoline at 1,690 ppm as well as relatively low concentrations of VOCs.  The highest concentration of gasoline in the south tank excavation measured 1,900 ppm in soil sample SS-17’.  

A petroleum sheen was present on the water that entered the UST excavations.  Up to 5,000 gallons of water were pumped from the pits, treated by aeration on site, and discharged to the storm water system under a NPDES permit.

Eight monitoring wells have been installed at the site since 2003.  Numerous probe investigations have been completed in an attempt to delineate soil and groundwater contamination, including a site assessment in July 2000.  In March 2003, thirteen borings (MW-1 to MW-4, DP-5 to DP-13) were installed.  Groundwater impacts have been confirmed through several monitoring events.",20379.0,0.0,CRODDA,2010-01-28 11:52:41.610
