17798,34-01-0029,34,01,0029,7551.0,2001-06-06,2001-06-07 15:20:21,ESTBY/ CORNELL SERVICE STATION,1729 NE CORNELL RD,HILLSBORO,97123,(503)648-4084,False,4.0,GW,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,True,False,"
Site ready for closure.  Need request from RP to end RAO, CMMP from consultant.  Doing public notice to impacted parties.  (RAH 5/15/2019)


Site is under a Remedial Action Order to complete a CAP and implement remedial actions.  CAP and CSM documents received by DEQ in March 2009.  CAP proposed enhanced bioremediation using portable equipment (not permanent system).  Three consecutive monthly events were proposed, with confirmation groundwater monitoring events afterward.  In August 2009, K&S performed site assessment activities at the adjacent school property.  Low levels of TPH-G and VOCs detected in furthest south boring (DP-4).  This shows GW impacts do not get within 100 feet of school building and therefore pose minimal vapor intrusion risks.  Monitoring well MW-13 installed at southwest corner of site. Water well survey revealed irrigation well at residence along northern property of school (1650 NE Cornell Road).  Initial confirmation sampling after initial three injection events did not reveal significant drops in TPH-G and constituents in MW-7 or MW-8.  Also, low levels of oxygen and nutrients in some locations suggested microbes were lacking in these materials. In July 2010, the 6th injection event was conducted at the site, with 3Q2010 monitoring in August 2010. Analysis by Crorey Mechanical indicated some downward trends in constituent levels.  Recommendation to monitor site for 1-2 additional quarters and evaluate progress of nitrate reductions to determine whether further injections are necessary.  DEQ notified that oprator planning to install addition diesel UST in southwest corner.  On 9-30-11, based on ND or low level detections in seveal wells, K&S requested permission to modify monitoring program to only include the most impacted wells. OCE approved the request in a letter dated 10-24-11.  In September 2012, the site operator installed a new diesel UST in the location of the former UST nest.  Prior to installation, apprroximately 425 tons of impacted soil was removed from the new tank location.  Also in September 2012, K&S performed bioremediation injections in the new tank hold.  Monitoring program further revised in 2014.  Sampled monitoring wells have been reduced to MW-4, MW-8, MW-10 and MW-13.  Except for ongoing groundwater monitoring the required work in the RAO has been completed. While TPH-G and ethylbenzene continue to fluctuate, concentrations of benzene in MW-8 and MW-13 have lowered dramatically.  Lon Yandell of GeoDesign evaluating data trends using Mann-Kendall methods.  DEQ will evaluate results and decided whether groundwater monitoring can be suspended. (JKs 2-11-19).",,"Site is under a Remedial Action Order to complete a CAP and implement remedial actions.  CAP and CSM documents received by DEQ in March 2009.  CAP proposed enhanced bioremediation using portable equipment (not permanent system).  Three consecutive monthly events were proposed, with confirmation groundwater monitoring events afterward.  In August 2009, K&S performed site assessment activities at the adjacent school property.  Low levels of TPH-G and VOCs detected in furthest south boring (DP-4).  This shows GW impacts do not get within 100 feet of school building and therefore pose minimal vapor intrusion risks.  Monitoring well MW-13 installed at southwest corner of site. Water well survey revealed irrigation well at residence along northern property of school (1650 NE Cornell Road).  Initial confirmation sampling after initial three injection events did not reveal significant drops in TPH-G and constituents in MW-7 or MW-8.  Also, low levels of oxygen and nutrients in some locations suggested microbes were lacking in these materials. In July 2010, the 6th injection event was conducted at the site. 
DEQ notified that operator planning to install addition diesel UST in southwest corner.  On 9-30-11, based on ND or low level detections in seveal wells, K&S requested permission to modify monitoring program to only include the most impacted wells. OCE approved the request in a letter dated 10-24-11.  In September 2012, the site operator installed a new diesel UST in the location of the former UST nest.  Prior to installation, apprroximately 425 tons of impacted soil was removed from the new tank location.  Also in September 2012, K&S performed bioremediation injections in the new tank pit.  Monitoring program further revised in 2014.  Sampled monitoring wells have been reduced to MW-4, MW-8, MW-10 and MW-13.  Except for ongoing groundwater monitoring the required work in the Remedial Action Order has been completed. While TPH-G and ethylbenzene continue to fluctuate, concentrations of benzene in MW-8 and MW-13 have lowered dramatically.  Contaminant concentratiosn in off-site well MW-10 are largely ND. Lon Yandell of GeoDesign and K&S Environmental evaluating data trends using Mann-Kendall methods.  DEQ will evaluate results and decided whether groundwater monitoring can be suspended and the RAO closed out.  A Contaminated Media Management Plan is being prepared. (JKS 2-11-19).",23123.0,0.0,RHOOD,2019-05-15 11:45:06.643
