18133,34-01-0039,34,01,0039,,2001-07-20,2001-07-25 08:25:02,WA COUNTY WEST UNION PROJECT,6585 NW CORNELIUS PASS RD,HILLSBORO,97214,,False,4.0,GW,,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"One gasoline UST and one diesel UST were reportedly present at the southern tip of the site, at the intersection of NW Cornelius Pass Road and West Union Road.  Both tanks were reportedly removed in 1983, along with a separate heating oil UST at the site.  (Note that there is no documentation for this).

In July 1990, four gasoline USTs were removed from the site of the West Union Garage.  167 cubic yards of PCS were excavated and aerated on-site.  Confirmation sampling found only up to 23 ppm TPH.  No groundwater was encountered.  DEQ issued an NFA letter on April 23, 1991.  (See LUST #34-90-0275).

Kleinfelder collected soil samples from eight borings at the site in May 1994 as part of a site characterization, followed by seven borings in August 1994.  Up to 3,300 ppm gasoline and 910 ppm diesel was detected in the soil, centered on the gas/diesel UST pit at the southern tip of the property.  In a November 1994 report, Kleinfelder recommended leaving the contamination in place.  (DEQ did not receive the report until 2004).

In February 2001 the property owner, James Smejkal, donated strips of land along Cornelius Pass and West Union roads to Washington County for a project to widen the intersection.  In July 2001 the county encountered contamination (157 ppm gasoline) in the right-of-way.  Forty cubic yards (67 tons) of PCS were excavated and shipped to TPS Technologies for thermal treatment.  Up to 899 ppm gasoline remained in the soil, along with 9,840 ppb benzene in the shallow groundwater.

Washington County and DEQ identified James Smejkal as being responsible for investigating the remaining contamination in the right-of-way.  However, an attorney for Smejkal responded that the county was responsible, since the county had not acquired the right-of-way through eminent domain and had not conducted a due diligence investigation prior to acquiring the right-of-way.

A 675-gallon HOT was removed from Smejkal's property in May 2010, along with about 3.7 tons of PCS.  DEQ issued an NFA letter on September 28, 2010.  (See file #34-10-0496 for more information).  (3/29/12 KPD)",LUST #34-90-0275 and #34-10-0496,,81617.0,0.0,KDANA,2012-03-29 10:43:55.777
