18312,26-01-0040,26,01,0040,3745.0,2001-06-28,2001-08-03 08:48:36,NORTH COAST CANVAS PRODUCTS CO,1235 NW 19TH AVE,PORTLAND,97209,(503) 227-2482,False,1.0,RBCA,4.0,NFA,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Some clarifications on the last page of the site maps available on WebDocs.  The gasoline detection in SG08 was 450 ug/m3, not 4500.  Sample locations SG10 and SG11 were south of the Former Tank Pit and are not shown on the map.  Finally, the concentration of gasoline in SG02 (120,000 ug/m3) was below the residential RBC of 140,000 in effect at the time the NFA letter was initially written (early Nov. 2011), and more importantly SG07 was assumed to represent post-removal site conditions to the east of the excavation pit.",LUST #26-91-0217,"Three 1,000-gallon gasoline USTs and one 500-gallon heating oil UST were removed from a former service station at this site in June 1991.  (See file #26-91-0217).  Ten years later (June 2001) additional contamination was discovered, concentrated near the former pump island.  Two monitoring wells were installed in January 2002, and a third in May 2002.  Soil contamination extended down to groundwater at 45 feet bgs, and groundwater in all three wells was contaminated.  DEQ placed restrictions on the property prohibiting groundwater use and residential redevelopment.  In December 2007, three soil gas samples were collected to see if conditions would allow for a mixed-use residential and commercial development.  Gasoline was detected in all three samples.  In August 2011, a total of 306.71 tons of petroleum-contaminated soil were removed from beneath the former pump island.  Twelve confirmation soil gas samples collected in September 2011 showed no residual vapor concerns.  Soil at the site is clean to 15 feet bgs, and groundwater in the area has no beneficial use as a domestic water supply source.",20512.0,0.0,KDANA,2012-02-09 07:06:51.290
18313,34-01-0041,34,01,0041,,2001-07-31,2001-08-03 09:06:15,LEWIS PROPERTY,22930 NW ALDER ST,HILLSBORO,97124,,False,7.0,MATRIX,,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"The on-site building was originally a country general store for the historic community of Orenco.  (The building has subsequently been converted into a private residence).  Gasoline pumps were reportedly present at the store from the late 1920s to about 1963.

Two 500-gallon gasoline USTs were decommissioned by removal from the site on July 31, 2001.  Tank #1 had previously been filled with pea gravel.  A total of 240 gallons of ""product and rinsate"" were removed from Tank #2 (meaning the tank should have been registered).  A total of 52.75 tons of PCS were removed from around Tank #1, and 45.30 tons of PCS were removed from around Tank #2.  Confirmation soil samples collected from the bottom four corners of the Tank #1 excavation pit found only up to 55.3 ppm gasoline.  Two confirmation soil samples from the Tank #2 excavation pit found up to 1,030 ppm gasoline.  Additional investigation was recommended but apparently not conducted.  (3/29/12 KPD)",,,39335.0,0.0,KDANA,2012-03-29 14:05:13.183
