19282,26-01-0065,26,01,0065,1889.0,2001-12-07,2001-12-07 16:31:07,ROSS ISLAND SAND & GRAVEL - TAIT PLANT,2611 SE 4TH AVE,PORTLAND,97214,(503) 239-5504,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,True,False,"A line leak detection system reported a release at this site in December 2001.  Diesel (up to 12,100 ppm) was found in the soil beneath a cracked pipe.  About 8.5 tons of PCS was removed in an excavation advanced in the area of the failed union fitting, but further investigation and cleanup was hampered by numerous piping runs and buried concrete slabs in the area.  Subsurface materials consist largelyof concrete, concrete debris, and metal to an approximate depht of 8 feet bgs, with a dense granular material from 8 to 15 feet bgs. 

K&S reported that the highest level of diesel were detected in soil just above a concrete slab  (up to 18 inches thick) found at 5.5 feet bgs.  K&S analyzed the samples with the two highest diesel detections for constituents.  Selected volatile organic compounds were detected at levels above the residential RBCs for the vapor intrusion pathway.  There were no detections above occupational worker RBCs.  K&S reports that during the soil removal, soil containing the highest levels of diesel were scraped off the surface of the concrete slab at 5.5 feet bgs.  Therefore, the worst of the contamination was likely removed.  The next highest diesel detection believed to remain on the site was 3,660 ppm at sample P4 collected near the souteast corner of the excavation.

K&S Environmental concluded that the release was likely limited in extent and should be closed in-place.  Greg Toran says a second release was reported in 2002, but there is no mention of this in the file.  A risk-based cleanup report is needed for the first release, and an assessment and/or cleanup of the second release is needed.  (7/12/10 TMB)

In August 2016, K&S broke up the concrete in an area just southwest of the release point so that soil conditions could be assessed below the concrete.  A sample collected below the point of release at a depth of 7 feet bgs contained diesle at 1,200 ppm and samples collected at depths of 9 and 13 feet bgs contained diesel at a maximum level of 272 ppm. In addition, an area approximately 25 feet west of the point of release was investigated to evaluate reports of a second piping release in 2002. Diesel was detected at a concentration of 63 ppm.

The concrete at depths ranging from 5.5 to 7 feet bgs partially impeded the downward migration of contaminants.  Also, there is approximately 20 feet of separation between soil contamination and the likely depth of groundwater.  The volume of soil contamination is relatively minor and is unlikely to result in unacceptable risks to future employees and workers.   Site contamination is not considered likely to impact groundwater.  Furthermore, the site is unlikely to pose a risk of recontamination to the Portland Harbor superfund site (JKS 11-1-16).","26-20-0959, Tait Plant","A line leak detection system reported a release at this site in December 2001.  Diesel (up to 12,100 ppm) was found in the soil beneath a cracked pipe.  About 8.5 tons of PCS was removed in an excavation advanced in the area of the failed union fitting, but further investigation and cleanup was hampered by numerous piping runs and buried concrete slabs in the area.  Subsurface materials consist largelyof concrete, concrete debris, and metal to an approximate depht of 8 feet bgs, with a dense granular material from 8 to 15 feet bgs. 

K&S reported that the highest level of diesel were detected in soil just above a concrete slab  (up to 18 inches thick) found at 5.5 feet bgs.  K&S analyzed the samples with the two highest diesel detections for constituents.  Selected volatile organic compounds were detected at levels above the residential RBCs for the vapor intrusion pathway.  There were no detections above occupational worker RBCs.  K&S reports that during the soil removal, soil containing the highest levels of diesel were scraped off the surface of the concrete slab at 5.5 feet bgs.  Therefore, the worst of the contamination was likely removed.  The next highest diesel detection believed to remain on the site was 3,660 ppm at sample P4 collected near the souteast corner of the excavation.

K&S Environmental concluded that the release was likely limited in extent and should be closed in-place.  Greg Toran says a second release was reported in 2002, but there is no mention of this in the file.  A risk-based cleanup report is needed for the first release, and an assessment and/or cleanup of the second release is needed.  (7/12/10 TMB)

In August 2016, K&S broke up the concrete in an area just southwest of the release point so that soil conditions could be assessed below the concrete.  A sample collected below the point of release at a depth of 7 feet bgs contained diesle at 1,200 ppm and samples collected at depths of 9 and 13 feet bgs contained diesel at a maximum level of 272 ppm. In addition, an area approximately 25 feet west of the point of release was investigated to evaluate reports of a second piping release in 2002. Diesel was detected at a concentration of 63 ppm.

The concrete at depths ranging from 5.5 to 7 feet bgs partially impeded the downward migration of contaminants.  Also, there is approximately 20 feet of separation between soil contamination and the likely depth of groundwater.  The volume of soil contamination is relatively minor",6791.0,0.0,GDECK,2020-10-27 16:18:56.317
