19604,21-02-4002,21,02,4002,,2002-02-05,2002-02-05 16:22:49,Surf Shop,4933 SW HWY 101,LINCOLN CITY,97367,,False,1.0,RBCA,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : TANKS EMPTY & OUT OF USE PRE-74.  NO PERMITS REQUIRED. ""OTHER"" CONTAMINANT = HEAVY OIL *** Tanks empty & out of use pre-74.
Assigned to JLG",,"Based upon this file review, the remedial investigation and cleanup information for this site includes the following:

1.    On February 4, 2002, the Department received a report of a petroleum (gasoline, diesel and heavy oil) release at the above referenced facility.

2.    During the decommissioning of two 1000-gallon fuel underground storage tanks (USTs) and on 340-gallon used oil UST, three additional 675-gallon USTs were discovered.

3.    The petroleum release was found to have been caused by corrosion in the USTs resulting in holes up to one inch in diameter.

4.    On February 6, 2001, the three 675-gallon USTs were emptied and decommissioned by removal.

5.    The petroleum release was reported to have been caused by corrosion of the USTs resulting in holes up to one inch in diameter.

6.    A total of approximately 81 tons (~54 yd3) of petroleum contaminated soils (PCS) were removed during the UST decommissioning and disposed of at Riverbend Landfill in McMinnville.

7.    After remedial excavation, the tank-nest sidewalls displayed TPH concentrations above Soil Matrix cleanup levels for gasoline and oil fraction total petroleum hydrocarbons (TPH).  The highest concentration of contaminated soil remaining was observed on the north-northwest sidewall (adjacent to the Surf-Shop building).  Remaining TPH concentrations range from: 
•    None-detected (ND) at 8’ below ground surface (BGS) to 4,600 mg/Kg  (ppm) at 3-6’BGS, gasoline fraction TPH (Also in excess of Generic Risk-Based concentrations (GRBCs); 
•    ND to 230 ppm for diesel fraction TPH; and,
•    ND to 630 ppm for “oil” fraction TPH.  
",44107.0,0.0,JGLASS,2008-06-27 10:53:42.930
