21895,06-02-1400,06,02,1400,1719.0,2002-11-21,2002-11-22 14:09:47,BASSETT-HYLAND ENERGY CO - CARD SITE #1,425 LOCKHART,COOS BAY,97420,(541) 267-2107,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : SOIL & GW IMPACTS OBSERVED DURING UST SYSTEM MODIFICATION WORK; ALSO DISCOVERED 2 TANKS WITH FUEL & WATER. *** Soil & GW impacts observed during UST system modification work; also discovered 2 tanks with fuel & water.

12/30/2010: additional petroleum release (gasoline) recorded in 2007 after reviewing groundwater data for sampling at east side of facility; UST contractor found contamination in October 2008 during upgrade with gasoline at 2300 ppm in soil; DEQ requested work plan for gasoline assessment in 12/2008 letter - C. Rodda",,"Site development did not occur until after 1955 based on a review of historic photos. By 1967, the property was developed. Previous land use of the site was a truck stop until 1989 when purchased by Bassett-Hyland Energy Company.
Contractors removed two 1,000-gallon diesel fuel tanks in 2002 and reported to DEQ a petroleum release from the tank system. The BHEC facility continued to use two additional underground tanks, installed in 1984 and upgraded in 2002. The two 5,000-gallon tanks store gasoline. BHEC continues to use the site as a commercial distributor and retailer of petroleum based products. The site currently stores fuel in several above ground tanks (AST) for distribution of fuel across SW Oregon.
An underground storage tank system leaked at the property. Contractors discovered the petroleum release during tank removal activities in 2002. Two 1,000-gallon tanks were decommissioned by removal as two other tanks were upgraded for continued use at the facility.
Contractors collected six soil samples from the tank excavation walls and floor, as well as two groundwater samples from two test pits. Groundwater entered the excavations at 3.5 feet bgs. Contractors excavated 75 tons of petroleum-contaminated soil from the tank cavity during the November 2002 actions.
Analysis for soil and groundwater samples included gasoline, diesel, and gasoline-related
constituents (benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, xylenes, naphthalene, and other volatile organic compounds, or VOCs).
Gasoline and diesel-range hydrocarbons remained in soil following excavation.
Recommendation:
Following tank removal and remedial excavation in 2002, petroleum hydrocarbons remained in soil. Based on sample results for soil and groundwater, acceptable risk levels are not exceeded with continued commercial use of the property and protective measures for workers if soil is exposed at the property or beneath W. Lockhart Avenue right of way to the north of the site. Implementation of a HASP and Contaminated Media Management Plant (CMMP) is necessary for future excavations by site workers or construction crews.",19130.0,0.0,ALEIDEL,2019-10-10 11:18:41.200
