21935,15-02-1440,15,02,1440,5483.0,2002-12-04,2002-12-04 11:36:23,TEXACO STATION (NOW SHELL) - MEDFORD,1968 CRATER LAKE HWY,MEDFORD,97501,(541) 772-9116,False,2.0,GENREM,4.0,NFA,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : LOW LEVELS OF GASOLINE CONSTITUENTS AND 660PPM GX DISCOVERED AT ONE SAMPLE POINT DURING SITE ASSESSMENT, ALL ELSE ND, SOURCE UNKNOWN, GENERIC REMEDY NFA PROPOSED..ESC *** low levels of various gasoline constituents and 660 ppm Gx at one sample point (MW-2), all else is ND, generic remedy NFA requested",,,1634.0,0.0,GDECK,2011-06-03 17:18:40.840
21936,26-02-1441,26,02,1441,,2002-05-16,2002-12-06 11:02:39,"BENAVIDEZ, LEOPOLDO",2120 SE 141ST AVE,PORTLAND,97211,,False,7.0,MATRIX,1.0,CRT,False,True,,,False,,,,,,,,47723.0,,CONV,2002-12-07 03:47:11.000
21937,24-02-1442,24,02,1442,5929.0,2002-12-04,2002-12-09 10:26:24,Woodburn Mobil,460-480 N PACIFIC HWY,WOODBURN,97071,(503) 981-3550,False,1.0,GW,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : SEE RELEASE FORM FOR LONG COMMENT ON RP DISPUTE. *** NPDES genreal discharge permit #114811 issued for wastewater discharge 6-6-2006.  Active pump and treatment in operation. rhr,,"The site was occupied by a gasoline and service station from sometime before 1968 until 1988. In 1988 the underground storage tanks were removed and the site was redeveloped into an auto body shop which has occupied the property since that time. Contamination is the result of leaks from product piping and a leak in a used oil tank. Soil in the northern half of the site and into the Pacific Highway right of way is impacted with gasoline and related constituents. These impacts are present between 8 and 14 feet below ground. A small pocket of soil located behind the former service station is impacted between 6 and 12 feet below ground. Groundwater remains impacted by gasoline beneath the building and extending to the north-northeast beneath the Laurel Avenue right of way. Groundwater has been shown to fluctuate between 5 and 14 feet below ground depending on the season. The USTs were decommissioned in 1988 and a groundwater pump and treat was operated onsite between 2006 and 2010. The site and all surrounding properties are expected to remain commercial and connected to the municipal water system. A Contaminated Media Management Plan was prepared for any future construction or utility repairs that may encounter contamination. Specifically the area beneath the footprint of the service station building, which remains significantly impacted at concentrations exceeding construction and excavation worker screening levels. An opportunity to comment on the proposed closure was given to neighboring property owners, utility providers, the City of Woodburn, and ODOT. No comments were received during the comment period. This investigation focused solely on petroleum contamination from the UST systems, including all waste oil constituents, and did not investigate possible contamination (e.g. solvents) resulting from the properties use as an automotive repair facility.",142569.0,0.0,GDECK,2019-10-22 15:04:56.933
