22051,03-02-1556,03,02,1556,2605.0,2002-12-26,2002-12-26 13:21:30,KMETIC RV STORAGE,18557 PORTLAND AVE,GLADSTONE,97027,(503) 652-8026,False,4.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"A 1,000-gallon gasoline UST and a 675-gallon diesel UST were removed from the site in December 2002, along with 31.13 tons of PCS.  Shallow groundwater samples (< 3 ft bgs) showed up to 8,620 ppb gasoline and 8,600 ppb diesel.  Three monitoring wells were installed in November 2004 and initially showed no contamination.  However, subsequent groundwater gradient measurements showed variable flow directions, from WNW to SE.  DEQ requested additional groundwater investigations but the RP balked.  The site was backlogged in July 2010 for lack of progress.  (7/19/10 KPD)

In August 2016, the project was taken off the wait list due to a pending transaction, wherein the City of Gladstone would purchase the property for commercial use. DEQ re-evaluated the site data, based in part on recent (June 2016) sampling of monitoring wells.  Consistent with data collected in November 2004, VOCs were not detected at levels above reporting limits.  RNSA submitted an August 2016 report that expands arguments for closure as presented in the April 2004 risk-based report.  RNSA reported that groundwater levels and flow in shallow groundwater were evaluated 7 times between November 2004 and June 2016.  Of those seven evensts, three are believed to represent fully stablized groundater data.  The groundwater data suggest a generally westerly flow in the wet months of the year and a south-southeasterly to southeasterly flow in the drier parts of the year.  Although the groundwater monitornig wells do not provide perfect delineation of contamination, soil data collected from the former UST pit and points to the west suggest limited impact.  Shallow groundwater is found within three feet of the surface and is assoicated with very low gradients. Groundwater contamination appears to be limited to the area immediately east of the former tank pit.  Shallow groundwater at the site has no likely future beneficial use.  Wetlands located within 250 feet west and north of the site are not located in the LOF and are unlikely to be impacted by site contamination.  Site conditions are not likley to pose unacceptable risks under the likely future use scenario.  (JKS 9-15-16).",,,88812.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2016-09-15 10:58:20.127
