22275,22-03-0199,22,03,0199,12280.0,2003-02-04,2003-02-05 11:41:57,Former American Cemwood Plant,595 GEARY ST NE,ALBANY,97321,(503) 932-4793,False,1.0,GW,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : DON'T KNOW WHEN TANKS WERE LAST USED.  PERMITS NOT REQUIRED. *** Sent letter requesting site status and/or GWMW reports 6-25-2004, nothing since 7-2003, rhr.

NFA with EES issued 4/17/2008.  Site assessment in 2019 prompted reopening of ECSI 1262 and LUST 22-03-0199 to reevaluate EES.  New 2019 EES removed previous restrictions. New NFA to be issued. ...gdeck 9/30/2019.",,"(ABC 9/30/19) Former LUST (#22-03-0199) and ECSI (#1262) site explanation. The former American Cemwood site was historically used as a sand and gravel facility, concrete producer, and manufacture of cement tile and other roofing products. In 1992 DEQ received a complaint that waste was being disposed to the adjoining Willamette River. The site was placed in DEQ’s ECSI database and given the identification #1262. In April 1992, a site inspection was completed by DEQ. No evidence of dumping or waste disposal to the river was identified; however, soil staining was observed in a used oil storage area. The 1992 ESCI file was subsequently closed. During site redevelopment in 2003, four USTs, numerous 55-gallon drums, timbers, and petroleum contaminated soil were discovered near a former refining building. The site was added to DEQ’s LUST database and given the identification #22-03-0199. Approximately 8,400 tons of contaminated soil was excavated from two locations and taken off the site. Residual contamination in a few locations exceeded RBCs. In 2008 the DEQ issued a conditional NFA determination for the site. The NFA included several land use conditions that were recorded with the property deed through an EES. During March 2019 shallow soil and soil gas sampling was conducted at the site to assess risks for property redevelopment to multi-family residential use. Results indicated low risk to future users of the site and land use restrictions from 2008 were lifted. A Contaminated Media Management Plan was written to address residual contaminated material that may be encountered during site redevelopment work and/or work near a City of Albany sewer line running through the site.",97812.0,0.0,GDECK,2020-03-18 13:39:19.187
