22870,26-03-0806,26,03,0806,,2003-04-30,2003-04-30 10:36:29,"CROWSE, ROBERT",3403 SE 58TH AVE,PORTLAND,97206,,False,7.0,MATRIX,1.0,CRT,False,True,,,False,,,,,,,,49077.0,,CONV,2003-05-01 07:07:26.000
22871,20-03-0807,20,03,0807,8133.0,2003-04-30,2003-04-30 11:06:17,Olympic Texaco,2303 OLYMPIC STREET,SPRINGFIELD,97477,(503) 746-4874,False,1.0,RBCA,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : SEE ALSO 20-88-4063 ***,20-88-4063 SUNNY Olympic,"Four 10,000-gallon underground tanks (3 gasoline, 1 diesel) were decommissioned by removal in April 2003. A push-probe investigation was completed earlier in January 2002 that indicated a petroleum release had occurred. As part of decommissioning, 460 tons of contaminated soil was removed from the tank cavity and thermally treated at TPS Technologies in Portland. Further cleanup included pumping 10,000 gallons of contaminated water from the excavation, which was treated on-site by aeration and discharged to the Springfield sanitary sewer. Confirmation soil samples were collected from each excavation sidewall. The highest concentration of gasoline was 1,650 ppm in UST2-SW-8. A groundwater sample was collected from an observation well (OW-1), as sheen was present on the pit water. The water sample from OW-1 had gasoline at 3,100 ppb and benzene at 311 ppb. In November 2003, contractors installed three borings (GWP-1 to GWP-3). Soil samples indicated some gasoline and VOCs, but groundwater samples had relatively high concentrations of contaminants (gasoline at 512,000 ppb, benzene at 6,440 ppb, 1,2,4-trimethylbenzene at 9,020 ppb, and naphthalene at 3,400 ppb in water from GWP-2). A single monitoring well (MW-1) was installed at this location adjacent to Olympic Street in March 2004. Quarterly monitoring showed a decrease in concentrations, and the contractor requested a determination for “no further action”. Further assessment was requested by DEQ. Petroleum concentrations in 2005 sampling showed a decrease in contaminant levels in MW-1. Contractors installed three monitoring wells (MW-13, MW-14, and MW-15) within the sidewalk north of Olympic Street and the subject property in 2009. Soil samples collected from the boreholes did not contain gasoline contaminants. Groundwater was encountered at 11 to 15 feet bgs in the newly installed wells, and samples were collected in August 2009.  Off-site well MW-13 had ethylbenzene at 5.3 ppb and gasoline at 140 ppb, and water from on-site well MW-9 had gasoline at 17,000 ppb, benzene at 47 ppb, and ethylbenzene at 1,200 ppb. Quarterly monitoring was conducted and concentrations decreased to within acceptable levels at each well since the October 2009 sample event. Soil-gas wells were installed in 2012 to evaluate potential vapor intrusion. Some contaminants were detected in vapor samples, but below risk levels.",23502.0,0.0,GDECK,2014-01-02 15:53:18.090
