24127,24-03-2061,24,03,2061,,2003-09-25,2003-09-29 12:29:16,City R-O-W at Circle K,2495 FAIRGROUNDS RD NE (AT HIGHLAND),SALEM,97303,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : TANK WAS IN CITY'S RIGHT OF WAY AT CORNER FAIRGROUNDS/HIGHLAND.  UST HAD BEEN PREVIOUSLY FILLED W/SAND. WHEN UST WAS REMOVED AS PART OF CITY'S ROAD/UTILITY WORK, H20 GUSHED INTO PIT; LATER DETERMINED THAT H2O CAME FROM RECENTLY INSTALLED UTILITY TRENCH ADJACENT TO UST, WHERE SURFACE H2O HAD COLLECTED. *** UST had been previously filled w/sand.  When UST was removed as part of City's road/utility work, H20 gushed into pit; later determined that H2O came from recently installed utility trench adjacent to UST, where surface H2O had collected.",,,78990.0,0.0,GDECK,2008-10-31 16:14:00.503
24128,24-03-2062,24,03,2062,,2003-09-25,2003-09-29 12:46:14,Fairgrounds at Capitol,FAIRGROUNDS RD NE @ CAPITOL ST NE,SALEM,97303,,False,7.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : 2 USTS FOUND UNDER CENTER OF FAIRGROUNDS RD, JUST N. OF CAPITOL WHEN CITY WAS DOING ROAD CONSTRUCTION.  UST #1 EMPTY, PULLED 4/29/03; HIGHEST % = 3.5 MG/KG GAS.  UST #2 HAD SAND IN IT, PULLED 6/25/03; TESTED ND FOR GAS & DIESEL. *** 2 USTs found under center of Fairgrounds Rd, just N. of Capitol when City was doing road construction.  UST #1 empty, pulled 4/29/03; highest % = 3.5 mg/Kg gas.  UST #2 had sand in it, pulled 6/25/03; tested ND for gas & diesel.",8/20/2012: there are multiple cleanup sites in this area of Salem - check database for nearby LUST or ECSI sites; this LUST does not appear related to other known releases (C. Rodda),"The City of Salem public works staff encountered a 1,000-gallon underground storage tank while excavating at Fairgrounds Road for a new stormwater line in April 2003. The tank (UST #1) was located just beneath the asphalt at 2 feet below ground surface near the intersection with Capitol Street. After notification to DEQ, the tank was decommissioned by removal on April 29, 2003. A second tank (UST #2) was found on April 30 approximately 20 feet southwest of the first tank location. The capacity for UST #2 was estimated at 1,500 gallons and the top of the tank was at 2.5 feet bgs. The tank was removed in June 2003 when construction activities permitted opening of adjacent traffic lanes. Contaminated soil was present beneath UST #1 at 4 to 6 feet bgs. Three soil samples were collected by a contractor (CJE) directly beneath the tank and trench sidewalls, and showed relatively low concentrations of petroleum remained at this location. Soil sample B-6 was collected from 6 feet bgs and contained gasoline at 3.85 parts per million, xylenes at 0.0595 ppm, and lead at 8.4 ppm. Contaminated soil was also found beneath UST #2. Samples were collected from 8.5 feet bgs after removing soil. No petroleum was detected in the confirmation soil samples. The tanks had not previously been registered with DEQ. Both tanks were empty and determined to be non-regulated, but had stored petroleum products. Almost 28 tons of petroleum-contaminated soil was excavated from the first tank location and utility trenches by a contractor (Gelco). An additional 39 tons of PCS was removed at the second tank location. The soil was transported to Riverbend Landfill in Yamhill County for disposal. Excavation was completed at both tank locations from 6 to 8.5 feet bgs. Groundwater was not encountered during tank decommissioning or soil removal activities.",78991.0,0.0,CRODDA,2012-11-08 10:57:45.283
