24262,36-03-2197,36,03,2197,12191.0,2003-10-13,2003-10-16 17:10:10,Former Asphalt Batch Plant,103 SE BOOTH BEND RD,MCMINNVILLE,97128,(503) 434-7502,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,3.0,False,False,False,False,False,Pjt Mgr = JLG 1 1500-gallon UST decommissioned as part opf TBA w/MAry Camarata as PM,,"For over 40 years, Martin & Wright Paving Company operated an asphalt plant at 103 SE Booth Bend Road on the edge of McMinnville. In the mid-1990s the property was abandoned, leaving a contaminated site with little hope of development. The property came into Yamhill County ownership in December 2003 through tax foreclosure proceedings.  In the fall of 2004, Yamhill County applied for an EPA Brownfields Cleanup Grant to facilitate redevelopment of the site. Yamhill County intends to transfer the two-acre property to the McMinnville School District for use as an elementary school parking lot. The construction of the future school is planned on an adjoining twelve-acre property. EPA awarded the Brownfields Cleanup Grant to Yamhill County in May 2005. The EPA Brownfields grant funded site cleanup work began in the spring of 2006.   Clean up of the two-acre property included: 
•    Removal of all surface equipment and debris.
•    Further investigation and monitoring of the areas identified during DEQ assessment.
•    Installation of monitoring wells and one year (four consecutive quarters) of groundwater monitoring.
•    Assessment of risk associated with any remaining contamination.
•    Steps to remove, reduce, or otherwise mitigate areas or pathways exceeding DEQ risk-based concentrations.  
McMinnville School District has, through a Perspective Purchaser Agreement with DEQ, acquired the property from Yamhill County. A new ""green"" school has been erected on the adjoining twelve-acre property. As a component of the reuse plan, the School District has placed a storm-water detention pond on the former asphalt batch plant property. The construction of the detention pond added new requirements to ensure the continued safety of human health and the environment.  Clean fill was used to line the detention pond and isolate petroleum contamination that remains under Booth Bend Road to the south and the railroad south east of the site.  With the removal of the pollution sources and the most heavily contaminated soils, the remaining contamination, was determined to not pose a threat under current and reasonably likely future use.  The closure plan proposes to allow the remaining contamination to degrade naturally in place, includes a formal restriction on the use of onsite groundwater, and requires maintence of a soil (and pavement in some ares) cap.",79588.0,0.0,JGLASS,2011-03-15 10:33:30.927
