24794,14-04-0108,14,04,0108,,2004-01-30,2004-02-02 16:27:50,"PARRISH, BILL",5840 HIGHWAY 35,HOOD RIVER,97041,,False,7.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,,,"(May 201] An abandoned 340-gallon gasoline storage tank was located on the property.  The tank was buried on end with approximately eight inches of the tank located above ground. 

Two soil samples were collected in January 2004 to evaluate subsurface conditions.  A concentration of gasoline was detected at 334 milligrams per kilograms (mg/kg). A release was reported to DEQ on January 30, 2004 and leaking UST (LUST) No. 14 04 108 was assigned to the site.

The tank cut open and cleaned on February 11, 2004.  Approximately 20 gallons of product was removed from the tank.  Six soil samples were collected to define the extent of contamination.  All six soil samples were analyzed for gasoline with concentrations being detected in three samples at levels ranging from 334 mg/kg to 40,600 mg/kg. This area was removed by excavation on February 24, 2004.

The tank was decommissioned by removal on February 24, 2004. A total of 9.18 tons of petroleum-contaminated soil (PCS) was excavated and transported to Wasco County Landfill for disposal.  Groundwater was not encountered.

Two soil confirmation samples were collected from the bottom of the excavation and analyzed for gasoline.  Concentrations of gasoline were not detected.

A no further action determination was issued for the site on May 12, 2010.  A copy of the NFA letter may be viewed in the site document section of this database.",84406.0,2643.0,KROBERT,2010-05-12 14:28:46.927
