26988,26-04-2303,26,04,2303,,2004-11-10,2004-11-16 10:21:52,PHOENIX 140,3034 SE 79TH  AVE,PORTLAND,97206,,False,1.0,RBCA,1.0,CRT,False,True,,,True,,,,,,,,95369.0,4316.0,CONV,2004-11-16 10:21:52.000
26989,20-04-2304,20,04,2304,998.0,2004-11-16,2004-11-16 11:02:47,CHEVRON USA INC - 95818,515 E BROADWAY,EUGENE,97401,(503) 484-0446,False,1.0,RBCA,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,RP for site is Chevron; owner as of June 2012 is private entity,,"Three 10,000-gallon gasoline tanks, one 1,000-gallon waste oil tank, and three hydraulic hoists were decommissioned by removal from the site in November 2004. Soil samples collected after decommissioning found gasoline at 6,200 ppm at W-1 within the tank excavation. Analysis for VOCs was not completed on soil samples from 13' deep excavation. Petroleum contaminated soil was not removed. Consultants installed six borings (SB-1 to SB-6) in May 2006 to 18 feet bgs. Samples were taken outside the tank cavity at 3' bgs and from the soil-groundwater interface at 10' bgs and analyzed for gasoline, diesel, VOCs, and PAHs. Relatively low concentrations of petroleum were found in samples. Groundwater was collected and gasoline was 16,000 ppb at SB-2 south of the former tank cavity. In 2008, further assessment was conducted and monitoring well MW-3 was abandoned. Soil borings SB-7 and SB-8 were installed within the excavation to acquire native soil data at 12.5 to 13 feet bgs. Gasoline was at 1,200 ppm in SB-7 from 13 feet bgs within the tank cavity. A sample from this boring at 17.5 feet bgs contained gasoline at only 16 ppm. Further analysis identified several less volatile constituents present in soil. Groundwater sampling of the new MWs was initiated in June 2008. Gasoline and diesel were in groundwater at MW-6 and MW-7, located at north and south ends of the former tank nest. Gasoline was 9,600 ppb at MW-6 and 1,400 ppb at MW-7, while diesel was respectively 10,000 ppb  and 1,200 ppb. Low VOCs were measured in water from MW-6 and MW-7, while samples from the remaining wells did not contain constituents above laboratory detection levels. Groundwater monitoring continued through 2011 and showed overall decreased contaminants. In the final year of sampling, groundwater did not have petroleum above risk levels. Because gasoline and ethylbenzene remained in soil above DEQ's risk standards for vapor intrusion, 2013 redevelopment plans called for soil removal upon site renovation. DEQ conditionally approved closure with restrictions on land and groundwater use through a recorded deed notification. The new property owner removed contaminated soil during construction early in 2014 and collected samples to determine if any petroleum remains above risk levels. Gasoline hydrocarbons remain in soil greater than 12 feet bgs, below the parking garage. Because first floor will not include apartments, remaining soil contamination does not pose a risk to the building occupants.",3502.0,0.0,GDECK,2014-06-19 16:34:43.237
