27724,06-05-0520,06,05,0520,9413.0,2005-03-09,2005-03-21 11:13:42,COOS BAY SERVICE CENTER,135 W LOCKHART AVE,COOS BAY,97420,(503) 464-6478,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"**** Contaminated soil at dispenser and tank pit, very shallow (~1 ft) water, slight sheen observed... 3/9/05.",,"The site has historically been utilized by PacifiCorp as a maintenance facility.  A petroleum release was reported in 2005 following the removal of diesel USTs.
Two USTs were decommissioned by removal from a single tank cavity in April 2005.  Over 10 cubic yards of material consisting mostly of gravel and asphalt were removed from the site during decommissioning activities and disposed of at Dry Creek Landfill in Medford.  Water entered the tank cavity at a rapid rate, and soil samples could not be collected from the tank pit by EMC following removal of the USTs.  An initial report submitted to the DEQ for the project indicated that groundwater entered the excavation at 3 feet bgs and a sheen or petroleum odor was present.

Assessment activities were completed in May 2008 by BioScape Technologies.  Five borings (PPL-1 through PPL-5) were installed for the collection of soil and groundwater samples.  All soil samples collected from the boreholes were analyzed for gasoline- and diesel-range hydrocarbons, and measured no concentrations above laboratory detection levels.

Groundwater samples (G-1 through G-5) were obtained from each borehole.  Oil-range hydrocarbons were detected in G-2 at 522 ppb, and gasoline-range hydrocarbons were detected in G-4 at 91.2 ppb and in G-5 at 341 ppb.  
Only naphthalene was detected in the sample from G-2 at 0.517 ppb.  In addition, MTBE  was detected in G-2 at 0.98 ppb, and G-5 measured isopropylbenzene at 6.54 ppb and 
n-propylbenzene at 21.4 ppb.  Lead was present in groundwater from G-4 at 146 ppb.",11221.0,0.0,GDECK,2009-05-07 12:24:54.993
