soil sample data submitted via email, pocket of PCS next to building undefined, no constituent data yet (Esc 11-aug-08)

Gx soil data > residential indoor air and leaching to groundwater (1-dec-08)",,"The site has operated as a market since the 1930s.  A suspected release of petroleum was reported to the DEQ by Western States Environmental in 2006 following a failed tank tightness test of the UST  system.  Two USTs installed in the 1940’s and 1950’s for fuel service operations were decommissioned by removal in 2008.  The initial assessment occurred in March 2008 during decommissioning of Tank 1.  This tank contained 40 gallons of oil-water mix, which was disposed of by Oil Re-Refining.  The product lines from Tank 1 were traced to the west side of the building and the cavity for Tank 2.  Six soil samples were collected from the tank cavity limits, and the highest concentration of gasoline was measured in the west wall sample at 1,100 ppm .  Following the excavation of 26 tons of PCS at Tank 1, three soil samples were collected and measured gasoline below laboratory detection levels.  In June 2008, Tank 2 was decommissioned by removal.  Six soil samples were collected from the tank cavity after the excavation of 200 tons of PCS.  Each confirmation soil sample measured no concentrations of petroleum hydrocarbons above laboratory detection levels.  The product lines and a dispenser for Tank 2 were removed but soil samples were not collected as part of the decommissioning activities.  The product line ran from Tank 2 towards the northwest corner of the market, with a dispenser located on the north side of the building.  This dispenser was used by the owner for personal use and not for retail fuel sales.  The retail dispensers were originally located directly above the UST nest.  

Additional excavation was conducted in September and December 2008 along the product lines leading east from Tank 1 south of the market building.  Contamination appeared to continue beneath a concrete slab at the southeast corner of the market building.  Soil samples collected from the final excavation limits indicated that concentrations of gasoline remained adjacent to the building and near Stewart Avenue.  An environmental assessment was also done in February 2009.  Two borings were placed outside of the remedial excavation for Tank 1 at the south side of the market building, and one was installed to the north of the building.  Groundwater was not encountered and soil samples were collected from each boring at depths of 14 feet bgs.  Results indicated that no gasoline-range hydrocarbons were present in soil at the selected sample points and intervals.",92987.0,6951.0,CRODDA,2009-07-16 14:27:22.277
