30193,36-06-0382,36,06,0382,10639.0,2006-03-02,2006-03-07 16:47:15.000,Sheridan FCI,27072 BALLSTON ROAD,SHERIDAN,97378,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"*** SEQUENT LUST COMMENTS : DURING THE DECOMMISSIONING OF A 550-GALLON OVERFLOW UST AT THE FACILITY, CONTAMINATED SOIL AND GROUNDWATER WAS DISCOVERED APPARENTLY DUE TO LEAKAGE FROM THE FITTING WHERE THE INFLOW PIPE ELBOWS INTO THE UST. UST IS EXEMPT FROM PERMITTING (JUST FOR OVERFLOW, NOT STORAGE) SEE ALSO 36-99-4116 *** During decom of a 550-gal overflow (exempt) UST at the facility, contaminated soil & GW was discovered, apparently due to leakage from the fitting where the inflow pipe elbows into the UST.","USTC 36-99-4116, ECSI #4083","The site operates as a federal prison with numerous storage tanks for chemical and petroleum products.The facility had two 550-gallon USTs to store hazardous materials from a furniture manufacturing operation. MEK was used as painting solvent. One UST was located at the Federal Prison Camp UNICOR Warehouse (FPC UST) at the western portion of the facility, with the other at the FCI UNICOR Factory (FCI UST) to the east. Petroleum was detected in soil at the USTs following decommissioning, and LUST 36-06-0382 was initiated. MEK-contaminated water was pumped from FCI UST prior to removal in 2006. Groundwater entered the cavity at 4 feet bgs and an oily sheen was apparent on the water surface. A solvent odor and contaminated soil were also evident and a soil sample measured gasoline, diesel, heavy oil, and several petroleum compounds, but did not contain PAHs or PCBs. As part of cleanup actions, 10.86 tons of soil were removed from the FCI UST location and disposed of at a landfill. Soil samples were not collected from the sidewalls. Further assessment was completed at FPC UST in October 2008. Soil and groundwater at FPC UST did not have petroleum or solvent-related contaminants. The assessment also included six borings (3 as monitoring wells) at FCI UST. Soil samples were collected with only 1,1-DCA at 1.49 ppm. Water testing found 1,1-DCA in two of the monitoring wells. From October 2008 to July 2009, no contaminants were detected in groundwater other than 1,1-DCA (max. 9.09 ppb).

In addition, a 550-gallon diesel spill from ASTs occurred in 2003. Emergency response actions included PCS removal, pumping diesel-contaminated groundwater from excavated utility trenches, and placing absorbent booms in a nearby drainage ditch. Soil removal could not be completed effectively around the utility trenches due to the number of buried lines. A diesel-water mix was pumped from a system vault and disposed at a treatment facility. Contractors installed borings in May 2004 to assess contamination related to the diesel spill. Samples identified diesel contamination remaining in soil, most notably at 13,000 ppm in a shallow soil sample adjacent to the ASTs. See also ECSI #4083.

MEK and other contaminants were detected in an irrigation well first reported to DEQ in 2010. The well water was sampled several times, with the most recent monitoring indicating no contaminants from MEK or other solvents in the well.",80977.0,7210.0,CRODDA,2016-08-25 10:46:49.270
