30945,22-06-1132,22,06,1132,3960.0,2006-06-13,2006-06-21 11:24:07.000,ARCO 4379,3110 PACIFIC BLVD SE,ALBANY,97321,(541)926-8929,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,(MOTT 12/1/16) Project lost activity in September 2015. If they re-enter the cleanup program the site should be assigned to Norman Read.,,"The ARCO site was a former gas station with three 12,000-gallon gas USTs and two dispenser islands. The gas USTs, pumps, and fuel lines were decommissioned by removal in June 2006. Petroleum contaminated soils were encountered during decommissioning. Residual gas concentrations in soil ranged up to 1,850 mg/kg. Gas constituents were detected at generally low concentrations. Between 2006 and 2007 soil borings were completed and wells MW-1 to MW-3 were installed. Gas was found at low concentrations in soil, ranging up to 143 mg/kg. Gas in groundwater were greatest down gradient (N-NW) of the former UST nest at MW-2, and with detections up to 58,900µg/l. Detections were much lower in up gradient well MW-1 (up to 434µg/l) and moderately high in cross-gradient well MW-3 (up to 14,300µg/l). In 2008, wells MW-4 through MW-7 were installed. Concentrations were identified up to 70,800µg/l at MW-2 and 40,400µg/l at MW-4. In 2010, MW-8 was installed across Highway 99E. The well did not contain detections above lab MRLs. During August 2011 six borings were advanced in the vicinity of the former UST nest. Results indicated soil contamination was limited to the SW area of the station building. Gas was detected up to 7,740 mg/kg within the former nest area. Detections of gas constituents were moderate to low. Structures were removed from the site in 2012, including the former service station building. During March 2018 four soil borings and one temporary groundwater well point were completed. Gas detections in soil representing left in-place contamination beneath the former service station building were low to moderate, with the highest detection found at 1,320mg/kg. Temporary well, TW1 was installed in the boring, and contained gas in groundwater at 24,300µg/l. Gas constituents were low to moderate and below applicable RBCs. Groundwater monitoring through 2018 indicated decreasing gas concentrations. Based on findings, unacceptable risks may be present to occupational users and construction and excavation workers at and near the site. Ethylbenzene in onsite soil exceeds the occupational vapor intrusion into buildings pathway and gas in groundwater exceeds direct contact by a construction and/or excavation worker RBC. As such, all site buildings must incorporate vapor intrusion mitigation and a Contaminated Media Management Plan was prepared prior to any site development and any potential future work in the Pacific Highway right of way.",3377.0,0.0,ACHAVEZ,2021-02-23 14:33:21.460
