31680,04-06-1866,04,06,1866,,2006-10-20,2006-10-20 15:10:10.000,COLUMBIA MEMORIAL HOSPITAL (MOB SITE),2265 EXCHANGE ST,ASTORIA,97103,,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"File was transferred from HOT program to LUST program on 12/2/2006.

In August 2006, geophysical and geotechnical and environmental investigations were conducted at the site prior to construction of a new medical office building. In September 2006, Geocon advanced borings near a heating oil UST associated with residential apartments near the north boundary of the property and a UST system (3 tanks) associated with a former gasoline station in the NE corner of the site.   Samples from both areas contained petroleum hydrocarbons and evidence of historical releases.  In March 2007, Robert D. Miller decommissioned four USTs from the site. Compliance sampling revealed gasoline and diesel at maximum concentrations of 16,000 mg/kg and 15,800 mg/kg respectively.  A pit water sample from the regulated UST excavation contained high levels of gasoline and VOCs.  Following decommissioning, Robert D. Miller supervised the removal of approximately 1,038 tons of contaminated soil from the site.  After the soil removal, a second pit water sample collected from the pit contained markely lower levels of gasoline and VOCs consistent with the removal of a significant source.  Based on the detection of VOCs and wood degradation byproducts (from historical use of sawdust as fill) in site media, Columbia Memorial Hospital constructed the medical office building with engineering controls including a 60-mil vapor barrier and passive vent.  Based on groundwater samples collected from the northern portion of the site, petroluem hydrocarbons and VOCs are not expected to extend a significant distance north or northwest of the site.  Site conditions do not pose unacceptable risks to current and likely future occupational workers (JKS 9-3-14)",,"n August 2006, geophysical and geotechnical and environmental investigations were conducted at the site prior to construction of a new medical office building. In September 2006, Geocon advanced borings near a heating oil UST associated with residential apartments near the north boundary of the property and a UST system (3 tanks) associated with a former gasoline station in the NE corner of the site.   Samples from both areas contained petroleum hydrocarbons and evidence of historical releases.  In March 2007, Robert D. Miller decommissioned four USTs from the site. Compliance sampling revealed gasoline and diesel at maximum concentrations of 16,000 mg/kg and 15,800 mg/kg respectively.  A pit water sample from the regulated UST excavation contained high levels of gasoline and VOCs.  Following decommissioning, Robert D. Miller supervised the removal of approximately 1,038 tons of contaminated soil from the site.  After the soil removal, a second pit water sample collected from the pit contained markely lower levels of gasoline and VOCs consistent with the removal of a significant source.  Based on the detection of VOCs and wood degradation byproducts (from historical use of sawdust as fill) in site media, Columbia Memorial Hospital constructed the medical office building with engineering controls including a 60-mil vapor barrier and passive vent.  Based on groundwater samples collected from the northern portion of the site, petroluem hydrocarbons and VOCs are not expected to extend a significant distance north or northwest of the site.  Site conditions do not pose unacceptable risks to current and likely future occupational workers (JKS 9-3-14)",101547.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2014-09-02 14:39:17.340
