31785,20-06-1971,20,06,1971,8139.0,2006-11-08,2006-11-08 12:46:27.823,Shell Station 121228,4197 FRANKLIN BLVD,EUGENE,97403,(503) 746-4494,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Site assessment by Delta Env. Consultants on 11/1-2/06.  Lab results rec'd 11/7/06 - Gx in GW 6,130 ppb & 8,940 ppb in 2 borings.  C.Rodda 11/8/06",ECSI #501 Sunny Service Station - Glenwood,"The site is currently abandoned but formerly operated as a fuel service station.  A residential building is located on the subject property but has also been abandoned.  Four USTs  and four pump islands (see attached site map) were most recently used as part of facility operations.  Previously, five other USTs were utilized at the site.  A 2006 environmental assessment identified petroleum contamination in soil and groundwater by fuel released from the tank system.

DEQ’s UST file indicates that this site has used nine permitted USTs, all of which have been decommissioned by removal.  

An environmental assessment was performed by Delta on behalf of Shell Oil in November 2006.  The assessment included the installation of three borings for the collection of soil and groundwater samples.  Petroleum contaminants were detected in soil and groundwater samples north of the UST basin and pump islands.  Concentrations of contaminants were detected in soil samples at 15 to 20 feet bgs .  Groundwater contained gasoline at 8,940 ppb , benzene at 8.6 ppb, 1,2,4-trimethylbenzene at 1,030 ppb, and naphthalene at 151 ppb.  

Three monitoring wells were installed in February 2007, and a GPR  survey was conducted the following month based on debris encountered in attempted borings.  The wells were advanced from 20 to 23 feet bgs and constructed with 10-foot screens.  Two additional wells were installed in July 2007 and were screened at the same interval.  Quarterly groundwater monitoring was initiated following the respective well installations.  Groundwater measured relatively low concentrations of gasoline-related contaminants throughout monitoring for gasoline- and diesel-range hydrocarbons, RBDM  VOCs , PAHs , and lead.  

To ensure the proper groundwater interval was being sampled, two additional monitoring wells were installed in March 2009.  The wells were screened from 8 to 23 feet bgs.  Soil samples obtained from the boreholes did not measure petroleum contaminants.  Lead was found in soil at 3.11 ppm.  Water samples collected from the new monitoring wells did not contain petroleum contaminants above laboratory detection levels.  However, lead was detected in each well up to 75.2 ppb.",90050.0,0.0,CRODDA,2009-12-09 09:01:10.507
