32001,20-06-2186,20,06,2186,,2006-12-22,2007-01-02 09:30:48.650,Mark Rogers Property,1100 Q St,SPRINGFIELD,97477,541-221-9104,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"Release erroneously reported as a HOT site.  This was a GASOLINE tank.  Robert DeSpain says it's a personal use 1000-gal gasoline UST.

11/6/2007: changed site name to Mark Rogers Property.  The site was originally reported as ""Rogers, Norman"", the son of the deceased Mark Rogers who owned the property where the UST release was discovered.  No reference to the son Norman Rogers should be made for this project.  Contact for this project is the daughter of Mark Rogers, Sharon Wigger (also trustee of the Mark Rogers Trust). - C. Rodda
FILE MOVED TO OFFSITE STORAGE",,"Staton Companies removed a 1,000-gallon gasoline UST  from the site on December 22, 2006.  Soil samples were collected from each sidewall at 5 to 6 feet below ground surface, and six inches below the west and east ends of the tank.  Gasoline was detected in one soil sample (NRTE1) beneath the UST, and was measured at 57.2 parts per million.  This sample was further analyzed for BTEX  constituents, and measured benzene at 0.982 ppm , toluene at 0.494 ppm, ethylbenzene at 0.986 ppm, and xylenes at 5.71 ppm.

Groundwater entered the excavation at 5 feet bgs .  A water sample was collected from the excavation on December 26, 2006, apparently after purging 380 gallons of water four days earlier.  The water sample (NRH2O) was analyzed for gasoline and diesel-range hydrocarbons, as well as BTEX constituents.  Diesel measured 532 µg/L  and xylenes were at 2.45 µg/L.  All other analyzed compounds were measured at less than the laboratory detection level in this pit water sample.",100574.0,9022.0,LFINCHE,2010-04-21 16:26:32.070
