32902,10-07-0885,10,07,0885,,2007-05-29,2007-06-20 10:23:56.983,(Former) Fast Stop Market,457 NW Garden Valley Blvd,ROSEBURG,97470,,False,1.0,,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"ECSI #629: petroleum orginally found offsite, USTs removed in 1986-1987; site assessment completed at adjacent property in April 2007 identified remaining petroleum in soil & GW; no source on this property - release appears to be related to Fast Stop Market.  (CJR 06/20/07)","ECSI #629:  petroleum originally found offsite, USTs removed in 1986-1987.  (CJR 06/20/07)","Xavier Environmental installed twelve push-probe borings and eight “pothole” borings for the collection of soil and groundwater samples at the site and the adjacent motel property.  The assessment was completed on August 21, 2008.  Soil samples collected at the dispenser islands identified gasoline-range hydrocarbons up to 2,990 ppm  in sample P10 from 4 feet bgs .  Benzene was not detected in any of the analyzed soil samples, while several other VOCs  including ethylbenzene, isopropylbenzene, n-propylbenzene, naphthalene, 1,2,4-trimethylbenzene, 1,3,5-trimethylbenzene, and xylenes were measured in soil.  

The highest concentration of gasoline in soil was detected at 4,060 ppm at P9 from 2.5 feet bgs.  This sample point was installed at the intersection of utility trenches on the southeastern portion of the Fast Stop Market property.

Groundwater was not encountered in several of the sample locations, including those along Garden Valley Boulevard and at the dispensers.  Water samples were obtained from the southern portion of the property as well as on the motel property, with the highest concentrations of petroleum contaminants present in PH4 and PH5 on the north side of the motel.  Groundwater from PH7, located within the asphalt patch on the motel property, did not contain petroleum hydrocarbons above laboratory detection levels. 

Sample locations at the northern property line along Garden Valley Boulevard revealed no detectable concentrations of petroleum compounds in soil.  Groundwater was not encountered in these borings.

The VOCs measured during the assessment were generally at relatively low concentrations, while total petroleum hydrocarbons were detected at levels exceeding “risk-based concentrations”.  This scenario is often indicative of an older release, as volatiles are removed from petroleum constituents through degradation but overall hydrocarbons remain present in soil or groundwater.  For example, groundwater from PH-4 measured gasoline-range hydrocarbons at 22,100 ppb , but detected benzene at only 0.8 ppb.  

Based on the results of the assessment conducted in August 2008, it appears that the dispensers, gasoline USTs, and the waste oil and heating oil USTs at the former Fast Stop Market property are sources of petroleum released to the environment.  The Quality Inn Motel (Meyer Motels, Ltd.) is being disregarded as a potential responsible party for the petroleum contamination under USTC #10-07-0885.
