33015,04-07-0998,04,07,0998,12309.0,2007-07-06,2007-07-10 08:07:31.930,TRAILS END RECOVERY,34661 AIRPORT LN,WARRENTON,97146,(503) 861-6030,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"One 2000-gallon UST removed in July 2007.  Impacted soils encountered beneath concrete pad.  Clearwater excavated 21 tons of impacted soils.  Groundwater located at 4 feet.  Sampling at soil-water interface revealed low levels of DRO and ORO.  Pit water sample indicated DRO and ORO and cPAHs.  Investigation outside immedate vicinity of UST excavation is warranted.  Letter to RP sent.  Letter requesting status of additional investigations sent 3-12-09.  Deadline of 4-22-09 given.  Spoke with Tim Wing and Bob Bogar (consultant) on 5-11-09.  Work plan for investigation received on 6-26-09.  Work plan conditionally approved on 7-2-09.  Call made to Bob Bogar on 10-23-09 as no investigation report had been received (left message) (JKS 10-23-09).  Call from Bob Bogar on 11-20-09.  He reported that work was planned for today but had to be called off due to weather on coast.  Test pit investigation completed in November 2009.  Water sample taken had sheen and TPH-D at levels approaching 50,000 ug/L. Discussed with consultant that monitoring wells should be installed.  Follow-up inquiries in made in February, April, and August 2010.  Text of report documenting test pit investigation and well installation work plan received in September 2010.  No figures, laboratory analytical report, or COC documents received until November 2010.  Letter commenting on investigaton report and well installation work plan sent on 11-17-10.  Await report documenting implementation of work plan (JKS 12-29-10).  DEQ contacted by Bob Bogar on 9-12-11 and informed that he is proceeding with installation of monitoring wells the week of September 12, 2011.  Quarterly monitoring performed at site between September 2011 and August 2012.  Report submitted to DEQ in September 2012.  DEQ commented on report in letter dated 10-11-12.  DEQ requested additional quarterly monitoring (at least two events) and a door-to-door survey of nearby properties for water wells.  Need for additional investigation will be evaluated after additional monitoring (JKS 2-6-13).

Additional groundwater monitoing events performed at site March 2013 to May 2014.  Variable groundwater flow directions but northerly to northwesterly flow observed on more than one occasion.  MW-4 near former UST had highest maximum detected concentrations of diesel (2300 ppb) and heavy oil (1200 ppb).  Down-gradient well MW-3 had max diesel and heavy oil detections of 1500 and 870 ppb, respectively.  Groundwater contamination likely extends beneath SE Airport Lane.  A beneficial use survey was completed for the site in 2014.  Properties in vicinity of site were surveyed and with one exception, found to be connected to municipal drinking water. 

In June 2018, a groundwater sample was collected from the water well at the Ron Collman residence to the southwest (34662 Airport Lane).  A water sample collected at the faucet contained gasoline, diesel and heavy oil at estimated concentrations (J flagged) between the laboratory reporting limit and detection limit.  The estimated concentrations are well below RBCs for groundwater ingestion.  Based on groundwater flow, it is unlikely contamination from the former UST extends onto the Collman property or has impacted the drinking water well.  

Concurrent sampling of water in retention pond 1a at the Trails End Recovery (southern portion of property) revealed elevated levels of petroleum hydrocarbons not likely to be related to the former UST.  It is possible that non-tank contamination is present in the southern portion of the site and accounts for the low levels of petroluem hydrocarbons detected in the Collman well.   

Clatsop County and nearby property owners notified of proposed NFA in DEQ letter dated June 5, 2019. No comments received during comment period. Site qualifies for NFA status (JKS 7-1-19).",,"Periodic monitoring performed at site between September 2011 and May 2014. Variable groundwater flow directions but northerly to northwesterly flow observed on more than one occasion.  MW-4 near former UST had highest maximum detected concentrations of diesel (2300 ppb) and heavy oil (1200 ppb).  Down-gradient well MW-3 had max diesel and heavy oil detections of 1500 and 870 ppb, respectively.  Groundwater contamination likely extends beneath SE Airport Lane.  A beneficial use survey was completed for the site in 2014.  Properties in vicinity of site were surveyed and with one exception, found to be connected to municipal drinking water. 

In June 2018, a groundwater sample was collected from the water well at the Ron Collman residence to the southwest (34662 Airport Lane).  A water sample collected at the faucet contained gasoline, diesel and heavy oil at estimated concentrations (J flagged) between the laboratory reporting limit and detection limit.  The estimated concentrations are well below RBCs for groundwater ingestion.  Based on groundwater flow, it is unlikely contamination from the former UST extends onto the Collman property or has impacted the drinking water well.  

Concurrent sampling of water in retention pond 1a at the Trails End Recovery (southern portion of property) revealed elevated levels of petroleum hydrocarbons not likely to be related to the former UST.  It is possible that non-tank contamination is present in the southern portion of the site and accounts for the low levels of petroluem hydrocarbons detected in the Collman well.   

Clatsop County and nearby property owners notified of proposed NFA in DEQ letter dated June 5, 2019. No comments received during comment period. Site qualifies for NFA status (JKS 7-1-19).",103226.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2019-07-01 12:44:44.833
