33185,02-07-1168,02,07,1168,2139.0,2007-08-08,2007-08-08 12:54:41.520,PIONEER TELEPHONE CO,1304 MAIN ST,PHILOMATH,97370,(503) 929-3135,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,(8/8/07 CJR)  Release discovered when petroleum odors were noticed in building.  Found leak at elbow in piping.  NWFF responded to conduct cleanup.  See also OERS #07-1739.,"OERS #07-1739, LUST # 02-94-4156","Petroleum was discovered in a utility vault at the Pioneer Telephone facility in August 2007.  The release was traced to an elbow joint in the product line that leads from a diesel UST to a backup generator at the site.  The utility vault, which is 8 to 10 feet deep, contained water with floating diesel product.

As part of spill response activities, NWFF pumped 1,700 gallons of impacted water and diesel from the utility vault, disposing of the material at Oil Re-Refining in Portland.  To address the soil impacts, NWFF excavated 15.21 tons of PCS  surrounding the elbow joint of the product line.  The PCS was disposed of at Riverbend Landfill in McMinnville.

Seven soil samples (Floor 1, Floor 2, N1, N2, S, W, E) were collected from the sidewalls and bottom of the excavation and were analyzed for diesel- and oil-range hydrocarbons.  The maximum concentration of diesel was detected in sample N2 at 11,400 ppm.  Lube oil was detected in sample W at 256 ppm.  Three of the soil samples were further analyzed for BTEX  and PAHs , with results indicating non-detect to relatively low concentrations of the petroleum constituents in Floor 1, N1, and N2.  
Site assessment activities were completed on March 4, 2008.  Four borings (B-1, B-2, B-3, and B-4) were installed for the collection of soil samples and installation of monitoring wells to complete groundwater sampling.  Soil samples were obtained from each boring and analyzed for petroleum contaminants, including gasoline- and diesel-range hydrocarbons using the NWTPH-HCID  method, BTEX  and PAH constituents, and MTBE .  

EnviroLogic originally proposed to install four monitoring wells for evaluating groundwater conditions.  Boreholes were constructed to 25 feet bgs and groundwater was not encountered.  The boreholes were advanced to 30 feet bgs where water was present, and wells MW-1 and MW-4 were installed at B-1 and B-4, with screens from 20 to 30 feet bgs.  Due to lack of water, borings B-2 and B-3 were abandoned and not completed as monitoring wells.  

Water was measured near 5 feet bgs in the newly installed wells prior to sampling.  Groundwater samples were obtained from the two newly installed wells on March 18, 2008.  Gasoline, diesel, and oil-range hydrocarbons were not detected above the laboratory detection levels.  The only constituent detected in groundwater was naphthalene, measured in water from MW-1 at 0.171 ppb and in the sample from MW-4 at 0.265 ppb.",19404.0,0.0,CRODDA,2009-07-08 10:53:25.110
