Release from a 675-gallon farm use tank reported September 2007.  Up to 2,820 ppm gasoline and 1,190 ppm kerosene in soil.  Over 218 tons of PCS shipped to Hillsboro Landfill.  Nine confirmation soil samples all reportedly non-detect.  May be a simple Soil Matrix NFA.  (6/30/09 KPD)",,,103938.0,10524.0,LKENT,2011-11-22 13:51:53.063
33478,20-07-1460,20,07,1460,318.0,2007-09-12,2007-09-27 13:32:03.927,Shell 168258 - W 11th,6085 W 11th Ave,EUGENE,97402,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,True,False,"Release was discovered through review of analytical data from a due diligence investigation.  The soil samples from SB-2 (NE corner of USTs) at this site 168258 has a detection of lead at 30.6 mg/kg (RBC of 30) which is just above the RBC for leaching to ground water.  SB-3 also has a detection of MTBE just above the MRL...Kevin McCarthy, DELTA Environmental 9/12/07.... Kevin McCarthy, Delta Environmental 9/12/07.

This facility previously had and address designation of 28851 W 11th Ave... Ginny Deck 9/27/07.",20-94-4100 Greenhill Arco,"An environmental site assessment was performed at the facility by Delta Consultants on behalf of Shell Oil in August 2007. The investigation included the installation of eight borings for the collection of soil and groundwater samples. Gasoline was not measured above laboratory detection levels in any of the soil samples. Lead was measured in soil from each boring, including a maximum concentration of 30.6 ppm at DS-SB4 from 15 to 20 feet bgs. Low levels of MTBE were detected in soil from 15 to 30 feet bgs at borings TK-SB2 and DS-SB3. No other petroleum VOCs were measured in soil from these borings. Diesel and PAHs were not identified in soil from TK-SB2. Groundwater was encountered in one borehole (TK-SB2) during the 2007 assessment at 25 feet bgs. A water sample detected MTBE at 1.95 ppb. No other VOCs or PAHs were detected in the single water sample collected at the site in 2007. In 2009, DEQ requested additional investigation of shallow soils at the pump islands and product lines. Because Shell Oil does not allow advancement of borings near active dispensers, piping or tanks, Delta instead provided a risk-based evaluation of contamination found in the 2007 assessment. The evaluation included water and land use at the site, as well as the potential exposure pathways for soil and groundwater contamination. A survey of groundwater use at the site identified an on-site water supply well serving the facility. DEQ requested that the well be tested for the presence of petroleum contamination in February 2010. Analysis for gasoline, diesel, and VOCs identified no contamination in the on-site water supply well in March 2010. Follow-up sampling was requested by DEQ and was conducted in April 2011 after station remodeling was completed. Analysis of the water samples collected from the men’s restroom sink and two interior sample points detected no petroleum above laboratory detection levels.",18174.0,10427.0,GDECK,2012-03-28 16:34:33.337
33479,36-07-1461,36,07,1461,7172.0,2007-09-14,2007-09-27 15:21:21.000,Shell 168257-McMinnville,2005 SW HIGHWAY 99W,MCMINNVILLE,97128,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Evidence of a release was noted during review of laboratory analytical data generated during due diligence subsurface investigation at the site. The reports show detections of petroleum constituents in soil and more so in groundwater that exceed the ODEQ RBCs.  The highest concentrations are in SBs #2 and 7.  Notable was a high NWTHP-Gx of 18,200 ug/L in the groundwater from SB-7...Kevin McCarthy, DELTA Environmental Consultants 9/14/07.

