(5/18/2015 CJR): corrected site location in LIT and address in LUST database from 1720 NE Hwy 99W to 2005 SW Hwy 99.","36-01-4026 Chevron McMinnville
36-96-4115 Mcminnville Chevron","The site is an active retail gasoline station (DEQ UST Facility #4964) located at 2005 SW Highway 99W in McMinnville. The underground storage tanks (USTs) currently in use are in the SW portion of the property. The facility previously had a petroleum release in 1993 when Peavey Oil Company owned and operated the service station. The Peavey Oil cleanup site (LUST #36-93-4094) was issued No Further Action in November 2001. 
The site has operated with retail fuel sales and a convenience store since the 1950’s, including Texaco and Shell branded stations. At least two tank systems have been permitted at the site. DEQ received a petroleum release notification in November 2007, submitted on behalf of Shell Oil. The report stated that gasoline and diesel contamination were found in soil and groundwater through a Phase II Environmental Site Assessment conducted by Delta Environmental. Delta advanced seven soil borings (DS-SB-1 through DS-SB-3, TK-SB-4 through TKTB-6 and TK-SB#7) to 20 feet below ground surface (bgs). Groundwater was encountered at 5 to 10 feet bgs. Soil and groundwater samples were collected from the borings for laboratory analysis. Soil samples were obtained from six of the borings above the groundwater interface at 7 feet bgs. Groundwater samples were collected from temporary wells at each of the seven borings in the 2007 assessment. None of the soil samples collected for analysis contained petroleum contaminants above laboratory detection limits. Groundwater samples from three borings at the southwest portion of the facility detected diesel, with a high concentration of 998 parts per billion (ppb). DEQ requested additional site work in 2012 correspondence based on the investigation conducted outside of the tank system footprint. The product lines and dispensers were not assessed, and these components are often the source of petroleum tank system releases. 

In December 2014, URS collected a groundwater sample from MW-5 and measured the groundwater elevation in this monitoring well west of the tank cavity. No petroleum-related contaminants (gasoline, diesel, VOCs, or PAHs) were detected in water from MW-5.",21281.0,10447.0,LLO,2016-03-25 13:41:14.430
33480,20-07-1462,20,07,1462,8137.0,2007-09-17,2007-09-27 19:45:36.060,Shell 121592 - Ivy St,825 Ivy St,JUNCTION CITY,97448,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Evidence of a release was noted during review of laboratory analytical data generated from due diligence subsurface investigation conducted at the site. These reports show detections of petroleum constituents that exceed the ODEQ RBCs in soil in SBs 1 and 4, and in groundwater in all 4 SBs.  The highest impacts are in SB-4 with notable high concentrations in groundwater of NWTHP-Gx at 62,000 ug/L and Benzene at 1,660 ug/L. ... Kevin McCarthy, Delta Environmental 9/17/07.

8/9/2012: Remedial Excavation Report from 8/2008 reports soil removal in April 2007 and USTs/product lines in April 2008. Entered Release Stopped and Cleanup Started as 4/22/2007 - C. Rodda

6/27/16 A ""No Further Action"" letter was sent on this project.","20-86-4001 Sunny J. City
ECSI # 727 Sunny Service Station - Junction City","In August 2007, a soil and groundwater investigation found contamination above screening levels and a petroleum release was reported. In April 2008, one 10,000 gallon diesel and three 10,000 gasoline undergound storage tanks were removed. Approximately 4,500 gallons of groundwater were removed from the excavation. In April and May 2008, 101.3 tons of petroleum contaminated soil was removed from around the former excavation and transported off-site for disposal at Riverbend Landfill. 

Soil gas samples were collected for four consecutive quarters at one location near the building and all detections were less than the screening levels. Soil sample results indicate the presence of contaminants in two isolated areas. The onsite monitoring well groundwater sample results are below the occupational screening levels. 

While the most recent concentrations are below the current occupational standards, contamination remains in the soil and groundwater at the site. Therefore, DEQ recommends that: 
•    If future sampling, development, construction, or excavation work is done in this area, preventative measures should be taken, such as a health and safety plan for excavation or utility workers. 
•    Appropriate management and disposal of contaminated soil and groundwater if encountered is required. The owner is responsible for the proper disposal and any associated liability costs. 
•    Any future urban residential building development should include additional testing to determine if a sub-floor vapor barrier is warranted. 
•    Groundwater use is not allowed onsite.",9156.0,10453.0,GDECK,2016-06-28 13:38:00.400
