33536,15-07-1518,15,07,1518,,2007-08-30,2007-10-08 15:41:07.067,Alba Village LLC (formerly Medite),"N Central Ave, .5 miles south of SR-238",MEDFORD,97501,541-944-4376,True,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"This is an undeveloped site which used to be a lumber mill among other things vacant for > 20+ years.  Tank size is ~ 125 gal it is an old truck fuel tank which as near as we can tell was buried full.  For all intents we are describing this as farm use the tank is at least 40 years old and the lab testing confirms aged gasoline.  In general I wouldn't classify it as a UST but it is a tank that was underground so it fit with the program parameters.... John Early, Western States Environmental Services, Inc... 8/30/07.

[C. Rodda 8/27/2009] New Cleanup site is to be initiated with Bryn Thoms as PM for solvents found in groundwater at NE portion of site during assessment of entire property.  UST issue at SW corner of property has been fully addressed and will be closed through Tanks Program.","FYI sites only
Medite, ECSI #1987
MEDCO, USTC #15-91-0047","The subject property was previously used by the Medite Corporation for lumber mill operations from 1922 to 1993.  A release of petroleum was reported to the DEQ by  in 2007 after a UST  was discovered adjacent to North Central Avenue during redevelopment and construction activities.  

One UST was found on the subject property in 2007 during utility trenching along North Central Avenue.  The tank was decommissioned through removal by Western States Environmental.  Petroleum contaminated soil was encountered at 8 feet bgs, with the north end soil sample measuring gasoline at 11,000 ppm , benzene at 2.1 ppm, and naphthalene at 9.3 ppm.  Remedial excavation was completed to 13.5 feet bgs.  Nine soil samples were collected from the excavation limits, with all but one confirmation sample measuring gasoline below laboratory detection levels.  
A pit water sample was collected and analyzed for gasoline, VOCs , and PAHs.  The water sample contained several gasoline-related contaminants.  

SMR Resources installed four borings at the site in November 2008 for the collection of groundwater samples.  Analysis identified the presence of petroleum contaminants in groundwater, including gasoline-range hydrocarbons from 20 ppb to 1,700 ppb, and benzene from 1.61 ppb to 3.3 ppb.

PBS installed six borings in January 2009.  Soil samples were obtained from 4 to 10 feet bgs in borings P1, P2, P3, and P10, and analysis did not measure gasoline-range hydrocarbons or RBDM  VOCs above the laboratory detection levels. 

Groundwater was present at 13 to 31 feet bgs in the boreholes, and three of the sample points identified contaminants, including benzene and gasoline-range hydrocarbons.",104779.0,10347.0,CRODDA,2009-08-27 11:05:57.910
33537,10-07-1519,10,07,1519,661.0,2007-10-08,2007-10-08 15:43:02.020,GLIDE AUTOMOTIVE / LIVING WATER VENTURES,20244 N UMPQUA HWY,GLIDE,97443,(541) 673-1490,False,2.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,(10/8/07 CJR)  Scheduled to pump out water from pit & resample groundwater.,,"In September 2007, EMC oversaw the decommissioning by removal of four USTs at the property, including two 4,000-gallon and two 6,000-gallon tanks.  The 6,000-gallon UST from the east side of the tank basin was removed on September 24, 2007, and soil samples (T1-N and T1-S) were collected from each end of the tank.  Two days later, the three remaining USTs were removed from the property.  Soil samples were collected from the north and south ends of each UST, providing a total of eight decommissioning samples from the single tank cavity at the site.  Each soil sample was analyzed using the NWTPH-HCID  method, and none of the samples measured concentrations of petroleum hydrocarbons above laboratory detection levels.
Five additional confirmation soil samples were collected during decommissioning activities, including one from the product lines at 2.5 feet bgs  and one sample at each of the four dispensers from 2 feet bgs.  Each sample was analyzed using the NWTPH-HCID method.  The soil samples from the dispensers did not measure petroleum above laboratory detection levels, but soil from beneath the product line (sample GA-0926-07-Piping) contained lube oil.  This soil sample was quantified using the NWTPH-Dx  method and measured lube oil at a concentration of 75.1 ppm .
Groundwater entered the UST excavation at 11 feet bgs.  A sample of the UST pit water was collected on September 26, 2007, and analyzed for gasoline- and diesel-range hydrocarbons using the NWTPH-Gx and -Dx methods.  The sample contained gasoline-range hydrocarbons at 720 ppb  and diesel-range at 773 ppb.  EMC suggested that the petroleum impacts to groundwater within the tank pit were related to fuel that spilled when the product lines were removed during decommissioning activities.

The native soils identified during the tank decommissioning consisted of very sandy, loam-like, coarse-grained soils.  Approximately 1,750 gallons of water were pumped from the tank cavity on October 17, 2007.  The groundwater was removed and disposed of by Oil Re-Refining Company from Portland, Oregon.  A second water sample was collected from the UST pit when groundwater entered the excavation.  The water sample was analyzed for the presence of petroleum hydrocarbons using the NWTPH-HCID method.  No petroleum was measured above laboratory detection levels in this follow-up water sample (GA-101707-Pit H2O) from the tank cavity.
