33911,24-07-1893,24,07,1893,3266.0,2007-11-21,2007-12-26 16:49:36.857,CP Lancaster #2705558,102 LANCASTER DR NE,SALEM,97301,(281) 293-5101,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,CAR-8/19/15-Please call Katy McQuinn (971-506-6840) when this site is picked up again.,"24-92-4006, Lancaster Exxon","(MOTT 07/18/2017) In October 2007, a due diligence assessment consisting of seven soil samples (B-1 through B-7) and six grab groundwater samples (B-1, B-2, and B-4 through B-7) was completed at the site. The highest TPH-G concentrations in groundwater were detected in B-6 (780µg/L) and B-7 (480µg/L), which are located near the dispenser islands in the western-central portion of the site. A Closure Request was submitted to DEQ in March 2014 and a No Further Action Letter was issued in July 2017. See also LUST 24-92-4006.",20175.0,0.0,MOTT,2017-07-18 09:36:17.200
33912,27-07-1894,27,07,1894,6442.0,2007-11-21,2007-12-26 20:48:46.670,Dallas Texaco,188 SW WASHINGTON ST,DALLAS,97338,(503)588-0455,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,LIT location verified with site map.,27-96-4156 Old Texaco Station,"The property was the location of a former gas station since 1950s that is currently a commercially zoned vacant lot.  The land and improvements are currently owned by Katherine Marsden.     

Petroleum contamination was initially reported to DEQ in early 1996 when petroleum was observed during a underground storage tanks system decommissioning. The property owner at that time (Truax Harris Energy, LLC) emptied and decommissioned the four underground storage tanks two in-place by filling them with sand and gravel and two by removal. In 2007 the former gas station building was removed allowing access to the two previously decommissioned in-place tanks and the discovery of three additional tanks.  Truax Harris Energy had all the tanks and an estimated 515 cubic yards of contaminated soil removed from the north-northeast corner of the property.  

After review of the soil and groundwater sample results and the historical groundwater monitoring, Truax Harris Energy conducted an investigation to determine the extent of the contamination on and to the north of the subject property.  Following the removal action, the amount of remaining soil contamination was determined to be less than 100 cubic yards total. 

Based on the results of the completed cleanup actions, the on-site monitoring and off-site investigations, and risk-assessment of the remain areas of contaminated soil and groundwater, DEQ has determined that, given the current and reasonably likely future land uses, the remaining contamination has been reduced to levels protective of human health and the environment. The proposed plan would allow any remaining contamination to naturally degrade in place.

... jlg 2/1/2011",17694.0,0.0,GDECK,2011-02-15 15:46:44.960
