34008,34-08-0078,34,08,0078,,2008-01-25,2008-01-25 10:12:21.720,Cook Property,10125 NW Helvetia Rd,HILLSBORO,97124,(503) 647-2087,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"A release of gasoline (apparently from a farm use tank) was reported to DEQ in January 2008.  No additional information was ever received.  (6/10/10 KPD).  In August 2012, evidence suggests that a release occurred from heating oil ASTs located north of the residence.  The details of the release are not clear.  The heating oil provided reportedly assessed soil in the area of the ASTs but did not report any contamination.  DEQ was not able to review any data from this sampling. 
In April 2015, 4Cs collected soil and groundwater samples in the area of the former gasoline UST.  Gasoline was detected at concentrations up to 11,200 ppm in soil and 16,300 ppb in groundwater.  Diesel was detected in groundwater at 6,340 ppb and selected VOCs.
In late April and May, 4Cs advanced borings north and west of the gasoline UST to further delineate contamination.  Gasoline and diesel were detected at relatively minor concentrations in soil.  However, diesel was detected at levels ranging from 242 ppb to 10,100,000 ppb in shallow groundwater.  Gasoline was detected in groundwater at concentrations ranging from 116 to 145,000 ppb.  Tim O'Gara concluded that contamination from the former UST and former ASTs was comingling north of the residence.  Soil gas samples collected from the residence, attached garage and shop building did not contain petroleum hydrocarbons and VOCs above soil gas RBCs.  Groundwater was encountered at 8 to 10 feet bgs.
Also in May 2015, the heating oil ASTs were removed and replaced with one AST.  Petroleum-contaminated soil (2.3 tons) in the area of the ASTs was removed to a depth of 3.5 feet bgs. Soil from the floor of the excavation contained diesel at concentrations up to 12,200 ppm and gasoline at 1,110 ppm. 
In June 2015, test pits were excavated in the area of borings B2, B10 and B13, where the highest levels of diesel in groundwater were previously detected. The goal was to remove water potentially containing product.  Significant groundwater was encountered only in B2, however, due to the onset of the dry season. 3.6 tons of contaminated soil was removed.  338 pounds of Regenox was mixed with groundwater and applied to the test pits before backfilling. 
In February 2016, borings were advanced in and near the pasture north of the Cook property to evaluate the extent of highly contaminated groundwater and product.  In addition boring B-14 was advanced at the location of boring B10 where 10,100,000 ppb of diesel was detected in 2015.  The highest concentration of diesel detected in the borings was 649 ppb and no indications of product were observed.  Soil was found to consist of plastic, stiff to very stiff clay.
The 2015 and 2016 investigations showed that soil and primarily groundwater contamination from the former gasoline UST and former heating oil ASTs has comingled  north of the residence.  Groundwater contamination (low level) extends a limted distance beyond the fence marking the property line.
Groundwater in area of site has a beneficial use.The site is supplied with water from a well located approximtely 130 feet southwest of the former ASTs.  The well in completed to 260 feet with and produces from a minimum depth of 180 feet bgs.  The well at the adjacent Hofer property is completed to 336 feet bgs and produces from a water bearing zone at 305 feet bgs.  The logs for both wells show thick clay formations in the subsurface. Both wells are topographically up-gradient and hydraulically up-gradient to cross-gradient. Sampling of the Cook well did not reveal detectable petroleum hydrocarbons. The creek 500 feet west of the site has not displayed any indications of impact (sheen, seeps, etc.).
Shallow groundwater is likely perched and only seasonally active.  It appears to be expressed in thin zones of variable depth that are laterally discontinuous.  The shallow perched GW is unlikely to be used as a source of drinking water.  Also, thick clay in the subsurface inhibits migration of impacted groundater and makes it unlikely that deeper aquifers will be impacted.  DEQ has requested that a contaminated media management plan (CMMP) be prepared.  The CMMP will help address risks from contact with groundwater containing high levels of dissolved contaminants and/or free product. 

In April 2016, a draft CMMP was submitted to DEQ.  DEQ provided comment in an email datd 4-28-16.  A revised CMMP was received on 5-17-16 and approved by DEQ. The CMMP is being provided to the adjacent proeprty owners (Jim and Roy Hofer) as a courtesy.  (JKS 5-31-16)",,"The 2015 and 2016 investigations showed that soil and primarily groundwater contamination from the former gasoline UST and former heating oil ASTs has comingled  north of the residence.  Groundwater contamination (low level) extends a limted distance beyond the fence marking the property line with the adjacent Hofer property.
Groundwater in area of site has a beneficial use.The site is supplied with water from a well located approximtely 130 feet southwest of the former ASTs.  The well in completed to 260 feet with and produces from a minimum depth of 180 feet bgs.  The well at the adjacent Hofer property is completed to 336 feet bgs and produces from a water bearing zone at 305 feet bgs.  The logs for both wells show thick clay formations in the subsurface. Both wells are topographically up-gradient and hydraulically up-gradient to cross-gradient. Sampling of the Cook well did not reveal detectable petroleum hydrocarbons. The creek 500 feet west of the site has not displayed any indications of impact (sheen, seeps, etc.).
Shallow groundwater is likely perched and only seasonally active.  It appears to be expressed in thin zones of variable depth that are laterally discontinuous.  The shallow perched GW is unlikely to be used as a source of drinking water.  Also, thick clay in the subsurface inhibits migration of impacted groundater and makes it unlikely that deeper aquifers will be impacted.  DEQ has requested that a media management plan be prepared.  This plan will help address risks from contact with groundwater containing high levels of dissolved contaminants and/or free product.  (JKS 4-12-16)",106000.0,11017.0,JSCHATZ,2016-05-31 16:11:49.670
