34215,20-08-0276,20,08,0276,1277.0,2008-03-07,2008-03-14 16:29:11.337,Former Williams Bakery,1760 E 13th Ave,EUGENE,97403,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Regulated and un-regulated tanks at the facility for gasoline, diesel, heating oil, and waste oil; removed and assessed in 2008-2009 as part of U of O Arena project - C. Rodda 3/14/2011","For information related to U of O Arena project, see also fmr University Texaco USTC #20-08-1156","The site previously operated as a bakery consisting of a production building, retail store, and maintenance shop for servicing delivery trucks. Several underground storage tanks were used for facility operations. The tanks were used to store gasoline, diesel, waste oil, and heating oil. In addition, a buried railcar tanker acted as a storage tank for the bakery. Hydraulic hoists were also present in the maintenance buildings for the bakery facility. The subject property is currently used for the University of Oregon Sports Arena Complex. Staton Companies conducted tank removal activities in 2008 with oversight by PBS Environmental. Three gasoline tanks were removed in March and two heating oil storage tanks were removed from beneath the former bakery building in August and September. In addition, the locations of two previously decommissioned tanks were excavated in December 2008 to assess potential contamination. Finally, a small gasoline tank was found along 13th Avenue and Moss Street at the northern portion of the site during construction activities. This tank was not decommissioned until April 2009. 

Samples were collected of contaminated soil from each tank location as part of decommissioning activities. Soil from the gasoline tank cavity at the southern property line identified gasoline and diesel contamination, and mostly diesel- and oil-range hydrocarbons were detected in soil at the heating oil tanks. Petroleum-contaminated soil was excavated from the tank cavity in several phases throughout 2008. Contaminated soil was also removed from the former hydraulic hoist location. Extensive petroleum contamination was found at the buried railcar used to hold heating oil. Soil samples were collected following the remedial excavation up to 15.5 feet bgs. Only one soil sample from the excavation limits contained petroleum, with sample Railcar-NE-15 containing diesel at 153 ppm and oil at 161 ppm.

Further excavation for construction of the arena complex through 2009 encompassed all areas found to have petroleum contaminated soil through previous assessments. Soil was removed up to 30 feet below ground surface across most of the former bakery property. Approximately 4,622 tons of contaminated soil was removed from the former Williams Bakery site and taken to Coffin Butte Landfill for disposal. Groundwater was pumped from the construction site excavation and the initial discharge water contained no petroleum contaminants.",10432.0,11177.0,ECLARK,2011-08-23 09:24:32.790
