34626,26-08-0687,26,08,0687,,2008-06-09,2008-06-10 07:08:00.417,"Spassov, James",2379 SW Cedar St,PORTLAND,97205,,False,2.0,,1.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"6,280 and 88 ppm.",,,107712.0,11623.0,EMCMORR,2008-10-14 10:57:00.427
34627,34-08-0688,34,08,0688,,2008-06-05,2008-06-10 13:23:27.183,Former Shell Station II,7170 SW BEAVERTON HILLSDALE HWY,PORTLAND,97225,,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"In June 2008, Hahn & Associates peformed a due diligence investigation for proposed property transaction.  Groundwater contamination detected with TPH-G at concentration up to 245,000 ppb.  Hahn reported release but did not prepare a report.  RP now represented by Point Source Solutions (PSS).  PSS refuses to release Hahn investigation data, citing that impacts likely come from off-site and other reasons.  Letter sent to RP requesting Hahn investigation data.  Deadline of April 1, 2009 passed and information was not recieved.  PSS attempted investigation to reproduce, in part, Hahn investigation results.  Groundwater not encountered. PSS has proposed to attempt investigation at same time of year as Hahn investigation. July 2009 investigation found groundwater impacts in areas of HAI borings P-4 and P-7, but at much less magnitudes.  PSS has argued that additional delineation is warranted but wells are not practicable (JKS 8-25-09).  Goldie McFadden, daughter of late Penelope Oules called on January 28, 2010 and reported that a meeting with a potential purchaser will likely occur next week.  The status of the project and August 2009 request for monitoring wells was discussed. Reiterated to her that DEQ will not stand in the way of the property sale, but additonal work is necessary to close out the release incident. (JKS 1-28-10).  No follow-up information received for site. Monitoring wells still not installed (JKS 9-28-10).  Called Goldie McFadden on October 4, 2010.  She reported that a 90-day contract with purchaser (sixth prospective buyer) was just signed.  Price of property dropped substantially so that buyer could take care of environmental issues.  Prospective purchaser is a construction company based out of Vancouver (MAJ Development Corp). Notified on February 22 by Point Source Solutions that MAJ Developmetn Corp has purchased proeprty and wishes to proceed with investigation and cleanup.  Sent intoduction letter and CRA on 2-23-11 (JKS 2-24-11). Work plan for well installation received 3-29-11.  Work plan approved with comments 4-4-11.  Work plan implemented and quarterly groundwater monitoring underway.  Four consecutive quarters of monitoring did not reproduce the detections repited by Hahn and Associates.  Public comment was initiated between July 9 and August 13 with no comments received.  NFA letter drated for internal approva (JKS 8-13-12)",LUST #34-89-0287,,142665.0,0.0,iPMO#38,2016-10-14 07:02:48.037
34628,20-08-0689,20,08,0689,12334.0,2008-06-10,2008-06-10 16:51:45.080,American Tire (former),2420 W 11th Ave,EUGENE,97402,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Some diesel in soil near UST (WATER = 0.408 ppm).  Plan is to redevelop property within next 3 years, demo site at which point they would decommission if UST is still in ground.  No heavy oil in water.  Ran 8260 on water, no VOC's detected. ....CJR 6/10/08",,"A waste oil tank was decommissioned by removal in December 2008.  Groundwater was not present in the tank excavation, completed to 8 feet bgs .  Two confirmation soil samples were collected from the limits of the excavation, and an additional soil sample was obtained from 4 feet bgs of suspected petroleum-contaminated soil at the fill port.  Petroleum as diesel and heavy oil was identified only in the shallow sample from the north sidewall.  Further analysis did not measure diesel or heavy oil present above laboratory detection levels in soil. 

An environmental assessment was performed by BB&A in May 2008 before tank decommissioning.  The assessment included the installation of eight borings for the collection of soil and groundwater samples.  Petroleum contaminants were not detected in the soil samples.  Diesel-range hydrocarbons were detected at 408 ppb in a groundwater sample obtained from boring P4.

In February 2009, groundwater samples were obtained from five additional borings advanced in the vicinity of the waste oil tank.  Petroleum contamination was found in groundwater from four of the temporary wells.  Diesel was measured at 248 ppb in a sample collected from within the UST cavity, and remaining concentrations ranged from 84.2 to 91.6 ppb.  The water samples were also analyzed for RBDM VOCs  and PAHs , all of which were measured below laboratory detection levels.",107719.0,0.0,CRODDA,2009-10-08 17:06:03.760
