34909,06-08-0970,06,08,0970,2791.0,2008-08-04,2008-08-05 15:11:35.067,Powers Ranger Station,42861 Hwy 242,POWERS,97466,541-439-6200,False,7.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"On August 1, 2008 the Rogue River-Siskiyou National Forest received test results from Anderson Environmental Contracting of Kelso, WA indicating detections of petroleum products in the soil underneath the dispensers at both the Powers Ranger Station.  The test samples were taken during underground storage tank decommissioning... Griff Berg, USFS, 8/1/08",,"The site is located at the Powers Ranger Station at 42861 Highway 242 in Coos County.  The subject property is currently used as a USFS facility, and includes maintenance, customer service, and storage buildings.
The decommissioning activities for one 2,500-gallon gasoline UST  and one 1,000-gallon diesel UST were initiated in the summer of 2008.  The USTs were permitted under DEQ Facility #2791 as BKKKH and BKKKG, respectively.
Petroleum-contaminated soil (PCS) was found beneath the two dispensers but could not be excavated due its proximity to a storage building.  
Eight soil samples (Pro-SS1 through Pro-SS8) were collected from the UST cavity on July 31, 2008 following removal of the tanks.  The soil samples were analyzed for the presence of petroleum hydrocarbons using the NWTPH-HCID  method.  Two soil samples contaminated measurable concentrations of petroleum.  
Sample Pro-SS1 detected petroleum as gasoline- and diesel-range hydrocarbons, and Pro-SS2 detected diesel.  Further analysis was completed using the NWTPH-Gx and -Dx  methods.  Gasoline was detected at 87 ppm  and diesel at 32,900 ppm in Pro-SS1, both of which exceed the most stringent Soil Matrix standards of 40 ppm and 100 ppm, respectively.  In soil sample Pro-SS2, diesel was measured at 191 ppm. 
On December 8, 2008, soil excavation was conducted adjacent to the UST cavity beneath the dispensers.  Four confirmation soil samples (also SS1 through SS4) were collected from the limits of the remedial excavation and analyzed using the NWTPH-HCID method.  The results of the soil sampling were provided to the DEQ on December 15, 2008, and indicated that no petroleum hydrocarbons remained in soil adjacent to the storage building.  
Groundwater did not enter the UST excavation during decommissioning and cleanup activities.  
AEC transported 57.31 tons of PCS to Riverbend Landfill in Yamhill County for disposal on December 22, 2008.
Screening indicated this site is eligible at the Level 2 soil matrix level, requiring concentrations of gasoline below 80 ppm and diesel below 500 ppm. ",19867.0,11906.0,CRODDA,2009-04-10 10:09:43.870
