34977,15-08-1038,15,08,1038,6172.0,2008-08-01,2008-08-15 16:08:57.783,ASTRO #250 II,2260 W MAIN,MEDFORD,97501,(503) 229-2929,False,7.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"7/28/08 - talked to Bob ( with Robert D Miller Consulting), claims its old release - I'll review old file information and make determination. CRodda

9/11/2008: additional assessment has been completed since decommissioning - pit water purged and sampled second time (ND) - C. Rodda

NFA issued 11/4/2008; Site REOPENED 12/4/2009 due to contamination discovered in other area of site; not sure if a new release will be reported or just address it through this log number.... C Rodda Site re-opened on : 12/23/2009 By Virginia Deck. Previous Final Invoice Request Date: 11/4/2008.",15-96-0051,"Three underground storage tanks (USTs) as well as the associated product lines and pumps were decommissioned by removal from the site on July 17, 2008.  The USTs consisted of one 20,000-gallon gasoline, one 10,000-gallon gasoline, and one 10,000-gallon diesel tank.  The facility previously held two different tank systems utilizing six USTs.  Initial soil samples obtained from the subject UST cavity contained concentrations of gasoline-range hydrocarbons up to 105 ppm .  Benzene was measured in this sample (#6*) at the south end of the tank cavity at 0.295 ppm.  Additional soil samples collected beneath the five dispensers in July 2008 had no detections of gasoline compounds.  Remedial excavation activities were conducted in September and October 2008 to comply with soil matrix standards.  Numerous confirmation samples were collected from the remedial excavation floor and sidewalls, and were analyzed for gasoline-range hydrocarbons and BTEX constituents.  Of the seventeen soil samples collected from the base of the excavation at 15 feet bgs, only one (#84) contained gasoline-range hydrocarbons at 7 ppm.  A buried water tank and improperly abandoned monitoring well (MW2) were found 10 feet north of the southern excavation boundary and removed as part of remedial activities.  Petroleum-contaminated soil was excavated to 21 feet bgs at the location of MW2, which appeared to be a conduit for contaminants to the subsurface.  A soil sample (#52) collected beneath the well location measured no detectable concentrations of gasoline.  Six confirmation soil samples were collected from the southern sidewall of the excavation, with the highest concentration of gasoline measured at 19.2 ppm in sample #50 from 10 feet bgs.  The remaining sidewall samples provided further evidence of effective remediation through source removal.  Soil sample #39b was obtained from the western excavation limits and detected gasoline at 6.87 ppm, while sample #84 detected gasoline at 7 ppm. At the northern sidewall, none of the six confirmation soil samples detected gasoline above laboratory detection levels.  The highest concentration of benzene remaining in soil at the site was in sample #84 at the southeast sidewall.   Benzene was measured at 0.0644 ppm, with toluene at 0.072 ppm, ethylbenzene at 0.104 ppm, and total xylenes at 0.369 ppm in soil sample #84.  3,177.81 tons of petroleum contaminated soil was excavated from the site, encompassing all UST cavities.",22133.0,0.0,CRODDA,2010-01-29 16:41:40.480
