Updated to connect it with UST Facility # 835 at 198 NW 123rd St, Newport (Beverly Beach State Park) and LUST # 21-92-4071, and all together in LIT...G Deck 9/15/08.",Might be same site as previous 1992 release  21-92-4071 Beverly Beach State Park.,,108893.0,12038.0,GDECK,2009-06-09 13:33:50.383
35095,20-08-1156,20,08,1156,582.0,2008-09-02,2008-09-08 16:09:58.920,Formerly University Texaco II,1880/1888 FRANKLIN BLVD,EUGENE,97403,(541) 346-5421,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"Site address changed from LUST #20-98-7059 (same facility).  Soil contamination and water in tank pit.  Free product on the pit water and in pockets of soil.  Staton Companies removed USTs and conducting PCS excavation.  PBS Environmental is completing sampling.  CJRodda 9/8/08

Tanks removed 9-4-2008.","See also LUST #20-98-7059 (University Texaco)

For U of O Arena project information, see also Former Williams Bakery USTC#20-08-0276","The site previously operated as a fuel service station and auto repair facility. An environmental assessment was performed in 2006 prior to site redevelopment by the University of Oregon. Previously a release of petroleum was reported for the tank facility in 1998 under operation as a Space Age station. The project (DEQ Tanks File 20-98-7059) was issued closure by DEQ in November 1998 after assessment and cleanup was conducted for contaminated soil. A new release was reported to DEQ based on the presence of petroleum-contaminated groundwater identified in the 2008 assessment. The subject property is currently used for the University of Oregon Sports Arena Complex.
Staton Companies conducted tank decommissioning activities in August and September 2008 with oversight by PBS Environmental. Three gasoline tanks (two 6,000-gallon and one 12,000-gallon) were removed and soil samples were collected from the end of each tank at 12 to 13 feet bgs. A pit dug within the tank excavation captured groundwater that was tested for petroleum contaminants.

Nineteen soil samples were collected from the gasoline tanks and associated dispenser, as well as the heating oil tank discovered in December 2008. Soil from the east end of a tank detected at 1,560 ppm. Gasoline, diesel and oil-range hydrocarbons were detected samples obtained from the tank excavation. Relatively low levels of VOCs were also present in soil surrounding the tank basin. A water sample was collected from excavation had petroleum contamination. 
Petroleum-contaminated soil was excavated from the tank cavity over several days in 2008. The remedial excavation encompassed all areas found through previous assessments to have petroleum contaminants in soil. Confirmation soil samples were obtained in September from the excavation sidewalls and some petroleum remained at the north and southeast areas of the excavation. However, as part of further cleanup and arena construction activities, this soil was removed in December 2008. Approximately 9,178 tons of contaminated soil was removed from the former University Texaco site and taken to Coffin Butte Landfill for disposal. 
Based on the depth of excavation for the construction project, no petroleum contaminated soil remains at the site. In addition, groundwater was pumped from the construction site excavation by dewatering wells. The initial discharge water was tested in March 2009 and contained no petroleum contaminants.",18324.0,0.0,ECLARK,2011-08-23 09:24:15.137
