35223,20-08-1284,20,08,1284,4449.0,2008-10-01,2008-10-02 14:44:37.697,RAMSAY SIGNS,204 JEFFERSON ST,EUGENE,97402,(503) 869-5864,False,7.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"UST decommissioned over 2 days (10/1/08 & 10/2/08); contaminated soil on north end of tank found 10/1/08; UST removed on 10/2/08 and PCS excavated approx. 0.5' below bottom of tank and into sidewalls.  CRodda Site re-opened on : 1/7/2009 By Virginia Deck. Previous Final Invoice Request Date: 12/2/2008.

FILE MOVED TO OFFSTIE STORAGE ",,"A 1,000-gallon gasoline UST  was decommissioned by removal between September 29 and October 2, 2008.  The steel tank, previously unregistered with the DEQ, was permitted under UST Facility #4449.  One dispenser pump and associated piping were also removed and assessed during decommissioning activities.  Approximately 60 gallons of a gasoline and water mixture were pumped from the UST by Oil Re-Refining Company and disposed of at its Portland facility.  

A sample from overburden (BRL01-SPOILS-3’-4’) was collected to characterize the petroleum released at the UST location.  Analysis using NWTPH-HCID method identified concentrations of gasoline-, diesel-, and heavy oil-range hydrocarbons below laboratory detection levels.  Remaining soil samples were analyzed for gasoline-range hydrocarbons based on the use of the tank for gasoline storage.

A soil sample (BRL01-DISP-1.5’) was collected beneath the product line at the dispenser, located immediately south of the UST.  The sample was analyzed for gasoline-range hydrocarbons and contained concentrations below laboratory detection levels.

Four sidewall samples and one bottom sample were collected from the UST excavation following removal of petroleum-contaminated soil (PCS).  The samples were analyzed for gasoline-range hydrocarbons, with the highest concentration measured in soil sample BRL01-WW-7’ at 43.2 ppm .  Further analysis of this sample for VOCs  was completed, with two constituents of potential concern measured above laboratory detected levels.  Naphthalene was detected at 0.265 ppm and n-propylbenzene was at 0.406 ppm in this sample from the west sidewall.

Sand was present in the tank cavity to 3 feet bgs, and was underlain by clay soils to the extent of the excavation at 7.5 feet bgs. Groundwater did not enter the UST excavation during decommissioning and cleanup activities.  Heavy rainfall occurred prior to backfilling of the UST cavity and was contained in the tank pit.  Approximately 600-gallons of water was pumped from the excavation on November 18, 2008 and disposed of at Oil Re-Refining in Portland.  Petroleum-contaminated soil was excavated from the tank cavity and stockpiled on-site.  A total of 14.72 tons of contaminated soil was disposed of at Short Mountain Landfill in Lane County on November 6, 2008.",1054.0,0.0,LFINCHE,2010-10-22 13:36:55.440
