35267,26-08-1328,26,08,1328,12333.0,2008-10-10,2008-10-13 07:43:23.883,Burnside Bridgehead,123 NE Third Ave,PORTLAND,97232,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"3580-ppm GRO;   16,300-ppm DRO.  Impacted soils removed to applicalble RBC standards.  Comment solicitation letter sent March 2009.  City of Portland requested CMMP for management of impacted soils in NE Couch Street.  CMMP for Block 76/67 property already prepared - sent to City of Portland but no comments received.  Per Mike Greenburg, agreements are in place between City of Portland and PDC.  Site conditions are protective of likely exposure scenarios.

Previous entered dates:
Final Invoice Requested:  8/5/2009
Closed:  9/28/2009
CR Agreement:  11/14/2008

This project was reopened on 11/21/2012 per Jeff  Schatz after conferring with management.
PDC notified DEQ that an unregulated UST was discovered at a property that DEQ has worked on in the past. Site re-opened on : 11/21/2012 By Gerald Gamolo. Previous Final Invoice Request Date: 8/5/2009.

In November 2012, a 2,000-gallon heating oil/fuel oil UST was discovered and decommissioned.  Following removal of the UST, indications of petroleum hydrocarbon contamination were observed in the excavation.  NRC removed 150 cubic yards of contaminated soil.  Confirmation sampling revealed the highest concentrations of diesel and heavy oil TPH near the east end of the fomer UST (combined 17,000 mg/kg).  Constituent analysis of the most impacted samples revealed ND levels of VOCs and PAHs largely below the most stringent of RBCs. Due to a building footing in the north sidewall, soil removal was terminated at a depth of 17 feet bgs. The excavation was backfilled with CDF and crushed rock.  In December 2012, two rounds of delineation investigations were performed.  Contaminatino extentds beneath the Convention Plaza building and adjacnet embankment to the east.  The extent of contamination was well delineneated south, southwest, west and northwest of the former UST.  Approximately 277 cubic yards of contamination remains at the site.  Diesel TPH and benzo(a)pyrene were detected in one location at levels exceeding RBCs for direct contact for occupational workers and construction workers, respectively.  Due to the depth of these samples and limited volume, this soil is not considered to pose a significant risk.  GW not encountered.  GW previously shown not to have a beneficial use as drinking water. (JKS 4-2-13).",,,109334.0,12252.0,JSCHATZ,2013-04-02 07:38:52.420
