35333,20-08-1394,20,08,1394,12348.0,2008-10-22,2008-10-30 10:49:12.273,Goshen Equipment,34024 Old Willamette Highway,EUGENE,97405,541-741-2025,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,3.0,False,False,False,False,False,"1/21/2009, C. Rodda: Sent RP application for SWLA for 1500 tons PCS still on-site.

6/10/2010: SWLA closed, C. Rodda","ECSI 5280: property being evaluated under Tanks/Petroleum rules as a single project, C. Rodda 6/7/2011","The site was used as Goshen Equipment for many years, but previously operated as a fuel service station. The USTs decommissioned in 2008 had not been used since the 1980’s. DEQ records show 6 USTs including 3 diesel tanks, 2 gasoline tanks, and a waste oil tank. Contaminated soil was excavated to 10 feet bgs and 26 confirmation soil samples were collected after the tanks were removed. Two of the samples had high diesel and gasoline. Borings were installed in 2009 to assess soil and groundwater. Samples showed little contamination in soil outside the tank cavity. Free product was present at a boring installed northwest of the diesel tank area. Water from the boring contained one-half inch of product. DEQ requested a monitoring well at this location (MW-1). Free product was not found in MW-1 during its initial sampling event of July 2010. The June-July 2010 investigation included 4 borings to obtain soil and groundwater samples and install 3 monitoring wells. Soil samples identified gasoline and diesel, as well as some PAH and VOC compounds. BTEX constituents were not detected in the soil. Water samples from borings contained diesel, chromium, benzo(a)pyrene, and dibenz(a,h) anthracene above drinking water standards for site workers. A well exists at the property, but is not used as a drinking water supply. A new cleanup site (ECSI #5280) was established after contaminated soil (mostly oil-related) was discovered under the lined on-site treatment cell. The area southwest of the tanks appeared to be impacted by surface spills of oil-related products. Groundwater monitoring was conducted and the site investigation was conducted by the property owner through 2013. The City of Eugene and Lane County Brownfields Coalition then entered the LUST site in an EPA grant-funded program and further assessment was completed. Because results of the investigation showed potential risk to residential receptors or a drinking water well, restrictions on land and groundwater use were recorded on the property in May 2016. Commercial use of the property is proposed.",109361.0,12298.0,CRODDA,2016-05-24 11:15:11.097
35334,24-08-1395,24,08,1395,,2008-10-01,2008-10-31 13:47:41.877,Former Shell Bulk Facility,2640-2686 Portland Rd NE,SALEM,97301,,False,1.0,,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"8/13/2012: per URS, no active cleanup but USTs were removed in 1989 - entered Release Stopped and Cleanup Started date as 12/31/1989; release not reported until PCS found during road work in 2008; there are two other LUST sites associated with the former Shell facility (Teamsters 24-08-0258 and Withnell Property 24-02-1582) - C. Rodda","24-08-0258 Teamsters Union Local 324
24-02-1582 Withnell Property","Shell Oil Company operated a bulk storage facility at the site from at least the mid 1920’s until the early 1960’s. By 1981, the site had been redeveloped with new commercial buildings including the current Teamsters office building. In 1989, the site was developed into its current configuration. In 2008 during utility work the City of Salem discovered contamination in front of the Withnell and Teamsters properties and releases were reported (24-08-1395 and 24-08-0258 respectively). The contamination at both properties was later determined to have originated from the former Shell bulk storage facility. The exact cause of the contamination is unknown due to the time since the facility was operated. Soil is impacted by gasoline, volatile organic compounds (VOCs), diesel, and polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) beneath much of the facility boundary and extends from just beneath the asphalt cover to a depth of about 25 feet. Groundwater, general found between 7 and 18 feet below ground, is also impacted across the entire site and extending beneath much of Portland Road to the northwest. The site has been investigated with 32 soil boring locations, 12 permanent monitoring wells, and four permanent soil vapor borings. Free product has also been removed from monitoring well MW-1.",109495.0,0.0,CRICHAR,2020-10-12 08:33:59.537
