35447,26-08-1508,26,08,1508,,2008-12-17,2008-12-17 08:56:47.203,"Haynes, Phyllis",4912 N Montana  Ave,PORTLAND,97217,503 287 9134,False,0.0,,0.0,,False,True,False,0.0,False,False,False,False,False,This is a seperate tank. It was determined that a secondary file would be necessary due to the fact that there are two pits and two addresses for this duplex as per my conversation with Edie in the tanks department on 12-17-08. Thank you.,,,109945.0,12455.0,EMCMORR,2008-12-17 08:56:47.203
35448,03-08-1509,03,08,1509,8733.0,2008-12-12,2008-12-18 13:23:46.517,LAWNFIELD UST,9200 SE LAWNFIELD RD,CLACKAMAS,97015,(503) 653-3086,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Release from a pump island discovered in December 2008.  Test pitting revealed TPH-D in soil detected at concentration as high as 1100 mg/kg; TPH-G as high as 420 mg/kg.  Delineation of soil and groundwater impacts not complete.  Constitutent analyses not run on samples with highest detected TPH. Work plan for additional investigation requested in letter dated 2-26-09.  Follow-up investigation performed in May 2010. Five borings (GP-1 through GP-5) advanced around the dispenser islands.  Relatively low detections of gasoline, diesel and constituents in the groundwater samples showed that contamination was limited in extent. Furthermore, groundwater contamination is not likely to extend significantly off-site.

In January 2013, DEQ was informed  that ODOT was planning to decommission the two 10,000-gallon USTs, dispensers and piping in the near future, due to the fact the site is near the footprint of the proposed Sunrise Corridor.  Although the site is listed in ECSI (139) and has a historic release incident, compliance sampling will be required per Rules during the decommissioning.  If evidence of a release is found, it seems reasonable to handle under the existing LUST incident number.  At the same time, the old release could likely be closed out if a CSM is developed (JKS 1-11-13).

In August 2013, ODOT decommissioned the former USTs and dispsenser islands, due to the fact the footprint of the proposed Sunrise Corridor highway passed over or very near these features.  Groundwater was shallow and abundant during the decommissioning work.  Tank 1 was inadvertently breached by the excavator, causing it to flood.  Attempts to evacuate groundwater from the tank were not successfull and during aggressive pumping, water with a sheen was observed to weep from the sidewalls.  UST No. 2 floated unexpectedly in teh excavation.  Based on these disturbances, pit water samples colected from the excavation following decomissioning contained extremely high detections of gasoline,diesel and constituents.  However, these detections are not considered representative of stead-state conditions, as evidenced by data collected prior to August 2013.   Soil samples collected from the sidewalls at the soil/water interface and from the piping runs revealed largely minor detections of gasoline and diesel. A release was reported (03-13-1169) but DEQ decided to handle under the existing incident.  The new incident will need to be closed administratively.

The former UST system is going to be beneath or near the proposed highway and a nearby county road.  No structures are expected to be built in the area.  Future use scenarios are limited to construction/excavation workers.  Shallow groundwater has no current or likely future beneficial use and such use is prohibited by an E&ES executed by ODOT.  Soil analytical data and groundwater analtyical data collected prior to August 2013 do not indicate contaminants at levels exceeding construction/excavation worker RBCs.  Site contamination does not pose uncaceptable health risks. (JKS 5-1-14).","LUST #03-92-0319, LUST #03-13-1169, and ECSI #139",,110491.0,0.0,LLO,2016-03-25 13:41:14.430
