35638,10-09-0166,10,09,0166,,2009-03-06,2009-03-06 16:58:41.800,Reedsport 5th St Sewer Line,5th Street between 270 Fir & 532 Fir,REEDSPORT,97467,,False,,,3.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"petroleum (gas & diesel) soil and groundwater impacts beneath 5th street are impacting the City sanitary sewer and WW treatment plant, exact source unknown but suspect adajacent USTC sites on either side of 5th street... Eric Clough 3/6/09.

This project administratively closed 7/9/2010 since the adjacent USTC projects (10-96-0021, 10-96-0022, and 10-04-0962) have been identified as the source of contamination.  The ARRA project for this USTC project has been aborted.  further investigation/cleanup will be conducted by the adjacent USTC project RPs. 

Site re-opened on : 1/9/2014 By Virginia Deck. Previous Final Invoice Request Date: 7/9/2010.

This project has again been administratively closed since Mast Brothers (10-96-0021 & 22) has agreed to act as RP and conduct further work (Esc, 26-jun-14)","10-96-0021, 10-96-0022, and 10-04-0962","The Site is located in the vicinity of the 100 block of North 5th Street in Reedsport, Oregon (Figure 1). The area is bounded by Fir Avenue (Oregon Highway 38) to the north, Winchester Avenue to the south, 4th Street to the east, and 6th Street to the west. An alleyway bisects the Site between Fir Avenue and Winchester Avenue, which includes the impacted sanitary sewer (which flows west to east). Significant petroleum hydrocarbon impacts (including explosive levels of combustible gases) have been observed in the sanitary sewer manhole located in 5th Street (identified as manhole 1-4-7).

Properties within the study area have historically included gasoline service stations that fronted the adjacent highway (Oregon Highway 38; Fir Avenue). These properties are currently vacant or used for other purposes (i.e., retail or automotive maintenance). Reportedly, the most recent operation of a gasoline
service station stopped in the early 1990s, after which gasoline odors were noticed in an adjacent storm sewer. The impacted storm sewer was historically connected to a combined sewer at manhole 1-4-7.

Subsequent modifications to the sewer system in the vicinity of the Site have included the separation of the storm sewer and the sanitary sewer, and the older storm sewer line has been disconnected from the manhole. Impacts to the new storm sewer have not been reported.

A Site Investigation is being performed at this site with ARRA funding.  The SI work plan is located in the Site Document Section of this database.

-----------

This project was administratively closed on 7/9/2010 since the adjacent USTC projects (10-96-0021, 10-96-0022, and 10-04-0962) have been identified as the source of contamination.  The ARRA project for this USTC project has been aborted.  Further investigation/cleanup will be conducted by the adjacent USTC project RPs.

------------

