35779,26-09-0307,26,09,0307,,2009-04-21,2009-04-22 15:27:55.350,Glasco Property,6615 SE 106th Ave,PORTLAND,97266,503-760-7055,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"A Site Assessment for City of Portland BES revealed a 750 gallon tank used to fuel farm equipment some 40 yrs. ago; full of gas/water.  Soil samples at north @ 68"" revealed 877 ppm gas and at south end @ 76"" revealed 90 ppm gas.  Additional soil samples collected at N. @ 80"" and S. @ 88"" are being analyzed via TPH-Gx also.  Tank is scheduled for pumping Wed. April 22, 2009 by ORRCO.  We know we will be in groundwater for that area.  Confirmed with City of Portland BES that they have purchased property and intend to demolish buildings and return area to green space.  Previous owners being allowed to live there for time being; Letter sent on 11 September 2009 commenting on CAESCO work plan; Need for additional investigation identified toward Johnson Creek (northwest) and east of UST.  Constituents were detected in soil and groundwater at levels exceeding residential vapor intrusion RBC standards.  Former UST between 75 and 100 feet from residences.  Under new vapor intrusion guidance, some evaluation is warranted. Letter to City requesting update and notifying them of vapor intrusion guidance being prepared (JKS 4-28-10).  Letter dated May 4, 2010 informed city of new requirements for assessment of vapor intrusion risks; Residents of dwellings were supposed to vacate by June 15, 2010. 

Discussed project status with Consultant in December 2010.  UST removed and removal of impacted soil completed.  Followed up with Consultant on 2-7-11 -  Informed draft report submitted to City of Portland for review and comment. (JKS 2-8-11). Checked in with consultant in November 2011 but they had not heard from City either.  Project is pretty low risk because residential dwellings have been leveled and there are no current or likely future human exposures (JKS 12-15-11).

Report documenting UST removal and subsequent soil removal received in February 2013.  Approximately 708 tons of contaminated soil were removed from the site and disposed offsite.  Following removal, gasoline was detected in one of 17 soil samples at a concentration above the most stringent RBC.  Although groundwater contamination was encountered near borings B3 and B5 in 2009, groundwater is discontinuous in occurrence and may be perched.  Also, there is evidence for rapid later attenuation between borings B5 and B3.   Johnson Creek is approximately 350 feet north of former USTs and unlikely to be impacted.  Based on lack of human receptors and removal of contaminated soil, the site does not pose any unacceptable risks (JKS 4-30-14).",,"Report documenting UST removal and subsequent soil removal received in February 2013.  Approximately 708 tons of contaminated soil were removed from the site and disposed offsite.  Following removal, gasoline was detected in one of 17 soil samples at a concentration above the most stringent RBC.  Although groundwater contamination was encountered near borings B3 and B5 in 2009, groundwater is discontinuous in occurrence and may be perched.  Also, there is evidence for rapid later attenuation between borings B5 and B3.   Johnson Creek is approximately 350 feet north of former USTs and unlikely to be impacted.  Based on lack of human receptors and removal of contaminated soil, the site does not pose any unacceptable risks (JKS 4-30-14).",111573.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2014-04-30 11:36:38.133
