36194,26-09-0722,26,09,0722,,2009-07-31,2009-08-05 07:31:13.863,"Passon, Julie",4333 NE 75th Ave,PORTLAND,97218,503-286-5194,False,2.0,,1.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,SO2 VES  ppm 1180,,,112397.0,13203.0,EMCMORR,2009-08-26 16:15:20.530
36195,26-09-0723,26,09,0723,6144.0,2009-08-04,2009-08-05 13:42:39.980,MULTNOMAH COUNTY FLEET SERVICES II,1620 SE 190TH AVE,PORTLAND,97233,503-988-3424,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,True,False,"Release at dispensers at County fueling facility in August 2009.  CAESCO on behalf of Multnomah County Capital Improvement Division removed approximately 850 tons of impacted soils in several stages of excavation.  Much of drive slab between the western and eastern banks of dispensers was removed to depths of 10 to 11 feet bgs.  Western dispensers contaminated with residual gasoline.  Eastern dispensers contaminated with significant concentrations of TPH-D (up to 9,400 mg/kg) which could not be removed from beneath dispsensers.  CAESCO submitted a work plan to advance 6 borings east, west and south of dispensers to further delineate soil impacts beyond the remedial excavation. Work Plan conditionaly approved 11-13-09 (JKS 11-13-09).  In March 2010, PBS advanced 10 borings around the perimeter of the USTs and dispenser islands to delineate soil contamination.  The highest levels of diesel contaamination in soil was found in the area of the USTs.  Low to moderate levels of oil-range hyrdrocarbons were detected on the west side of the dispensers, although the source is unclear (PBS has suggested that imported landfill materials may be the source).  Groundwater encountered in selected borings at approximately 15 feet bgs.  High levels of diesel hydrocarbons and benzene detected in boring PBS-1.  UST system decommissioned in February 2011 because County plans to convert to AST fueling system.  Diesel-range hydrocarbons detected in compliance soil samples in the area of the USTs, indicating a likely release.  In June 2011, in partial accordance with an approved work plan, PBS advanced borings around the margins of the decommissioned UST pit, with the goal of sampling shallow (possibly perched) groundwater if encountered.  Groundwater was not encountered in the borings.  Contamination in the former UST pit shown to extend to depth of 20-25 feet bgs.   Soil in the area of the northern end of the former west UST detected diesel at 4,020 mg/kg.  Delineation sample west of the former USTs detected diesl at 1,500 mg/kg.  In letter dated 9-10-11, DEQ requested limiited investigation west and northwest of sample Tank1-N.  In October 2011 email communications and follow-up meeting, PBS indicated investigation would be prohibitively expensive and requested to be allowed to calculate extent based on laterial attenuation.  Extent of contamination not fully delineated but presence of debris associated with historic landfill and cobbly/bouldery nature of soil makes further attempts problematic.  DEQ requested further information on nature of work performed in area of USTs and figure showing inferred extent of diesel contamination.  Information received 12-14-11 and found to be adequate.  Public comment period initiated on 2-6-12. Comment period ended on 3-8-12 with no comments received (JKS 3-28-12)",LUST #26-98-0271,,80.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2012-06-28 09:28:22.547
