36335,02-09-0863,02,09,0863,4438.0,2009-09-03,2009-09-08 16:23:04.657,Truax Corporation #29,1914 Main St,PHILOMATH,97370,(541) 929-3308,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,True,False,"Likely groundwater impacts... J Parr 9/1/09.
All 8 USTs removed 9/11/09.","BP Station, 02-95-4153","In 1995 a release of gasoline was reported to DEQ as the result of an Underground Storage Tank overfill.  In 2002, DEQ issued a “soil contamination only” No Further Action letter. In September of 2009, a second petroleum release was discovered during the decommissioning by removal of the UST systems. The Truax Corporation removed all the USTs and a total of approximately 1240 tons of the most heavily contaminated soil from two locations. An investigation to determine the extent of soil and groundwater contamination was completed without DEQ oversight by October 2010. A risk-assessment of the remaining area of contamination was completed.  This assessment documented that subsurface soil-gas migration into indoor air was still above DEQ occupational Risk-Based Cleanup levels.  Sampling of the outdoor air and air inside the building (Coffee business) demonstrated that residual gasoline vapor levels at the point of the Occupational receptors are within DEQ’s Risk-Based Concentrations (RBCs) for that use. Other buildings on and off the subject property were within the required risk-based concentrations.  As such, given the current and reasonably likely future use of the property, DEQ has determined that with maintenance of the existing pavement “cap” and the limitations on land and groundwater use though an institutional control, the contamination remaining at the site no longer poses an unacceptable risk.  As such, No Further Action is required at this time.",20962.0,0.0,GDECK,2017-11-28 09:16:10.337
