36347,03-09-0875,03,09,0875,7976.0,2009-09-04,2009-09-14 16:08:12.530,OAK GROVE 76,13939 SE MCLOUGHLIN BLVD,MILWAUKIE,97267,(503) 654-9570,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Release reported in September 2009 associated with removal of 4,000, 8000, and 10,000-gallon USTs.  Approximately 895 tons of petroleum contaminated soil was excavated from site.  Pit water sample indicated significant gasoline contamination.  Approximately 8000 gallons of water removed from excavation associated with installation of replacement USTs.  Treated via aeration in baker tank and discharged.  Residual soil with TPH-G, benzene, ethylbenzene, and trimethylbenzenes at levels exceeding urban residential and/or occupational worker RBC standards for vapor intrusion.  Wohlers recommended installation of monitoring well network and has proposed preparation of work plan (JKS 12-21-09).  Called Chris Wohlers on 12-30-09 and discussed comments to UST report in draft DEQ letter.  Monitoring wells MW-4 and MW-5 were installed in September 2010.  MW-5, advanced on the property north of the site, consistently showed no evidence of impact. Gasoline and constituents were detected in MW-4 (property to west) at levels exceeding groundwater ingestion RBCs.  Also, benzene levels in MW-3 exceed urban residential vapor intrusion RBCs.  As a result, a soil-gas evaluation is warranted.  Wohlers would like to consider additional monitoring to determine if concentrations drop below groundwater ingestion RBCs in MW-4.  Work plan for soil-gas investigation received 10-17-11.  Soil-gas points at western edge of property adjacent proposed.  Scope of work approved by DEQ on 10-25-11.  Wohlers attempted to implement the investigation scope of work on 11-30-11 but water was present in the soil-gas sampling point.  Sampling rescheduled for early December 2011 during dry spell.  In December 2011 and May 2012, soil-gas samples were collected from SGP-1 and SGP-2.  There were no detections exceeding soil-gas RBCs under even urban residenital scenarios. Public Comment period conducted between April 10 and May 13, 2013.  DEQ contacted by Mike Bezner, Transporation Manager with Clackamas County Transportation and Development Department by telephone.  Discussed the proposed closure, including the inferred extent of contamination in SE Courtney Avenue.  The County was encouraged to submit comments.  Comments were received on 5-14-13, one day later than the specified timeframe.  The County requested that DEQ provide a release of liability to the County and that DEQ set aside funds for performing future cleanup in SW Courtney.  DEQ responded to the County comments in letter dated 5-28-13.  Based on gasoline and benzene in groundwater at MW-3 exceeding construction/excavation worker RBCs for direct contact in excavations, the possibility of groundwater posing unacceptable risks to future workers could not be ruled out.  DEQ notified the County that it would hold off on issuing NFA until the owner had come to a settlement with the County.  The owner expressed concern about sigining an liability agreement and further closure discussions halted.
In July 2018, GeoDesign on behalf of Stein Oil advanced borings DP-1 and DP-2 on the south side of SW Courtney Avenue to evaluate groundwater conditions in the ROW.  While contamination was detected, especially in DP-2 (downgradient of MW-3), the detected concentrations were minimal and did not exceed construction/excavation worker RBCs for direct contact.  Monitoring wells MW-3 and MW-4 were also sampled.  The investigation results suggest that groundwater containing contaminants above excvaation worker RBCs is restricted to the immediate vicinity of MW-3 and does not extend appreciably into the adjacent ROW.  As a result, contamination in SW Courtney is unlikely to pose unacceptable risks to future workers. (JKS 4-15-19)",LUST #03-93-0034,"Release reported in September 2009 associated with removal of 4,000, 8000, and 10,000-gallon USTs.  Approximately 895 tons of petroleum contaminated soil was excavated from site.  Five monitoring wells installed in area of site in 2009 and 2010. High concentrations of gasoline and VOCs detected in area of MW-3 near the northwest corner of the site.  MW-5 north of site across SE Courtney Street is not impacted. Soil gas sampling in December 2011 and May 2012 did not reveal VOCs above urban residential RBCs for soil gas near the areas of greatest contamination, indicating no unacceptable vapor intrusion risk. 

One water well installed to 397 feet indentified 0.2 miles northwest of site. Use survey of nearby properties did not reveal any other wells.  Shallow GW has no beneficial use.

DEQ notified nearby property owners of proposed issuance of NFA in April 2013.  Clackamas County objected to proposal due to possible future costs of characterizaton and disposal of contamination in SE Courtney.  Because gasoline and benzene concentrations greater than direct contact RBCs could not be ruled out, DEQ agreed to hold off on issuing the NFA until Stein Oil agreed to sign an agreement. Stein Oil ultimately determined that it was not interested in pursuing an agreement with the County and all progress toward closure stalled.

In July 2018,GeoDesign advanced additional borings on the south side of SE Courtney Street to evaluate conditions in the ROW. The results showed that contamination above direct contact RBCs is largely restricted to the vicinity of MW-3 and did not extend appreciably into SE Courtney Street.  In April 2019 DEQ notified the County of its intent to proceed with issuing the NFA.",23393.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2019-04-30 13:14:52.850
