36700,34-09-1228,34,09,1228,,2009-12-02,2009-12-03 07:34:08.093,Baseline Commercial Property,1328 Baseline St,CORNELIUS,97113,,False,1.0,,,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"Site assumed to be historical retail fueling station based on building configuration and histrical use data.  However, no tanks were found during a subsequent geophysical survey, indicating that they had been previously removed. Gasoline and diesel range contamination present in soil and groundwater near the western and southern sides of the building.  

Soil and groundwater impacts, with up to 24,000 ppb gasoline in groundwater.  The site is occupied by a dog grooming business and residence.  Groundwater and soil impacts not fully delineated. There are magnetic anomolies.  Requested additional work June 28, 2010. LLC.  Danielson, owner, is looking into insurance.",,"An initial environmental site assessment was performed in November and December of 2009. 

In December 2010, a removal action was conducted to remove the hydraulic hoist and contaminated soil. 

Oregon Department of Transportation (ODOT) performed a Level II HazMat Assessment on March 2011. A beneficial use survey and an additional environmental assessment was conducted in 2011. 

A Corrective Action Plan (CAP) submitted in January 2013 proposed excavation of contaminated soils and in-situ chemical oxidation as the preferred remedial option. In July 2013, groundwater in the right of way was further delineated. 

Monitoring well installation was conducted in August of 2013. Groundwater monitoring was conducted from September 2013 through December 2016. 

An Analysis of Cleanup Alternatives, conducted in June 2015, examined: 1.) air sparging/soil vapor extraction, 2.) in-situ chemical oxidation (ISCO) with approximately 50 injection points, and 3.) ISCO with excavation and placement of oxygen releasing compound (ORC). The Analysis of Cleanup Alternatives chose ISCO with excavation and placement of ORC as the preferred remedial solution . 

The remedy of contaminated soil removal and ISCO with placement of ORC was approved by Oregon Department of Environmental Quality (DEQ) in August 2015. 

The remedy was implemented in September 2015. The remedy included removal of contaminated soils in 3 excavations, placement of Oxygen Releasing Compound (ORC) in the excavations and clean backfill. 

Quarterly groundwater monitoring continued through December 2016. A Residual Risk
Assessment report submitted in February 2017 concluded that the petroleum impacted soil removal and in-situ chemical oxidation was successful enough to support no further action regulatory closure.",114152.0,13715.0,CKENNED,2017-08-28 15:20:41.247
36701,36-09-1229,36,09,1229,,2009-12-01,2009-12-04 15:12:55.370,Key Bank Parking Lot,2nd & Davis Streets,MCMINNVILLE,97128,(503) 472-2161,True,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,,,"Petroleum contamination was discovered while the City of McMinnville was installing a utility trench under the sidewalk west of Davis Street and on the north side of 2nd Street. The discovery was reported to DEQ in November 2009. The City of McMinnville removed contaminated soil and conducted an investigation of potential sources onto the Key Bank parking lot. The former use of the Key Bank property as a bakery in the late 1920s may have included UST system for oil storage. Petroleum contamination was confirmed to have impacted soil and no groundwater was encountered during the investigation or cleanup.  Based on the results of the site investigation, the completed cleanup actions, and the follow up risk-assessment of the remain areas of contaminated soil, DEQ has determined that given the current and reasonably likely future land use, the remaining contamination has been reduced to levels protective of human health and the environment. The proposed plan would allow any remaining contamination to naturally degrade in place.",113692.0,0.0,JGLASS,2010-06-15 16:55:02.980
