36900,03-10-0114,03,10,0114,,2010-02-19,2010-02-22 07:56:15.190,"Boucher, Brian",3090 Diane Dr,LAKE OSWEGO,97034,503-730-7080,False,7.0,,1.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,,,,114414.0,13910.0,MJGILLE,2010-03-22 14:28:18.393
36901,26-10-0115,26,10,0115,,2010-02-19,2010-02-22 13:03:35.260,Umi Sushi LLC,914 NE BROADWAY ST,PORTLAND,97232,503-684-7521,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"Release reported in February 2010 when gasoline and diesel-range hydrocarbons were detected in soil near the former location of USTs.  No narrative report submitted to DEQ for the initial phase of the investigation.  DEQ notified by letter dated 9-30-10 that RP had retained services of attorney Brian Chenoweth.  Mr. Chenoweth has requested 30 days to come up to speed on project and prepare narrative report (JKS 10-15-10).  Work plan received 5-20-11, with a scope of work in general accordance with a meeting held at NWR on 3-11-11.  DEQ conditionally approved work plan 5-26-11 (JKS 5-26-11).  RPB-BER Ltd submitted a partial draft report (unsigned) lacking borings logs, signature, and other information.  RPB-BER Ltd. subsequently was released from the project.  DEQ received a report from MSBA in February 2012 documenting investigations performed by RPS-BER Ltd and MSBA.  DEQ provided comments on report in an April 2012 email.  DEQ requested soil-gas investigation in area of RPS-BER boring B4 and an additional boring to delineate the extent of contaminatiion in the northeast corner of the site.  In March 2013, MSBA advanced boring SB-5 to further delineate the extent of soil contamination near the northwest corner of the building.  This boring did not detect gasoline, diesel or heavy oil and confirms that contamination does not extend significantly offsite.  MSBA also collected a soil-gas sample (SV-B) at location of February 2010 boring B4.  Gasoline, ethylbenzene, naphthalene and 1,2,4-TMB detected in soil gas at concentrations above urban residential and occupational worke RBCs. However, June 2011 soil gas sample SV-A near the northwest corner of the building did detect gasoline or VOCs in soil gas at levels above residential RBCs.  This indicates contaminants in soil gas near February 2010 boring B4 attenuate rapidly with distance and there does not appear to be a significant vapor intrusion risk to current occupants of the building.  In the event that future development puts a building over the location of boring B4, there could be unacceptable human health risks.  RP agreed to E&ES requiring the incorpation of a vapor mitigation engineering control on future residential/commercial buildings constructed in red-dashed area shown on MSBA Revised Figure 3. In addition, the E&ES requires DEQ review and approval of future development plans.  Letter describing elements of proposed closure was mailed to adjacent property owners on 9-20-13.  Comment period closed 10-14-13 with no comments received.   E&ES signed and notarized by DEQ on 11-5-13.  Site appropriate to close (JKS 11-5-13).",,"Release reported in February 2010 when gasoline and diesel-range hydrocarbons were detected in soil near the former location of USTs.  No narrative report submitted to DEQ for the initial phase of the investigation.  DEQ notified by letter dated 9-30-10 that RP had retained services of attorney Brian Chenoweth.  Mr. Chenoweth has requested 30 days to come up to speed on project and prepare narrative report (JKS 10-15-10).  Work plan received 5-20-11, with a scope of work in general accordance with a meeting held at NWR on 3-11-11.  DEQ conditionally approved work plan 5-26-11 (JKS 5-26-11).  RPB-BER Ltd submitted a partial draft report (unsigned) lacking borings logs, signature, and other information.  RPB-BER Ltd. subsequently was released from the project.  DEQ received a report from MSBA in February 2012 documenting investigations performed by RPS-BER Ltd and MSBA.  DEQ provided comments on report in an April 2012 email.  DEQ requested soil-gas investigation in area of RPS-BER boring B4 and an additional boring to delineate the extent of contaminatiion in the northeast corner of the site.  In March 2013, MSBA advanced boring SB-5 to further delineate the extent of soil contamination near the northwest corner of the building.  This boring did not detect gasoline, diesel or heavy oil and confirms that contamination does not extend significantly offsite.  MSBA also collected a soil-gas sample (SV-B) at location of February 2010 boring B4.  Gasoline, ethylbenzene, naphthalene and 1,2,4-TMB detected in soil gas at concentrations above urban residential and occupational worke RBCs. However, June 2011 soil gas sample SV-A near the northwest corner of the building did detect gasoline or VOCs in soil gas at levels above residential RBCs.  This indicates contaminants in soil gas near February 2010 boring B4 attenuate rapidly with distance and there does not appear to be a significant vapor intrusion risk to current occupants of the building.  In the event that future development puts a building over the location of boring B4, there could be unacceptable human health risks.  RP agreed to E&ES requiring the incorpation of a vapor mitigation engineering control on future residential/commercial buildings constructed in red-dashed area shown on MSBA Revised Figure 3. In addition, the E&ES requires DEQ review and approval of future development plans.  Letter describing elements of proposed closure was mailed to adjacent property owners on 9-20-13.  Comment period close",114688.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2013-11-05 11:56:09.003
