36999,20-10-0213,20,10,0213,12381.0,2010-03-04,2010-03-17 17:38:59.183,CITY OF VENETA,24981 W BROADWAY AVE,VENETA,97487,(541) 935-2191,False,7.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,Soil Matrix site - water entered UST excavation but did not re-enter after purging (tank cavity water),,"Lane County Public Works Department encountered underground storage tanks while trenching along Veneta’s Broadway Avenue in January 2010. The tanks were used to store gasoline and diesel fuel products.

In February 2010, an assessment was completed in the vicinity of the tanks. Five push-probe borings were installed to 15 feet below ground surface. Petroleum odors were present in soil from 11 to 13 feet bgs at boring P1 immediately adjacent to the tanks and the sidewalk. Borings P4 and P5 were placed on each side of P1 to determine if contamination extended beyond the tank cavity. Other than soil from P1, no samples demonstrated field indications of petroleum contamination.

Soil samples collected from the push probe borings P1 and P3 did not indicate the presence of petroleum contamination through the hydrocarbon identification testing method.   

Groundwater entered the excavation during decommissioning activities. Contractors pumped the groundwater into the tanks that already held some fuel and water. Water did not re-enter the tank excavation.

Three confirmation samples were collected from the native soil beneath the tanks and backfill material. Soil from the bottom of the excavation contained gasoline at 6.36 ppm and xylenes at 0.12 ppm. The remaining soil samples did not have concentrations of petroleum contamination above laboratory detection levels.",114518.0,0.0,CRODDA,2010-07-29 17:19:37.647
