37548,10-10-0761,10,10,0761,7110.0,2010-07-27,2010-08-02 17:37:23.957,Wilferd Kincaid,2310 NE Stephens St,ROSEBURG,97470,(503)673-5831,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Yes, the correct spelling is Wilferd (not Wilfred)

2/12/12 deassignment letter due to lack of work onsite.

8/9/2012: in researching Release Stopped/Cleanup Started date for this site, found a receipt indicating 12,000 gallon UST removed and paid for 9/23/1990. The pump island was left in place and in 2010 the Cow Creek Tribe identified PCS on their property. The source of contamination was likely the Kincaid property and pump island. Entered stopped and cleanup date of 9/7/1990 when tank was removed and hole backfilled, though 2010 release notice indicates further work is needed - C. Rodda",,"The site was developed with a residence as early as 1920. A former owner of the property used a 12,000 gallon diesel tank to fuel equipment and logging trucks from two dispensers. Documents indicate that the tank and dispensers were removed in 1990, but it is unknown if any impacted soils were excavated at that time. In 2010 the neighboring property owner to the north, the Cow Creek Band of Umpqua Tribe of Indians, discovered diesel contamination along the property line in the vicinity of the former northern fuel dispenser. Soil contamination was found at both locations and low levels of contamination were found in groundwater present in the backfill material of the former tank pit. Sampling results indicate soil contamination remains from about 5 to 15 feet below ground in a roughly 30 foot diameter area around the former tank pit. Impacted groundwater appears limited to the more porous backfill material in the former tank pit. Soil contamination also remains around the former northern dispenser area from about five to eight feet below ground in a roughly 15 foot diameter area. Samples to the south, east, and west were non-detect for petroleum approximately thirteen feet away from the former dispenser. No samples were collected offsite to the north, beneath the adjacent property, but contamination is not expected to have migrated further than about 20 feet from the dispenser, or roughly 15 feet beneath the property to the north. The current site owner has indicated the existing residence will be torn down. The site owner and neighbor have both indicated that future use of the properties will be occupational. DEQ assumes that all properties within the area are expected to be used occupationally and remain connected to municipal water. A three week opportunity to comment was given to the neighboring property to the north which is owned by the Cow Creek Band of Umpqua Tribe of Indians, specifically the Natural Resource Director, and no comments were received. This determination applies only to the identified petroleum releases from the former tank pit and the northern dispenser areas discussed herein.",14555.0,0.0,GDECK,2018-09-11 17:58:03.733
