37757,26-10-0969,26,10,0969,11151.0,2010-09-07,2010-09-27 13:21:19.070,Mobil Station 99SPB (Former),8107 SE McLoughlin Blvd,PORTLAND,97202,503-222-7629,False,1.0,,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"6.2014:  Les Schwab attny ok with dnfa language for thsi re-opener.  nfa to manager for review/approval.  RAH

4.2014:  closure request by Cardno.  Drafted shorter nfa since a re-opener, language to les schwab-new owner to make sure they are ok with it.  (RAH).

10.16.12: Recently received building construction report.  waiting on soil gas work plan from Cardo. (RAH)

2.14.12:  soil conc. still exceed vi.  Les schwab moving forward with construction, so they are requesting exxon pay for protections since they haven't had a chance to collect soil gas data, yet.  Maul Foster submitted a proposed vapor barrier and passive. mit. on behalf of Les Scwhab.  In DEQ engineer review. (RAH)

1.2012:  soil excavation completed since Les Scwhab wanted  a cleaner closure.  Report from Cardno ERI in review. (RAH).

2.4.11:  Met w/consultant today.  looking to sample MW-10 one more time, take some additional FP measurements @ MW-12. No new known release, so not much change really since Bijan closed site.  Will do some soil gas sampling.  Consultant should provide meeting wrap-up in next week or so.  (RAH) 

Five soil borings were advanced at Kasch's Garden Center in April 2009 as part of a Phase II site assessment.  One-quarter inch of free-product was encountered on the groundwater in one of the borings, with 217,000 ppb gasoline and 312,000 ppb diesel.  The boring was on the vacant east end of the site, in the location of a former Mobil service station.  The discovery was not reported to DEQ until September 2010.  ExxonMobil seems willing to assume responsibility for the site.  (9/29/10 KPD)",LUST #26-91-0094,,16188.0,0.0,RHOOD,2014-06-19 15:57:02.553
37758,15-10-0970,15,10,0970,1274.0,2010-09-27,2010-09-27 14:12:54.003,LIL' PANTRY LLC - WHITE CITY,7600 CRATER LAKE HWY,WHITE CITY,97503,(541) 660-2170,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,True,False,,"15-91-0019 CHEVRON U.S.A., INC. - 91509","The property operated as a retail fuel station that utilized underground tanks to store gasoline, diesel, heating oil, and waste oil. A petroleum release was originally identified at the facility in 1991, with investigation and cleanup activities performed by Chevron. Five soil samples were collected at the waste oil tank cavity in 1991, and identified oil-range hydrocarbons in soil at 11,000 ppm in sample WP-1 from 3.5 feet bgs. No significant concentrations of BTEX constituents were detected in this sample. New petroleum storage tanks were installed as part of the 1991 facility upgrades, including a 1,000-gallon waste oil tank. During 2010, the property was redeveloped by Lil’ Pantry to add a car wash and remodel the station building. The retail fuel service station will continue to operate at the facility. An agreement between Lil’ Pantry and Chevron reportedly dictates that Chevron maintains responsibility for the investigation and cleanup of the 1991 petroleum release, which is not included in the waste oil tank site evaluation. The waste oil tank was decommissioned by removal in September 2010. Four soil samples were collected to assess the waste oil tank, located at the southeast corner of the station building. Soil was analyzed for total petroleum hydrocarbons, with oil-range contaminants detected in samples from the northern end and center of the tank at 4,730 ppm and 4,140 ppm, respectively. A sample collected from the bottom of the east excavation sidewall detected oil at 5,490 ppm. Several VOCs and PAHs were detected in select soil samples at relatively low concentrations. As part of decommissioning activities, M&M Services removed 57.75 tons of petroleum contaminated soil from the waste oil tank excavation. The soil was taken to Dry Creek Landfill for disposal. Some petroleum-contaminated soil remains at the former waste oil tank location.",2221.0,0.0,CRODDA,2011-05-31 15:31:26.550
