38859,26-11-0801,26,11,0801,,2011-08-10,2011-08-11 16:07:11.337,Land O' Lakes Purina Feed LLC,2705 SE 8TH AVE,PORTLAND,97202,503-659-3281,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,True,False,False,False,False,"
In May 2011, Geodesign  Inc. (GeoDesign) advanced borings  at the site to investigate recognized environmental conditions identified by Terracon Consultants, Inc. (Terracon) in an August 2010 Phase I Environmental Site Assessment.  Diesel and heavy oil were detected in soil samples collected near the former boiler room in the northwest corner of the building (DP-6) and in shallow soil near surface staining in the area of the truck and box scale (DP-4).  Diesel and heavy oil were detected at maximum concentrations of 17,000 milligrams per kilogram (mg/kg) and 3,330 mg/kg, respectively. 

In August 2011, GeoPotential performed a geophysical survey at the site to identify anomalies consistent with the presence of abandoned USTs.  Two anomalies interpreted to be USTs with the tops cut off and filled were identified in the sidewalk east of the site.  An anomaly interpreted to be a former UST excavation was also indentified in this area.  An anomaly interpreted to be a possible UST was identified near the northwest corner of the building.  Lastly, evidence of a possible UST pit and stained concrete near a floor drain were observed in the former boiler room.

In September 2011, Terracon advanced direct push borings EB-1 through EB-10 at the site to investigate geophysical anomalies and further evaluate areas of contamination identified by GeoDesign. Six borings were converted to temporary monitoring wells and four borings were converted to permanent monitoring wells (EB-2/MW-1, EB-5/MW-2, EB-4/MW-4 and EB-3/MW-4).  Based on detections of gasoline, diesel and heavy oil in soil and groundwater, Terracon confirmed evidence for historical releases in the former boiler room and in the location of former USTs in the sidewalk east of the building.  The highest concentrations diesel and heavy oil were detected in soil and groundwater samples from borings EB-1 and EB-7 near the northwest corner of the building.  However, the detected concentrations of associated volatile organic compounds (VOCs) and polynuclear aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) were very low.  Based on depth to water data from the monitoring wells, groundwater was determined to flow in a generally southwesterly direction.

In April 2012, Terracon advanced borings EB-11 through EB-14 near the location of GeoDesign boring DP-4 to further delineate the magnitude and extent of heavy oil contamination detected in shallow soil.  A soil sample collected from boring EB-11 (advanced at the location of GeoDesign boring DP-4) at 10 feet below ground surface (bgs) revealed significantly reduced concentrations of diesel (25 mg/kg) and heavy oil (400 mg/kg), while soil at a depth of 30 feet bgs did not contain detectable concentrations of gasoline, diesel, heavy oil, VOCs or PAHs.  In delineation borings EB-12 through EB-14, relatively low concentrations of heavy oil (34 mg/kg to 500 mg/kg) were detected at a depth of 2.5 feet bgs. 

Groundwater monitoring was performed at the site between September 2011 and July 2012.  With the exception of the January 2012 event, depth-to-water data indicate primarily a west-southwest to southwest flow direction.  Groundwater was encountered at depths ranging from approximately 29 to 32 feet bgs.  All monitoring wells contained detectable concentrations of dissolved diesel and low concentrations of gasoline and heavy oil.  Monitoring well MW-2, located down-gradient of possible former USTs in the northwest corner of the building, contained the highest concentrations of diesel (2,500 ppb).  All other gasoline detections in the monitoring wells were less than or equal to 2,000 ppb.  VOCs and PAHS were detected in the wells at very low concentrations, consistent with a significantly degraded release.

Minor pockets of arsenic and heavy oil-contaminated soil were shown to be limited in extent (lateral and vertical) and unlikely to pose unacceptable risks to site employees and workers though ingestion, direct contact and inhalation.

Based on screening agains applicable RBCs, low levels of gasoline, diesel and VOCs detected in groundwater do not pose unacceptable vapor intrusion risks. 

A search of WRD records revealed two water wells believed to be historically present at the adjacent Darigold property.  Darigold confirmed that a water supply at its property had been out of service for decades and was not aware of any other wells.  It is likely that the other well was decommissioned previously and documentation is missing.  The well at the Darigold facility, completed to a depth greater than 400 feet, was permanently decommissioned in February 2015.  A door-to-door survey of the site and surrounding area did not locate any other water wells. As a result, the shallowest groundwater has no current and future beneficial use as groundwater.  Based on monitoring data, the groundwater plume likely extends a limited distance onto the Darigold property, but does not contain contaminants at levels above applicable RBCs. 

A Contaminated Media Management Plan should be prepared for DEQ review and comment priod to future construction at the site. (JKS 9-22-15)","Petroleum Hydrocarbon contamination  identified in soil and groundwater in the area of historic USTs foremr truck repair, body and paint operations.  In addition, an area of stained pavement near the scales was observed.  Shallow soil in the area was found to be impacted by high levels of oil-range TPH. Monitoring wells and delineation borings were installed at the site in several phases of investigation. Site assessment and groudnwater monitoring showed that contaminants in soil and groundwater largely did not exceed RBCs for applicable pathways under likely futur occupational worker scenarios.  Shallow soil contamination in the area of the scale is limited in vertical and lateral extent and has not impacted groundwater. Groundwater impacts extent to nearby properties. No likely beneficial use of groudnwater as drinking water.

Risk-Based Closure Report received 1-16-13.  Need to review for closure (JKS 1-16-13)",,142724.0,0.0,iPMO#38,2016-10-14 07:10:29.517
