39503,26-12-0129,26,12,0129,,2012-02-10,2012-02-13 07:28:11.120,"Krohlow, Christine&Robert",7241 SE Lincoln St,PORTLAND,97215,,False,1.0,,1.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,,,,142743.0,16533.0,iPMO#38,2016-10-14 07:10:44.363
39504,22-12-0130,22,12,0130,3742.0,2012-01-26,2012-02-14 16:33:26.447,Reddaway Truck (Former),1050 JACKSON ST SE,ALBANY,97321,(503)926-4051,False,2.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,Petroleum release reported to DEQ in January 2012 following ESA for site prior to purchase by Linn County for new jail facility. ESA noted contractor removed contaminated soil in 1991 following tank removal. Diesel and oil-range hydrocarbons detected in groundwater sample collected in 12/2011. (C. Rodda 2/16/2012),,"UST Environmental conducted the tank decommissioning in 1991. DEQ generally does not accept the results provided by this company following an investigation of the company practices in the 1990’s. Reports from UST Environmental included these results: Two 2,000-gallon diesel tanks were previously used at the property. The tanks were decommissioned by removal in July 1991 and were not replaced. UST Environmental reported “all lines removed to foundation”. Approximately 60-100 cubic yards of contaminated soil was excavated from the tank cavity (top 12-inches at fill pipe) and piled on-site. The soil was spread to 24-inch thickness and treated through aeration. In 2011, contractor ETG installed four borings (B-1 to B-4) at apparent tank location and dispensers. Soil was obtained from 12 and 13 feet bgs at the ends of the tank cavity, and from 3.5-4 feet bgs at the pumps. Soil samples from B-1 to B-4 were non-detect for diesel, so no constituent analysis was performed on the samples. Analysis for gasoline-related contaminants was not completed by ETG on any samples collected in December 2011. Another boring (B-5) was installed northwest of the former tank nest but only a groundwater sample was collected that contained diesel at 400 ppb and oil-range hydrocarbons at 5,000 ppb. The water sample was further analyzed for BTEX (not detected) and PAHs, with several constituents detected below risk levels. DEQ requested further assessment to confirm levels of contamination in soil and groundwater. In March 2012, five borings (B-6 to B-10) were constructed, four of which were located near the former diesel tank system. One boring was placed on the western portion of the site where a fuel dispenser (noted as “gasol” pump) was historically located according to a 1948 Sanborn map. 
Borings were advanced to 25 feet bgs, and soil samples were collected from 1 to 1-1/2 feet above the water table. Field screening of soil showed no indications of contamination present in the borings. Groundwater was encountered at 14 to 16 feet bgs, and temporary well points were installed in each boring to collect samples. 
Laboratory analysis was completed for gasoline, diesel, VOCs, and PAHs on select samples. Soil did not contain petroleum compounds above detection levels. One groundwater sample from B-10 contained naphthalene at 4.6 ppb. 
",20509.0,0.0,CRODDA,2012-05-23 11:19:21.130
