39699,03-12-0325,03,12,0325,,2012-04-04,2012-04-04 13:56:59.223,Fannie Mae,9451 S Gribble Rd,CANBY,97013,,False,4.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,True,False,False,False,False,"In May 2012, Dana Thompson Tanks & Soil (DTTS) advanced borings near the gasoline UST.  Soil from the north end of the UST contained gasoline at 1,200 ppm and ethylbenzene at 31 ppm.  The gasoline UST was decommissioned in late May 2012.
In June 2012, DTTS advanced borings to further assess the magnitude and extent of soil and groundwater contamination.  The highest concentrations of gasoline were encountered near the former gasoline dispenser near the southwest corner of the garage.  Borings were also advanced at the location of a historical heating oil UST north of the garage.   Groundwater samples collcted from each of these areas in the immediate vicinity of the former tanks revealed high concentration of gasoline and diesel contamination. 
Following decommissioninng, DTTS removed contaminated soil from the vicinty of the former USTs.  57 tons of contaminated soil were removed and disposed at Hillsboro Landfill.

DTTS performed further confirmation/delineation sampling and soil-gas testing.  Soil gas testing did not reveal unacceptable vapor intrusion conditions.  
The site has a drinking water well and dug irrigation well.  The water well is located approximately 30 feet west of the fomer gasoline UST.   The water well was sampled in June 2012, August 2014 and February 2015.  There were no detections of petroleum hydrocarbons and associated constituents in the wells.  A BWUD confirmed that deeper groundwater in the vicinity of the site is used for drinking water.  Shallow groundwater is not used as a source of drinking water, although it is used for irrigation at the site. (JKS 5-12-15)",,,123485.0,16737.0,JSCHATZ,2015-05-12 07:43:54.070
39700,04-12-0326,04,12,0326,,2012-04-04,2012-04-04 17:35:34.123,Mercier Commercial Property,38077 Highway 26,SEASIDE,97138,,False,7.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"A February 2011 Phase I ESA identified the former presence of a gasoline/filling station as an REC.  In October 2011, an abandoned UST was discovered near the site building.  A follow-up investigation consisting of 10 direct-push borings was completed in October 2011.  Gasoline, diesel or oil-range TPH were not detected in soil samples from borings advanced around the tank pit.  Two of nine groundwater samples exhitited gasoline-range TPH and low levels of gasoline constituents.  Low levels of acetone were detected in a handful of borings. All detected concentrations were below the the most stingent of RBCs.  In February 2012, two abandoned USTs were decommissioned by removal from the site.  Compliance and pit water samples exhibited detectable levels of gasoline- and diesel-range TPH and associated VOC constituents.  In April 2012, approximately 57 tons of soil were excavated from the vicinity of the former USTs. Confirmatiion sampling did not reveal detectable levels of gasoline or diesel TPH.  In addition, a pit water sample collected following the remedial excavation work did not contain detectable levels of gasoline-range TPH or associated constituents.
Although groundwater impacts were documented, they were limited to the immediate vicinity of the  former USTs and of very low magnitude.  Closure under the Matrix rules (OAR 340-122-0355(3) seems appropriate based on several factors.  **Pending real estate transaction** (JKS 8-16-12).",,,43700.0,16739.0,iPMO#38,2016-10-14 07:10:53.023
