39890,20-12-0516,20,12,0516,,2012-05-15,2012-05-16 07:40:30.030,Acorn Park LLC,3144 W 11th Ave,EUGENE,97402,,False,2.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"Received report without certfication page and filing fee. 6/4/2012
We believe there were releases both from overfill and tank corrosion.

Site originally reported as HOT, changed to LUST Non-Regulated due to gasoline impacts as well.  4/18/2013 g deck. ",20-92-4166 U.S. Bank Property,"Prior to redevelopment, the site and adjacent properties were used for an auto repair shop, a furniture store, and other commercial activities. In May 2012, contractors began decommissioning of two underground storage tanks at the property. One gasoline tank (1,000-gallons) and one heating oil tank (675 gallons) were located in the center of the property. The gasoline tank had been filled with concrete in 1992. Each soil sample collected from the ends of the gasoline tank did not contain petroleum contaminants. Petroleum product was found below the heating oil tank upon removal. Contractors pumped five gallons of product from the dry excavation and collected a soil sample from each end of the heating oil tank. Soil from 6.5 feet below ground surface contained diesel at 29,800 parts per million and 11,610 ppm. Gasoline was also detected at 1,300 ppm and 2,000 ppm. Contractors proceeded with cleanup by removing 150 tons of petroleum-contaminated soil. The soil was transported to Coffin Butte Landfill for disposal. Six confirmation soil samples were collected from 12 feet bgs. All but one soil sample was “non-detect” for petroleum contamination. A soil sample from the south wall of the excavation measured diesel at 33 ppm. No water had entered the tank excavation that revealed native clay soil below 1.5 feet of backfill material. The following day workers found water had entered the tank pit, leaving two inches of water pooled in the excavation. Sheen was present on the water, though it was suggested to result from contaminated water migrating from the adjacent site through the permeable surface materials. The open pit was backfilled in anticipation of sampling groundwater from existing monitoring wells. Because of uncertainty surrounding the water found in the tank excavation, DEQ recommended minimal assessment to evaluate groundwater conditions. In April 2013, contractors completed the installation of two push-probe borings outside the excavation footprint to collect soil and groundwater samples. Probe 1 was advanced to 19 feet bgs and sufficient water for sample collection entered the borehole after two hours. Probe 2 was advanced to only 4 feet bgs and water did not enter this borehole. No petroleum was detected in the groundwater or soil samples collected from the borings in 2013.",16205.0,16917.0,GDECK,2013-06-18 13:53:57.593
