40156,26-12-0782,26,12,0782,,2012-07-12,2012-07-16 09:10:19.760,"Ciernak, Letitia",2526 NE 62nd Ave,PORTLAND,97213,,False,7.0,,1.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,51 ppm STE.  NTE inaccessible.,,,124489.0,17190.0,MJGILLE,2012-12-24 09:44:05.360
40157,34-12-0783,34,12,0783,,2012-07-13,2012-07-16 09:15:49.230,Peter Boscow Elementary School,452 NE 3rd Ave,HILLSBORO,97124,503-209-6893,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"Heating oil USTl tank discovered during at site.  Also, a waste oil UST was formerly located west of the site building.  The waste oil UST is believed to have been decommissioned and removed in the late 1980s. 

In July 2012, during excavation related to paving, a heating oil UST was disocovered and decommissioned by removal.  Compliance sampling revaled that a release had occurred, with diesel detected at a concentration of 1,460 mg/kg.  PBS subsequently removed 26 tons of contaminated soil and collected confirmation samples.  No petroleum hydrocarbons were detected in the confirmation samples. 
In addition, PBS removed contaminated soil from the vicinity of the former waste oil UST.  Gasoline and diesel were detected in soil beneath the former UST.  PBS excavated approximately 24 tons of contaminated soil in this location.  In addiition, PBS advanced borings and excavated test pits to delineate the extent of contamination.  The investigations showed that soil and groundwater contamination was restricted to the viciniity of the former USTs.
PBS attempted to collect soil-gas samples near the buidling to evaluate vapor intrusion risks.  Low permeability silts prevented the recovery of samples.  PBS then collected a soil-gas sample from backfill materials for a sewer lateral near the former UST.  The soil-gas sample did not contain volatile organic compounds at levels posing vapor intrusion risks to occupational workers. (JKS 7-25-14)",,"In July 2012, during excavation related to paving, a heating oil UST was disocovered and decommissioned by removal.  Compliance sampling revaled that a release had occurred, with diesel detected at a concentration of 1,460 mg/kg.  PBS subsequently removed 26 tons of contaminated soil and collected confirmation samples.  No petroleum hydrocarbons were detected in the confirmation samples. 
In addition, PBS removed contaminated soil from the vicinity of the former waste oil UST.  Gasoline and diesel were detected in soil beneath the former UST.  PBS excavated approximately 24 tons of contaminated soil in this location.  In addiition, PBS advanced borings and excavated test pits to delineate the extent of contamination.  The investigations showed that soil and groundwater contamination was restricted to the viciniity of the former USTs.
PBS attempted to collect soil-gas samples near the buidling to evaluate vapor intrusion risks.  Low permeability silts prevented the recovery of samples.  PBS then collected a soil-gas sample from backfill materials for a sewer lateral near the former UST.  The soil-gas sample did not contain volatile organic compounds at levels posing vapor intrusion risks to occupational workers. (JKS 7-25-14)",61099.0,17191.0,JSCHATZ,2014-07-25 07:22:47.590
