40283,34-12-0909,34,12,0909,10637.0,2012-08-02,2012-08-07 15:18:35.597,LAURELWOOD ACADEMY INC,38855 SW Laurelwood Rd,GASTON,97119,(503) 985-7912,False,,,3.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,,"ECSI #1839, LUST #34-91-0011, and LUST #34-12-0922",,142786.0,0.0,iPMO#38,2016-10-14 07:12:38.287
40284,26-12-0910,26,12,0910,,2012-08-02,2012-08-07 15:50:47.590,Trashco Inc.,1630 SE 8TH AVE,PORTLAND,97214,503-232-4084,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Historic LUST incident for site (26-90-0186).  In 1967, a 10,000-gallon UST was installed at the site.  The UST was reportedly removed in 1984.  In 1990, the vicinity of the former UST was investigated and found to be contaminated.  A No Further Action letter was issued in May 1992.  However, the former pump island was not assessed due to limited access caused by the canopy. The NFA letter indicated that further assessment was required in the event that the site use was to change significantly.

In July 2012, Evergreen Environmental Management LLC performed assessment in the area of the previously unassessed former pump island.  Six direct push borings were advanced in the area of the former pump island.  Gasoline and diesel were detected at maximum concentrations of 31.2 mg/kg and 29.9 mg/kg in the soil samples.  Groundwater was encountered at depths of 18-19 feet bgs.  Low levels of soil contamination extended as far as 22 feet bgs. Groundwater samples collected from the borings contained gasoline and diesel at maximum concentrations of 386 ppb and 372 ppb, levels not exceeding the occupational worker RBCs for ingestion in groundwater.   Only napthalene was detcted in one groundwater sample (GP-6) at a concentrations exceeding the occupational worker RBC for groundwater ingestion. The groundwater samples wilth detections were on the up-gradient side of the fomrer pump island. Based on the low levels detected, contamination is not expected to extend off-site.
Groundwater in area of site has no current use as drinking water.  No unacceptable risk conditions from the remaining pump island contamination. (JKS 4-10-17).",,"Historic LUST incident for site (26-90-0186).  In 1967, a 10,000-gallon UST was installed at the site.  The UST was reportedly removed in 1984.  In 1990, the vicinity of the former UST was investigated and found to be contaminated.  A No Further Action letter was issued in May 1992.  However, the former pump island was not assessed due to limited access caused by the canopy. The NFA letter indicated that further assessment was required in the event that the site use was to change significantly.

In July 2012, Evergreen Environmental Management LLC performed assessment in the area of the previously unassessed former pump island.  Six direct push borings were advanced in the area of the former pump island.  Gasoline and diesel were detected at maximum concentrations of 31.2 mg/kg and 29.9 mg/kg in the soil samples.  Groundwater was encountered at depths of 18-19 feet bgs.  Low levels of soil contamination extended as far as 22 feet bgs. Groundwater samples collected from the borings contained gasoline and diesel at maximum concentrations of 386 ppb and 372 ppb, levels not exceeding the occupational worker RBCs for ingestion in groundwater.   Only napthalene was detcted in one groundwater sample (GP-6) at a concentrations exceeding the occupational worker RBC for groundwater ingestion. The groundwater samples wilth detections were on the up-gradient side of the fomrer pump island. Based on the low levels detected, contamination is not expected to extend off-site.
Groundwater in area of site has no current use as drinking water.  No unacceptable risk conditions from the remaining pump island contamination. (JKS 4-10-17).",54801.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2017-04-10 12:54:14.020
