40906,26-12-1532,26,12,1532,,2012-12-11,2012-12-12 11:42:09.890,"Snider, John",7607 SE Henderson St,PORTLAND,97206,503-260-1461,False,7.0,,1.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,,,,126112.0,17953.0,MJGILLE,2013-05-28 10:44:01.613
40907,36-12-1533,36,12,1533,8945.0,2012-12-09,2012-12-13 14:01:32.440,Sheldon Oil Willamina,692 NE Main St,WILLAMINA,97396,503-842-2172,False,7.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,True,False,"Two 4000-gallon UST's were pulled from the ground. Contaminated soil was hauled under permit to River Bend landfill. The tank pit had 27"" of water after the tanks were pulled. The pit water was pumped into a empty tank. Water and soil samples were collected. Soil and Water samples were all below RBC's... Scott Michaels (4C's) 12/9/2012",36-97-4050 Bryants Service Center,,24071.0,17943.0,NSAWKA,2013-07-03 10:34:13.087
40908,15-12-1534,15,12,1534,1619.0,2012-12-12,2012-12-13 15:44:28.170,LITHIA LEASING INC,401 E 4TH ST,MEDFORD,97501,(541) 776-6401,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,True,False,"source of Gx and Dx and VOCs vound in soil and water is suggested to be from an historic and/or off-site source, highest remaining Gx in soil afterremdial excavation is 5,780, highest Dx is 440 (Esc 19-feb-13)",,"DEQ records include two registered underground storage tanks at this property in Medford. One 8,000-gallon gasoline tank (permit #BAJGE) was removed in 2012. During remedial excavation, the location of previously-removed tank was identified on the property’s southeast edge near Apple Street. This is possibly the former 520-gallon tank (permit #GGBA) of unknown contents reportedly removed in 1988. This tank is included in DEQ records, though a Phase 1 environmental site assessment (2007) reported a 250-gallon tank was once located on-site and removed in 1993. Based on its capacity and location next to the building, the smaller tank may have been used to store waste oil, heating oil, or possibly mineral spirits when a paint store operated at the site. Katalyst completed site assessment related to the decommissioned gasoline tank. Soil samples were collected after the tank was removed and petroleum-contaminated soil was excavated as part of remedial actions. Results of confirmation sampling are shown in Figure 4. Observation wells at the gasoline tank cavity were sampled in November 2012 prior to decommissioning activities. Groundwater from OW-1 and OW-2 did not contain gasoline or diesel-related contamination. Pit water samples were collected following removal of the gasoline tank in December 2013. One sample (PW-2) detected gasoline at 700 parts per billion (ppb) and diesel at 1,260 ppb. Metals were also detected in PW-2, as was MTBE. Further assessment was completed in 2016 to delineate contamination. Four borings were installed to collect soil and groundwater samples. Gasoline was detected in soil but at concentrations below risk levels for likely receptors at the site.",19057.0,0.0,CRODDA,2016-12-08 10:41:33.937
