41560,34-13-0600,34,13,0600,,2013-05-16,2013-05-16 16:16:16.280,Slaughter,15055 SW 100th Avenue,TIGARD,97224,,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"Please note there is a leaking residential gasoline tank and a leaking residential heating oil tank at this property.

In May 2013 sampling near the ends of the HOT and former gasoline UST revealed elevated concentrations of gasoline- and diesel-range TPH in soil. The USTs were decommissioned and removed in June 2013. Approximately 10.8 tons of contaminated soil was removed from the vicinity of the gasoline UST. Confirmation sampling revealed high levels of gasoline, up to 51,200 ppm.  Groundwater was not encountered to a maximum exploration depth of 24 feet bgs. A sub-slab sample collected from the basement of the single-family residence contained gaosline, benzene and ethylbenzene above RBCs.  

The property owner decided to investigate having a vapor mitigation measure installed but then abruptly stopped work.
In March and August 2019, additional sub-slab soil gas samples were collected from the residence. This time, there were no detections above residential RBCs. 

A search of WRD records and door-to-door survey revealed only a deep irrigation well at the adjacent property to the west.  Based on the depth of soil contamination relative to depth to water at the site, groundwater impacts are unlikely and groundwater is not being used for drinking water in the area of the site. 

High levels of gasoine and diesel TPH remain in soil down to approximately 20 feet bgs.  There is a low probability of site redevelopment in the future.  Despite high levels of petroleum contamination in site soil, soil gas data do not indicate unacceptable vapor intrusion risks to residents. (JKS 12-9-19).",,Site,128002.0,18626.0,JSCHATZ,2019-12-09 15:43:26.953
