41890,20-13-0930,20,13,0930,,2013-07-16,2013-07-17 16:38:01.073,TAX LOT 11200,926 E MAIN ST,COTTAGE GROVE,97424,,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,True,False,False,False,False,"Site address originally reported as NE CORNER OF E MAIN ST AND HWY 99.  Updated to 926 East Main Street, Cottage Grove. ... 5/7/2018 gdeck


OLD UST (SUSPECTED GASOLINE) WAS DISCOVERED DURING RIGHT-OF-WAY REALINGMENT AND INSTALLATION OF STORMWATER UTILITY.  THERE IS UNCERTAINTY AS TO WHO THE PROPERTY OWNER IS.  THIS INFORMATION MAY BE UPDATED OR CHANGED SOON.... R. Boese 7/16/2013.

WRD querry done on well records in the area, the presence or use of the wells in the area have not been confirmed...E.Kelley 8/19/2013.

Former property owners Marlene Swetland/Marilyn Stock )aka SwetlandStock, LLC assumed ownership after Herb Hill vacanted due to ill health.  Sent new CRA mar.swetland@gmail.com  rhr 9-13-2016",,"The site historically operated as a service station until the mid-1980s when the property became a paved lot. Contamination is the result of former site use as an automotive service station. Additionally, the underground storage tank discovered under what is now Highway 99 apparently had a fuel release. Gasoline and diesel have contaminated soil and groundwater under most of the site. The contamination extends offsite to the northwest. In 2013, an Oregon Department of Transportation contractor removed the discovered tank, excavated, and disposed of about 235 cubic yards of contaminated soil.They also pumped a total of 12,400 gallons of impacted water from the excavation and recycled the water at Oil Re-Refining Company. Contaminated soil and groundwater remain beneath most of the site, under Highway 99 and beneath neighboring properties. The groundwater contamination plume is stable and should shrink over time since a large amount of contaminated source area soils were excavated. A beneficial water use determination, including a door to door well survey, was performed and found no beneficial use of groundwater in the area of contamination. The site and surrounding properties are expected to remain commercial businesses that are all connected to municipal water. Based on these land and water use assumptions the remaining contamination is below DEQ screening levels and does not present a risk to human health. Potentially impacted parties were given an opportunity to comment on the proposed closure.  Only ODOT responded that leaving the remaining contamination in place is acceptable.",129078.0,18954.0,CRICHAR,2018-05-10 14:09:05.490
