42045,34-13-1085,34,13,1085,1046.0,2013-08-15,2013-08-15 10:48:40.967,RS Petroleum,12075 SW Canyon Rd,BEAVERTON,97005,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"PCS encountered during excavation associated with installation of utility vault and trenches during construction of a convenience store in the southwest corner of the site.  Previous UST releases at site were closed in October 1989 (34-88-0106) and July 2006 (34-90-0033).  Also, a due diligence investigation in July 2007 detected contamination determined to be historical in nature and 34-07-1500 was administratively closed.

Investigations in 2013 and 2015 revealed contamination in the area of a utlity vault and shallow utility trenches in the southern portion of the site.  Soil in the floor and east sidewall of the excavation contained gasoline at concentrations as high as 2000 mg/kg and elevated concentrations of benzene (7.4 mg/kg), ethylbenzene (34 mg/kg) and naphthalene (13 mg/kg).   Approximately 128 tons of PCS was removed and disposed off-site at Hillsboro Landfill.  Low levels of gasoline and VOCs were detected in groundwater from boring DP-1, advanced near historical boring B-6.  Based on low concentrations of VOCs detected in groundwater from DP-1, this contamination is considered to be historical in nature.   

Due to residual soil contamination in the utility vault exavation, soil gas samples were collected from the area of the vault and utlity trenches.  No VOCs were detected at concentrations above urban residential RBCs in soil gas.  Soil contamination therefore does not pose unacceptable vapor intrusion risks under the most stringent of use scenarios.   Groundwater has no current or future beneficial use.  Contamination does not extend appreciably beneath the right-of-way south of the site. Site qualifies for NFA status. (JKS 6-10-15).","LUST #34-88-0106, LUST #34-90-0033, LUST #34-07-1500","PCS encountered during excavation associated with installation of utility vault and trenches during construction of a convenience store in the southwest corner of the site.  Previous UST releases at site were closed in October 1989 (34-88-0106) and July 2006 (34-90-0033).  Also, a due diligence investigation in July 2007 detected contamination determined to be historical in nature and 34-07-1500 was administratively closed.

Investigations in 2013 and 2015 revealed contamination in the area of a utlity vault and shallow utility trenches in the southern portion of the site.  Soil in the floor and east sidewall of the excavation contained gasoline at concentrations as high as 2000 mg/kg and elevated concentrations of benzene (7.4 mg/kg), ethylbenzene (34 mg/kg) and naphthalene (13 mg/kg).   Approximately 128 tons of PCS was removed and disposed off-site at Hillsboro Landfill.  Low levels of gasoline and VOCs were detected in groundwater from boring DP-1, advanced near historical boring B-6.  Based on low concentrations of VOCs detected in groundwater from DP-1, this contamination is considered to be historical in nature.   

Due to residual soil contamination in the utility vault exavation, soil gas samples were collected from the area of the vault and utlity trenches.  No VOCs were detected at concentrations above urban residential RBCs in soil gas.  Soil contamination therefore does not pose unacceptable vapor intrusion risks under the most stringent of use scenarios.   Groundwater has no current or future beneficial use.  Contamination does not extend appreciably beneath the right-of-way south of the site. Site qualifies for NFA status. (JKS 6-10-15).",92066.0,19121.0,JSCHATZ,2015-06-10 10:45:12.580
