42743,26-14-0089,26,14,0089,1113.0,2014-01-30,2014-01-30 15:18:53.467,CHEVRON USA INC - 94432,9025 SW BARBUR BLVD,PORTLAND,97219,(925) 785-2563,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"In January 2014, during station upgrades, contaminated soil was observed beneath the northwest dispenser island.  Analysis of a sample from beneath the dispenser revealed gasoline at a concentration of 360 mg/kg.  Universal Aplicators removed approximately 2 c.y. of contaminated soil from and temporarily stockpiled it at the site. Later the same month, Leidos performed additional screening of soil from the excavation.  Based on the results, Leidos removed additional contaminated soil from the excavation.  Confirmation sampling revealed gasoline at concentrations ranging from 7 to 310 mg/kg, exceeding only the occupational worker RBC for leaching to groundwater.  Groundwater was not encountered during the removal actions.  Soil contamination is limited to the vicinty of the northwest dispenser island.  Beneficial use evaluations performed in conjunction with historical releases revealed that shallow groundwater does not have a beneficial use as drinking water.  Based on likely future commercial use, residual soil contamination does not pose unacceptable risks to current and likely future employees (JKS 9-25-14).","LUST #26-89-0038, #26-93-0164, & #26-98-0496","In January 2014, during station upgrades, contaminated soil was observed beneath the northwest dispenser island.  Analysis of a sample from beneath the dispenser revealed gasoline at a concentration of 360 mg/kg.  Universal Aplicators removed approximately 2 c.y. of contaminated soil from and temporarily stockpiled it at the site. Later the same month, Leidos performed additional screening of soil from the excavation.  Based on the results, Leidos removed additional contaminated soil from the excavation.  Confirmation sampling revealed gasoline at concentrations ranging from 7 to 310 mg/kg, exceeding only the occupational worker RBC for leaching to groundwater.  Groundwater was not encountered during the removal actions.  Soil contamination is limited to the vicinty of the northwest dispenser island.  Beneficial use evaluations performed in conjunction with historical releases revealed that shallow groundwater does not have a beneficial use as drinking water.  Based on likely future commercial use, residual soil contamination does not pose unacceptable risks to current and likely future employees (JKS 9-25-14).",1953.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2014-09-25 10:29:14.460
