42857,24-14-0203,24,14,0203,,2014-03-05,2014-03-05 13:10:27.650,Mallorie's Dairy II,10985 Hazelgreen Rd NE,SILVERTON,97381,(503) 949-4552,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"Farm Tank.

10/5/18 entered FIR date; requested estimated invoice.",,Site is part of a 623 acre former dairy.  Petroleum contamination was found in soil and groundwater beneath the site during initial investigations in 2014.  Soil cleanup and insitu groundwater cleanup completed in 2016 followed by quarterly groundwater monitoring. Conditional site closure proposed in 2015.  No public comments received. Conditions include a deed restriction on residential property use in the shop area and groundwater wells in and downgradient of the contamination area. See DEQ 9/14/2018 staff memorandum in WebDocs for details.,131090.0,0.0,GDECK,2018-11-07 15:35:16.000
42858,34-14-0204,34,14,0204,10333.0,2014-03-06,2014-03-06 16:16:38.467,CHEVRON USA INC #200496,11015 SW CANYON RD,BEAVERTON,97005,(925) 785-2563,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"In February 2014, station upgrades were performed at the site.  As part of the upgrades, sumps and piping were removed and piping was relocated to the opposite side of the dispenser islands.  Gasoline, diesel and heavy oil was detected In a trench for new piping on the west side of the west pump islands.  Gasoline was detected at a concentration of 5,100 mg/kg in one sample.  Approximately 39.5 tons of contaminated soil was removed and disposed at Hillsboro Landfill. 
In September 2015,Leidos collected a soil gas sample (SVP-1) near the station building to evaluate current and future vapor intrusion risks.  There were no detections excceeding urban residential RBCs for vapor intrusion. Groundwater was not encountered at any time during the investigations.  Shallow groundwater does not have a current or future beneficial use.  Although the site is likely to remain developed for commercial use, current zoning does allow urban residential property use.
Because the soil-gas sample showed that vapor intrusion risks were not unacceptable and the contamination pocket is extremely limited in extent, it was not necessary to open a comment period (JKS 1-20-16).",LUST #34-92-0081 & #34-03-1943,"In February 2014, station upgrades were performed at the site.  As part of the upgrades, sumps and piping were removed and piping was relocated to the opposite side of the dispenser islands.  Gasoline, diesel and heavy oil was detected In a trench for new piping on the west side of the west pump islands.  Gasoline was detected at a concentration of 5,100 mg/kg in one sample.  Approximately 39.5 tons of contaminated soil was removed and disposed at Hillsboro Landfill. 
In September 2015,Leidos collected a soil gas sample (SVP-1) near the station building to evaluate current and future vapor intrusion risks.  There were no detections excceeding urban residential RBCs for vapor intrusion. Groundwater was not encountered at any time during the investigations.  Shallow groundwater does not have a current or future beneficial use.  Although the site is likely to remain developed for commercial use, current zoning does allow urban residential property use.
Because the soil-gas sample showed that vapor intrusion risks were not unacceptable and the contamination pocket is extremely limited in extent, it was not necessary to open a comment period (JKS 1-20-16).",2058.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2016-01-20 08:39:07.250
