42939,26-14-0284,26,14,0284,,2014-03-19,2014-03-19 10:24:33.273,"Kao, Teresa",9037 SW 35th Ave,PORTLAND,97219,,False,1.0,,1.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"1,600 ppm DX",,,131241.0,20018.0,IGAFFNE,2014-07-24 10:54:53.940
42940,03-14-0285,03,14,0285,6349.0,2014-03-12,2014-03-19 12:48:06.933,Burke's Texaco (Former),486 E MAIN ST,ESTACADA,97023,(503) 829-2122,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"In October 2013, Cornerstone Transport spilled 150 gallons of gasoline at the site. Emergency reponse actions prevented gasoline from entering a storm sewer in Main Street west of the site.  The gasoline flow path coincided with an area of stained asphalt.
December 2013 hand auger borings revealed shallow gasoline contamination at borings HA-1 and HA-2 (702 mg/kg and 3,480 mg/kg, respectively).  Follow-up borings in the inferred flow path of the spill did not reveal evidence of a significant release to the subsurface.  In addition, follow-up sampling at the location of HA-2 showed that the pocket of gasoline contamination was limited in vertical and lateral extent. Soil impacts associated with the gasoline spill were not found to extend deeper than about 2 feet bgs.  Evidence deeper contamination observed in boring B-7 is believed to be historical contaminatino from a 1989 release discovered during decommissioning of a previous UST system.  The fact that shallow samples from B-7 did not detected petroleum hydrocarbons supports this conclusion.  The pockets of gasoline contamination exceeding RBCs for the Soil Vapor Intrusion into Building pathway are very limited in volume and not considred to pose an unacceptable human health risk (JKS 10-13-14).",LUST #03-89-0094 & #03-94-0114,"In October 2013, Cornerstone Transport spilled 150 gallons of gasoline at the site. Emergency reponse actions prevented gasoline from entering a storm sewer in Main Street west of the site.  The gasoline flow path coincided with an area of stained asphalt.
December 2013 hand auger borings revealed shallow gasoline contamination at borings HA-1 and HA-2 (702 mg/kg and 3,480 mg/kg, respectively).  Follow-up borings in the inferred flow path of the spill did not reveal evidence of a significant release to the subsurface.  In addition, follow-up sampling at the location of HA-2 showed that the pocket of gasoline contamination was limited in vertical and lateral extent. Soil impacts associated with the gasoline spill were not found to extend deeper than about 2 feet bgs.  Evidence deeper contamination observed in boring B-7 is believed to be historical contaminatino from a 1989 release discovered during decommissioning of a previous UST system.  The fact that shallow samples from B-7 did not detected petroleum hydrocarbons supports this conclusion.  The pockets of gasoline contamination exceeding RBCs for the Soil Vapor Intrusion into Building pathway are very limited in volume and not considred to pose an unacceptable human health risk (JKS 10-13-14).",22248.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2014-10-13 10:36:23.087
