In February 2014, Tim O'Gara advanced borings in the vicinity of the Premium UST, and also an asphalt patch marking the locatino of a previous line break and repair.  Proximity to the turbine sump in some locations were limited by possible subsurface utilities.  Soil and groundwater samples contained non-dected to very low concentrations of gaosline and VOCs.  These results did not suggest evidence for a significant release having occurred.  Based on the very low concentrations of VOCs, the minor groundwater contamination likely reflects historical releases. 

In April 2014, the.area east of and beneath Dispenser 10 was excavated to effect repairs to leaky piping entry boots.  Following the repair and soil removal, samples were collected at the limits.  Gasoline was detected at concentrations as high as 19,600 mg/kg (west sidewall). Following the repairs, the excavation was backfilled with contaminated overburden. 

In May 2014, DEQ directed the owner/operator to re-excavate the backfilled soil.  Following removal, confirmation samples revealed gasoline at a maximum concentration of 2,390 mg/kg at 40 inches.  In June 2014, a deeper sample (55 inches bgs) was collected from the location of the west sidewall sample. Gasoline was detected at a much reduced concentration of 418 mg/kg.  Lateral delineation samples south and west of Dispenser 10 did not detect gasoline.

Benz(a)anthracene and benzo(a)pyrene were detected in one sample beneath the dispenser at concentrations exceeding residential RBCs for direct contact.  Site is in highly commercial corridor and residential use is not considered likely.  Groundwater contamination not considered related to this release. (JKS 8-14-14). ",LUST #34-89-0234 & #34-07-1079,,2055.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2014-08-14 10:45:20.417
