43223,24-14-0568,24,14,0568,12468.0,2014-05-07,2014-05-12 16:18:17.270,GEM Equipment,1010 N Main St,MOUNT ANGEL,97362,503-982-9902,False,7.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,True,False,False,False,False,"Two previously unregistered USTs (~500 gal) discovered during SA, to be registered before decommissionig by removal in May 2014.",,,131391.0,0.0,GDECK,2014-07-10 10:07:34.087
43224,26-14-0569,26,14,0569,,2014-05-12,2014-05-13 13:23:02.690,Precision Motor Car,132 NE Grand Avenue,PORTLAND,97232,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"The site was historically occupied by a gasoline station in the northwest corner but it was taken out of service by sometime in the mid-1940s to make way for construction of the current site building in 1950.  It is believed the USTs were removed at that time. Following demolition of the station, the site was utilized for automobile sales and servicing operations.  Following construction of the building, a 1,000 gallon UST was installed near the southwest corner of the shop.

In 2012, EIS decommissioned and removed the UST.  Analysis of samples from the excavation reportedly revealed non-detect levels of diesel.  However, DEQ was not provided with complete documentation for the EIS assessment.

In April 2014, a geophysical survey at the site did not not reveal metallic anomalies consistent with the presence of historical tanks.

In May 2014, Alpha Environmental Services (Alpha) advanced borings in the NW corner of the site to determine if the historical USTs had leaked.  Gasoline was detected at a maximum concentration of 3,600 mg/kg at a depth of 7.5 feet.  Further analysis of this sample revealed very low levels of associated diesel, volatile organic compounds and PAHs, consistent with a significantly degraded release. The investigation showed that contamination is present in a north-south oriented ellipsoid that extends into NE Grand Avenue and NE Davis Streets west and north of the site, respectively.  Gasoline was not detected at concentrations exceeding construction/excavation workers for direct contact.  Groundwater was not encountered during the investigation to a depth of 20 feet bgs.  Groundwater estimated to be at a depth of 47 feet bgs. Groundwater in the vicintiy of the site does not have a beneficial use as drinking water. 

Based on the possibility of possible future urban residential use, Alpha collected soil-gas data from the site in November 2014.  There were no detections above even residential RBCs, indicating no unacceptable vapor intrusion risks under any use scenario.  

Contamination is degraded and low level.  No use restrictions are necessary.  (JKS 1-7-15).",,"The site was historically occupied by a gasoline station in the northwest corner but it was taken out of service by sometime in the mid-1940s to make way for construction of the current site building in 1950.  It is believed the USTs were removed at that time. Following demolition of the station, the site was utilized for automobile sales and servicing operations.  Following construction of the building, a 1,000 gallon UST was installed near the southwest corner of the shop.

In 2012, EIS decommissioned and removed the UST.  Analysis of samples from the excavation reportedly revealed non-detect levels of diesel.  However, DEQ was not provided with complete documentation for the EIS assessment.

In April 2014, a geophysical survey at the site did not not reveal metallic anomalies consistent with the presence of historical tanks.

In May 2014, Alpha Environmental Services (Alpha) advanced borings in the NW corner of the site to determine if the historical USTs had leaked.  Gasoline was detected at a maximum concentration of 3,600 mg/kg at a depth of 7.5 feet.  Further analysis of this sample revealed very low levels of associated diesel, volatile organic compounds and PAHs, consistent with a significantly degraded release. The investigation showed that contamination is present in a north-south oriented ellipsoid that extends into NE Grand Avenue and NE Davis Streets west and north of the site, respectively.  Gasoline was not detected at concentrations exceeding construction/excavation workers for direct contact.  Groundwater was not encountered during the investigation to a depth of 20 feet bgs.  Groundwater estimated to be at a depth of 47 feet bgs. Groundwater in the vicintiy of the site does not have a beneficial use as drinking water. 

Based on the possibility of possible future urban residential use, Alpha collected soil-gas data from the site in November 2014.  There were no detections above even residential RBCs, indicating no unacceptable vapor intrusion risks under any use scenario.  

Contamination is degraded and low level.  No use restrictions are necessary.  (JKS 1-7-15).",131814.0,20312.0,JSCHATZ,2015-01-07 15:17:52.463
43225,03-14-0570,03,14,0570,,2014-05-13,2014-05-13 17:46:07.880,Rick Schroder,44272 SE Phelps Road,SANDY,97055,,False,7.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"550-gallon farm UST decommissioned by removal at property. Diesel detected in compliance samples using HCID.  Follow-up with NWTPH-Dx revealed diesel at 561 mg/kg benath the south end .  K&S Environmental excavated contaminated soil near the south end of the former UST. Confirmation samples from the south, east and west sidewalls and the bottom of the excavation did not contain detectable diesel after removal of soil. 5.4 tons of soil was takent to HIllsboro Landfill.

The site obtains drinking water from a well located at least 50 feet away.  Based on limited depth of contamination (7 feet bgs) and distance to the well, it is unlikely to be impacted. (JKS 8-25-14)",,"550-gallon farm UST decommissioned by removal at property. Diesel detected in compliance samples using HCID.  Follow-up with NWTPH-Dx revealed diesel at 561 mg/kg benath the south end .  K&S Environmental excavated contaminated soil near the south end of the former UST. Confirmation samples from the south, east and west sidewalls and the bottom of the excavation did not contain detectable diesel after removal of soil. 5.4 tons of soil was takent to HIllsboro Landfill.

The site obtains drinking water from a well located at least 50 feet away.  Based on limited depth of contamination (7 feet bgs) and distance to the well, it is unlikely to be impacted. (JKS 8-25-14)",131817.0,20318.0,JSCHATZ,2014-08-25 10:17:39.537
