W88 = ND Dx",,,132613.0,20677.0,MJGILLE,2014-09-15 10:26:46.647
43579,26-14-0924,26,14,0924,,2014-07-15,2014-07-15 15:43:54.033,4041 MLK Property,4041 NE Martin Luther King Jr Blvd,PORTLAND,97212,,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"Contaminated soil was detected in the vicinity of the historic gasoline service station formerly located on the east side of the property.  Diesel and gasoline range petroleum hydrocarbons were detected in subsurface soils at the site during a preliminary site assessment.

In September 2014, Brady Environmental, Inc. (BEI) conducted test pitting in the southern portion of the site. This activity resulted a complaint about petroleum hydrocarbon odors being made to DEQ by a resident of the residential apartment building south of the the site (375 NE Shaver Street). During excavation, a UST tank nest was discovered.  Preliminary sampling along a  product line revealed gasoline and diesel hydrocarbons in soil.  The test pit excavations were backfilled. 
In April 2015, three 1,000-gallon USTs were decommissioned and removed from the site.  The USTs were out of service prior to 1974 and therefore permitting was not necessary.   Following removal, 625 tons of petroleum-contaminated soil was removed.  One confirmation sample (RE7-8) from the south sidewall contained diesel at a concentration of 6,060 ppm.

In June and July 2015, additional investigations were performed in the area of sample RE7-8 to delineate the extent of residual contamination.  The pocket of contamination was found to extend across the property boundary onto the residential property (375 NE Shaver Street), although levels of associated VOCs were below vapor intrusion RBCs.   Contamination largely at depths between 8 and 12 feet bgs.  Groundwater expected to be at depth of 60 feet bgs. 

A review of historical information indicates that fueling operations historically occurreda at both the site and adjacent residential property.  BEI believes the former USTs in the southern portion of the site were utilized by the adjacnent property as well.  This conjecture is based on the fact that there were formerly three USTs but only two product lines were located at the site.  BEI believes a third product line connected the UST with N. Albina Fuel Company, which operated at the south-adjacent property by 1929.

Based on the limited volume of the pocket, lack of VOCs at levels above vapor intrusion RBCs, and history of fueling operations at both properties, DEQ is not requiring further investigation or cleanup at either property (JKS 10-19-15).",,"Contaminated soil was detected in the vicinity of the historic gasoline service station formerly located on the east side of the property.  Diesel and gasoline range petroleum hydrocarbons were detected in subsurface soils at the site during a preliminary site assessment.

In September 2014, Brady Environmental, Inc. (BEI) conducted test pitting in the southern portion of the site. This activity resulted a complaint about petroleum hydrocarbon odors being made to DEQ by a resident of the residential apartment building south of the the site (375 NE Shaver Street). During excavation, a UST tank nest was discovered.  Preliminary sampling along a  product line revealed gasoline and diesel hydrocarbons in soil.  The test pit excavations were backfilled. 
In April 2015, three 1,000-gallon USTs were decommissioned and removed from the site.  The USTs were out of service prior to 1974 and therefore permitting was not necessary.   Following removal, 625 tons of petroleum-contaminated soil was removed.  One confirmation sample (RE7-8) from the south sidewall contained diesel at a concentration of 6,060 ppm.

In June and July 2015, additional investigations were performed in the area of sample RE7-8 to delineate the extent of residual contamination.  The pocket of contamination was found to extend across the property boundary onto the residential property (375 NE Shaver Street), although levels of associated VOCs were below vapor intrusion RBCs.   Contamination largely at depths between 8 and 12 feet bgs.  Groundwater expected to be at depth of 60 feet bgs. 

A review of historical information indicates that fueling operations historically occurreda at both the site and adjacent residential property.  BEI believes the former USTs in the southern portion of the site were utilized by the adjacnent property as well.  This conjecture is based on the fact that there were formerly three USTs but only two product lines were located at the site.  BEI believes a third product line connected the UST with N. Albina Fuel Company, which operated at the south-adjacent property by 1929.

Based on the limited volume of the pocket, lack of VOCs at levels above vapor intrusion RBCs, and history of fueling operations at both properties, DEQ is not requiring further investigation or cleanup at either property (JKS 10-19-15).",132941.0,20673.0,JSCHATZ,2015-10-19 10:02:13.443
