43705,26-14-1050,26,14,1050,,2014-08-05,2014-08-06 07:58:08.587,City of Portland Wellhead 35 HOT,12150 NE Marine Dr,PORTLAND,97220,,False,0.0,,0.0,,False,True,False,0.0,False,False,False,False,False,"NRC was informed on August 1st, 2014 that this release had been reported to DEQ, but learned today that this was not the case.  The listed discover date and Mail Contact information may need to be revised once additional information is made available by the Responsible Party.

This tank was previously unknown and was discovered during backhoe installation of a trench on the subject property.  The tank was apparently damaged by the backhoe and a release of heating oil occurred at that time.",,,132865.0,20814.0,MJGILLE,2014-08-06 07:58:08.587
43706,26-14-1051,26,14,1051,,2014-08-05,2014-08-06 10:43:10.680,Klee Trust,13615 NE Klickitat Ct,PORTLAND,97230,,False,1.0,,1.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"7,920 PPM below south end of tank & 5,200 PPM detected below north end of tank",,,132870.0,20815.0,CSALNAV,2017-05-30 14:03:01.983
43707,26-14-1052,26,14,1052,4913.0,2014-08-06,2014-08-06 12:46:51.610,"DSU-PETERBILT & GMC, INC.",4810 N Basin Avenue,PORTLAND,97217,503-535-3100,False,7.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Five USTs decomissioned at site in July 2014, and this includes one HOT decommissioned in place.  On both 2,000-gallon gasoline USTs, contamination was observed where the pipes from the suction pumps connect to the tanks benath the spill buckets.  4C claimed the contamination was restricted to the top of the USTs.  In addition, contamination was observed beneath the suction pumps but could not be removed.  
The information submitted was not clear on whether the magnitude and extent of contamination was delineated. In addition, the samples were dropped off to the lab 6-7 days after collection and the temperature at which they were received is not indicated.  Clarification is needed. (JKS 11-20-14).

In February 2017 4Cs Environmental submitted information including improved tables and figures, photographs taken a the time of decommssioining and information pertaining to management of soil samples following collection and submittal to the laboratory.  4Cs confirmed that samples were stored in the sample refrridgerator until submitted to the laboratory under chain of custoday protocol.  Clarifying figures and photographs support contention that soil containing high levels of gaosline was very limited and restricted to the top of the tanks near the spill buckets.  Photographs of the UST and analytical data for samples collected from the excavation bottom confirm that contamination did not run downthe sides of the tank.  Following removal, only one diesel detection exceeded the Soil Matrixl Level II cleanup level (500 ppm) and it did not exceed the most stringent of soil RBCs.  The volume of soil exceeding the Soil Matrix Level II standard for diesel is minimal.

The information submitted adequately addresses questions raised by DEQ in the letter of November 20, 2014.  Based on the limited extent and low magnitude of soil contamination, it does not pose unacceptable risks to human health and an NFA should be issued. Contamination does not appear to extend offsite (JKS 2-7-17).",,"Five USTs decomissioned at site in July 2014, and this includes one HOT decommissioned in place.  On both 2,000-gallon gasoline USTs, contamination was observed where the pipes from the suction pumps connect to the tanks benath the spill buckets.  4C claimed the contamination was restricted to the top of the USTs.  In addition, contamination was observed beneath the suction pumps but could not be removed.  
The information submitted was not clear on whether the magnitude and extent of contamination was delineated. In addition, the samples were dropped off to the lab 6-7 days after collection and the temperature at which they were received is not indicated.  Clarification is needed. (JKS 11-20-14).

In February 2017 4Cs Environmental submitted information including improved tables and figures, photographs taken a the time of decommssioining and information pertaining to management of soil samples following collection and submittal to the laboratory.  4Cs confirmed that samples were stored in the sample refrridgerator until submitted to the laboratory under chain of custoday protocol.  Clarifying figures and photographs support contention that soil containing high levels of gaosline was very limited and restricted to the top of the tanks near the spill buckets.  Photographs of the UST and analytical data for samples collected from the excavation bottom confirm that contamination did not run downthe sides of the tank.  Following removal, only one diesel detection exceeded the Soil Matrixl Level II cleanup level (500 ppm) and it did not exceed the most stringent of soil RBCs.  The volume of soil exceeding the Soil Matrix Level II standard for diesel is minimal.

The information submitted adequately addresses questions raised by DEQ in the letter of November 20, 2014.  Based on the limited extent and low magnitude of soil contamination, it does not pose unacceptable risks to human health and an NFA should be issued. Contamination does not appear to extend offsite (JKS 2-7-17).",132875.0,20820.0,JSCHATZ,2017-02-09 15:24:49.743
