44831,03-15-0443,03,15,0443,,2015-04-03,2015-04-06 08:50:45.630,Northwest Farm Credit Services,33142 S Barlow Rd,WOODBURN,97071,,False,0.0,,0.0,,False,False,True,0.0,False,False,False,False,False,,,,135649.0,0.0,KDANA,2015-04-06 08:50:45.630
44832,03-15-0444,03,15,0444,,2015-04-01,2015-04-06 08:56:57.900,Margaret M. Mansfield Trust,28386 S ELISHA RD,CANBY,97013,,False,7.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"340-gallon diesel UST and 1,000-gallon gasoline UST decommissioned and removed from the site in March 2015. Groundwater was observed in the excavation and was pumped out.  Groundwater returned to the excavation and a sample was collected. Gasoline, diesel and heavy oil were not detected in the pit water sample.  HCID analysis of compliance samples revealed only gasoline in one sample from the north end of the gasoline UST.  Quantification using Method NWTPH-Gx revealed gasoline at 114 mg/kg.  Approximately 24.4 tons of soil was disposed at Hillsboro Landfill.  Eight confirmation samples were collected at the soil/water interface.  None of the samples contained detectable gasoline or BTEX.

The site is a rural residential property.  A 5-foot diameter hand-dug well is located 85 feet west-southwest of the former USTs.  A 6-inch water well is also located 50 feet north of the former USTs. 

There is no evidence that shallow groundwater (6-7 feet) has been impacted by the release. Release appears to qualify for closure under the Matrix rules. (JKS 6-15-15)",,,135650.0,0.0,JSCHATZ,2015-06-15 07:58:52.440
44833,03-15-0445,03,15,0445,1001.0,2015-03-30,2015-04-06 12:29:42.093,SOUTH WILSONVILLE 76,30085 SW PARKWAY AVE,WILSONVILLE,97070,(503) 358-6555,False,0.0,,0.0,,True,False,False,0.0,False,False,False,False,False,,,,8810.0,0.0,GGAMOLO,2015-04-06 12:29:42.093
44834,26-15-0446,26,15,0446,,2015-04-06,2015-04-06 13:43:37.183,Ann M LeMarte Trust,3718 SW Marigold Street,PORTLAND,97219,,False,7.0,,1.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,Cause of release is unkown at present possibly tank or piping failure.,,,0.0,22033.0,IGAFFNE,2015-08-25 08:42:25.520
44835,26-15-0447,26,15,0447,,2015-04-07,2015-04-07 13:25:19.830,Former Rocket Station,7130 NE HALSEY ST,PORTLAND,97213,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"The site was formerly occupied by a Rocket gasoline station as early as 1934. Although the commercial building has been demolished the foundation and footings remain, defining the former garage and service bays. 
In 2015, a geophysical survey revealed two areas of disturbed soils representing the likely former UST nest. In March 2015, four of sixteen soil samples collected from these areas contained maximum concentrations of diesel (480 ppm) and heavy oil (4,900 ppm).  The most contaminated samples contained very low levels of associated volatile organic compounds and polynuclear aromatic hydrocarbons. Contamination was detected at depths of 15 and 20 feet bgs. 
In April 2015, samples were collected in the area of the disturbed aeas to delineate the lateral extent of soil contamination. Samples from the borings showed that soil contamination terminated by a depth of 27.5 feet bgs. In August 2015 at the request of DEQ, samples were collected within three feet of the surface to evaluate possible risks under the direct contact pathway.  Diesel and heavy oil were not detected at levesl above method reporting limits.  Also, samples were collected from the former service bays to evaluate the suitability of infiltrating storm water.  Diesel and heavy oil were not detected in the samples.  Metals were detected in the samples at concentrations below RBCs for applicable pathways.
The 2015 investigations delineated the lateral and vertical extent of contamination.  Groundwater was not encountered at any time during the investigations and is believed to be at depths exceeding 150 feet in the area of the site.  The site is connected to City water and site groundwater is not reasonably likely to be used as a source of drinking water.  Soil contamination is sufficiently deep that residents, occupational workers and construction/excavation workers are unlikely to encounter it. The contamination appears to be very old and devoid of volatile organic compounds. As a result, the contaminatino does not pose unacceptable risks from vapor intrusion. Site conditions are considered protective and an NFA determination is warranted (JKS 3-16-16).",,"The site was formerly occupied by a Rocket gasoline station as early as 1934. Although the commercial building has been demolished the foundation and footings remain, defining the former garage and service bays. 
In 2015, a geophysical survey revealed two areas of disturbed soils representing the likely former UST nest. In March 2015, four of sixteen soil samples collected from these areas contained maximum concentrations of diesel (480 ppm) and heavy oil (4,900 ppm).  The most contaminated samples contained very low levels of associated volatile organic compounds and polynuclear aromatic hydrocarbons. Contamination was detected at depths of 15 and 20 feet bgs. 
In April 2015, samples were collected in the area of the disturbed aeas to delineate the lateral extent of soil contamination. Samples from the borings showed that soil contamination terminated by a depth of 27.5 feet bgs. In August 2015 at the request of DEQ, samples were collected within three feet of the surface to evaluate possible risks under the direct contact pathway.  Diesel and heavy oil were not detected at levesl above method reporting limits.  Also, samples were collected from the former service bays to evaluate the suitability of infiltrating storm water.  Diesel and heavy oil were not detected in the samples.  Metals were detected in the samples at concentrations below RBCs for applicable pathways.
The 2015 investigations delineated the lateral and vertical extent of contamination.  Groundwater was not encountered at any time during the investigations and is believed to be at depths exceeding 150 feet in the area of the site.  The site is connected to City water and site groundwater is not reasonably likely to be used as a source of drinking water.  Soil contamination is sufficiently deep that residents, occupational workers and construction/excavation workers are unlikely to encounter it. The contamination appears to be very old and devoid of volatile organic compounds. As a result, the contaminatino does not pose unacceptable risks from vapor intrusion. Site conditions are considered protective and an NFA determination is warranted (JKS 3-16-16).",142967.0,0.0,iPMO#38,2016-10-14 07:41:41.350
