45485,34-15-1097,34,15,1097,,2015-08-05,2015-08-06 11:40:43.337,"Grove, Robert",7425 SW Canyon Ln,PORTLAND,97225,,False,2.0,,1.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,1500 ppm,,,136982.0,22698.0,DGRANGE,2016-10-27 09:20:11.773
45486,26-15-1098,26,15,1098,,2015-08-06,2015-08-06 17:03:23.563,NW 4th and Everett,404 NW Everett St,PORTLAND,97209,360-256-5764,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"Based on historical documentation, UST(s) were previously removed from the site.  During a site investigation contaminated soil was discovered when lab results were received.

Site, currently a parking lot, was developed as a gasoline station in the late 1950s and 1960s.  In August 2015, site assessment showed the northeast corner of the site was contaminated with gasoline-range hydrocarbons at levels up to 11,000 mg/kg.  In November 2015 during removal of contaminated soil two previously decommissioned USTs were discovered.  They were filled with concrete. The USTs and contaminated soil were removed to a depth of 12 feet bgs.  Confirmation samples collected near the ends of the tanks, product lines and pump island at depths of 10 to 12 feet bgs did not contain detectable concentrations of gasoline.  A small pocket of residual contamination was observed in the east sidewall from 9 to 12 feet but could not be reached due to requirement to maintain 1:1 slope in the excavation.  A composite sample of the contamination was found to contain high levels of gasoline and VOCs including ethylbenzene and naphthalene.  Following review of the March 2016 PBS report documenting the soil removal, DEQ rejected a PBS request for closure.  DEQ believed that the extent of the contamination pocket was not delineated and the high levels of gasoline and VOCs detected in the sidewall composite sample raised concerns about vapor intrusion

In June 2016 PBS advanced borings in the sidewalk and street east of the contamination pocket. Gasoline and VOCs were not detected in any soil samples from the borings. A soil gas sample from boring SB-1 deteted benzene but at a level below the most stringent RBC.  Groundwater was not encountered at any time during the soil removal and investigation  work.  The pocket of soil is de minimus and unlikely to extend a significant distance into the right of way. No unacceptable risk conditions associated with the contamination remain at the site and a No Further Action determination is appropriate (JKS 8-19-16).",,"Based on historical documentation, UST(s) were previously removed from the site.  During a site investigation contaminated soil was discovered when lab results were received.

Site, currently a parking lot, was developed as a gasoline station in the late 1950s and 1960s.  In August 2015, site assessment showed the northeast corner of the site was contaminated with gasoline-range hydrocarbons at levels up to 11,000 mg/kg.  In November 2015 during removal of contaminated soil two previously decommissioned USTs were discovered.  They were filled with concrete. The USTs and contaminated soil were removed to a depth of 12 feet bgs.  Confirmation samples collected near the ends of the tanks, product lines and pump island at depths of 10 to 12 feet bgs did not contain detectable concentrations of gasoline.  A small pocket of residual contamination was observed in the east sidewall from 9 to 12 feet but could not be reached due to requirement to maintain 1:1 slope in the excavation.  A composite sample of the contamination was found to contain high levels of gasoline and VOCs including ethylbenzene and naphthalene.  DEQ rejected a request for closure due to the fact that the pocket's extent was not delineated and concerns about possible vapor intrusion.
In June 2016 PBS advanced borings in the sidewalk and street east of the contamination pocket. Gasoline and VOCs were not detected in any soil samples from the borings. A soil gas sample from boring SB-1 deteted benzene but at a level below the most stringent RBC.  Groundwater was not encountered at any time during the soil removal and investigation  work.  The pocket of soil is de minimus and unlikely to extend a significant distance into the right of way. No unacceptable risk conditions associated with teh contamation remain at the site and a No Further Action determination is appropriate (JKS 8-19-16).",0.0,22700.0,JSCHATZ,2016-08-22 07:41:23.920
