45627,22-15-1239,22,15,1239,,2015-08-16,2015-09-03 10:44:07.543,Samaritan Lebanon Hospital,525 N Santiam Highway,LEBANON,97355,541-451-7145,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Abandoned ~1500-gal UST (no product) discovered next to highway while digging to install a new sewer line for the hospital. Site is Regulated, but UST is not to be registered.  UST is from an old gas station and NOT related to the UST facility number 3731, which is connected to the hospital.... K VanZandt 9/3/2015",,"The site is on the Lebanon Community Hospital property, located in the landscaped median west of the main building along N Santiam Highway. Before the hospital was built in 1952, the site was occupied by a gasoline service station.    A 1,500-gallon abandoned UST was found beneath the site during trenching for a new sanitary sewer line in August 2015. The UST, related petroleum contaminated debris and about 384 tons of petroleum contaminated soil was excavated and removed from the site.  The soil was disposed of at Coffin Butte Landfill in Corvallis.  Follow-up investigations in July 2018 show that there is groundwater contamination remaining between 7 and 13 feet below ground around and between the area of the former UST and buried debris.  Some of the contamination has migrated a short distance (5 to 10 feet) offsite to the west beneath the N Santiam Highway. The site and surrounding properties are provided municipal water by the city and the area is zoned for public use. The remaining contamiantion beneath the site is below the acceptable risk levels for the current and future site use which could include construction, excavation, and/or maintenance workers. Based on this DEQ determined that the remaining contamiantion does not pose an unacceptable risk to public health and the environment, if left in place, in accordance with Oregon Administrative Rules (OAR) 340-122-0205 through 340-122-0360 and that no further investigation or cleanup is needed.",137257.0,22752.0,NSAWKA,2021-08-25 18:39:13.900
45628,14-15-1240,14,15,1240,241.0,2015-09-02,2015-09-03 11:45:02.590,CLEM'S COUNTRY STORE and LAUNDROMAT,3398 Odell Highway,ODELL,97044,5418062587,False,4.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,,,"In September 2015, the UST system at Clem’s Country Store was decommissioned by removal.  The removal included three 2,000 gallon USTs, the associated piping, and the dispenser island.  17.1 tons of contaminated soil was excavated and disposed of in the Wasco County Landfill.  A total of 9 soil samples were taken from the bottom and side walls of the excavation, along the piping run, and under the dispenser island.  Samples were analyzed using methods for Total Petroleum Hydrocarbons – Gasoline Range (NWTPH-Gx), Total Petroleum Hydrocarbons – Diesel Range (NWTPH-Dx), Volatile Organic Compounds (VOCs by EPA method 8260B), Poly-Aromatic Hydrocarbons (EPA 8270D SIM), and Total Metals (EPA 6020).  Results of the analysis indicated that unacceptable risk was still present for several VOCs in the area of the dispenser island.  Groundwater was not encountered in the excavation; therefore no groundwater samples were taken.

In September 2016, additional excavation was conducted to remove the remaining contaminated soil in the dispenser island area.  Approximately 300 yards tons of soil was excavated from the dispenser area and a total of four soil samples were taken from the walls and bottom of the excavation.  All samples were analyzed using EPA method 8260B and were non-detect for all contaminants of concern.  After analysis, the excavation was filled with clean fill.  As a result of this removal, there is no longer unacceptable risk associated with the UST system release.",18097.0,22851.0,TENGLAN,2016-11-10 09:34:12.223
