45802,34-15-1414,34,15,1414,7819.0,2015-10-05,2015-10-05 14:27:08.970,LAUREL VALLEY STORE,14025 SW Campbell Road,HILLSBORO,97123,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Historical release (34-96-0377).  In 1996 a 1,000-gallon UST was decommissioned and found to be extremely corroded at the time of removal.  Following removal of contaminated soil, a replacement UST was installed west of the dispenser island. In January 1997, repairs were made to the 2,000-gallon UST because of indications that water was getting in the tank.  Sampling showed that groundwater in the area of the UST was impacted by low levels of VOCs.  In June 2000 further assessment was performed in the area of the UST.  Low levels of VOCs were detected in groundwater.  An NFA was issued for the release in December 2000.

In September 2015 a soil sample that was collected adjacent to the east side of the dispenser island (boring B5) at 12 feet below grade had a detection of diesel (990 ppm) and gas (128 ppm). Additional borings from around the tanks and dispenser island did not show additional impacts. In January 2016, Alpha advanced twelve additional borings in the area of the UST pit and dispsenser to further delineate the extent of contaminination detected in boring B5.  The maximum detected concentrations of gasoline and diesel were 275 ppm and 2,240 ppm, respectively.  In February 2016, Alpha collected soil gas samples in two locations to evaluate vapor intrusion risks. Neither sample contained VOCs at levels greater than residential RBCs.  Groundwater was not encountered at any time during the 2015 and 2016 investigations. 

Contamination lies at approximate depths of 12 to 14 feet bgs in a narrow band extending from the former 1,000 gallon UST excavation to the vicinity of boring B5.  It is possible that B5 detected, in part, contamination from the 1996 release.   There is no indication that contamination extends offsite. 

The site is in rural Washington County.  One individual periodically lives in the upper floor of the building. Groundwater in the area of the site is used for drinking water.  The site well is located on the west side of the building and is topographically up-gradient. The site well is completed to 320 feet bgs and produces from 305 feet bgs.  The nearest well is located 210 feet east of the site and is topographically cross-gradient.  Logs for wells in the area of the site suggest first water at depths ranging from 150 to 344 feet.  Shallow soil contamination is unlikely to impact area wells. (JKS 5-23-16)",,"Historical release (34-96-0377).  In 1996 a 1,000-gallon UST was decommissioned and found to be extremely corroded at the time of removal.  Following removal of contaminated soil, a replacement UST was installed west of the dispenser island. In January 1997, repairs were made to the 2,000-gallon UST because of indications that water was getting in the tank.  Sampling showed that groundwater in the area of the UST was impacted by low levels of VOCs.  In June 2000 further assessment was performed in the area of the UST.  Low levels of VOCs were detected in groundwater.  An NFA was issued for the release in December 2000.

In September 2015 a soil sample that was collected adjacent to the east side of the dispenser island (boring B5) at 12 feet below grade had a detection of diesel (990 ppm) and gas (128 ppm). Additional borings from around the tanks and dispenser island did not show additional impacts. In January 2016, Alpha advanced twelve additional borings in the area of the UST pit and dispsenser to further delineate the extent of contaminination detected in boring B5.  The maximum detected concentrations of gasoline and diesel were 275 ppm and 2,240 ppm, respectively.  In February 2016, Alpha collected soil gas samples in two locations to evaluate vapor intrusion risks. Neither sample contained VOCs at levels greater than residential RBCs.  Groundwater was not encountered at any time during the 2015 and 2016 investigations. 

Contamination lies at approximate depths of 12 to 14 feet bgs in a narrow band extending from the former 1,000 gallon UST excavation to the vicinity of boring B5.  It is possible that B5 detected, in part, contamination from the 1996 release.   There is no indication that contamination extends offsite. 

The site is in rural Washington County.  One individual periodically lives in the upper floor of the building. Groundwater in the area of the site is used for drinking water.  The site well is located on the west side of the building and is topographically up-gradient. The site well is completed to 320 feet bgs and produces from 305 feet bgs.  The nearest well is located 210 feet east of the site and is topographically cross-gradient.  Logs for wells in the area of the site suggest first water at depths ranging from 150 to 344 feet.  Shallow soil contamination is unlikely to impact area wells. (JKS 5-23-16)",137682.0,23031.0,JSCHATZ,2016-05-23 16:43:42.707
45803,26-15-1415,26,15,1415,,2015-10-01,2015-10-05 15:56:49.100,Viola Shannon Jones Trust,11515 NE SACRAMENTO ST,PORTLAND,97220,,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,,LUST #26-12-0792,"A 26-gallon used oil UST was removed from the site in late September 2015, along with 8.73 tons of PCS.  Approximately 18.2 cubic yards of PCS remains at the site.  The residual contamination does not pose a vapor intrusion threat, is too deep to pose a direct contact threat, and does not extend to groundwater.",137684.0,0.0,KDANA,2016-04-18 07:41:26.157
