45878,26-15-1490,26,15,1490,,2015-10-22,2015-10-26 15:39:27.383,NW Freeman Industrial Site,2328 NW REED ST,PORTLAND,97210,,False,1.0,,,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"The site was formerly an agricultural equipment manufacturing company.  A 1000 gallon oil UST was formerly located north of the site building beneath the sidewalk.  It was reportedly removed in the 1960s but no documentation was available. The site also had features including an oil-water separator, vent pipe, fill port, pump island, and spray painting booth and pain drying area.
In September 2015, a geophysical survey of the area north of the building did not reveal a UST, although disturbed soil was believed to mark the location of the former UST.  A soil sample collected from this area contained gasoline at a concentration of 15,000 ppm.  Other samples advanced near the oil-water separator, pump island, fill port, vent pipe and paint booth did not contain detectable levels of petroleum hydrocarbons.  

Surface samples were collected at five locations in the paint drying area and composited for analysis.  VOCs, metals and PCBs were detected in the composite sample.

In September 2015, soil gas samples were collected across the site.  The samples contained volatile organic compounds and solvents.  

In February 2016, DEQ requested additional soil-gas data from near the former UST and sample DP-1, where gasoline was detected in soil at a concentration of 15,000 ppm.   DEQ also requested that the lateral extent of gasoline-contaminated soil be delineated.  EcoTech submitted a work plan on May 9, 2016. (JKS 5-23-16).

In June 2016, EcoTech advanced borings near the former UST to delineate contamination detected in the area of boring DP-1.  One sample west of the former UST detcted gasoline at a much lower concetnration of 285 mg/kg.  All other samples were ND for gasoline, diesel and heavy oil. A soil gas sample was also collected in the atrium near the former UST - there were no detections exceedign applicable RBCs.  Contamination detected at DP-1 is unlikely to extend more than a few feet into the adjacent right-of-way to the north of the site. 


This NFA applies only to soill impacted by petroleum hydrocarbons and selected constitutents in the area of the former UST.  It does not extend to shallow soil contaminated with PCBs and metals in the area of the former paint drying area (JKS 8-29-16). Site re-opened on : 9/5/2017 By Gerald Gamolo. Previous Final Invoice Request Date: 10/11/2016.

Site address changed per Dawn Ismerio's email dated 9/12/2017.

In April 2017, a portion of the concrete floor in the Spray Booth area was removed along with the removal of the upper 1-foot of soil (Figure 4 in Attachments). A limited area of petroleum-stained soil in the southeastern corner of the Spray Booth was excavated to an approximate depth of 2.5 feet below ground surface (bgs). The petroleum-contaminated soil is not believed to be associated with the former 1,000-gallon UST.  Approximately 60 tons of contaminated soil was removed and transported to Hillsboro Landfill for disposal.  

The metals arsenic, barium, cadmium, chromium and lead were detected in confirmation samples at levels below DEQs RBCs or were within the range of naturally occurring metals in soil in the region.

Gasoline- and diesel range hydrocarbons and selected volatile organic compounds were detected below applicable RBCs in sample SS-5, collected from the southern portion of the excavation near the petroleum stained area.  The diesel concentration in sample SS-5 exceeded only the urban residential RBC for the Soil Ingestion, Dermal Contact and Inhalation pathway. 

Under the proposed future commercial use, a connecting structure will be built at the location of the current atrium.  As a result, the excavation area will be covered with an engineered surface that will prevent workers coming in contact with shallow soil contamination. (md 10/23/2017)
