45929,26-15-1541,26,15,1541,1538.0,2015-11-03,2015-11-03 15:32:05.960,City of Portland Water Bureau,1900 N Interstate Avenue,PORTLAND,97227,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Alpha was responsible for tank decommissioning, but City handled laboratory analysis and reporting internally. Discussions with City were that reporting should probably be handled by owner (City) since they were managing lab reports but this may have not been  confirmed. 

USTs associated with a former gasoline station at the site were decommissioned prior to the 1990s.  In May 2015, two USTs (675 gallon HOT and 1,700-gallon fuel) were discovered adjacent to a retaining wall at the site.  Sampling beneath the ends of the 1,700-gallon  tank revealed detectable gasoline and diesel.  The tank was decommissioned and removed from the site in June 2015 and 104 tons of soil was removed from the excavation.  Post removal confirmations sampling confirmed gasoline (5,060 ppm) and diesel (860ppm) near the west end of the tank.

Following the tank decommissioning and soil removal, the area adjacent to the retaining wall was excavated to a depth of 20 feet bgs, after which the excavation was backfilled with multiple feet of CDF. Delineation borings in the area of the former UST confirmed that residual contamination north of the retaining wall is limited in volume and extent.  Additional confirmation samples were not collected prior to backfilling the excavation with CDF.  However, the remaining pocket is very limited and the wall andCDF will inhibit contact by workers and inhibit intrusion of vapors.  Groundwater was not encountered during the work. (JKS 8-17-16)

Release report given in October 2015 when report was issued. 

