46158,26-15-1770,26,15,1770,,2015-12-23,2015-12-23 11:44:36.017,RE Investments 006 LLC,2104 SW Iowa St,PORTLAND,97239,,False,0.0,,0.0,,False,True,False,0.0,False,False,False,False,False,"5010 PPM detected off north end of tank, 6490 PPM detected off south end of tank.",,,138530.0,23396.0,MJGILLE,2015-12-23 11:44:36.017
46159,26-15-1771,26,15,1771,12506.0,2015-12-23,2015-12-23 12:04:36.900,OR213 SE 82nd MP 6.25,6521 SE 82nd Ave,PORTLAND,97266,,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"NOT AN HOT SITE!!!   This is a non regulated site. Misc Gasoline.
An abandoned UST was encountered during drilled shaft excavation work for the construction of a new signal pole.  The tank is located on property recently purchased by ODOT (in 2014) from Ms. Deana Gray to facilitate construction of ADA ramps, TriMet bus pullout and new signal pole.  The UST is likely 50+ years old based on a historical aerial photographic and city directory review conducted by ODOT in 2014.  A 

In February 2016, during the decommissioning of of a 340-gallon UST (Tank 1) and a 1,000-gallon UST (Tank 2), an additional 340-gallon tank (Tank 3) was discovered.   During construction related to upgrades to the nearby intersection one additional 500-gallon UST was discovered.  All of the tanks were permitted, decommissioned and removed.  

The highest levels of contamination were found near the south end of Tank 1. Gasoline was detected near Tank 1 at concentations as high as 5,500 ppm and diesel as 3,300 ppm.  1,2,4-TMB and naphthalene were detected in one sample collected near Tank 1 at levels exceeding residetntial/urban residential RBCs for the vapor intrusion pathway.  

Small pockets of contamination were also observed near tanks 2 and 4, although none of the detections exceeded applicable RBCs. Contamination was observed at approximate depths of 6 to 9 feet bgs.  The pockets of contamination near Tanks 1,2 and 4 were estimated to be a maximum 1-2 cubic yards in volume.

The pocket near Tank 1 is not expected to result in unacceptable vapor intrusion risks due to distance from site buildings and limited volume of the pocket.  Groundwater was not encountered at any time during the tank removal or site assessment activities (JKS 10-10-16)",,"This is a non regulated site. Misc Gasoline.
An abandoned UST was encountered during drilled shaft excavation work for the construction of a new signal pole.  The tank is located on property recently purchased by ODOT (in 2014) from Ms. Deana Gray to facilitate construction of ADA ramps, TriMet bus pullout and new signal pole.  The UST is likely 50+ years old based on a historical aerial photographic and city directory review conducted by ODOT in 2014.  A 

In February 2016, during the decommissioning of of a 340-gallon UST (Tank 1) and a 1,000-gallon UST (Tank 2), an additional 340-gallon tank (Tank 3) was discovered.   During construction related to upgrades to the nearby intersection one additional 500-gallon UST was discovered.  All of the tanks were permitted, decommissioned and removed.  

The highest levels of contamination were found near the south end of Tank 1. Gasoline was detected near Tank 1 at concentations as high as 5,500 ppm and diesel as 3,300 ppm.  1,2,4-TMB and naphthalene were detected in one sample collected near Tank 1 at levels exceeding residetntial/urban residential RBCs for the vapor intrusion pathway.  

Small pockets of contamination were also observed near tanks 2 and 4, although none of the detections exceeded applicable RBCs. Contamination was observed at approximate depths of 6 to 9 feet bgs.  The pockets of contamination near Tanks 1,2 and 4 were estimated to be a maximum 1-2 cubic yards in volume.

The pocket near Tank 1 is not expected to result in unacceptable vapor intrusion risks due to distance from site buildings and limited volume of the pocket.  Groundwater was not encountered at any time during the tank removal or site assessment activities (JKS 10-10-16)",138531.0,23397.0,JSCHATZ,2016-10-10 11:08:57.340
46160,26-15-1772,26,15,1772,,2015-12-23,2015-12-23 16:35:08.897,Naito/Pine UST,221 SW Naito Parkway,PORTLAND,97204,407-626-4514,False,1.0,,,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"Release identified during decommissioning of five USTs from site. Impacts located along north side of site, along SW Pine Street.",,"The site was developed as a retail fueling facility in about 1951 and operated until the mid-1970s when the site apparently became a parking lot.  Five underground storage tanks (USTs) historically located on the north end of the site property were decommissioned and removed in December 2015.The contamination was caused from corrosion holes in three of the five tanks removed.  The holes ranged in size from less than a quarter of an inch to nearly an inch in diameter.Compliance sampling revealed that subsurface soil and pit water on the north side of the tank pit were contaminated with high concentrations of gasoline. Approximately 500 tons of impacted soil was excavated and disposed of at the Wasco County Landfill. About 5,600 gallons of impacted tank and pit water was removed and recycled.Post-removal confirmation sampling showed that almost all of the contamination has been removed.  Low levels of petroleum hydrocarbons may extend a limited distance into the sidewalk and eastbound lane of SW Pine Street north of the site.The property is being redeveloped for urban residential use and will be connected to municipal water.  Residual soil and groundwater contamination in the area of the former tank pit is not considered to pose unacceptable risks to future residents or construction/excavation workers.DEQ notified the City of Portland, who indicated that the property owner may be responsible for costs related to management and disposal of contaminated media in SW Pine Avenue if encountered in the future.
",141751.0,23399.0,CRICHAR,2018-03-19 10:01:35.137
