46557,26-16-0369,26,16,0369,3438.0,2016-02-12,2016-03-28 10:24:43.000,PEPSI BEVERAGE CO.,2505 NE PACIFIC ST,PORTLAND,97232,(503) 238-7000,False,1.0,,1.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Contamination was discovered in January 2016 during decomissioning of a 12,000-gallon UST. at the site.  Although contamintion was confirmed in January 2016 it was not reported to DEQ until February 12, 2016.   Pepsi hired Stantec as a general environmental contractor who in turn hired BB&A Environmental as the Service Provider.  A referral was made to Enforcement for failing to report contamination in a timely manner. Pepsi-Cola and BB&A were issued a Notice of Civil Penalty and Order in October 2016.  

In January 2016, the 12,000-gallon diesel UST was decommissioned and crushed in place. Sampling in the area of the east end of the UST revealed diesel contamination (2,620 ppm) at a depth of 10 feet bgs.  In addition, diesel contamination was discovered near the fill port (4,049 ppm) at a depth of 2 feet bgs and near fuel pump 43 (1,300 ppm) at 2.5 feet bgs.  The latter is reportedly associated with a spill of product out of broken lines during the decommissioning work.
Limited soil removal was performed along the east sidwall of the UST excavation, the fill port area and the vicinity of dispenser 43.  Approximately one cubic yard of contaminated soil was removed from these areas and disposed at Waste Management's Hillsboro Landfill.  Following soil removal, confirmation sampling revealed generally reduced levesl of diesel contamination in the east sidewall (318 ppm at 14 feet bgs) and fill port (643 ppm at 4 feet bgs).  A sample collected near dispsenser 43 at 4 feet bgs contained a slightly higher level of diesel (1,920 ppm). However, this contamination is expected to be shallow in nature and attenuate rapidly with depth.

Groundwater was not encountered during any of the decommissioinign and soil removal work.  The site is located in an area of mixed residetnial and commercial use but is likely to continue to be used for future commercial purposes.  Residual diesel contamination in soil does not exceed RBCs for the direct contact pathway.  There is no evidence of vapor intrusion risks.  Site conditions appear to be protective. (JKS 1-4-17).",,"Contamination was discovered in January 2016 during decomissioning of a 12,000-gallon UST. at the site.  Although contamintion was confirmed in January 2016 it was not reported to DEQ until February 12, 2016.   Pepsi hired Stantec as a general environmental contractor who in turn hired BB&A Environmental as the Service Provider.  A referral was made to Enforcement for failing to report contamination in a timely manner. Pepsi-Cola and BB&A were issued a Notice of Civil Penalty and Order in October 2016.  

In January 2016, the 12,000-gallon diesel UST was decommissioned and crushed in place. Sampling in the area of the east end of the UST revealed diesel contamination (2,620 ppm) at a depth of 10 feet bgs.  In addition, diesel contamination was discovered near the fill port (4,049 ppm) at a depth of 2 feet bgs and near fuel pump 43 (1,300 ppm) at 2.5 feet bgs.  The latter is reportedly associated with a spill of product out of broken lines during the decommissioning work.
Limited soil removal was performed along the east sidwall of the UST excavation, the fill port area and the vicinity of dispenser 43.  Approximately one cubic yard of contaminated soil was removed from these areas and disposed at Waste Management's Hillsboro Landfill.  Following soil removal, confirmation sampling revealed generally reduced levesl of diesel contamination in the east sidewall (318 ppm at 14 feet bgs) and fill port (643 ppm at 4 feet bgs).  A sample collected near dispsenser 43 at 4 feet bgs contained a slightly higher level of diesel (1,920 ppm). However, this contamination is expected to be shallow in nature and attenuate rapidly with depth.

Groundwater was not encountered during any of the decommissioinign and soil removal work.  The site is located in an area of mixed residetnial and commercial use but is likely to continue to be used for future commercial purposes.  Residual diesel contamination in soil does not exceed RBCs for the direct contact pathway.  There is no evidence of vapor intrusion risks.  Site conditions appear to be protective. (JKS 1-4-17).",620.0,0.0,GGAMOLO,2017-01-10 09:30:02.540
