46999,26-16-0810,26,16,0810,,2016-06-23,2016-06-24 09:51:55.337,Stephen and Gloria Hudson Property,14708 NE Glisan St,PORTLAND,97230,,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Site is a residential property.  Two nonregulated USTs (gasoline and diesel) and one HOT south of the residence were decommissioned in place in June 2016.  Samples collected beneath the diesel UST (tank 2) detected only 29.6 mg/kg diesel-range hydrocarbons.  Gasoline was detected in one sample collected beneath the gasoline UST (tank 3) at a concentration of 9 ppm.  Both detections were below Soil Matrix Level I standards.

Samples collected beneath the HOT (tank 1) at 6 feet bgs contained diesel-range hydrocarbons at  concentrations of 6,690 mg/kg (west end) and 16,800 mg/kg (east end).  Further analysis of the east end sample revealed low concentrations of VOCs and PAHs.  The VOCs did not exceed RBCs for vapor intrusion.  The diesel detections under the ends of the tank exceed the construction worker RBC for the Soil Ingestion, Dermal Contact and Inhalation pathway.  However, delineation sampling showed that contamiantion is limited to the immediate vicinity of the HOT and does not extend deeper than 17 feet bgs.  The site is unlikely to be redeveloped in the future.  Based on these facts, the diesel contamination pocket is not considered to pose an unacceptable risk to future construction workers.  Petroleum-contaminated soil was not removed from the site.

A review of WRD well logs revealed no domestic wells within a quarter mile of the site.  Based on the limited depth of contamination, impacts to groundwater are considered unlikely.
Site conditions are protective of human health and environment.  (JKS 10-11-17).","Addendum file and tank removal from Xavier Environmental. Rec. Nov 18, 2019. CS","Site is a residential property.  Two nonregulated USTs (gasoline and diesel) and one HOT south of the residence were decommissioned in place in June 2016.  Samples collected beneath the diesel UST (tank 2) detected only 29.6 mg/kg diesel-range hydrocarbons.  Gasoline was detected in one sample collected beneath the gasoline UST (tank 3) at a concentration of 9 ppm.  Both detections were below Soil Matrix Level I standards.

Samples collected beneath the HOT (tank 1) at 6 feet bgs contained diesel-range hydrocarbons at  concentrations of 6,690 mg/kg (west end) and 16,800 mg/kg (east end).  Further analysis of the east end sample revealed low concentrations of VOCs and PAHs.  The VOCs did not exceed RBCs for vapor intrusion.  The diesel detections under the ends of the tank exceed the construction worker RBC for the Soil Ingestion, Dermal Contact and Inhalation pathway.  However, delineation sampling showed that contamiantion is limited to the immediate vicinity of the HOT and does not extend deeper than 17 feet bgs.  The site is unlikely to be redeveloped in the future.  Based on these facts, the diesel contamination pocket is not considered to pose an unacceptable risk to future construction workers.  Petroleum-contaminated soil was not removed from the site. 

A review of WRD well logs revealed no domestic wells within a quarter mile of the site.  Based on the limited depth of contamination, impacts to groundwater are considered unlikely.
Site conditions are protective of human health and environment.  (JKS 10-11-17).",140792.0,24278.0,CSALNAV,2019-11-21 09:55:44.563
