47179,03-16-0990,03,16,0990,12543.0,2016-08-02,2016-08-02 13:24:32.587,The Owen C. Nielsen Gifting Trust,15185 Holcomb Boulevard,OREGON CITY,97045,,False,1.0,,1.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"Site consists of three rural residetnial properites in Oregon City.  Sites were developed for residential and agricultural use in the 1930s through the 1960s.  In the 1970s a private air strip was constructed on Tax Lot 300.  A UST fueling system was installed in the northern portion of the site in the mid 1970s. 

In June 2016 assessment preparatory to a prospective purchaser agreement revealed soil and groundwater contamination in the area of the UST system. Additional investigation in the area of the former orchard areas, drum storage areas, paint storage areas, scrap tank storage areas, engine part storage and a possible dry well near the hangar building did not reveal evidence for impacts.  The site entered into a Prospective Purchaser Agreement with DEQ in December 2016.

The scope of work for the PPA was comprehensive.  The scope of work related to contamination from the USTs included decommissinioning of the UST system, removal of petroleum-contaminated soil, post removal confirmation sampling, additional delineation of groundwater contamination, groundwater treatment if warranted, completion of a Beneficial Use Determination and a remedy to prevent vapor intrusion risks to future residents if warranted.
Site assesment work in June and July 2016 revealed that groundwater contamination extended a limited distance south and west of the UST system.  Gasoline was detected in groundwater in the area of the UST pit at concentrations as high as 55,000 ppb.  Six USTs were decommissioned and removed from the site in September 2017. Following removal of the tanks, in October 2017, petroluem contaminated soil was removed from the excavation to a maximum depth of 23 feet bgs.  Approximately 2,275 tons of contaminated soil were removed and disposed at Riverbend Landfill.  Groundwater in the excavation was believed to be runoff due to rain events and did not recharge after evacuation. In November 2017, borings were advanced near the former UST excavation to characterize groundwater.  Benzene was detected in three of five grab samples at concentrations ranging from 6.1 to 470 ppb.  Gasoline was detected in two groundwater samples at concentrations of 510 and 2,700 ppb.  Although the maximum benzene concentration exceeded the residential RBC for the vapor intrusion pathway, the arithmetic mean of the groundwater data did not exceed residential RBCs for the VI pathway.
Proximity to a power pole near the north side of the former remedial excavation prevented the complete removal of soil contamination.  However, 28 of 31 confirmation samples did not contain detectable levels of gasoline, diesel or heavy oil hydrocarbons.  The highest concentration of gasoline detected in the confirmation samples was 180 ppm. Prior to backfilling, ORC was applied to the floor of the excavation.  The residual pocket of soil contamination is limited in extent and volume and not considered to pose unacceptable vapor intrusion risks.
The site properties are provided with drinking water.  Although deep groundwater in the general vicinity of the site has a beneficial use as drinking water, shallow groundwater is not considered to have a beneficial use.  An out of service brick-lined dug well located on Tax Lot 1400 was permanently decommissioned. Site assessment has shown shallow groundwater contamination is limited to the site boundaries.
Residual contamination related to the release from the UST system does not pose unacceptable risks to future residents.  In addition to satisfying the PPA scope of work, the site qualifies for an unrestricted NFA determination. (JKS 1-5-18).",,"In June 2016 assessment preparatory to a prospective purchaser agreement revealed soil and groundwater contamination in the area of the UST system. 

Site assesment work in June and July 2016 revealed that groundwater contamination extended a limited distance south and west of the UST system.  Gasoline was detected in groundwater in the area of the UST pit at concentrations as high as 55,000 ppb.  Six USTs were decommissioned and removed from the site in September 2017. Following removal of the tanks, in October 2017, petroluem contaminated soil was removed from the excavation to a maximum depth of 23 feet bgs.  Approximately 2,275 tons of contaminated soil were removed and disposed at Riverbend Landfill.  Groundwater in the excavation was believed to be runoff due to rain events and did not recharge after evacuation. In November 2017, borings were advanced near the former UST excavation to characterize groundwater.  Benzene was detected in three of five grab samples at concentrations ranging from 6.1 to 470 ppb.  Gasoline was detected in two groundwater samples at concentrations of 510 and 2,700 ppb.  Although the maximum benzene concentration exceeded the residential RBC for the vapor intrusion pathway, the arithmetic mean of the groundwater data did not exceed residential RBCs for the VI pathway.
Proximity to a power pole near the north side of the former remedial excavation prevented the complete removal of soil contamination.  However, 28 of 31 confirmation samples did not contain detectable levels of gasoline, diesel or heavy oil hydrocarbons.  The highest concentration of gasoline detected in the confirmation samples was 180 ppm. Prior to backfilling, ORC was applied to the floor of the excavation.  The residual pocket of soil contamination is limited in extent and volume and not considered to pose unacceptable vapor intrusion risks.
The site properties are provided with drinking water.  Although deep groundwater in the general vicinity of the site has a beneficial use as drinking water, shallow groundwater is not considered to have a beneficial use.  An out of service brick-lined dug well located on Tax Lot 1400 was permanently  decommissioned. Site assessment has shown shallow groundwater contamination is limited to the site boundaries.
Residual contamination related to the release from the UST system does not pose unacceptable risks to future residents.  The site qualifies for an unrestricted NFA determination. (JKS 1-5-18).",141332.0,24468.0,JSCHATZ,2018-01-25 10:39:12.613
