47631,26-16-1442,26,16,1442,,2016-10-28,2016-10-28 15:54:03.190,Rumbaugh,5714 SE Lincoln St,PORTLAND,97215,,False,2.0,,1.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,,,,143192.0,24922.0,CSALNAV,2017-07-26 15:40:27.853
47632,24-16-1443,24,16,1443,12532.0,2016-10-20,2016-10-28 17:55:51.877,Alice Howe Property,6492 PORTLAND RD NE,SALEM,97305,(503) 559-5400,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"discovered tank, registered.  To be decommissioned 10/24/2016.",NW RV Sales,"(MOTT 12/27/16) A Phase I completed in August 2016 identified that the site was previously used a service station from at least 1964 to 1973. Based on the findings of the Phase I, a Phase II was completed in August/September 2016 which consisted of a geophysical investigation and the collection of soil and groundwater samples. During the geophysical survey, one UST was identified at approximately 3 feet bgs along the western property boundary. Following discovery of the UST, five soil borings (SB1 through SB5) were advanced near the UST and former service station building.  Three of the five soil borings indicated elevated concentrations of TPH-G and VOCs. One groundwater sample was collected from SB2 and was non-detect for TPH-G and VOCs.  In October/November 2016, two 520-gallon and one 1,000-gallon gasoline USTs were removed from the site. The two 520-gallon USTs were excavated together and the 1,000-gallon UST was excavated separately.  Petroleum impacted soil was observed in the UST excavations at a depths ranging from 6 to 9 feet bgs. Approximately 227.5 tons of impacted soil was removed from the site. A total of 27 final confirmation soil samples were collected from the 520-gallon UST excavation and analyzed for TPH-G (select samples were also analyzed for VOCs and lead).  All of the sample results indicated concentrations below the applicable RBCs with the exception of 2@7’, 3@7’, 20@9.5’ and 27@9.5’ where either TPH-G, benzene, ethylbenzene, naphthalene, or 1,2,4-trimethylbenzene were  detected at concentrations above the occupational leaching to groundwater RBCs. However, a groundwater sample collected from this area did not indicate the presence of these compounds.  Additionally, ethylbenzene was detected above the occupational vapor intrusion into buildings RBC in soil sample 2@7’; however, soil sample 2@7’ is located over 300 feet from the onsite buildings, along the property line, beneath a fence, and adjacent to a ditch; therefore, it is unlikely that a building would ever be within 50 feet of this sampling location. (MOTT 03/29/17) The site was issued a NFA letter on March 29, 2017.",143097.0,0.0,MOTT,2017-03-30 11:00:55.370
