Regulated tank = 2340-gallon Bunker C.",,"The site appears to have been first developed between 1930 and 1950 as a butter and cheese manufacturer before being converted into a commercial retail building in 1971. The site has been operated as a bookstore since about 2006. Contamination is the result of releases from a diesel/heavy fuel oil tank that collapsed in on itself beneath the northwest corner of the property. Soil in the western half of the site and extending onto adjacent property to the west (305 NE 6th S) remains impacted with diesel/heavy fuel oil and related compounds at a depth between 5 and 16 feet. Groundwater beneath much of the site and extending beneath the E Street right of way is also impacted by this release. After the tank collapsed, it was removed, along with about 20 cubic yards of the most heavily contaminated soil. Nine soil borings were completed to collect soil and groundwater samples across the site, along with a soil gas sample near the former tank pit. Some chemicals associated with gasoline were detected in the soil gas sample, which DEQ believes are unrelated to the leaking diesel/heavy fuel oil tank. Consistent with the ¬¬Central Commercial District zoning, the site and surrounding properties are expected to remain commercial businesses, or mixed use buildings where the first floor is only commercial. All properties in the vicinity are connected to the municipal water system and are expected to remain connected. An opportunity to comment on the proposed file closure was given to all surrounding and impacted properties. No comments were received.",159738.0,0.0,CRICHAR,2020-07-23 13:47:53.493
