47873,26-17-0013,26,17,0013,,2017-01-06,2017-01-06 09:01:44.970,"Morrison Built, LLC",5027 SE 59th Ave,PORTLAND,97206,,False,7.0,,1.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"15,310 ppm",,,143887.0,25164.0,CSALNAV,2018-05-29 13:22:05.903
47874,20-17-0014,20,17,0014,,2017-01-03,2017-01-06 10:02:27.350,Plummer Charitable Trust Property,4251 Franklin Blvd,EUGENE,97403,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,Former gasoline UST located in Glenwood. The UST was apparently decommissioned prior to 1992 and we recently completed soil and groundwater assessment for the property.  Gasoline range petroleum hydrocarbons were reported in soil and groundwater samples.  ... Dan Landry 1/3/2017,,"(MOTT 06/09/17) In September 2010, a Phase I identified three drywells, approximately 3.5 feet by 3.5 feet in size, located inside the southern portion of the site building and the potential for a gasoline UST located near the northern property boundary.  The UST was presumably used to fuel distribution vehicles and was reportedly removed in 1992 (no decommissioning report). A geophysical survey completed in May 2011 confirmed that the UST had been removed from the site.  Multiple investigations consisting of soil and groundwater samples collected from beneath and adjacent to the three drywells and near the former UST area were conducted between 2011 and 2016. Elevated concentrations of PCBs and lead were detected in soil and groundwater immediately beneath the three drywells and elevated concentrations of TPH-G were detected in soil and groundwater near the former UST area. Two sub-slab soil gas samples were collected from beneath the building slab in 2012 (no VOCs were detected above the residential soil gas RBCs). The three drywells along with approximately 4.79 tons of impacted sediment/soil was removed from the site in December 2016. Concentrations of TPH-G and lead remain in soil above the leaching to groundwater RBCs. TPH-G, lead, and total PCBs remain in site groundwater at concentrations above the ingestion and inhalation from tapwater RBCs; however, the extent of groundwater impacts is limited to groundwater directly beneath the site, and groundwater is not used or likely to be used for beneficial use in the future. (MOTT 09/08/17) The site was issued a NFA on 9/8/2017.",143890.0,0.0,GDECK,2017-09-13 11:16:29.177
