48040,26-17-0180,26,17,0180,,2017-03-01,2017-03-02 08:21:29.450,"Bujak, Shane",4055 N Montana Ave,PORTLAND,97227,,False,0.0,,0.0,,False,True,False,0.0,False,False,False,False,False,,,,144482.0,25325.0,IGAFFNE,2017-03-02 08:21:29.450
48041,20-17-0181,20,17,0181,5060.0,2017-02-24,2017-03-03 14:29:20.970,COTTAGE GROVE STATE AIRPORT,78833 AIRPORT RD,COTTAGE GROVE,97424,(503) 580-0879 (cell,False,,,,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"(ARL 01/12/2021) From Dylan Eckert's 01/12/2021 Decommissioning Memo: The Department of Environmental Quality (DEQ) has received and reviewed underground storage tank (UST) decommissioning documents for the closure of (1) UST by “filled-in-place” at facility #5060, located at 78833 Airport Rd in Cottage Grove, Oregon. The purpose of this letter is to document UST closure as required by Oregon Administrative Rule (OAR) 340-150-0168(9).

Based on DEQ review of the documents received, the work appears to have met the requirements of OAR 340-150-0168, for decommissioning by “filled-in-place”. DEQ has changed the status of tank BJBD  from “active” to “filled” with a decommissioning date of 02 November 2020.  DEQ database records show the tank permit for BJBD as “terminated”.  The documents received for the decommissioning are on file and available electronically upon request.  

1.    Analytical results of (1) of (7) soil samples are reported to have detectable levels of petroleum hydrocarbons (by NWTPH-HCID). 

2.    Water was reported as not being encountered during the decommissioning. 

This site is associated with open LUST Project number 20-17-0181. ",,,5710.0,0.0,ALEIDEL,2021-01-12 20:14:31.750
48042,26-17-0182,26,17,0182,,2017-03-06,2017-03-06 14:47:59.250,3024 NE MLK Blvd,3024 NE MLK Boulevard,PORTLAND,97212,,False,7.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"Tank was assumed to be a 675-gallon heating oil tank. Upon excavation, the empty tank smelled like it may have formerly contained Stoddard Solvent.",,"In February 2017, a previously unknown underground storage tank was discovered during work related to construction. The 650-gallon Stoddard Solvent Tank was decommissioned by removal. The tank was found to be empty. The tank was recycled the Far West Recycling, Inc. in Beaverton, Oregon. Compliance samples collected below the ends of the tank were analyzed and found to contain petroleum hydrocarbons in the gasoline range at concentrations as high as 1,320parts-per-million. 49.44 tons of petroleum-contaminated soil below the former tank was removed and disposed of at Waste Management Hillsboro Landfill, Inc. in Hillsboro, Oregon. Confirmation soil sampling indicates that all petroleum-contaminated soil was removed from the property relating to this release. The attached site map shows soil sampling locations. Groundwater was not encountered at any time during the Underground Storage Tank decommissioning and soil removal activities. [KMH 6-27-2018]",151500.0,25342.0,KHACK,2018-06-27 16:32:54.423
48043,06-17-0183,06,17,0183,,2017-03-01,2017-03-06 17:45:17.963,Granite Plus,1501 W Central Blvd,COQUILLE,97423,,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"DEQ has determined that no further action is required for this site, because the actions taken previously by the property owner to remove underground storage tanks and evaluate environmental conditions are sufficient to address residual petroleum contamination discovered at the site.",,"•    Petroleum-related contaminants were detected in soil sampled from the site, at concentrations that were below DEQ’s generic risk-based concentrations used for risk screening.  DEQ considers the following exposure pathways applicable for soil at this site: 1) soil ingestion, dermal contact, and inhalation for occupational, construction worker, and excavation worker receptors, 2) vapor intrusion into buildings for occupational receptors, and 3) volatilization to outdoor air for occupational receptors.
•    Petroleum-related contaminants were detected in shallow groundwater, at concentrations that were below DEQ’s generic risk-based concentrations used for risk screening of groundwater.  The exposure pathways considered applicable were: 1) ingestion, dermal contact, and inhalation for construction and excavation workers, 2) vapor intrusion into buildings for occupational receptors, and 3) volatilization to outdoor air for occupational receptors.
•    The extent of groundwater contamination does not appear to extend beyond the boundaries of the property.  

DEQ concludes that petroleum contamination remaining in soil and shallow groundwater at the site is not a threat to public health and the environment.",144510.0,0.0,GDECK,2018-12-26 13:42:07.417
