48059,26-17-0199,26,17,0199,,2017-03-06,2017-03-07 09:39:37.117,"George, Charles",5276 NE 15th Ave,PORTLAND,97211,,False,7.0,,1.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"This appears to be a second tank, either in the same hole or a location secondary to original.  So- it appears to represent a new release on the site.",,,144530.0,25339.0,CSALNAV,2017-07-21 16:10:29.700
48060,20-17-0200,20,17,0200,5592.0,2017-03-02,2017-03-07 10:14:52.667,Oregon Ice Cream Company,885 GRANT ST,EUGENE,97402,360-713-6800,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,True,False,False,False,False,,,"The site has been developed for commercial use as an ice cream manufacturing company since at least 1950, according to tax assessor records. The most recent redevelopment of the site occurred in 2017 in which a portion of one of the buildings on the site was demolished for new construction. During excavation of soil for the new building foundations, a 10,000 gallon underground storage tank (UST) was discovered. The UST is believed to have been used to store fuel for a boiler or furnace. Petroleum contamination was discovered during the 2017 excavation work and was reported to DEQ. The cause of contamination appears to be the result of a leak at a joint in the product piping. Only soil is known to be impacted with heavy oil range impacts at low concentrations, less than 200 mg/Kg. Water in the UST excavation was not analyzed by the licensed UST decommissioning contractor during the decommissioning, which is a requirement of DEQ rules. However, based on the low levels of contamination found in the soil and beneficial uses of land and water for this site, DEQ decided not to require additional groundwater investigation. During the decommissioning, the product line was capped where it entered or passed beneath the adjacent building. The remaining product line has not been assessed. The concentration of petroleum hydrocarbons remaining in soil is below the RBC for diesel fuel as a surrogate because an RBC does not exist for fuel oil.  The concentration was also well below the applicable soil matrix cleanup level of 500 mg/Kg. The property was recently redeveloped for additional commercial use, the LUST area is now covered by a new industrial building, and the site is connected to municipal water. The land use and city water use are not expected to change in the near future. This determination only applies to the release described above and in the area shown on the attached figure. Other current or historical industrial site operations may have impacted soil and/or groundwater and were not evaluated for this LUST project.",21716.0,0.0,CRICHAR,2020-01-22 08:09:20.240
48061,17-17-0201,17,17,0201,,2017-03-06,2017-03-07 13:43:22.880,Leonard F Pike Cr Equiv Trust,117 NE F St,GRANTS PASS,97526,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,True,False,False,False,False,,,"The property was operated as a gas station and lube facility from at least 1950 through 1961 and has been redeveloped into a commercial building currently occupied by a bank. The cause of the release is unknown but is likely the result of a leaking gasoline tank based on the localized gasoline contamination around a former tank pit. The property and surrounding properties are currently commercially zoned, connected to municipal water, and this decision assumes these uses will not change in the future.
•    There is limited contamination to soil and groundwater which appears to be restricted to just the former tank pit area. Based on ground penetrating radar and site investigation it appears that the tank(s) were removed and some contaminated soils were excavated at an unknown time. Contaminated soil and groundwater remain onsite to the west under the parking lot of the current commercial building. Soil samples were taken from the tank pit area.  Less than 150 cubic yards of petroleum contaminated soil is conservatively estimated to remain in the vicinity of the former tank pit. Remaining contamination is below applicable risk based screening levels for commercial use and occupational scenarios for soils and groundwater exposure. Based on sampling results contamination in soil or groundwater does not appear to extend beyond the property boundary in any direction.",144531.0,0.0,GDECK,2017-07-19 16:23:26.583
