48088,26-17-0228,26,17,0228,,2017-03-10,2017-03-10 14:52:39.640,Helen & Timothy Finkle Trust,11909 NE Fargo St,PORTLAND,97220,,False,1.0,,1.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,,,,144622.0,25367.0,CSALNAV,2017-06-01 12:38:57.977
48089,36-17-0229,36,17,0229,,2017-03-13,2017-03-14 10:16:55.183,Carlton Veterinary Hospital,230 S Pine St,CARLTON,97111,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,True,False,False,False,False,"Tanks were part of a filling station/auto repair shop that closed in 1960. The tanks were not used after 1960. According to the current owner, who removed the tanks 25 years ago, the tanks were empty when he removed them.  Erik Anderson 3/13/2017",,"The site was first developed as a gasoline filling station in 1931 and was later expanded to include an automotive service garage.  The station closed in 1959 and remained vacant until the late 1970s when the property was redeveloped as a veterinary clinic. In 1991, two 1,500-gallon underground fuel tanks were removed from the street side of the building.  The tanks were reported to be in good condition, and there was no evidence of contamination.  However, no soil samples were collected at the time of decommissioning.  In 2017, soils and groundwater near the former tank pits and dispenser island were investigated.  Soil gas around the former dispenser island, the apparent source zone, was also investigated.  No soil was removed from the site.  Gasoline contaminated soil remains onsite at concentrations up to 1,960 milligrams per kilogram in the vicinity of the former dispenser island.  There is no apparent contamination around the former tank pits. Gasoline contaminated groundwater remains beneath the former dispenser island at concentrations up to 458 micrograms per liter. Soil Gas samples show slight detections of gasoline compounds in the vicinity of the former dispenser island.  However, these detections are well below risk based screening levels. The property is expected to remain commercial use with residential property in the area.  A beneficial use determination of groundwater has shown that there is no current or likely future use of groundwater within the vicinity of the site. The contamination may extend offsite into the Pine Street right-of-way by no more than roughly ten feet. This contamination is at levels below DEQ’s applicable risk based concentrations. Public notice was given to the City of Carlton Public Works Department for potential impacts to the storm sewer system.  No comments were received.",144694.0,0.0,GDECK,2017-06-19 16:13:14.473
48090,15-17-0230,15,17,0230,12606.0,2017-03-07,2017-03-14 15:48:32.283,Claycomb Plaza,40 N Main St,ASHLAND,97520,541-944-2111,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,True,False,False,False,False,"10/8/19 - Site Investigation complete.  Confirmation samples collected at the limits of the excavation and from additional borings around the site indicated residual soil and groundwater impacts from gasoline and diesel fuel. The contamination in both soil and groundwater is the highest around boring S-17, roughly in the center of the northern part of the property and at depths between 10 and 15 feet below ground surface.  No notable soil contamination extended off site, but groundwater impacts extend to the northwest of the property and beneath Bluebird Park.  Concentrations diminish away from the former tank locations and are below human health risk-based concentrations at the property limit. Further, the data indicated no risk to Ashland Creek.

EES was recorded with a Contaminated Media Mgmt Plan.  Public comment received by ODOT concurring with CMMP restriction. Conditional NFA being issued.


The tanks were removed March 12-15, 2019.  

3/13/19 A. Farris.  One large diesel tank (formerly thought to be HOT, but now believed to be diesel), 1 gasoline tank, dispenser island, and piping.  Also investigating another area of interest found on GPR. 

3/7/19 per Dave Fawcett, tanks are scheduled to be pulled within the month. Has notified UST staff.  

Dave Fawcett of Coleman Creek is dealing with this site in Ashland where a HOT was discovered during utility upgrades. They then did a Phase I/Phase II of the property and discovered what appears to be another UST using GPR. They collected soil samples and found diesel and lube oil in soil near the suspected second UST. Dave filled out a release form and 20 day report (which are attached). Dave has the Phase II report with the lab data confirming the release.   the property owner is currently planning to redevelop the property and is waiting until his plans are complete to decide what to do with the tanks. … Mallory Ott 3/8/2017",,"Tanks were removed in March 2019.  Additional site investigation completed during and after the tank removal.  Confirmation samples collected at the limits of the excavation and from additional borings around the site indicated residual soil and groundwater impacts from gasoline and diesel fuel. The contamination in both soil and groundwater is the highest around boring S-17, roughly in the center of the northern part of the property and at depths between 10 and 15 feet below ground surface.  No notable soil contamination extended off site, but groundwater impacts extend to the northwest of the property and beneath Bluebird Park.  Concentrations diminish away from the former tank locations and are below human health risk-based concentrations at the property limit. Further, the data indicated no risk to Ashland Creek.

EES was recorded with a Contaminated Media Mgmt Plan.  Public comment received by ODOT concurring with CMMP restriction. Conditional NFA being issued.",144714.0,0.0,GDECK,2019-10-21 16:31:49.330
