48757,27-17-0897,27,17,0897,,2017-07-28,2017-08-03 14:37:42.430,"Kroenig, Albert",11165 Kroenig Rd,AMITY,97101,,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,False,False,False,False,False,"Farm use tank.

7/26/17 Discovered field evidence of petroleum contaminated soil in contact with groundwater after tank removal.  Reported 7/28/17 after laboratory confirmation (E floor = 1,600 ppm; W floor = 260 ppm) ... Bill Burnett, 7/28/2017.

8/7/17 After pulling the tank at 11165 Kroenig Rd, I entered the excavation to collect soil samples.  When I used a shovel to collect soil samples, water filled the areas where I had dug for my samples.   I collected both samples and reported this site to the DEQ as a groundwater site.  I returned two days later to collect a sample to identify the vertical extent of petroleum impact and backfill the excavation.  As I advanced the auger, nowater entered the boring, so the water must have been perched in a very small void beneath the tank. - Dana Thompson   

Perched water still counts as groundwater in the strictest sense.  20 Day report Indicates there was a sheen or odor observed on water in the excavation. PM should make determination about requiring water samples. - CAR 8/7/17",,"On July 26, 2017, diesel contamination in soil was discovered during decommissioning by removal of a 340-gallon UST on an agricultural use property located on tax lot 200 at 11165 Kroenig Road, Amity Oregon. Two decommissioning confirmation soil samples collected at 6.5 feet were analyzed for hydrocarbon identification by NWTP-HCID. The samples contained diesel-range hydrocarbons at 1,600 and 260 ppm. The DEQ’s allowable RBC for residential ingestion of diesel in soil is 1,200 ppm. Neither sample contained gasoline- or heavy oil-range hydrocarbons above lab reporting limits. Additionally, ethylbenzene and naphthalene exceeded the RBC for soil leaching to groundwater. Samples did not exceed vapor intrusion into buildings or volatilization to outdoor air RBCs. Follow up subsurface sampling completed near the former UST identified limited diesel-range hydrocarbons in soil. Diesel was detected in soil at two locations 8 feet west of the former UST at depths of 9.5 and 14 feet bgs at 810 and 140 ppm, respectively. A boring was completed on the property to 24 feet bgs and did not encounter groundwater. During the assessment an unregistered water well was identified on the property. The well was sampled and found to not contain diesel-range hydrocarbons above lab reporting limits. On October 2, 2018, the water well was abandoned by an Oregon licensed driller. The property and surrounding area are connected to a local municipal water supply. According to the decommissioning report, there does not appear to be beneficial use of the shallow groundwater table in the vicinity of the site. Depth to shallow groundwater in the area is expected to be greater than 20 feet bgs. Soil contamination remains adjoining to the north of a farm building at the southern end of the tax lot and appears limited to the area of the former UST. A soil cleanup was not conducted at the property and groundwater does not appear to be impacted. Based on findings, RBC pathway exceedances for residential ingestion of soil for diesel and for ethylbenzene and naphthalene leaching to groundwater, do not appear to pose a risk to human health or the environment. This LUST file was issued a NFA letter in April 2019.",146605.0,0.0,GDECK,2019-05-07 14:19:06.637
