49717,26-18-0369,26,18,0369,,2018-04-04,2018-04-10 11:17:29.377,"Brandon, Lizzie",50 NE Stafford St,PORTLAND,97211,,False,1.0,,1.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Soil Solutions;  gabby@soilsolutionsenvironmental.com;  ETE - 10,000 ppm
WTE - 7,000 ppm",,,150236.0,27059.0,MCOBB,2018-10-09 15:18:30.730
49718,26-18-0370,26,18,0370,,2018-04-09,2018-04-10 11:26:00.993,"Bashan, Baruch",7124 SE 32nd Ave,PORTLAND,97202,,False,2.0,,1.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Soil Solutions;  gabby@soilsolutionsenvironmental.com;  S1 - 3,300 ppm",,,150237.0,27076.0,LDIMOCK,2018-07-27 09:02:46.133
49719,15-18-0371,15,18,0371,,2018-04-05,2018-04-10 11:26:51.290,Collier Creative Center,50 N FIR ST,MEDFORD,97501,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,A Phase I identified old USTs at the site so the consultant went back and did a Phase II. During the Phase II sampling they encountered soil with petroleum staining/odor indicating the tanks had leaked at some point. The tanks are still in the ground so they are working with Eric Clough on how to handle that aspect. ~M. Ott,,"The property has been developed since at least 1898. A Sanborn Fire Insurance Map identifies a small “wagon parts” building on the NW corner of the property and a pair of railroad tracks along the east side of the site labeled Rogue River Trolley. A 1927 map indicates a gas station was present near the southwest corner of the site. Historical documents list the gas station as Valley Fuel Co, Standard Station, and Oregon Gasoline Station. By 1950 the railroad tracks were gone. Crater Lake Motors Auto Dealers occupied the property by 1946. The property was redeveloped around 1989 to its current configuration with the Scan Design retail furniture store and warehouse occupying the majority of the property. Interviews from 1989 remodel personnel indicated the following occurrences during redevelopment: two USTs were found in the area of the historical gas station (current parking lot) and were backfilled with gravel; a small oil tank was removed from the parking lot and had “leaked into the storm drain, with DEQ involvement,” and a former lube oil pit near the NE middle portion of the building was filled-in with concrete. Documentation supporting proper response (i.e., confirmation sampling) to these occurrences have not been identified.

Sampling completed during March and April 2018 encountered low-level contaminants across
the site. Sources of contamination include the former UST nest area (reportedly with two filled
USTs), historical property use as an automotive dealership and repair, and historical rail line.
Contaminants include petroleum hydrocarbons and its constituents.

Detections in soil and groundwater did not exceeded applicable RBCs for the site and vicinity.
As such, there does not appear to be human health risks associated with the identified contamination at the site. DEQ’s July 8, 2019 No Further Action Determination was devised for the historical gas station near the southwest corner of this site and indicates that unknown contamination may be encountered if the onsite building were to be renovated or demolished in the future",150238.0,0.0,GDECK,2019-07-10 14:20:24.017
