50312,24-18-0964,24,18,0964,4072.0,2018-08-08,2018-08-16 11:48:54.273,FUEL N MART,21690 DOLORES WAY NE,AURORA,97002,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"BB&A Environmental;  smomo@bbaenv.com
Lab results: Soil: 55,000Dx, 4,800Gx; GW: 6,060Dx, 1,110Gx",,"(NAS 10/30/2019)The site was developed as a petroleum fuelling facility in 1983.  The site is currently owned by Jubitz Corporation and is occupied by a convenience store and retail/commercial fuelling facilities. Bergeson-Boese & Associates (BBA) reported a petroleum release to DEQ in August 2018 after discovering gasoline and diesel contamination in soil and groundwater beneath the site during a Phase II Environmental Assessment (ESA). The Phase II ESA was completed in July 2018 by BBA on behalf of Jubitz Corporation. The contamination was likely related to a past leak in the diesel fuel piping that was discovered and repaired in November 2014.  Wohler's Environmental completed a Supplemental Phase II ESA to further define and evaluate the risks of the contamination remaining beneath the site. Contaminants of concern included gasoline, diesel, petroleum-related volatile organic compounds, and polynuclear aromatic hydrocarbons.  Remaining contamination beneath the site was identified in the soil and groundwater at depths between 14.5 and 30 feet below ground.  The contamination was limited to the southern area of the underground storage tank pit and was not migrating off of the site. Based on the concentrations of the remaining contamination, DEQ determined that the contamination did not pose a risk to the current use of the property, but could pose a potential future risk if groundwater at the site was used for drinking water purposes, the site was redeveloped for residential use or excavation work was conducted in the contaminated area.  To address the potential future risk from the remaining contamination, a deed restriction was recorded on the property that restricts residential use or development, groundwater use, and construction or excavation work without first preparing a contaminated media management plan. The deed restriction was recorded with Marion County on 10/9/2019. A public notice was sent to adjacent property owners on 9/25/2019.  There were no comments or objections to the closure.  With the implementation of the deed restriction, the remaining contamination does not pose a risk to the current or future uses of the property and DEQ plans to issue a no further action determination for the site.",20727.0,27692.0,NSAWKA,2020-01-16 09:02:31.170
50313,02-18-0965,02,18,0965,,2018-08-10,2018-08-16 14:05:12.303,Peavy Arboretum,8392 to 8399 NW Peavy Arboretum Road,CORVALLIS,97330,541-737-3562,False,1.0,,4.0,,False,False,True,,True,False,False,False,False,"Site address is 8392 TO 8399 NW PEAVY ARBORETUM RD, Benton County Assessor ID 104310001900

Farm/personal use; no commercial use.

ODF owns the property, but OSU has been leasing the property/facility since 1964. OSU is in the process of purchasing the property from ODF, and the Phase I/II identified contamination. The tank(s) are long gone, unknown what was in tank(s) or where for sure it was located. GW has not encountered up to 20' in push probes. 11/6/18 CAR",,"The site was a tree nursery from 1936 to 1964 run by Oregon Department of Forestry before being leased to Oregon State University for research. A release of gasoline occurred from or near a historic underground storage tank. Soil between 7 and 21 feet below ground is impacted with gasoline in an oval shaped area approximately 90 feet long by 50 feet wide extending from the former tank pit to the southeast. Groundwater to the southeast from the former tank pit is also impacted with gasoline and related constituents in an area approximately 140 feet long by 50 feet wide. This contamination has not affected the onsite drinking water well. A second onsite well located by the lodge about a quarter mile southeast of the plume is unlikely to be impacted by this release. The site is assumed to remain open to the public and a research facility for OSU. The site is expected to continue to use the onsite drinking water well. If in the future an additional or replacement drinking water well is to be installed it should not be located within 100 feet of the known area of groundwater impacts. The attached figure shows an approximate 100-foot buffer zone. ECSI Site #6308 investigated contamination resulting from a telephone pole treatment area at the north end of the tax lot. Only the areas noted above were investigated. Due to the nature of the site as a research facility various chemicals may have been used, applied, or stored at the site that were not investigated.",152070.0,27702.0,CRICHAR,2019-08-23 08:46:25.280
50314,16-18-0966,16,18,0966,,2018-08-16,2018-08-16 14:33:21.267,AG West Supply,36 NW A Street,MADRAS,97741,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,HydroCon LLC;  bpletcher@hydroconllc.net;  Release discovered during due diligence site assess soil and groundwater sampling for a potential property  sale. It has been reported that the USTs were removed in the early 1990s. The site soil and groundwater may also be contaminated by upgradient LUST site  (16-91-0101).,ECSI 5522,,152071.0,27724.0,TENGLAN,2019-12-03 11:12:19.597
