50349,10-18-1001,10,18,1001,4371.0,2018-08-21,2018-08-23 16:39:24.120,JITNEY SHOP RFP II,10650 OLD HWY 99S,DILLARD,97432,541-679-2695,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Observed field evidence of a petroleum release during the Jitney Shop UST decommissioning project field work. The lab report confirmed releases at both the diesel tank and from one or more of the five lube oil tanks (the lab results indicated diesel, gas, and heavy oil, as well as chlorinated solvents, PCE, TCE). Reported release to DEQ. The decommissioning work was not completed as a result of encountering the field indications of a release. We are actively working on plans to complete the decommissioning work and address impacted soil present within the tank excavations, however, there are building structural issues to be worked out before additional work commences. We will provide an update once we schedule the work to finalize the tank decommissioning. Nate Webb, Roseburg Forest Products 8/23/18

drilling for additional site assessment is scheduled for 11-12 Oct-2018 (Esc)",10-11-0161 JITNEY SHOP - RFP,"During 2011, contaminated soils were identified from a damaged spill bucket for a 12,000-gallon diesel UST at the Jitney Shop. LUST file No. 10-11-0161 was opened. Reportedly, one cubic yard of contaminated soil was removed, and the LUST file was closed. The 12,000-gallon diesel tank remained in use. LUST 10-11-0161 did not include assessment for the other five, smaller USTs located in a separate area in the Jitney Shop. During August 2018, soil and groundwater contamination was discovered during decommissioning assessments at both UST system locations. This resulted in opening LUST file No. 10-18-1001. Additional testing completed found contamination was limited to within the two UST areas. During January 2019, all six USTs, located in two separate areas within the Jitney Shop, were decommissioned by removal. Contaminated soils were also removed from the UST pits and confirmation samples were collected. Water entering both UST excavations did not recharge after its removal, and was not considered to be groundwater. Soil containing low levels of diesel and oil-range hydrocarbons remains in each of the former UST areas. Concentrations are far below site applicable risk-based concentrations and should not present an unacceptable risk to workers at the site.",20935.0,0.0,GDECK,2020-05-29 16:25:55.050
