50444,20-18-1096,20,18,1096,,2018-09-17,2018-09-20 12:00:37.890,Yord Dee Property,901 Franklin Boulevard,EUGENE,97403,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,3.0,False,False,False,False,False,"Stantec;  leonard.farr@stantec.com;  During a test pit excavation of a geophysical anomaly, an approximately 30-gallon UST and associated gasoline release was discovered. 

9/20/18 There was a 2nd 30-gal tank also discovered, but had no fluids and no indication of a release. Based on the size of the tanks and given that the property previous had an auto repair facility, they are likely used oil and/or solvent tanks. Per Mark Drouin, tanks are considered regulated and need to be registered. JClaussen","ESCI#1455-Laurita Ruth, 901-903 Franklin Blvd. NFA 1995","The site was purchased by the Ruth family in 1944 from Eugene Sand and Gravel.  In 1946, the site was developed as a truck service and sales facility and continued operations into the mid-1980s.  At the time, the building consisted of an office, shop, and motor repair shop. Records indicate that two underground storage tanks (USTs), one 675-gallon and another 1,000-gallon UST were believed to be present on the site.  In 1993, the City of Eugene purchased the property from Laurita Ruth.  

Several environmental site assessments were completed from 1990 to 1994.  The site was listed in DEQ’s ECSI database (ECSI #1455) in 1993.  DEQ issued a No Further Action (NFA) determination on August 10, 1995 for the ECSI site based on the available information in the assessment reports at that time.

The property changed hands over the years.  The City of Eugene sold the property to Yord Dee, LLC in 2014.  Most recently, the University of Oregon purchased the property in August 2018.  
•    A Phase I Environmental Site Assessment completed in March 2018 identified underground storage tanks might still be present beneath the site.  During site assessment activities in September 2018, two small 30-gallon USTs were decommissioned from the SE portion of the property.  Minor gasoline staining of soil was noted beneath the western UST.
•    Soil samples were collected from four locations during site assessment activities.  Low levels of gasoline range hydrocarbons and lead were found in soil samples collected from the site. 
•    Groundwater was not encountered during site assessment activities and groundwater samples were not collected at that time. 
•    The concentration of gasoline detected in one soil sample collected from a depth of 12 feet below ground surface (bgs) beneath the leaking UST was slightly above DEQ’s Risk Based Concentration (RBC) for urban residential vapor intrusion into buildings pathway.  It appears that the release was very small and affected a localized area in the vicinity of the former tanks. Therefore, this contamination should not present an unacceptable risk to future occupants at the site.
•    Gasoline and naphthalene were also detected in the soil sample collected beneath the leaking UST at levels above the residential and occupational receptors for the leaching to groundwater pathway.  Lead was detected in two soil samples collected from 9 feet bgs in two separate excavations.  However, the leaching to groundwater pathway is considered incomplete",152366.0,27855.0,CJACOBS,2019-02-20 16:11:46.357
