50548,02-18-1200,02,18,1200,10353.0,2018-10-17,2018-10-18 15:16:54.427,FORMER MONROE FUEL,125 South 5th Street,MONROE,97456,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,3.0,False,False,False,False,False,"6/20 no public comments received.  proceeding with nfa.

5/2020 - DEQ Staff Memorandum Recommending No Further Action is completed and going for public comment.

2/2020- UST decommissioning completed alogn with investgiation of other areas of interest.  Soil contamination encountered below relevant risk-based concentrations.  No groundwater contamination identified.  Site being recommended for NFA.

10/2019 - Phase II completed identfying soil contamination near USTs.  Recommended UST decommissiioning and further investigation.

BB&A Environmental;  smomo@bbaenv.com

1/28/19 Email from Mary Camarata to City of Monroe Rick Hohnbaum:
Hi Rick,

DEQ understands the City Councilors are meeting this evening to discuss whether or not to make the City’s Monroe Fuel gas station operational or decommission the tanks. You asked me about the costs associated with making the gas station operational. You have received information from Dan Talley (SME) that repairing the fueling system will run between $38,000 and $52,000. This cost estimate wouldn’t include fixing the canopy. Below is a list of things the City must complete before the City can operate the gas station. We cannot estimate the cost to complete these activities. Please consult with SME. 

DEQ Tank Program:
•Perform tanks tightness tests
•Verify operational spill and overfill projection equipment
•Ensure tank leak detection equipment (tank monitor) is operational
•Conduct a cathodic/corrosion test on the tanks
•Empty the tanks because the City is in temporary closure
•Pay the 2019 UST permit fee of $490

DEQ Leaking Underground Storage Tank (LUST) Program:
The City’s contractor (BB&A) reported petroleum contamination associated with the gas station. Prior to starting up the gas station, the City should determinate if there is a leak in the system and then repair the leak. DEQ will work with the City on when and how the City wants to address the release. 

If the City decides to decommission the tanks and address the release, DEQ will help the City apply for a funds under the Cascade West Coalition EPA grant. The decommission route will probably be least cost to the City because of the grant.  The tanks can be decommissioned in place. This option requires DEQ approval of a site assessment sampling plan and sampling prior to decommissioning. Work must be done by a licensed DEQ decommissioning service provider. https://www.oregon.gov/deq/FilterDocs/USTDecom30DayNotice.pdf",,"A phase I Environmental Site Assessment was complete in 2017 that identified gasoline underground storage tanks, a dispenser island, and associated piping from the Former Monroe Fuels fuel and service station.  In addition, there was an above ground storage tank for diesel.  In the fall of 2019, a Phase II assessment was completed that indicated soil contamination associated with the USTs so in late 2019, the USTs were decommissioned by removal, alogn with removal of impacted soil, the dispenser islands, and all associated piping.  The AST was investigated and other areas around the site that might have been impacted by historical operations.  No additional sources of significant contamination were encountered.  This is summarized in detail in DEQ's Staff Memorandum Recommending No Futher Action, May 2020.",152621.0,27967.0,GDECK,2020-12-07 16:48:56.140
