S@64"" = 17,500 ppm Dx",,,155803.0,28839.0,MCOBB,2020-01-10 13:51:59.180
51385,26-19-0627,26,19,0627,1379.0,2019-07-01,2019-07-03 16:37:42.770,Jackson Energy 5009,3515 SE 122nd Avenue,PORTLAND,97236,,False,,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Alpha Environmental Services, Inc;  jim@alphaenvironmental.net;  Eric Kelley was notified about suspected release on 6/20/19.",,The site is suspected to be a former service station in the northern portion of the property currently occupied by Jackson’s Shell Gas Station. In June 2019 ODOT discovered eight tanks in a common nest in the area of a planned intersection realignment. There were five 100-gallon vertically orientated tanks on the Jackson Property and three similarly oriented tanks in the ODOT Right of Way. At least one tank is believed to have leaked based on evidence of corrosion. The tank identified as T-4 had the highest levels of heavy oil in the soil beneath it at the time of tank decommissioning. Up to 575 ppm of heavy oil was identified in the soil beneath tank T4 at 5 feet below ground surface and that petroleum contaminated soil was left in place. No groundwater was encountered during tank decommissioning or sampling. Gasoline and diesel were not detected in the soil samples. The tanks were decommissioned and recycled at ORRCO. [KH 1-14-2020],155843.0,28841.0,EKELLEY,2020-10-13 16:01:10.980
51386,26-19-0628,26,19,0628,1379.0,2019-07-01,2019-07-03 16:48:34.063,3515 SE 122nd Avenue - ODOT Right of Way,3515 SE 122nd Avenue,PORTLAND,97236,,False,,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,,,"The site is suspected to be a former service station in the northern portion of the property currently occupied by Jackson’s Shell Gas Station. In June 2019 ODOT discovered eight tanks in a common nest in the area of a planned intersection realignment. There were three 100-gallon vertically orientated tanks in the ODOT right of way and five similarly oriented tanks on the Jackson property. At least one tank is believed to have leaked based on evidence of corrosion. The tank identified as T-3, located closest to the east property boundary of the Jackson property, had the highest levels of heavy oil in the soil beneath it at the time of tank decommissioning. Up to 781 ppm of heavy oil was identified in the soil beneath tank T3 at 5 feet below ground surface. No groundwater was encountered during tank decommissioning or sampling. Gasoline and diesel were not detected in the soil samples. Approximately 0.86 tons of petroleum contaminated soil was removed and disposed of at Hillsboro Landfill in Hillsboro, Oregon. The tanks were recycled at ORRCO. Some petroleum contaminated soil was left in place sampled at up to 277 ppm for heavy oil. Residual concentrations of heavy oil meet Soil Matrix Level II cleanup standard of 500 ppm. This site received a NFA on October 11th, 2019 [KH 10-11-2019]",155843.0,0.0,EKELLEY,2020-10-13 16:01:46.310
