51439,10-19-0681,10,19,0681,,2019-07-18,2019-07-18 13:37:13.577,"Reeves, Larry",2161 W Foothill Dr,ROSEBURG,97471,,False,7.0,,1.0,,False,True,False,,False,False,False,False,False,First Strike Enviromental;  Spencer@fseco.com;  During heating oil tank decommissioning tank was observed to have corrosion and some visible soil contamination on one side of tank. Excavating and stockpiling PCS for later disposal.,,,156063.0,28898.0,MCOBB,2019-09-13 11:36:21.840
51440,24-19-0682,24,19,0682,308.0,2019-07-18,2019-07-18 13:43:20.383,Bellinger Bros. LLC,315 12th St NE,SALEM,97301,971-645-2331,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Anderson Geological, Inc.;  erik@andersongeo.com;  Minor contamination was discovered in soil in a former heating oil tank pit. Tank was removed in 1997. More significant contamination (gasoline, heavy oil) was discovered in soil around a leaky sump inside of the former service station building. Impact to groundwater unknown. Equipment refusal in gravel before groundwater was encountered.

Originally reported as a HOT site.  Changed to Regulated UST site.

11/26/2019 (Entered by Gerald Gamolo per Alyssa Leidel). Reason for the change, the consultant provided additional sampling information showing petroleum contamination from samples taken around the former tank nest. There is heating oil contamination; however, there is also a leaking sump with petroleum contamination. Therefore, it seems that the best fit for this site would be in the LUST program as a regulated tank site.",,"The site was historically used as a fueling and service station from about 1960 to 1972. Since that time the property has been used for automobile glass repair, automobile repair, and most recently a contract parking lot. Contamination is the result of a leaking heating oil tank as well as leaks and/or spills at a former in-ground vehicle hoist. Localized soil contamination is present on the western end of the heating oil tank. Soil, groundwater, and soil gas is impacted beneath the center of the onsite building and extends beneath the neighboring apartment building. All of the underground storage tanks were removed historically. All known sources of potential contamination were investigated with 23 borings across the site. A little over four tons of impacted soil was excavated from beneath the former vehicle hoist and disposed of at Hillsboro Landfill. Diesel impacted soil remains beneath the former heating oil tank at a depth of 6 to 20 feet below ground. Gasoline and lubricating oils have impacted the soil and groundwater beneath the former vehicle hoist in the center of the onsite building. These impacts remain from about 8 feet below ground down to about 16 feet below ground in soils and the groundwater table is present around 20 feet below ground. A Contaminated Media Management Plan has been prepared for the site and should be followed for all subsurface work that may encounter contamination. The site and all surrounding properties are zoned Retail Commercial, which does allow for conditional use as urban residences. Municipal water is provided to the entire area and all properties are expected to continue to use the city water supply. An opportunity to comment on the proposed closure was given to all neighboring properties down gradient. No comments were received.",156064.0,28899.0,CRICHAR,2020-07-23 13:28:18.827
