Coordinated with Rick Siedemann of RYS Environmental, LLC regarding January 2020 site assessment activities.  RYS submitted results and request for closure late February 2020.","15-90-0161 GORDON TRUCKING, INC.","By 2019 the property was owned by Heartland Express Trucking Inc. Heartland planned to remove the existing two 10,000-gallon USTs and replace them with a single above ground diesel storage tank (AST) for future use at the facility.  Prior to UST decommissioning at the facility, Vortex Geo-Environmental Consulting, LLC (Vortex) completed a preliminary investigation around the UST tank pit.  Sample results reported diesel contamination in shallow soil and groundwater near the tank pit prior to decommissioning activities, which resulted in DEQ opening LUST file No. 15-19-0969. 
In September 2019, Pump Pipe and Tank Services, LLC decommissioned the USTs, product piping and fuel islands. A total of 458.17 tons of diesel contaminated soil were removed from the excavation and disposed of at Dry Creek Landfill; and 56,493 gallons of tank pit water was disposed of by Oil Re-Refining Co. In October 2019, RYS completed an additional hand-auger boring assessment.Rsults indicated that diesel contamination was outside of the tank pit limits.  In January 2020, 15 push-probe borings were completed to further examine the extent of contamination from the diesel release at the site.  Petroleum impacted soil and groundwater is limited to the area of the UST excavation and to the west and southwest of the excavation area.  Sample results were not above applicable RBCs and/or were not above an unacceptbale risk level.",288.0,29194.0,GDECK,2021-02-04 10:47:40.603
51728,03-19-0970,03,19,0970,7261.0,2019-09-27,2019-09-30 13:12:56.543,Cascade Oil,13645 S Holcomb Boulevard,OREGON CITY,97045,,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,,False,False,False,False,False,"Beveridge Geological Services, LLC;  carrie.beveridge@bevgeo.com;  Was a UST facility in the 1970s. Tanks were removed in 1983. Contamination discovered during routine Phase II. A geophysical survey found two pits that could correspond to UST excavations. One location appears to be impacted with diesel and oil. Appears to be caused by impacted backfill placed into pit after USTs were excavated.

A bulk fueling facility and/or gasoline station operated at the site 1974 to 1986.  DEQ records indicate six USTs were removed prior to 1988, although information about subsurface conditions at the time of decommissioing is not available. 
A September 2019 geophysical survey revealed two backfilled excavations, likely former UST pits in the southern portion of the property.  Boring B-3 advanced in the southern pit detected diesel and heavy oil petroleum hydrocarbons and generally very low levels of VOCs and PAHs.  Attempts to delineate contamination in the area of B-3 met with refusal at depths of 8 to 9 feet bgs. Diesel was detected at concentrations as high as 3,480 mg/kg at 12 feet bgs but deeper samples could not be obtained due to refusal.  Groundwater was not encountered in any of the borings.
Only naphthalene exceeded RBCs for the soil leaching to groundwater pathway.  Based on anticipated depth to groundwater and limited volume of contaminated soil, area residents and employees are unlikely to be exposed to contaminants in drinking water.  Site is zoned for General Commercial, although multi-family residential use is allowed by zoning. Site contamination does not pose unacceptable risks under current or likely future use scenarios (JKS 10-8-19).",,"A bulk fueling facility and/or gasoline station operated at the site 1974 to 1986.  DEQ records indicate six USTs were removed prior to 1988, although information about subsurface conditions at the time of decommissioing is not available. 
A September 2019 geophysical survey revealed two backfilled excavations, likely former UST pits in the southern portion of the property.  Boring B-3 advanced in the southern pit detected diesel and heavy oil petroleum hydrocarbons and generally very low levels of VOCs and PAHs.  Attempts to delineate contamination in the area of B-3 met with refusal at depths of 8 to 9 feet bgs. Diesel was detected at concentrations as high as 3,480 mg/kg at 12 feet bgs but deeper samples could not be obtained due to refusal.  Groundwater was not encountered in any of the borings.
Only naphthalene exceeded RBCs for the soil leaching to groundwater pathway.  Based on anticipated depth to groundwater and limited volume of contaminated soil, area residents and employees are unlikely to be exposed to contaminants in drinking water.  Site is zoned for General Commercial, although multi-family residential use is allowed by zoning. Site contamination does not pose unacceptable risks under current or likely future use scenarios (JKS 10-8-19).",157017.0,29198.0,JSCHATZ,2019-10-09 08:48:41.390
