52160,15-20-0158,15,20,0158,12633.0,2020-02-20,2020-02-25 14:33:10.303,101-113 South Grape St,101-113 S Grape St,MEDFORD,97501,541-840-3984,False,1.0,,4.0,,True,False,False,3.0,False,False,False,False,False,"Stantec;  leonard.farr@stantec.com;  The Property was formerly occupied by a Texaco fueling station from at least 1963 through at least 1976 based on information provided in available city directories, historical aerial photographs and Sanborn fire insurance maps. The current owner acquired the property following closing of the fueling station.  The current owner  redeveloped the property in 1978.

DEQ to determine if USTs in the ground need to be registered.... gdeck... 3/11/2020.

UST has been registered (UST # 12633).  To be decommissioned 4/15/2020.

In April, 2020 Stantec comleted UST decommissioning and a limited soil removal event.",,"Between 1963 and 1976, a Texaco fueling station operated at the property but little is known about the historic structures and fuel-dispensing systems since Texaco operated prior to State of Oregon regulatory requirements.  The property now contains one 3,648-square-foot commercial building built in 1978. Current building configuration (from north to south) includes a restaurant, convenience market and two-story office building.  The building occupies the eastern half of the Property.  The western half of the Property is developed with an asphalt surface parking lot. Green Environmental Management (GEM) completed a Phase I Environmental Site Assessment (ESA) in November 2019.  GEM identified a Texaco auto service and retail gasoline fueling operation at the property from 1963 through 1976.  In February 2020, Stantec completed a Focused Phase II ESA at the property to investigate the presence of hydrocarbon and related contaminants identified in the Phase I ESA.  This work was completed without DEQ oversight at that time.  The Phase II fieldwork consisted of a geophysical survey to identify the presence and location of any USTs at the property.  Stantec identified the presence of at least one UST beneath the southwest portion of the property adjacent to South Grape Street. In March 2020, the DEQ and Stantec agreed to a UST removal and limited site assessment work plan.  In April 2020, Stantec completed the UST removal and assessment activities.  The UST excavation was limited to the west by the South Grape Street sidewalk and to the north by the active water line to the existing building.  The excavation was extended to the east near the suspected fuel island to a depth of three feet below ground surface (bgs).  The overall excavation was 13.0 feet X 11.5 feet X 9.4 feet deep. The 2020 field sampling activities included the collection of 20 soil samples, three groundwater samples and two soil vapor samples from the beneath the building sub slab.  Only soil samples collected from the western and eastern edge of the UST tank pit excavation in the 7 to 8 feet bgs range exceeded the construction worker in excavation pathway. Groundwater and soil vapor samples were either reported below applicable RBCs or were reported below laboratory MRLs. Soil contamination at the site resides between seven and eight feet bgs.  According to the City of Medford Public Works Derpartment, utility lines beneath the sidewalk and public right-of-way at the property at 3 feet bgs.",159637.0,29644.0,CJACOBS,2020-08-03 12:04:27.173
